window.CISI_DATA = window.CISI_DATA || {};
window.CISI_DATA.derivatives = {"key": "derivatives", "title": "Derivatives", "subtitle": "CISI Level 3", "examFormat": "100 questions - 2h00 - CBT (multiple choice)", "chapters": [{"id": "der-ch1", "number": 1, "title": "Introduction to Derivatives", "examWeight": "17 of 100 questions (~17%)", "summaryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Derivatives</strong> are contracts whose value is derived from an underlying asset (physical or financial). The two core building blocks are <strong>futures/forwards</strong> (an obligation to buy/sell at a future date and price) and <strong>options</strong> (a right, not an obligation, to buy (call) or sell (put)). Four key risks apply across all derivatives: <strong>counterparty risk</strong> (much lower for exchange-traded products due to CCP novation), <strong>liquidity risk</strong>, <strong>market risk</strong>, and <strong>operational risk</strong> (Basel II: \"the risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people and systems, or from external events\"). Derivatives are used for <strong>speculation</strong>, <strong>hedging</strong> (a perfect hedge is risk-free), and <strong>arbitrage</strong> (intertemporal, geographic, and value-chain forms).</p>\n<h4>Gearing</h4>\n<p>Gearing (leverage) means a small outlay controls a much larger exposure, so a small percentage move in the underlying causes a much larger percentage move in the derivative's value. Classic example: a £100,000 flat bought with a £10,000 deposit; a 10% price rise (to £110,000) doubles the equity (£10,000 → £20,000) = a 100% return. Options gearing depends on the premium relative to the underlying price — the smaller the premium, the higher the gearing. Futures gearing arises from the margining system: only a fraction of the contract's face value is posted as initial margin, but profit/loss is calculated on the full contract value (eg, 20% margin on a JPY100 million JGB future).</p>\n<h4>Options</h4>\n<p>A <strong>call</strong> gives the right to buy; a <strong>put</strong> gives the right to sell, at the <strong>strike price</strong>, for a non-refundable <strong>premium</strong>. Exercise styles: <strong>European</strong> (expiry day only), <strong>American</strong> (any day), <strong>Bermudan</strong> (specified dates). Exotics include Asian, lookback, barrier (knock-in/knock-out), binary/digital, chooser, compound, and rainbow options. Long positions have loss limited to the premium paid with unlimited (call) or substantial (put) profit potential; short positions have profit capped at the premium received, with unlimited (call) or substantial (put) loss potential. Break-even for a call = strike + premium; for a put = strike − premium.</p>\n<h4>Futures and Forwards</h4>\n<p><strong>Futures</strong> are standardised, exchange-traded, and fungible (novation via a CCP removes most counterparty risk, and contracts can be closed out by taking an equal and opposite position). <strong>Forwards</strong> are customisable OTC contracts, settled only at maturity, offering flexibility (size, date, delivery point) but carrying greater counterparty and liquidity risk. Both are legal obligations to buy/sell an asset at a fixed future date and price.</p>\n<h4>CFDs</h4>\n<p>Contracts for Differences let investors gain exposure to price movements without owning the underlying, settling the difference between opening and closing prices. They are margin-traded (typically 10–30%), have no fixed maturity (rolled over daily, attracting overnight interest), and avoid stamp duty and standard broker dealing fees. <strong>Spread betting</strong> is a similar product but, in the UK, is treated as gambling and is tax-free, whereas CFD gains are subject to capital gains tax.</p>\n<h4>Swaps</h4>\n<p>Swaps are OTC contracts exchanging cash flows between two parties, usually on a notional principal, and form the largest derivatives market by nominal value. Interest rate swaps can be fixed/floating (plain vanilla/coupon swap), floating/floating (basis swap), or fixed/fixed. Other important types include swaptions, inflation swaps and zero-coupon inflation swaps, currency swaps (hedging, cheaper financing, matching assets/liabilities, speculation), equity swaps (including bullet, variance, and dividend swaps), asset swaps, total return swaps, mark-to-market swaps, commodity swaps (eg, aluminium fixed-for-floating), and structural variants such as amortising, accreting, rollercoaster, and forward start swaps.</p>\n<h4>Derivatives Trading</h4>\n<p>FLEX options blend exchange (low credit risk via central clearing) and OTC (customisable strike/style/expiry) features. EFP/EFS transactions allow OTC positions to be exchanged for futures, reducing credit exposure and margin requirements. Liquidity is measured via volume (contracts traded per day) and open interest (positions still outstanding), plus immediacy, market depth, and resilience. Exchange-traded products offer low counterparty risk, standardisation, and price transparency; OTC products offer flexibility and precise hedging but carry more counterparty and liquidity risk, and less transparency. Market makers (price-makers) quote two-way prices and profit from the spread; price-takers deal on those quotes. The sell-side (brokers, banks) executes and originates business; the buy-side (funds, insurers, hedge funds) invests and takes on risk for returns.</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "1.1 Risk and Reward", "html": "\n<h4>What is a Derivative?</h4>\n<p>A derivative is a contract whose value is <em>derived</em> from an underlying asset. The classic CISI \"sofa\" analogy: agreeing today to buy a sofa for £1,000 in two months is like a <strong>future/forward</strong> (an obligation); paying £30 now for the right (but not the obligation) to buy the sofa for £1,000 later is like an <strong>option</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Long</strong> = agreed to buy at a future date (or holds the right to buy/sell in options).</li>\n<li><strong>Short</strong> = agreed to sell at a future date (or has written/sold the option).</li>\n<li>A <strong>future</strong> is an exchange-traded, standardised contract; a <strong>forward</strong> is the OTC, customisable equivalent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Risks of Derivatives (LO 1.1.1)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Risk</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Counterparty risk</td><td>Risk that the other side cannot/does not fulfil its obligations. Much lower for exchange-traded derivatives because the clearing house (CCP) becomes counterparty to both sides via novation.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liquidity risk</td><td>Risk of being unable to trade a contract/asset because no one will quote a reasonable price; matters most when closing out large positions; low liquidity increases price volatility.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Market risk</td><td>Risk of an adverse price movement relative to your position (eg, a long position loses if price falls). Can be disaggregated by underlying (equity risk, interest rate risk, etc).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Operational risk</td><td>Risk from failures in people, systems, or processes (not used to generate profit). Basel II definition: \"the risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people and systems, or from external events.\" Includes fraud, legal, physical, and environmental risk.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Uses/Rewards of Derivatives (Section 1.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Speculation</strong> – taking a directional view to profit from price movement; short holding periods; high risk; derivatives attractive due to gearing.</li>\n<li><strong>Hedging</strong> – protecting an existing or anticipated position by taking an opposite position in the derivatives market. A <strong>perfect hedge</strong> is risk-free. Effectiveness depends on correlation between the hedge and the underlying portfolio (the <strong>basis</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Arbitrage</strong> – exploiting price differences for the same/related asset between two markets for risk-free profit, realised when prices converge. Three forms:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intertemporal</strong> – prices between contract months (eg, one-month vs six-month LME zinc) out of line.</li>\n<li><strong>Geographic</strong> – identical contracts on different exchanges out of line (eg, SGX vs CME Eurodollar futures).</li>\n<li><strong>Value-chain</strong> – between related products (eg, crude oil vs refined products).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Gearing (LO 1.1.2)</h4>\n<p>Gearing (leverage) measures the cash/initial investment used to establish a derivatives position compared to the full value of the underlying it controls. A small % change in the underlying can cause a much larger % change in the derivative's value because the initial outlay (premium or margin) is small relative to the underlying's face value.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example – Mortgage analogy:</strong> Buy a £100,000 flat with £10,000 deposit + £90,000 mortgage. Flat rises 10% to £110,000. Equity = £110,000 − £90,000 = £20,000, a <strong>100% increase</strong> on the £10,000 invested (vs only a 10% gain if bought outright with cash).</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example – Options gearing:</strong> Buy an XYZ plc 850 call for a premium of 20 when the share price is 800. At expiry the share is 880. Exercise profit = (880 − 850) − 20 = <strong>10</strong>. Return on investment = 10 ÷ 20 = <strong>50%</strong>. Buying the share instead (800 → 880) gives only 80 ÷ 800 = <strong>10%</strong>. But if the share only rises to 808 (below the 850 strike), the option expires worthless — a <strong>100% loss</strong> of the 20 premium — while the share itself still gains 8 ÷ 800 = 1%. The smaller the premium relative to the underlying, the higher the potential gearing.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example – Futures gearing (JGB):</strong> SGX JGB future has a face value of JPY100 million; initial margin is 20% = JPY20 million, but profit/loss accrues on the full JPY100 million.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example – Futures gearing (FTSE 100):</strong> Go long a FTSE 100 future at 7200; margin required = 300 points × £10/point = <strong>£3,000</strong> collateral. Close out at 7260 → profit = 60 points × £10 = <strong>£600</strong> = <strong>20%</strong> return on the £3,000 margin, even though the index itself only moved <1% (60 ÷ 7200).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "1.2 Options", "html": "\n<h4>Definition (LO 1.2.1)</h4>\n<p>An <strong>option</strong> is a contract giving the buyer (holder) the right, but not the obligation, to buy (<strong>call</strong>) or sell (<strong>put</strong>) an underlying asset at a specified price (the <strong>strike/exercise price</strong>) on or before a specified date. The seller (writer) assumes an obligation. Options exist on cash products, OTC forwards, and futures (<em>options on futures</em> — a derivative of a derivative; exercise makes the holder long/short the underlying future at the strike).</p>\n<h4>Key Terminology</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Long / Short</td><td>Buyer (holder/owner) / Seller (writer)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Call / Put</td><td>Right to buy / Right to sell</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Strike (exercise) price</td><td>Price at which the option may be exercised</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Settlement price</td><td>Price used to determine pay-off at expiry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Premium</td><td>Non-returnable cost of the option, paid by holder to writer via broker/clearing house</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In-the-money (ITM) / Out-of-the-money (OTM) / At-the-money (ATM)</td><td>Extent to which the option is in profit/loss vs current underlying price; ATM = breakeven</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intrinsic value (IV)</td><td>Only exists when ITM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extrinsic value</td><td>Premium value beyond intrinsic value (time value, implied volatility, interest rates)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time value</td><td>Extrinsic value reflecting probability of the option becoming/staying ITM; erodes as expiry nears</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Exercise Styles</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>European</strong> – exercisable only on expiry day.</li>\n<li><strong>American</strong> – exercisable on any day up to expiry.</li>\n<li><strong>Bermudan</strong> – exercisable on specified dates only; premium between European (cheapest) and American (most expensive), all else equal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>European and American are \"standard\"/\"plain vanilla\". Asian and Bermudan are \"non-standard\". Common exotics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lookback</strong> – path-dependent; strike set at the lowest/highest price over a preceding period.</li>\n<li><strong>Barrier</strong> – path-dependent; existence/pay-off depends on hitting a predetermined price. <strong>Knock-in</strong> = activates once barrier hit; <strong>knock-out</strong> = ceases to exist once barrier hit.</li>\n<li><strong>Binary/digital</strong> – pays a fixed amount or nothing, based on underlying price at/before maturity.</li>\n<li><strong>Chooser</strong> – holder decides later whether it is a call or a put.</li>\n<li><strong>Compound</strong> – option to buy another option; 4 types (call-on-call, call-on-put, put-on-call, put-on-put); two strikes, two expiry dates.</li>\n<li><strong>Rainbow</strong> – option on multiple underlying assets (basket/multi-asset/correlation option).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>P&L Profiles at Expiry (LO 1.2.2)</h4>\n<p><strong>Long Call</strong> (eg, March ABC plc 700 Call @ 30): pay premium 30p for right to buy at 700p. Worthless if price < 700p. Exercised and profitable above breakeven. <strong>Breakeven = strike + premium = 730p.</strong> Max loss = premium (30p). Max profit = unlimited.</p>\n<p><strong>Short Call</strong> (same contract): receive premium 30p, obligation to deliver if exercised. Breakeven = 730p. Max profit = premium received (30p). Max loss = unlimited.</p>\n<p><strong>Long Put</strong> (eg, March XYZ plc 450 Put @ 17): pay premium 17p for right to sell at 450p. Worthless if price > 450p. <strong>Breakeven = strike − premium = 433p.</strong> Max loss = premium (17p). Max profit = strike − premium (433p), if underlying falls to zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Short Put</strong> (same contract): receive premium 17p, obligation to buy if exercised. Breakeven = 433p. Max profit = premium received (17p). Max loss = strike − premium (433p), if underlying falls to zero.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Position</th><th>Maximum Loss</th><th>Maximum Profit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Long call</td><td>Limited to premium</td><td>Unlimited</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short call</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>Limited to premium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long put</td><td>Limited to premium</td><td>Strike − premium (underlying to zero)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short put</td><td>Strike − premium (underlying to zero)</td><td>Limited to premium</td></tr>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Position</th><th>Expiry price &lt; strike</th><th>Expiry price &gt; strike</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Long call</td><td>Loss = premium</td><td>Gain = (expiry − strike) − premium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short call</td><td>Gain = premium</td><td>Loss = (strike − expiry) + premium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long put</td><td>Gain = (strike − expiry) − premium</td><td>Loss = premium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short put</td><td>Loss = (expiry − strike) + premium</td><td>Gain = premium</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Counterparty Risk</h4>\n<p>An option seller has no counterparty risk once the premium is received (OTM options will not be exercised). The option buyer has counterparty risk relative to the seller only when the option has intrinsic value (ATM or ITM), since the seller must then transact at a loss based on current market price.</p>\n<h4>Payoff diagrams</h4><div class=\"payoff-diagrams-row\"><div class=\"payoff-diagram\" data-type=\"long-call\"></div><div class=\"payoff-diagram\" data-type=\"short-call\"></div><div class=\"payoff-diagram\" data-type=\"long-put\"></div><div class=\"payoff-diagram\" data-type=\"short-put\"></div></div>"}, {"heading": "1.3 Futures and Forwards", "html": "\n<h4>Futures (LO 1.3.1)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>future</strong> is a legal agreement between two parties to make or take delivery of a specific quantity/quality of an asset on a fixed future date, at a price agreed today. Originated in agricultural markets (rice futures, Japan 1730; cotton, Egypt 1861). Futures are exchange-traded contracts (ETCs) traded on organised exchanges (ICE Futures Europe, CME Group, SHFE) with terms standardised in a <strong>contract specification</strong>. Price is the only negotiable element; the exchange sets minimum price movement (<strong>tick size</strong>) and quotation method.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example – wheat futures:</strong> quoted per bushel; minimum movement (full tick size) = 0.25 cents ($0.0025)/bushel; contract = 5,000 bushels, so <strong>full tick value = $12.50/contract</strong>. CBOT wheat delivery months: March, May, July, September, December.</p>\n<p><strong>Fungibility</strong> – contracts with a common underlying and delivery date are identical/substitutable, enabling easy trading, liquidity, and closing out via an equal and opposite position (exiting/closing out the trade).</p>\n<h4>How Futures Work (LO 1.3.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Long (buyer) profits if price rises; short (seller) profits if price falls.</li>\n<li>Advantages: <strong>fungibility</strong> (draws liquidity), <strong>counterparty risk</strong> reduced via <strong>novation</strong> (CCP becomes counterparty to both sides), and <strong>low cost</strong> (low counterparty risk + high liquidity → low brokerage fees).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Long futures P&L:</strong> max loss if underlying falls to zero (lose the full agreed price); reward theoretically unlimited.<br/><strong>Short futures P&L:</strong> mirror image — max loss theoretically unlimited (if price rises); max reward limited to the agreed futures price (if underlying falls to zero).</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example (LMEX):</strong> Investor sells (short) 20 contracts of LME June LMEX futures at 3400 ($10/index point). One month before maturity, price is 3190. Since short, profit = (3400 − 3190) × $10 × 20 contracts = 210 × $10 × 20 = <strong>$42,000 profit</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Forwards (LO 1.3.1)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>forward</strong> is economically similar to a future (legally binding, agreed price today, delivery/settlement at a future date) but is usually traded <strong>OTC</strong> (banks/investment firms) rather than on an exchange, and is settled only on the delivery date. Not typically marked-to-market daily (or if so, profits not paid until maturity), reducing working-capital intensity.</p>\n<p><strong>Example – FX forward:</strong> A French company with future JPY payments, worried EUR will weaken vs JPY, locks in rates today via a series of EUR/JPY forwards tailored to its exact payment dates/amounts. Forward rates derive from the spot rate adjusted for the interest rate differential between the two currencies.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Futures</th><th>Forwards</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Venue</td><td>Exchange-traded</td><td>OTC (usually)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Terms</td><td>Standardised</td><td>Customisable (size, date, grade, delivery point)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Counterparty risk</td><td>Low (CCP/novation)</td><td>Higher (bilateral credit/default risk)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mark-to-market</td><td>Daily</td><td>Usually not, or only settled at maturity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liquidity/fungibility</td><td>High</td><td>Can be low (less fungible)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Margining/collateral</td><td>Initial + variation margin required</td><td>Often better/negotiated terms</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Lower fees</td><td>Higher fees</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Forwards are common in FX (outright forwards) and physical commodities (eg, an airline locking in jet fuel prices).</p>\n<h4>Payoff diagrams</h4><div class=\"payoff-diagrams-row\"><div class=\"payoff-diagram\" data-type=\"long-future\"></div><div class=\"payoff-diagram\" data-type=\"short-future\"></div></div>"}, {"heading": "1.4 Contracts for Differences (CFDs)", "html": "\n<h4>What is a CFD? (LO 1.4.1)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>CFD</strong> lets an investor benefit from capital gains on an underlying index, stock, currency, or commodity without owning it. The investor agrees with a CFD provider to settle the <em>difference</em> between the opening and closing price, multiplied by the contract's value-per-point.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> Buy a FTSE 100 CFD at 7200, close at 7350 (gain of 150 points). If the broker mimics the ICE Futures Europe FTSE contract (£10/point), profit = 150 × £10 = <strong>£1,500</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Key Features</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>No stamp duty or standard broker share-dealing fees (cost efficient); easier way to go \"short\" than physically shorting shares.</li>\n<li>Margin-traded (leveraged) — brokers typically require <strong>10–30%</strong> margin. Often include automatic stop-loss orders.</li>\n<li><strong>No fixed maturity</strong> — position rolled over daily if held open; overnight holding incurs an interest charge. Mostly used for intra-day trading.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Investor sells (shorts) a CFD on 5,000 ABC plc shares at 145p. Sale proceeds = 5,000 × £1.45 = £7,250. Broker's margin (15%) = £1,087.50. Shares fall to 137p; cost to close = 5,000 × £1.37 = £6,850. Profit = £7,250 − £6,850 = <strong>£400</strong>. Return on margin = 400 ÷ 1,087.50 = <strong>36.8%</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Spread Betting (LO 1.4.2)</h4>\n<p>An alternative way to gain CFD-like exposure. Key differences from CFDs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the UK, spread betting is treated as <strong>gambling</strong> — winnings are <strong>tax-free</strong> (no CGT), whereas CFD gains are subject to <strong>capital gains tax</strong>.</li>\n<li>CFDs may charge a commission; spread-betting firms are compensated only through the bid/offer spread they quote.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> FTSE 100 at 7200; spread firm quotes 7250/7275 (3 months forward). Investor places an \"up bet\" (like buying a CFD) of £10/point at 7275. A month later, index at 7350, quote now 7375/7400. Investor closes with a \"down bet\" (like selling a CFD) of £10/point at 7375. Gain = (7375 − 7275) × £10 = <strong>£1,000</strong>, tax-free.</p>\n<p>Both CFDs and spread betting are prohibited or heavily restricted for retail investors in several major jurisdictions (eg, prohibited in the US, China and India; many EU countries restrict/ban spread betting) due to leverage risk.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "1.5 Swaps", "html": "\n<h4>Definition (LO 1.5.1)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>swap</strong> is an OTC derivative contract in which two counterparties exchange cash flows or liabilities from two different financial instruments, usually based on a notional principal. Swaps are the largest derivatives market by nominal value; interest rate swaps are the largest type.</p>\n<h4>Interest Rate Swaps and Swaptions (LO 1.5.2)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed/floating swap</td><td>\"Plain vanilla\"/coupon swap; exchange fixed for floating interest cash flows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Floating/floating swap</td><td>\"Basis swap\"; both legs floating</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed/fixed swap</td><td>Type of FX/currency swap; both legs fixed, in different currencies</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Key dates: <strong>reset date</strong> (floating rate determined), <strong>effective date</strong> (interest starts accruing), <strong>payment date</strong> (cash flow exchange, netted), <strong>termination date</strong> (swap ends). Example: 3-year plain vanilla swap, £6m principal, quarterly payments, fixed vs SONIA, effective 1 Jan 2025 — first payment end of March 2025.</p>\n<p>A <strong>swaption</strong> is an option to enter into a swap by a pre-agreed future date, for an upfront premium — a wholesale, non-retail market.</p>\n<h4>Inflation-Linked Swaps (LO 1.5.3, 1.5.4)</h4>\n<p>An <strong>inflation swap</strong> exchanges cash flows where one/both legs reference an inflation index (UK RPI, eurozone HICP, US CPI); protects against rising prices; maturities typically 5–30 years.</p>\n<p>A <strong>zero coupon inflation swap</strong> has only <em>one</em> cash flow, at maturity (fixed vs floating amount) — no intermediate coupons. Used to hedge/speculate on inflation, value inflation-linked securities (eg, TIPS), and hedge against central bank tightening.</p>\n<h4>Currency Swaps (LO 1.5.5)</h4>\n<p>An FX transaction trading principal and interest in one currency for another over an agreed period. Four main uses: (1) hedging FX exposure, (2) accessing cheaper financing (borrow cheaply domestically, swap into needed currency), (3) matching assets/liabilities (eg, multinationals matching cash inflows/outflows), (4) speculation/arbitrage on rate differentials. Types: fixed-for-floating (different currencies), fixed-for-fixed, floating-for-floating.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> A UK company expecting USD export receipts over 5 years uses a fixed-fixed currency swap (rather than a strip of forwards) to convert USD flows to GBP, protecting against USD depreciation but forgoing upside if USD strengthens.</p>\n<h4>Equity Swaps (LO 1.5.6, 1.5.7)</h4>\n<p><strong>Equity swaps</strong> link payments on one/both legs to an equity/equity index return — used to avoid withholding tax, gain leverage, access restricted emerging markets, or create a synthetic portfolio cheaply. A <strong>bullet swap</strong> pays everything at maturity.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Investor A pays ICE SOFR + 0.05% on $10m notional and receives the Hang Seng return over 6 months (180/360 day count). If SOFR = 5.5%: floating leg = (5.5% + 0.05%) × $10,000,000 × 180/360 = <strong>$277,500</strong> owed by A. If Hang Seng rises 9.3% (23,100 → 25,250): equity leg = 9.3% × $10m = <strong>$930,000</strong> owed by B to A; net = $930,000 − $277,500 = <strong>$652,500 to A</strong>. If Hang Seng instead falls 4.3% (to 22,100): A owes B $430,000 (index fall) + $277,500 (floating leg) = <strong>$707,500 from A to B</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Equity basket swaps</strong> use a non-index, non-standardised basket of shares (always OTC). An <strong>equity forward</strong> is an OTC contract to buy/sell a share, basket, or index at a future date/price agreed today.</p>\n<p><strong>Variance swaps</strong> – one leg pays based on the underlying's <em>realised variance</em> (daily price moves), the other a fixed amount agreed upfront; gives pure volatility exposure without needing delta hedging (unlike options); P&L depends on implied vs actual (realised) volatility. More common in equities than volatility swaps.</p>\n<p><strong>Dividend swaps</strong> – fixed leg (predetermined payment) vs floating leg (actual dividends paid on a share/basket/index), multiplied by a notional number of shares; ignores the underlying price itself. At inception, fixed leg = average expected dividends (zero initial value).</p>\n<h4>Asset and Total Return Swaps (LO 1.5.8)</h4>\n<p><strong>Asset swap</strong> – changes the interest rate/currency exposure of an investment (eg, buy a fixed-rate bond + swap to receive fixed/pay floating = synthetic FRN). Lets investors separate the choice of underlying asset (credit quality, liquidity) from the fixed/floating rate decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Total return swap (TRS)</strong> – one leg pays the <em>total return</em> (capital gain/loss + income) of an asset; other leg pays a floating rate. Lets an investor gain synthetic exposure (eg, to restricted foreign markets) without tying up funds in the physical asset, maintaining liquidity.</p>\n<h4>Mark-to-Market (MTM) Swaps (Section 5.6)</h4>\n<p>Settlements calculated by revaluing (MTM) the swap regularly, paying/receiving the gain/loss since the last settlement — removes built-up credit risk, equivalent to closing out and rewriting the swap at current rates. Applies to single-currency or cross-currency (<strong>FX resettable</strong>) swaps; typically reset quarterly.</p>\n<h4>Commodity Swaps (LO 1.5.9, 1.5.10)</h4>\n<p>Cash flow determined by a commodity's price/return. <strong>Fixed-for-floating commodity swap</strong> – no physical exchange of the commodity; one party pays fixed × quantity, receives floating (indexed) × quantity.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> An aluminium producer expecting falling prices swaps to receive a fixed price and pay the floating market price monthly, on an agreed quantity — hedging revenue while still selling physically to customers (creates basis risk if the swap index differs from actual sale prices). Also: <strong>commodity price-for-interest swaps</strong> (fixed commodity value vs floating/fixed interest). Common areas: bullion (gold, silver, platinum, palladium), industrial inputs (eg, fertiliser), energy (crude oil, natural gas, gasoline).</p>\n<h4>Other Swap Types (LO 1.5.11)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amortising swap</strong> – notional principal decreases over life (matches an amortising loan).</li>\n<li><strong>Accreting swap</strong> – notional principal increases over life.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollercoaster swap</strong> – notional rises and falls (eg, seasonal borrowing).</li>\n<li><strong>Forward start swap</strong> (deferred/delayed start) – agreed today, cash exchange starts at a future date; often priced as two partially offsetting swaps (eg, a 1-year and a 5-year swap create a 4-year forward-starting swap in 1 year).</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "1.6 Derivatives Trading", "html": "\n<h4>FLEX Options and Wholesale Facilities (LO 1.6.1)</h4>\n<p><strong>FLEX options</strong> (Flexible Exchange), pioneered by the CBOE in 1993, are hybrid exchange-traded products (ETPs) letting users customise exercise price, exercise style, and expiry date (eg, on the S&P 500) while retaining exchange clearing — substantially reducing <strong>credit risk</strong> (a subset of counterparty risk: an OTM counterparty may lack capital to pay an ITM one) versus pure OTC. Market, liquidity, and operational risks remain.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exchange for Physical (EFP)</strong> – an off-market swap of an OTC position for a futures position; OTC and futures legs must be substantially similar in value/quantity. Benefits: releases counterparty credit exposure/limits, reduces balance sheet and margin requirements (netting), can be negotiated 24 hours (but registered within exchange hours). Risk: increased operational risk from MTM/daily margining, which can burden non-financial corporates.</li>\n<li><strong>Exchange for Swap (EFS)</strong> – similar, exchanging an OTC swap for a series of futures; requires price correlation between the swap and futures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Warrants</strong> – security similar to an option (right to buy an asset at a set price), often attached to/part of a bond, and separately tradeable (detailed in Chapter 2).</p>\n<h4>Liquidity (LO 1.6.2)</h4>\n<p>Liquidity = ease of converting to cash / trading without moving price or incurring excessive cost. Established via <strong>price discovery</strong> (bid/offer quotes); tighter <strong>bid/offer (dealing) spread</strong> = more liquid = cheaper to trade (low price elasticity of demand). Elements of a liquid market: many buyers/sellers, small spreads, low commissions, large size tradeable without major price impact.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Measure</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume</td><td>Number of contracts traded on a given day (total longs or shorts entered that day)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Open interest</td><td>Total number of long/short positions <em>outstanding</em> (not yet closed/settled) at end of day — indicates market depth and commitment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Immediacy</td><td>Time needed to trade a given amount at a specific cost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Market depth</td><td>Size of the order book above/below the current price; thin depth risks a large order \"gapping\", causing slippage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Resilience</td><td>Speed with which prices return to former levels after a large transaction (measured over time)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Exchange-Traded vs OTC Products (LO 1.6.3)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Exchange-Traded</th><th>OTC</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Contract terms</td><td>Standardised (product specification)</td><td>Customised, negotiated, confidential, flexible, can be large size</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Standardised, fixed dates</td><td>Negotiable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liquidity</td><td>Excellent (major contracts), fast execution, electronic</td><td>Variable, can be limited, slower execution</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Financial integrity</td><td>CCP removes counterparty risk; daily MTM/variation margin</td><td>Counterparty default possible; credit rating matters; prime brokers can reduce risk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Margin</td><td>Always required: initial + variation margin</td><td>Collateral case-by-case, not fixed, may reset periodically (eg, quarterly)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Documentation</td><td>Standard, concise</td><td>Complex, often bespoke, though ISDA provides standard templates</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulation</td><td>Significant regulation</td><td>Increasingly regulated post-2008 (EMIR in EU, Dodd-Frank in US); many trades must clear via CCP and be reported to a trade repository</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Price quotes</td><td>Highly transparent, public</td><td>Limited, need to \"shop around\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transaction costs</td><td>Standardised, lower</td><td>Individually priced, more expensive</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Initial margin = capital required to open/maintain a position; variation margin = daily settlement of profit/loss. OTC margin/collateral standards are increasingly formalised (BCBS/IOSCO schedule; since Sept 2022 even smaller counterparties with month-end notional OTC values of €8 billion must post initial and variation margin).</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Exchange-Traded</th><th>OTC</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fungibility</td><td>Full, for same expiry</td><td>Limited</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hedging precision</td><td>May not precisely match exposure (fixed sizes/expiries)</td><td>Can be tailored to exactly match the exposure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speculation</td><td>Restricted to listed products</td><td>Almost unlimited product innovation, but counterparty availability may restrict</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Public information</td><td>Real-time trade/price publication (participants stay confidential)</td><td>Little/no real-time publication historically; CFTC/EMIR now require real-time reporting</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Markets, Participants and Trading Environment (LO 1.6.4)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Jurisdiction</th><th>Key regulators</th></tr>\n<tr><td>UK</td><td>Bank of England (PRA, FPC), FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EU</td><td>ECB, EBA, ESMA, plus national regulators (AMF France, Central Bank of Ireland, BaFin/Bundesbank Germany)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>US</td><td>Federal Reserve, SEC, CFTC, FDIC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Japan</td><td>FSA, SESC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>India</td><td>RBI, SEBI</td></tr>\n<tr><td>China</td><td>CSRC, CIRC, CBRC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Trading has largely moved to <strong>electronic platforms</strong> (ICE went fully electronic in 2005). Exchanges retaining floor trading: CME Group, CBOE, and the LME (\"The Ring\" — the only London exchange with floor trading; briefly suspended in 2020 COVID, later resumed; CME made electronic-only permanent for nearly all contracts in 2021 except Eurodollar/SOFR).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Market makers</strong> (price-makers/price-givers) quote two-way (bid and offer) prices, profiting from the spread; a market reliant on them is <strong>price-driven</strong>. Exchange members trading mainly for their own account are called \"locals\".</li>\n<li><strong>Price-takers</strong> deal on someone else's quoted price, paying away the spread.</li>\n<li><strong>Sell-side</strong> – brokers/investment banks that execute orders, make markets, and produce research/recommendations for buy-side clients; generally do not seek trading risk as their main profit source.</li>\n<li><strong>Buy-side</strong> – institutional investors (mutual funds, pension funds, hedge funds, private equity, insurers, proprietary traders) that purchase securities/derivatives for their own or clients' accounts, taking on risk to generate returns.</li>\n</ul>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "An investor buys a £100,000 flat by putting up a £10,000 deposit and taking a £90,000 mortgage. The flat rises in value by 10% to £110,000. What is the percentage increase in the investor's equity?", "options": ["10%", "20%", "50%", "100%"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Equity rises from £10,000 to £110,000 − £90,000 = £20,000, a 100% increase on the original £10,000 invested, versus only a 10% gain if the flat had been bought outright — this illustrates gearing."}, {"question": "An investor buys an XYZ plc 850 call option for a premium of 20p when the share price is 800p. At expiry, the share price is 880p. What is the investor's return on investment?", "options": ["10%", "20%", "50%", "100%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Exercise profit = (880 − 850) − 20 = 10p. Return on investment = 10 ÷ 20 = 50%, compared with only 10% (80 ÷ 800) had the investor bought the share outright."}, {"question": "An investor goes long one FTSE 100 Index future at 7200. The exchange requires margin of 300 points, each point worth £10. The position is later closed at 7260. What is the investor's percentage return on the margin posted?", "options": ["0.8%", "2%", "20%", "60%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Margin = 300 × £10 = £3,000. Profit = (7260 − 7200) × £10 = £600. Return on margin = 600 ÷ 3,000 = 20%, even though the index itself moved less than 1%."}, {"question": "The SGX Japanese Government Bond (JGB) futures contract has a face value of JPY100 million. If the broker requires 20% initial margin, how much must the client deposit per contract?", "options": ["JPY2 million", "JPY10 million", "JPY20 million", "JPY100 million"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Initial margin = 20% × JPY100,000,000 = JPY20,000,000, while profits and losses still accrue on the full JPY100 million face value — this is how gearing arises in futures trading."}, {"question": "Which of the following best defines operational risk under the Basel II framework?", "options": ["The risk of an adverse movement in the value of a derivative position", "The risk that a counterparty fails to fulfil its contractual obligations", "The risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people and systems, or from external events", "The risk that a position cannot be closed out at a reasonable price"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is the Basel II definition of operational risk, distinct from market risk, counterparty risk and liquidity risk."}, {"question": "Why is counterparty risk generally much lower for exchange-traded futures than for equivalent OTC forwards?", "options": ["Futures have lower liquidity", "Futures are always cash-settled", "The clearing house becomes the counterparty to both the buyer and seller through novation", "Futures contracts are not marked-to-market daily"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Once a futures trade is executed, the central clearing house is novated in as counterparty to both sides, largely removing bilateral counterparty risk. OTC forwards typically remain bilateral, unless centrally cleared."}, {"question": "You buy a March ABC plc 700 call for a premium of 30p. What is your break-even share price at expiry?", "options": ["670p", "700p", "730p", "760p"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Break-even for a long call = strike + premium = 700p + 30p = 730p."}, {"question": "You buy a March XYZ plc 450 put for a premium of 17p. What is your break-even share price at expiry?", "options": ["433p", "450p", "467p", "417p"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Break-even for a long put = strike − premium = 450p − 17p = 433p."}, {"question": "What is the maximum possible loss for the writer (seller) of an uncovered call option?", "options": ["Limited to the premium received", "Strike price less premium", "Theoretically unlimited", "Zero, once the premium is received"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A short call has theoretically unlimited loss potential because the underlying asset's price can rise without limit, while the seller's maximum profit is capped at the premium received."}, {"question": "What is the maximum possible loss for the buyer of a put option, and when does the maximum profit occur?", "options": ["Maximum loss unlimited; maximum profit when price rises", "Maximum loss limited to the premium paid; maximum profit (strike less premium) if the underlying falls to zero", "Maximum loss is the strike price; maximum profit is unlimited", "Maximum loss and profit are both limited to the premium paid"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A long put's maximum loss is the premium paid (if the option expires worthless). Maximum profit is achieved if the underlying falls to zero, equal to strike price less premium paid."}, {"question": "Which exercise style allows the holder to exercise on any business day up to and including expiry?", "options": ["European-style", "American-style", "Bermudan-style", "Asian-style"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "American-style options can be exercised on any day during their life ('A' for American, 'any day'). European-style options can only be exercised on the expiry day itself."}, {"question": "An option can only be exercised on a specified series of dates during its life, spaced at regular intervals. What type of option is this?", "options": ["European-style", "American-style", "Bermudan-style", "Asian-style"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A Bermudan-style option restricts early exercise to specific dates, sitting between European and American styles — hence its premium is typically higher than a European option but lower than an American option, other factors equal."}, {"question": "A barrier option ceases to exist the moment the underlying asset reaches a predetermined price. What type of barrier option is this?", "options": ["Knock-in option", "Knock-out option", "Lookback option", "Chooser option"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A knock-out option exists at purchase but is extinguished if the underlying reaches the barrier price; a knock-in option is the opposite — it only comes into existence once the barrier is reached."}, {"question": "Which type of exotic option allows the holder to decide, at a predetermined future date, whether the option will be a call or a put?", "options": ["Rainbow option", "Chooser option", "Binary option", "Compound option"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A chooser option lets the holder decide, at a set point during its life, whether it becomes a call or a put."}, {"question": "An option pays a fixed cash amount if the underlying asset closes above a certain level at maturity, and nothing otherwise. What is this type of option called?", "options": ["Lookback option", "Barrier option", "Binary (digital) option", "Rainbow option"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A binary (or digital) option pays a fixed amount or nothing at all, depending on the underlying's price at maturity or at stated times before maturity."}, {"question": "A wheat futures contract on the CBOT has a minimum price movement (full tick size) of $0.0025 per bushel and represents 5,000 bushels. What is the full tick value per contract?", "options": ["$0.25", "$2.50", "$12.50", "$25.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Full tick value = $0.0025 × 5,000 bushels = $12.50 per contract."}, {"question": "An investor sells (goes short) 20 contracts of the LME's June LMEX futures at 3400. Each index point is worth $10. One month before maturity, the contract is trading at 3190 and the investor closes out. What is the investor's profit or loss?", "options": ["$4,200 loss", "$21,000 profit", "$42,000 profit", "$63,000 profit"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "As the investor is short, profit = (3400 − 3190) points × $10 × 20 contracts = 210 × $10 × 20 = $42,000 profit."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a key difference between an exchange-traded future and an OTC forward?", "options": ["Forwards are always marked-to-market daily, futures are not", "Futures are standardised and exchange-traded; forwards are usually customisable and OTC", "Futures always involve physical delivery, forwards never do", "Forwards carry no counterparty risk, unlike futures"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Futures are by definition exchange-traded with standardised terms; forwards are typically OTC and customisable in size, date, quality and delivery point, though this flexibility brings greater counterparty risk."}, {"question": "An investor buys a FTSE 100 CFD at 7200 and closes the position at 7350. If the CFD mimics the exchange-traded contract at £10 per index point, what is the investor's profit?", "options": ["£150", "£1,000", "£1,500", "£3,500"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Profit = (7350 − 7200) points × £10 = 150 × £10 = £1,500."}, {"question": "An investor sells a CFD on 5,000 ABC plc shares at 145p, with the broker requiring 15% margin. The shares later fall to 137p and the position is closed. What is the investor's approximate return on the margin posted?", "options": ["5.5%", "15%", "36.8%", "55%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Sale proceeds = 5,000 × £1.45 = £7,250; margin = 15% × £7,250 = £1,087.50. Cost to close = 5,000 × £1.37 = £6,850. Profit = £400. Return = 400 ÷ 1,087.50 = 36.8%."}, {"question": "Which statement correctly distinguishes UK spread betting from CFDs?", "options": ["Spread betting profits are subject to capital gains tax, CFD profits are not", "Spread betting is treated as gambling and is tax-free in the UK, whereas CFD profits are subject to capital gains tax", "CFDs have a fixed maturity date, spread bets do not", "Spread betting requires no margin, unlike CFDs"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In the UK, spread betting is treated as gambling so winnings are free of capital gains tax, while gains on CFDs are subject to CGT. Neither product typically has a fixed maturity in the same way as futures/options."}, {"question": "A UK company expects to receive US dollar income over the next five years and wants predictable sterling cash flows without using a strip of separate forward contracts. Which instrument is most appropriate?", "options": ["A variance swap", "A currency swap", "An asset swap", "A dividend swap"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A currency swap packages the series of future USD flows into a single agreement to exchange them for GBP cash flows over the life of the swap, avoiding the need for multiple individual FX forward contracts."}, {"question": "What is a swaption?", "options": ["A swap where the notional principal changes over time", "An option to enter into a swap agreement by a pre-agreed future date, for an upfront premium", "A swap that is settled at multiple dates rather than one", "A type of interest rate swap traded only by retail investors"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A swaption gives its buyer the right, but not the obligation, to enter into a swap on pre-agreed terms by a set future date, in exchange for an upfront premium — the same concept as an option, applied to swaps."}, {"question": "What distinguishes a zero coupon inflation swap from a standard inflation swap?", "options": ["It has no notional principal", "It has only a single cash flow exchange, at maturity, with no intermediate coupons", "It cannot be used to hedge inflation risk", "It is based on equity indices rather than price indices"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A zero coupon inflation swap involves just one financial flow at the end of the swap (a fixed amount versus a floating/inflation-linked amount), unlike swaps with periodic coupon-like exchanges."}, {"question": "Investor A enters an equity swap on a $10 million notional, paying ICE SOFR + 0.05% and receiving the return on the Hang Seng index over 180 days (day count 180/360). If SOFR is 5.5%, how much does Investor A owe on the floating leg?", "options": ["$55,000", "$138,750", "$277,500", "$555,000"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Floating leg = (5.5% + 0.05%) × $10,000,000 × 180/360 = 5.55% × $10,000,000 × 0.5 = $277,500."}, {"question": "In a total return swap (TRS), what does the total return leg typically include?", "options": ["Only the capital appreciation of the underlying asset", "Only the dividend or coupon income of the underlying asset", "Capital appreciation or depreciation plus all interest, dividends and fees generated by the underlying asset", "Only the realised variance of the underlying asset's price"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The total return leg of a TRS reflects the full economic benefit (or loss) of holding the asset — capital gains/losses plus coupons, dividends and fees — against a typically floating-rate leg on the other side."}, {"question": "What is the main attraction of a variance swap compared with using options to gain exposure to volatility?", "options": ["It provides pure exposure to volatility without needing delta hedging", "It has unlimited downside protection built in", "It requires no notional principal", "It is always cheaper than buying a straddle"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A variance swap gives direct, 'pure' exposure to the realised volatility (variance) of the underlying, unlike options which combine volatility exposure with other Greeks and typically require delta hedging to isolate volatility risk."}, {"question": "An interest rate swap's notional principal decreases over its life to match a loan being repaid in instalments. What type of swap is this?", "options": ["Accreting swap", "Rollercoaster swap", "Amortising swap", "Forward start swap"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "An amortising swap has a notional principal that decreases over time, mirroring an amortising loan. An accreting swap increases; a rollercoaster swap rises and falls; a forward start swap begins accruing at a future date."}, {"question": "An aluminium producer worried about falling prices enters a commodity swap to receive a fixed price and pay the floating market price each month on an agreed quantity, while continuing to sell aluminium physically to its customers as normal. What best describes the outcome?", "options": ["The producer eliminates all price risk with no residual exposure", "The producer achieves an approximately fixed net price for its output, subject to basis risk between the swap index and its actual sale prices", "The producer is now exposed to unlimited losses if prices rise", "The swap requires physical delivery of aluminium to the swap counterparty"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A fixed-for-floating commodity swap involves no physical delivery; it runs alongside normal physical sales. The producer effectively locks in close to a fixed net price, but basis risk remains if the swap's price index doesn't exactly match its actual realised sale prices."}, {"question": "What is a FLEX option?", "options": ["An OTC option that is entirely bespoke and uncleared", "A hybrid exchange-traded option allowing customisation of strike, exercise style and expiry, while retaining exchange clearing", "An option that can only be exercised on Fridays", "A futures contract with flexible delivery dates"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "FLEX options, pioneered by the CBOE in 1993, are exchange-traded products that let users customise terms normally fixed by the exchange (strike, style, expiry) while still benefiting from central clearing, which substantially reduces credit risk versus a pure OTC option."}, {"question": "What is an Exchange for Physical (EFP) transaction?", "options": ["A trade where an OTC position is exchanged (swapped) for an equivalent futures position", "A physical delivery of the underlying commodity at contract expiry", "A transaction only available to retail investors", "An option that settles in physical shares rather than cash"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "An EFP is an off-market transaction swapping an OTC position for a substantially similar futures position (in value and/or quantity), used to release credit limits and reduce margin/balance sheet requirements."}, {"question": "How does open interest differ from trading volume in a derivatives market?", "options": ["They are the same measure, calculated differently", "Volume measures contracts traded on a given day; open interest measures the total number of long/short positions still outstanding at day end", "Open interest measures daily turnover; volume measures outstanding contracts", "Volume can only be calculated for OTC markets"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Volume counts contracts traded during a specific day (a flow measure), while open interest is a stock measure of contracts that remain open (not yet closed out or settled) at the end of the day — both are indicators of market liquidity/depth."}, {"question": "In a price-driven market, who typically quotes both a bid and an offer price and profits from the spread?", "options": ["The price-taker", "The market maker (price-maker)", "The clearing house", "The regulator"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Market makers (price-makers/price-givers) quote two-way prices, aiming to profit from the bid/offer spread. A price-taker instead deals on someone else's quoted price, paying away the spread."}, {"question": "Which best describes the difference between buy-side and sell-side market participants?", "options": ["The sell-side only buys derivatives for clients; the buy-side only sells them", "The sell-side (eg, brokers, investment banks) executes orders, makes markets and provides research; the buy-side (eg, pension funds, hedge funds) purchases securities/derivatives for its own or clients' accounts, taking on investment risk", "There is no meaningful distinction between buy-side and sell-side in derivatives markets", "The buy-side always acts as a central counterparty to the sell-side"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Sell-side firms (brokerages, investment banks) originate and execute trades and provide research to generate trading ideas, generally without seeking trading risk as their main profit source. Buy-side firms (institutional investors) take on risk to generate returns for themselves or clients."}, {"question": "Under the standardised BCBS/IOSCO margin framework referenced in the CISI workbook, since September 2022 which OTC market participants have been required to post initial and variation margin?", "options": ["Only central banks", "Only the largest global banks with notional OTC values above €1 trillion", "Even smaller OTC participants with month-end notional OTC values of €8 billion", "All retail investors trading any OTC derivative"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The BCBS/IOSCO standardised margin schedule has been phased in progressively; since September 2022 it extends to smaller OTC participants with month-end notional OTC values of €8 billion, requiring both initial and variation margin."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Long (in a derivatives context)", "back": "The buyer/holder of a contract — has agreed to buy at a future date (futures/forwards) or holds the right under an option."}, {"front": "Short (in a derivatives context)", "back": "The seller/writer of a contract — has agreed to sell at a future date (futures/forwards) or has written the option, assuming an obligation."}, {"front": "Counterparty risk", "back": "Risk that the other side of a contract fails to fulfil its obligations; much lower for exchange-traded derivatives due to CCP novation."}, {"front": "Market risk", "back": "Risk of an adverse price movement relative to your position; can be disaggregated by underlying (equity, interest rate, etc)."}, {"front": "Operational risk (Basel II definition)", "back": "The risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people and systems, or from external events."}, {"front": "Hedging", "back": "Taking an opposite position in the derivatives market to protect an existing or anticipated position in the underlying; a perfect hedge is risk-free."}, {"front": "Arbitrage", "back": "Exploiting price differences for the same/related asset in two markets to lock in a risk-free profit as prices converge. Three forms: intertemporal (different maturities of the same contract, eg one-month vs six-month LME zinc), geographic (same contract on different exchanges, eg SGX vs CME Eurodollar futures), and value-chain (related products, eg crude oil vs refined products)."}, {"front": "Gearing (leverage)", "back": "The ratio of cash/initial investment used to open a derivatives position versus the full value of the underlying it controls; small price moves cause disproportionately large P&L moves."}, {"front": "Options gearing driver", "back": "The size of the premium relative to the price of the underlying asset — the smaller the premium, the higher the potential gearing."}, {"front": "Futures gearing driver", "back": "The margining system — only a small % of the contract's face value (initial margin) is posted, but P&L accrues on the full contract value."}, {"front": "Call option", "back": "Gives the holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying asset at the strike price on or before a specified date."}, {"front": "Put option", "back": "Gives the holder the right, but not the obligation, to sell the underlying asset at the strike price on or before a specified date."}, {"front": "Premium", "back": "The non-returnable price paid by the option buyer to the option writer for the rights conferred by the option, giving the right to transact at the pre-agreed strike (exercise) price."}, {"front": "ITM / OTM / ATM", "back": "In-the-money (ITM): option has intrinsic value (profitable to exercise). Out-of-the-money (OTM): no intrinsic value, would not be exercised profitably. At-the-money (ATM): strike equals current underlying price, considered breakeven."}, {"front": "Intrinsic value vs extrinsic (time) value", "back": "Intrinsic value: the in-the-money portion of an option's premium; only exists for ITM options. Extrinsic (time) value: premium value beyond intrinsic value, reflecting time to expiry, implied volatility and interest rates; erodes as expiry approaches (time decay)."}, {"front": "Option exercise styles (European/American/Bermudan)", "back": "European-style: exercisable only on expiry day ('E' = expiry). American-style: exercisable any day up to expiry ('A' = any day). Bermudan-style: exercisable only on specified dates; premium sits between European (cheapest) and American (priciest), other factors equal."}, {"front": "Barrier option (knock-in vs knock-out)", "back": "Knock-in: activates (comes into existence) only once the underlying reaches a predetermined barrier price. Knock-out: exists at purchase but ceases to exist if the underlying reaches the barrier price."}, {"front": "Binary (digital) option", "back": "Pays a fixed amount or nothing at all, depending on the underlying's price at or before maturity."}, {"front": "Long call: max loss / max profit / break-even", "back": "Max loss = premium paid; max profit = unlimited; break-even = strike + premium."}, {"front": "Short call: max loss / max profit / break-even", "back": "Max loss = unlimited; max profit = premium received; break-even = strike + premium."}, {"front": "Long put: max loss / max profit / break-even", "back": "Max loss = premium paid; max profit = strike − premium (if underlying falls to zero); break-even = strike − premium."}, {"front": "Short put: max loss / max profit / break-even", "back": "Max loss = strike − premium (if underlying falls to zero); max profit = premium received; break-even = strike − premium."}, {"front": "Future vs Forward — key difference", "back": "Futures are always exchange-traded with standardised terms and low counterparty risk via novation (the CCP becomes counterparty to both sides). Forwards are usually OTC, customisable, settled only at maturity, and carry bilateral counterparty risk."}, {"front": "CFD (Contract for Difference)", "back": "An OTC contract to receive/pay the difference between the opening and closing price of an underlying, without owning it; margin-traded, no fixed maturity."}, {"front": "Spread betting vs CFDs — tax treatment", "back": "UK spread betting is treated as gambling and is free of capital gains tax; CFD profits are subject to capital gains tax."}, {"front": "Swap & fixed/floating IRS", "back": "A swap is an OTC derivative contract in which two counterparties exchange cash flows or liabilities, usually based on a notional principal amount. The fixed/floating interest rate swap (plain vanilla / coupon swap) exchanges fixed-rate cash flows for floating-rate cash flows and is the most common type."}, {"front": "Swaption", "back": "An option to enter into a swap agreement by a pre-agreed future date, for an upfront premium."}, {"front": "Zero coupon inflation swap", "back": "A swap with a single cash flow at maturity (fixed vs floating/inflation-linked amount) with no intermediate coupons."}, {"front": "Currency swap — main uses", "back": "Hedging FX exposure, accessing cheaper financing, matching assets/liabilities in the same currency, and speculation/arbitrage on rate differentials."}, {"front": "Equity swap", "back": "A swap where one/both legs' payments are linked to the return on an equity, basket, or index, often used to gain synthetic exposure without owning shares."}, {"front": "Variance swap", "back": "A swap giving pure exposure to an underlying's realised volatility, without the need for delta hedging (unlike options); P&L depends on implied vs realised volatility."}, {"front": "Dividend swap", "back": "A swap exchanging a fixed payment for the actual dividends paid on a share/basket/index, ignoring the underlying's price level."}, {"front": "Asset swap", "back": "A swap used to change the interest rate or currency exposure of an investment, eg, converting a fixed-rate bond into a synthetic FRN."}, {"front": "Total return swap (TRS)", "back": "A swap where one leg pays the total return (capital gain/loss plus income) of an asset, and the other pays a floating rate — provides synthetic exposure without holding the physical asset."}, {"front": "Amortising / accreting / rollercoaster swaps", "back": "Amortising: notional principal decreases over the swap's life (matches an amortising loan). Accreting: notional principal increases over time. Rollercoaster: notional rises and falls repeatedly (eg, to match seasonal borrowing needs)."}, {"front": "Forward start (deferred start) swap", "back": "A swap agreed today, but where the exchange of cash flows only begins at a future date; often priced as two partially offsetting swaps."}, {"front": "Commodity swap (fixed-for-floating)", "back": "One party pays fixed price × quantity, the other pays floating (indexed) price × quantity, with no physical exchange of the commodity."}, {"front": "FLEX option", "back": "A hybrid exchange-traded option (pioneered by the CBOE, 1993) allowing customisation of strike, style and expiry while keeping the credit-risk benefits of central clearing."}, {"front": "EFP / EFS", "back": "EFP: an off-market transaction exchanging an OTC position for a substantially similar futures position (in value/quantity), used to release credit limits and reduce margin requirements. EFS: similar, but exchanges an OTC swap for a series of futures contracts with correlated pricing."}, {"front": "Volume (liquidity measure)", "back": "The number of contracts traded on a particular trading day."}, {"front": "Open interest (liquidity measure)", "back": "The total number of long/short positions in a contract that remain outstanding (not closed out or settled) at the end of a trading day."}, {"front": "Market depth", "back": "The size of the order book above and below the current price; thin depth risks a large order 'gapping' and causing slippage. Resilience (a related liquidity measure) is the speed with which prices return to former levels after a large transaction, measured over time."}, {"front": "Market maker vs price-taker", "back": "A market maker (price-maker/price-giver) quotes both a bid and an offer price, profiting from the spread; a market reliant on them is 'price-driven'. A price-taker instead deals on another participant's quoted price, effectively paying away the bid/offer spread."}, {"front": "Sell-side vs buy-side", "back": "Sell-side: brokerage firms, investment banks and similar entities that execute orders, make markets, and provide research/recommendations to buy-side clients. Buy-side: institutional investors (pension funds, mutual funds, hedge funds, insurers) that purchase securities/derivatives for their own or clients' accounts, taking on investment risk."}, {"front": "Initial vs variation margin", "back": "Initial margin: capital a participant must post to open/maintain a derivatives position (a fraction of the contract's full value). Variation margin: the daily profit or loss on an open position, settled between counterparties (typically via the CCP)."}]}, {"id": "der-ch2", "number": 2, "title": "Underlying Markets", "examWeight": "16 of 100 questions (~16%)", "summaryHtml": "\n<p>Chapter 2 covers the <strong>cash/underlying markets</strong> on which derivatives are based: money markets, FX, bonds, equities and commodities. It provides roughly 16 of the 100 exam questions, so precise definitions and numeric conventions here are high-yield.</p>\n<p><strong>Money markets</strong> cover short-term (under one year) instruments. Key benchmark rates: <strong>SONIA</strong> (GBP, overnight, published by BoE at 9am GMT from data submitted by 7am GMT since April 2018); <strong>€STR</strong> (euro, wholesale unsecured overnight borrowing, transactions over €1 million, published each TARGET2 business day); <strong>SOFR</strong> (USD, based on Treasury repo, first published 2018 by NY Fed, volume-weighted from actual GC repo trades, not a survey). <strong>T-bills</strong> are government promissory notes issued at a discount and redeemed at par (no coupon) — UK: 91/182 days, weekly DMO issuance; US: 28 days plus 3/6 months. <strong>CDs</strong> are bank promissory notes, typically 3/6-month maturity, purchased at nominal value and redeemed at nominal plus interest; early redemption incurs a penalty. <strong>CP</strong> is unsecured short-term corporate debt, 2–9 months, minimum US$500,000 denominations; US CP is capped at 270 days for exemption, UK/ECP typically ≤364 days. Northern Rock (2007) illustrates funding/confidence risk for a borrower reliant on the money market.</p>\n<p><strong>FX</strong> is the largest, most liquid, 24-hour (weekdays) OTC market, split into Tokyo/London/New York sessions. Spot settlement is normally <strong>T+2</strong>. In a quote like GBP/USD = 1.3010/15, the bid (1.3010) is where the bank buys the base currency; the offer (1.3015) is where it sells. <strong>Forward rates</strong> reflect interest rate differentials (interest rate parity): the higher-interest-rate currency trades at a forward <strong>discount</strong>; the lower-interest-rate currency at a forward <strong>premium</strong>. Formula: Forward = Spot × [1+(n×r1)]/[1+(n×r2)], where r1 = traded/foreign currency rate, r2 = base/domestic currency rate. A <strong>pip</strong> is the smallest quoted price change. <strong>NDFs</strong> are cash-settled forwards on non-convertible/thinly-traded currencies, with a fixing date and settlement date, usually settled in USD.</p>\n<p><strong>Bonds</strong> pay coupons and repay principal at maturity. Know national conventions: UK Gilts (semi-annual, up to 50yr, T+1); US Treasuries (semi-annual; bills≤1yr, notes 2-10yr, bonds 20/30yr; T+1); French OATs (annual, T+2); German Bunds/Bobls/Schatz (annual, T+2); Italian BTPs (semi-annual, T+2); JGBs (semi-annual, domestic T+1/cross-border T+3). <strong>Clean (quoted) price</strong> excludes accrued interest; <strong>dirty (traded) price</strong> = clean + accrued interest. Bond maturity buckets: short (&lt;7yr), medium (7–15yr), long (&gt;15yr). <strong>Index-linked gilts</strong> have an indexation lag (8 months pre-2005 issues, 3 months from 2005). <strong>STRIPS</strong> separate coupons/principal into tradeable zero-coupon instruments; <strong>ZCBs</strong> pay no coupon, bought at a discount. Credit ratings (Moody's/S&P/Fitch) determine yield: higher rating = lower yield. <strong>Yield curves</strong>: normal (upward-sloping, liquidity preference), inverted (short rates &gt; long rates, signals expected rate cuts/recession), flat/horizontal.</p>\n<p><strong>Equities</strong>: ordinary (common) shares confer dividends, capital repayment (last, after creditors), voting rights, pre-emption rights on rights issues, and free transferability. Non-voting (A/B) shares trade at a discount. Corporate actions: rights issues (dilutive, often price-negative short-term), bonus/scrip issues (no cash cost, more shares), stock splits (lower price, more liquidity) vs reverse splits, M&A (friendly/hostile), share buybacks (reduce share count, support price).</p>\n<p><strong>Commodities</strong> split into softs/agriculturals (cocoa, coffee, sugar, FCOJ, grains, livestock), energy (crude oil, gas, electricity) and metals (base/ferrous/non-ferrous and precious). Crude oil is classified by field of origin, density (light/heavy) and sulphur content (sweet &lt;0.5%, sour &gt;0.5%); WTI is a stored (delivered to storage) contract that famously went negative in April 2020, unlike Brent (shipped). Key exchanges: CME/CBOT/COMEX/NYMEX, ICE, LME, SHFE, TOCOM, DCE, MCX. Also know \"other markets\": cryptoassets/NFTs, weather derivatives (HDD/CDD × $20), emissions (EUAs), freight (FFAs via Baltic Exchange), and volatility (VIX).</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "2.1 Introduction", "html": "\n<p>Chapter 2 covers the underlying (\"cash\") markets that give rise to derivative products, spanning both <strong>financial markets</strong> (money markets, FX, bonds, equities) and <strong>commodity markets</strong>. Although commodities were where derivatives markets first developed, financial derivatives trading volumes have grown to be significantly larger in recent years.</p>\n<p>This chapter provides approximately <strong>16 of the 100</strong> examination questions, covering: Money Market Instruments, Foreign Exchange (FX), Bonds, Equities Markets and Commodities.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.2 Money Market Instruments", "html": "\n<p>Money markets deal in <strong>cash, short-term deposits and instruments maturing within one year</strong> of issue, in contrast to bond markets (medium/long-term capital raising). An interbank market exists because banks' balances with their central bank rise and fall daily; a bank with a shortfall borrows from a bank with a surplus.</p>\n\n<h4>Benchmark Overnight Rates</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Rate</th><th>Currency</th><th>Key Facts</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>SONIA</strong> (Sterling Overnight Index Average)</td><td>GBP</td><td>Effective overnight rate for unsecured transactions in London sterling market. Since April 2018, calculated/published by the Bank of England. Institutions submit prior day's transaction data by <strong>7am GMT</strong>; BoE runs plausibility checks; rate published at <strong>9am GMT</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>€STR</strong> (Euro Short-Term Rate)</td><td>EUR</td><td>Wholesale euro unsecured overnight borrowing cost of eurozone banks. Published each TARGET2 business day, based on prior TARGET2 business day's transactions. Calculated on transactions <strong>over €1 million</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>SOFR</strong> (Secured Overnight Financing Rate)</td><td>USD</td><td>Benchmark for USD-denominated derivatives/loans. First published <strong>2018 by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York</strong>. Based on the single-day repo rate for US Treasuries (general collateral/GC repo market). <strong>Volume-weighted from actual market data</strong> (not a survey), published each business day.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Risk Implications</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>As a depositor</strong>: main risk is <strong>counterparty risk</strong> — that the borrower cannot repay at maturity. Lenders rely on internal credit limits/guidelines and credit ratings.</li>\n<li><strong>As a source of funding</strong>: risk is that confidence dries up and funding becomes unavailable or only available at a much higher rate. <strong>Northern Rock</strong> (UK bank) relied on the money market for up to <strong>75% of funding</strong> in August 2007, immediately before the global financial crisis; loss of investor/lender confidence caused a funding crisis leading to its <strong>nationalisation</strong>. The ECB played an active funding role for European banks during the <strong>2010–12 euro government bond crisis</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Treasury Bills (T-bills)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Short-term government debt; in the UK issued weekly by the <strong>Debt Management Office (DMO)</strong>.</li>\n<li>UK maturities: typically <strong>91 days</strong> (~3 months), some <strong>182 days</strong> (~6 months).</li>\n<li>US Treasury: weekly issuance, maturities of <strong>28 days</strong> (1 month) plus 3 and 6 months.</li>\n<li>Called <strong>promissory notes</strong> since backed by government promise. <strong>No coupon</strong> — issued at a discount to nominal (par/face) value, redeemed at nominal value; the discount is the investor's return.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Certificates of Deposit (CD) vs Commercial Paper (CP)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>CD</th><th>CP</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Issuer</td><td>Banks/financial institutions</td><td>Large financial and non-financial institutions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nature</td><td>Promissory note / savings-type deposit</td><td>Unsecured short-term borrowing, backed by unused bank credit lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical maturity</td><td>3 and 6 months (most common)</td><td>2 to 9 months</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purchase/redemption</td><td>Bought at nominal value; redeemed at nominal + fixed interest at maturity</td><td>N/A — priced on market conditions and issuer credit rating</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Early redemption</td><td>Possible but incurs a penalty linked to a pre-agreed rate</td><td>N/A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minimum size</td><td>Any denomination (e.g. RMB 200,000 \"large-denomination\" CDs)</td><td>Minimum US$500,000 or equivalent</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h4>US vs UK CP/CD Market Differences</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>UK CP market is smaller/less liquid than the US market.</li>\n<li>US issuance process is more standardised; UK has more currency flexibility (GBP/EUR/USD) vs US almost entirely USD.</li>\n<li>Rating agencies (S&P, Moody's, Fitch) are more active covering US issuers.</li>\n<li>Terminology: in the US, \"CD\" often means a retail savings product; in the UK, CD is almost exclusively institutional/wholesale.</li>\n<li>UK/international CP is often called <strong>Euro-Commercial Paper (ECP)</strong> even if GBP-denominated.</li>\n<li>Maturity conventions: US CP capped at <strong>270 days</strong> for certain exemptions; UK/ECP typically <strong>≤364 days</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "2.3 Foreign Exchange (FX)", "html": "\n<p>The FX market is a worldwide, decentralised OTC market for trading currencies, operating <strong>24 hours a day except weekends</strong> (from 7pm GMT Monday to 9pm GMT Friday), classified into three main sessions: <strong>Tokyo, London, New York</strong>. It determines the relative value of free-floating currencies. Unique features: huge trading volume/high liquidity, geographical dispersion, continuous operation, and diverse rate-affecting factors.</p>\n\n<h4>Spot FX Market</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Exchange of one currency for another at an agreed rate, settling almost immediately — standard wholesale settlement is <strong>T+2</strong>.</li>\n<li>Settlement requires both relevant domestic markets to be open (e.g., EUR/JPY needs both EU and Japan banks open).</li>\n<li>Quote convention: in GBP/USD = 1.3010/15, GBP is the <strong>base currency</strong>. <strong>1.3010 = bid</strong> for GBP (bank buys sterling/sells dollars — cheaper rate). <strong>1.3015 = offer</strong> for GBP (bank sells sterling/buys dollars — more expensive rate).</li>\n<li>Worked example: EUR/USD = 1.1805/10. A US company buying €15m pays at the <strong>offer</strong> (1.1810): debits $17,715,000 (€15m × 1.1810), credits €15m to its EUR account, both settling in 2 days.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Forward FX Market</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Locks in an exchange rate for a future date. Forward rate = spot rate adjusted by <strong>interest rate differentials</strong> between the two currencies over the forward period.</li>\n<li>Used for: hedging, commercial (trade payments/receipts), speculation on interest-rate differential changes (not FX rate changes per se — that's done via spot), and portfolio management (fund managers hedge transaction/translation risk).</li>\n<li><strong>Translation risk</strong>: risk that foreign-currency assets on a balance sheet/portfolio decline in value purely from FX translation. Different from <strong>transaction risk</strong>, incurred at the point of an actual FX transaction.</li>\n<li>Forward points example (USD/JPY): Spot 110.00/110.05; 2-month forward points 0.65/0.60 (subtracted since decreasing = discount); 2-month outright 109.35/109.45. Base currency (USD) is at a <strong>discount</strong>; JPY is at a <strong>premium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule</strong>: when forward points decrease (bid decrease &gt; offer decrease), points are <em>subtracted</em> from spot = discount for forward delivery. When forward points increase, points are <em>added</em> to spot = premium for forward delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Interest rate parity</strong>: the higher-interest-rate currency trades at a forward <strong>discount</strong>; the lower-interest-rate currency trades at a forward <strong>premium</strong>. Forward points move to eliminate arbitrage between holding currency and earning interest vs. dealing forward.</li>\n<li>Arbitrage example: GBP/EUR spot 1.1636; GBP 12-month rate 5.2%, EUR 12-month rate 3.5%. If the 1-year forward were mispriced at 1.1600, an arbitrageur could borrow EUR, convert to GBP spot, deposit at 5.2%, and sell GBP forward — generating a risk-free profit of <strong>€159,400</strong>. The arbitrage-free 1-year forward should be <strong>1.1448</strong> (=€12,043,260/£10,520,000).</li>\n<li>Forward rate formula (for periods &lt;1 year, accounting for day-count conventions — EUR/USD use Actual/360, GBP uses Actual/365):<br/><strong>Forward = Spot × [1 + (n × r1)] / [1 + (n × r2)]</strong><br/>where r1 = traded/foreign currency interest rate, r2 = base/domestic currency interest rate, n = period (days/360 or /365).</li>\n<li>Worked example: Spot EUR/USD = 1.0950; USD 6-month SOFR (r1) = 0.45%; EUR 6-month EURIBOR (r2) = 0.57%. 6-month forward = 1.0950 × [1+(180/360×0.0045)] / [1+(180/360×0.0057)] = <strong>1.0943</strong>. The adjustment is $0.0001 = <strong>1 pip</strong> (smallest quoted rate change). EUR is at a slight discount to USD since EUR's rate is higher.</li>\n<li>Premium/discount application to spot: for <strong>premiums</strong>, bid forward points added to bid spot, offer points added to offer spot. For <strong>discounts</strong>, bid points subtracted from offer/ask spot, offer points subtracted from bid spot. The forward bid/offer spread is always wider than the spot spread (compensation for market maker's added forward risk).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Non-Deliverable Forwards (NDFs)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Cash-settled, short-term forward FX contract on a <strong>thinly traded or non-convertible</strong> currency.</li>\n<li>P&amp;L = difference between agreed rate and spot rate at settlement, applied to an agreed notional.</li>\n<li><strong>Fixing date</strong>: when the difference between market rate and agreed rate is calculated. <strong>Settlement date</strong>: when payment of the difference is due.</li>\n<li>Usually quoted/settled in <strong>USD</strong> (also EUR, GBP common); tenor typically 1 month to 1 year. Large NDF market exists for the Chinese yuan.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "2.4 Bonds", "html": "\n<p>A bond is a debt instrument: the issuer (borrower) pays interest (coupon) at set dates and repays principal at maturity to the holder (lender) — essentially a loan in security form. Government bonds finance the shortfall between government revenue and spending (the national debt); in the UK this annual shortfall is the <strong>public sector net cash requirement (PSNCR)</strong>.</p>\n\n<h4>Major Government Bond Markets</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Country</th><th>Name</th><th>Coupon Frequency</th><th>Maturity</th><th>Trade Settlement</th></tr>\n<tr><td>UK</td><td>Gilts</td><td>Semi-annual</td><td>Up to 50 years</td><td>T+1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>US</td><td>T-bills / T-notes / T-bonds</td><td>Semi-annual</td><td>Bills ≤1yr; Notes 2,3,5,7,10yr; Bonds 20/30yr</td><td>T+1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>France</td><td>OATs (Obligations Assimilables du Trésor)</td><td>Annual</td><td>Up to 50 years</td><td>T+2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Germany</td><td>Schatz, Bobl, Bund</td><td>Annual</td><td>Schatz 1.75–2.25yr; Bobl 4.5–5.5yr; Bund 8.5–10.5yr</td><td>T+2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Italy</td><td>BTPs (Buoni del Tesoro Poliennali)</td><td>Semi-annual</td><td>3 to 30 years</td><td>T+2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Japan</td><td>JGBs</td><td>Semi-annual</td><td>Long (10yr, most common), super-long (20yr)</td><td>Domestic T+1; cross-border/offshore T+3</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>UK issued its first <strong>Green Gilts</strong> in 2021 (proceeds for environmentally beneficial public projects).</p>\n\n<h4>Coupons and Maturity</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Bond specifies nominal amount, coupon (interest rate), and maturity date. Most government bonds pay coupons semi-annually (half-yearly).</li>\n<li>Maturity classification (varies by market): <strong>short</strong> &lt;7 years, <strong>medium</strong> 7–15 years, <strong>long</strong> &gt;15 years. Bonds transition from long→medium→short as they age.</li>\n<li><strong>Century bonds</strong>: 100-year maturity. <strong>Undated/perpetual/irredeemable bonds</strong>: no fixed maturity.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Floating Rate and Index-Linked Bonds</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Index-linked bonds</strong> (\"inflation-linked\"/\"index linkers\"): coupons and maturity proceeds linked to an inflation index (RPI or CPI).</li>\n<li><strong>Floating rate notes (FRNs/\"floaters\")</strong>: coupons tied to a short-term benchmark rate (e.g. SONIA, SOFR).</li>\n<li><strong>Indexation lag</strong>: time between the inflation measurement period and its use in the coupon calculation (needed since official inflation data takes time to publish). UK index-linked gilts issued <strong>before 2005</strong>: 8-month lag; issued <strong>from 2005</strong>: 3-month lag. This lag means inflation is not reflected in the bond's return for its final interest period (last 6 months for gilts).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Accrued Interest and Clean/Dirty Price</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accrued interest</strong>: portion of the next coupon the seller is entitled to, from the last coupon date up to (not including) settlement date.</li>\n<li><strong>Clean (quoted) price</strong>: excludes accrued interest, expressed as % of par. E.g., quoted at 98.50 = 98.50% of par ($985 on a $1,000 bond).</li>\n<li><strong>Dirty (traded/full) price</strong>: actual settlement amount. <strong>Dirty Price = Clean Price + Accrued Interest.</strong> Example: Clean $985.00 + Accrued $8.33 = Dirty $993.33.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Currencies — Sovereign Eurobonds</h4>\n<p>Bonds issued by a government in a currency other than its home currency are <strong>sovereign eurobonds</strong> (not necessarily EUR-denominated — any foreign currency). Issuer bears FX risk if the foreign currency strengthens versus local currency (in which revenues are typically earned). Example: Kenya issued 7- and 12-year <strong>USD eurobonds in May 2019 at 7%/8%</strong>, versus 11–12% for equivalent local-currency maturities. Best suited to large exporters earning revenue in \"hard\" currencies; importers face greater long-term repayment risk.</p>\n\n<h4>Credit Ratings</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Moody's (Long)</th><th>S&amp;P (Long)</th><th>Fitch (Long)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Investment grade</td><td>Aaa to Baa3</td><td>AAA to BBB–</td><td>AAA to BBB–</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speculative grade</td><td>Ba1 to Ca</td><td>BB+ to C</td><td>BB+ to C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Default</td><td>C</td><td>SD/D</td><td>RD/D</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Higher rating = lower assessed credit risk = lower required yield. Non-investment grade = high yield/speculative/\"junk\". Example: 2011 eurozone debt crisis saw yields rise/prices fall sharply for Greece, Portugal, Ireland, Spain, Italy.</p>\n\n<h4>Yield Spreads</h4>\n<p>Difference between quoted yields of two bonds with similar maturity — reflects relative perceived risk. Wider spread = greater perceived default risk on the higher-yielding bond. Example: 10-year Greek govt bond spread over German bunds was <strong>29bps in 2011</strong>, widening to <strong>4,696bps in Q1 2012</strong> (Greek 10yr yields hit 48.6% vs bunds ~1.85%). 1bp = 0.01%.</p>\n\n<h4>STRIPS and Zero Coupon Bonds (ZCBs)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>STRIPS</strong> (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities): \"strippable\" bonds allow individual coupons and the principal to be traded separately, creating instruments tailored to specific investor cash-flow needs.</li>\n<li><strong>ZCBs</strong>: pay no coupons; purchased at a discount to face value, redeemed at full nominal value at maturity — a single payment. Some are inflation-indexed.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Yield Curves</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Normal (upward-sloping)</strong>: reflects investors' liquidity preference — lower yield accepted for liquid short-dated bonds, higher yield demanded for less liquid long-dated bonds (also compensates for inflation/default risk over time).</li>\n<li><strong>Inverted (downward-sloping)</strong>: short yields exceed long yields; occurs when a significant future interest rate cut is expected; liquidity preference still exists but is outweighed by rate-cut expectations. Often seen as signalling an economic slowdown.</li>\n<li><strong>Flat/horizontal</strong>: short and long rates are similar, e.g. when the government temporarily raises short rates without the market reflecting this in long yields.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Corporate Bonds and Other Issuers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Issued by corporations, generally maturity ≥12 months. Can be issued in any currency; often listed (exchange or ECN) but the vast majority of trading occurs OTC/dealer-based (developed and emerging markets alike).</li>\n<li>Generally pay a <strong>higher yield</strong> than government bonds of equivalent tenor, reflecting higher default risk (dependent on issuer, market conditions, credit rating). Government bonds serve as the benchmark yield.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "2.5 Equities Markets", "html": "\n<p>Companies raise capital via <strong>share capital</strong> (ownership) or <strong>loan stock</strong> (debt/borrowing). Loan stockholders are creditors (owed a debt, no ownership); shareholders are owners (bear more risk, more potential reward). Bondholders receive fixed interest and capital return regardless of company performance (barring default); shareholders' returns (dividends, capital growth) vary with company performance and can go to zero.</p>\n\n<h4>Ordinary Shares</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called <strong>common stock/common shares</strong>; usually issued in <strong>registered form</strong> (ownership recorded in a register by a registrar).</li>\n<li>Contrast with <strong>bearer shares</strong>: no register; physical possession of the certificate is sole evidence of ownership; dividends paid by clipping a coupon from the certificate; negotiable without endorsement, transferred on delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Shareholders' Rights</h4>\n<p>Set out in the company's Articles of Association. Principal rights of an ordinary shareholder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dividends</strong> — discretionary cash distributions from profits.</li>\n<li><strong>Capital repayment</strong> — on winding up; ordinary shareholders are repaid <strong>last</strong>, after all creditors.</li>\n<li><strong>Voting rights</strong> — e.g. director appointment/removal, dividend levels, takeover approval.</li>\n<li>Right to advance notice of meetings and to attend/speak/vote.</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-emption rights</strong> — right to subscribe pro rata to a rights issue (prevents dilution/control shift to select shareholders).</li>\n<li>Right to sell/transfer shares without restriction — LSE requires <strong>free transferability</strong> for listed shares.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Voting vs Non-Voting Shares</h4>\n<p>Non-voting shares (sometimes \"A\" or \"B\" shares, or \"participation certificates\") give the same dividend/capital rights but no formal voting rights. Exist to raise equity without diluting voting control. Typically trade at a <strong>discount</strong> to voting shares to compensate holders (sometimes offset by a guaranteed dividend).</p>\n\n<h4>Corporate Actions</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Action</th><th>Mechanics</th><th>Typical Price/Valuation Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Rights issue</strong></td><td>Existing shareholders invited to buy new shares at a discount to market price; rights are transferrable</td><td>Often perceived as a sign of cash weakness; share price tends to fall pre-issue; dilution effect as share count rises</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Bonus/scrip/capitalisation issue</strong></td><td>New shares issued to shareholders pro rata to existing holdings instead of paying cash dividends</td><td>Initial price fall (more shares) but added liquidity tends to raise valuation longer-term</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Stock split</strong></td><td>e.g. 2-for-1: share count doubles, price halves, no cost to shareholders (\"positive share split\")</td><td>Makes shares more affordable/attractive; long-term price often rises</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Reverse split</strong></td><td>e.g. 1:3 — shareholder holds 1 share for every 3 previously held; fractional shares rounded up</td><td>Used by distressed companies to avoid delisting</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Merger</strong></td><td>Two companies combine into a new joint organisation</td><td>Variable — no set rule</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Acquisition</strong></td><td>One company takes over another, usually buying its shares; can be friendly (recommended) or hostile (not recommended, often costlier as bidder raises price)</td><td>Variable</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Share buyback</strong></td><td>Company uses spare cash/borrowing to purchase its own shares (tender offer often above market price, or open market)</td><td>Reduces shares outstanding, intended to lift share price and return cash to shareholders</td></tr>\n</table>\n"}, {"heading": "2.6 Commodities", "html": "\n<p>A commodity is a raw/primary product, split into three broad categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Agriculturals and softs</strong> — staples (wheat, corn, livestock) and \"softs\" cash crops (sugar, coffee, cotton, cocoa).</li>\n<li><strong>Energy</strong> — crude oil, refined products, power, natural gas, renewables.</li>\n<li><strong>Minerals</strong> — base/refined metals (copper, lithium) and precious metals (gold, platinum).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>CBOT</strong> (established 1848) claims to be the oldest futures exchange and origin of the modern futures market form, though commodity trading pre-dates it by centuries.</p>\n\n<h4>Key Commodity Exchanges</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Exchange</th><th>Key Products</th></tr>\n<tr><td>CME Group (CBOT/COMEX/NYMEX, platform: Globex)</td><td>CBOT: agriculturals (soybeans, wheat); COMEX: gold, base metals (copper, aluminium, iron ore); NYMEX: crude oil, natural gas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Euronext Derivatives</td><td>Agriculturals (milling wheat, corn, rapeseed)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dubai Mercantile Exchange</td><td>Crude oil, jet kerosene, fuel oil</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ICE</td><td>Crude oil, iron ore, refined energy, natural gas, softs (cotton, cocoa, coffee, sugar)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LME</td><td>Base metals, steel rebar, some precious metals, minor metals (cobalt, molybdenum), EV-related metals</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TOCOM (Tokyo)</td><td>Base metals, oil, rubber</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DCE (Dalian)</td><td>Coking coal, coke, iron ore, plastics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MCX (India)</td><td>Softs/agriculturals, gold, ferrous/base metals, energy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SHFE (Shanghai)</td><td>Non-ferrous metals, ferrous metals, precious metals, rubber/woodpulp, energy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jakarta Futures Exchange</td><td>Gold, olein (palm oil), coffee, cocoa</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ECX (part of ICE)</td><td>Environmental contracts</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Softs and Agriculturals</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Softs\": cocoa, sugar, coffee, frozen concentrated orange juice (FCOJ). Agriculturals: grains (wheat, soya beans), livestock, seed oils.</li>\n<li>Price drivers — <strong>demand</strong>: national deficits/surpluses, population wealth, economic/industrial growth, consumer tastes, tax incentives. <strong>Supply</strong>: land use, weather, disease/insects, technology, transport/warehousing. Russia-Ukraine war (Feb 2022) disrupted wheat supply, driving food inflation.</li>\n<li>Greater diversity of production sources = more stable supply. Political factors: subsidies, tariffs (import tariffs tax goods entering; export tariffs tax goods leaving; generally protectionist, raise domestic prices).</li>\n<li>ICE contract examples:</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Product</th><th>Contract Size (tonnes)</th><th>Tick Size (per tonne)</th><th>Tick Value (per contract)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cocoa</td><td>10</td><td>$1.00</td><td>$10.00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Robusta coffee</td><td>10</td><td>$1.00</td><td>$10.00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>White sugar</td><td>50</td><td>$0.10</td><td>$5.00</td></tr>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Options on these futures are <strong>American-style</strong>; premiums paid \"futures-style\" (portion posted as initial margin, variation margin settled against daily mark-to-market of the premium). All physically delivered; CCP is <strong>LCH SA</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>EDSP</strong> (exchange delivery settlement price) ≈ price at which the future traded at the end of the trading period on the last trading day. Sellers choose delivery location from exchange-specified points. Options on futures expire the month before the delivery month.</li>\n<li><strong>Holding cost</strong> = commodities' version of carrying cost (warehousing, spoilage, obsolescence, interest, taxes). Spoilage is a particular concern for softs/agriculturals.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Base and Precious Metals</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Trading concentrated on three exchanges: <strong>LME, CME Group, SHFE</strong>. LME added precious metal futures (2017) and hot-rolled coil steel futures (2019); SHFE added copper cathode.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply</strong> drivers: raw material availability, extraction/production costs. If <strong>marginal cost of mining</strong> exceeds price for a sustained period, miners may cut/cease production (marginal cost = market-clearing price; low-cost miners earn profit). Non-extraction costs: royalties, taxes (affected by political stability, environmental law), sustaining capex.</li>\n<li><strong>Demand</strong> drivers: global economic cycle, industrialisation (China, India), the green transition (EV demand for copper, cobalt, lithium, nickel), investor demand (hedge funds, commodity funds), producer hedging. Gold is a traditional <strong>safe haven</strong>/inflation hedge — record CME Gold futures prices 2021–2023 during the inflationary period.</li>\n<li>All metals incur holding/warehousing costs, increasing with holding period.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Metal</th><th>Major Uses</th></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Ferrous</td><td>Iron ore</td><td>Steelmaking</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Steel (hot-rolled coil)</td><td>Construction, car manufacture</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Steel (rebar)</td><td>Construction</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"11\">Non-ferrous</td><td>Aluminium</td><td>Aerospace, packaging, kitchen equipment, windows, cars, construction, canning</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cobalt</td><td>Lithium-ion batteries (EVs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Copper</td><td>Electrics, electronics, building</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gold</td><td>Jewellery, dentistry, computers/electronics, investment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lead</td><td>Batteries, petrol additive, buildings</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Molybdenum</td><td>Steelmaking</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nickel</td><td>Stainless steel and alloys</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Palladium</td><td>Catalytic converters, jewellery, dentistry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Platinum</td><td>Car manufacture, catalytic converters, jewellery, medical, dentistry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Silver</td><td>Jewellery, ornaments, photography, dentistry, electronics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tin</td><td>Packaging, power</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zinc</td><td>Galvanising, brass production</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Energy Products</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Includes crude oil, refined fractions (naphtha, butane, kerosene, petrol, heating/gas oil), and natural gas. Supply can be constrained by producers (e.g. <strong>OPEC</strong>). Demand driven by consumption (manufacturing, transport).</li>\n<li>Crude oil defined by three factors: <strong>field of origin</strong> (Brent, West Texas, Dubai, Oman), <strong>density</strong> (light/heavy), <strong>sulphur content</strong> (sweet &lt;0.5%, sour &gt;0.5%).</li>\n<li><strong>WTI</strong> is a <strong>stored-oil</strong> contract (delivery = nominate a US storage facility); <strong>Brent</strong> is a <strong>shipped</strong> contract (delivery = port). In April 2020, WTI briefly traded <strong>negative</strong> as financial participants failed to roll out of expiring contracts in time and physical storage was scarce/unavailable, forcing distressed selling.</li>\n<li>Producer cartels (e.g. <strong>OPEC</strong>) — an <strong>oligopoly</strong> — maintain minimum prices via agreed maximum production levels among members; success depends on member compliance.</li>\n<li>Political factors: government refusal to sell to certain countries; large buyer boycotts (e.g. US embargo on Russian oil) also move prices.</li>\n<li>Natural gas: replacing coal-fired power (lower CO2). Main futures: <strong>CME Henry Hub, ICE Dutch TTF, ICE UK NBP</strong>. Russia/Iran sanctions (major gas producers) have driven significant volatility.</li>\n<li>IMO lowered global shipping fuel sulphur limit from <strong>3.5% to 0.5% from 2020</strong>, widening the sweet/sour crude price discount.</li>\n<li><strong>Electricity futures</strong> (ICE, CME Group): a \"manufactured\" product from varied inputs; priced by region, time of day, and time of year.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Other Markets — Cryptoassets</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cryptoassets</strong>: broad term for cryptocurrencies plus non-currency digital assets like <strong>NFTs</strong>. <strong>Cryptocurrency</strong>: digital/virtual medium of exchange secured by cryptography, no central issuer/bank — uses <strong>decentralised control via a distributed ledger (blockchain)</strong>, verified via \"mining\".</li>\n<li><strong>Coins</strong> reside on their own blockchain; <strong>tokens</strong> sit on top of an existing blockchain. Well-known cryptocurrencies: Bitcoin (BTC), Litecoin (LTC), Ethereum (ETH), Ripple (XRP), Solana (SOL).</li>\n<li><strong>NFTs</strong>: unique, non-fungible digital assets (art, game items), unlike fungible cryptocurrency units.</li>\n<li>CME launched <strong>Bitcoin futures in 2017</strong> and <strong>Ether futures in February 2021</strong>.</li>\n<li>Risks: <strong>regulatory risk</strong> (evolving/jurisdictional, potential bans, KYC/AML burdens), <strong>volatility risk</strong> (especially less liquid coins/tokens), <strong>ESG risk</strong> (energy consumption/emissions from mining, e.g. Bitcoin mining in coal-heavy grids like China).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Other Commodities</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weather derivatives</strong>: protect against losses from unexpected weather. Based on <strong>heating degree days (HDDs)</strong> or <strong>cooling degree days (CDDs)</strong>. Settlement price = relevant month's HDD/CDD × <strong>$20</strong>. First traded OTC in 1997; exchanges (CME, LIFFE, CBOT) began listing futures/options in 1999.</li>\n<li><strong>Emissions</strong>: contracts on CO2, SO2, methane, NOx. <strong>Kyoto Protocol</strong> (signed 1997, 84 signatories) set reduction targets. <strong>EUA</strong> (European Union Allowance) = right to emit 1 tonne of CO2 (or equivalent). Market includes spot and forwards/futures. ICE offers EUA futures and physically-deliverable California Carbon Offset futures; CME has Global Emissions Offset Futures.</li>\n<li><strong>Freight derivatives</strong>: FFAs (forward freight agreements), container freight swaps, and options on freight rates for dry bulk, tankers, container ships. Settled against indices from the <strong>Baltic Exchange</strong> (dry/most wet) and <strong>Platts</strong> (Asian wet). Traded OTC via FFABA brokers or increasingly screen-based; cleared via NOS Clearing, ICE Futures Europe, NYMEX, or SGX.</li>\n<li><strong>Volatility</strong>: CBOE introduced the <strong>VIX</strong> in the early 1990s (initially based on S&P 100 option implied volatility). Underlying changed to <strong>S&P 500 in 2003</strong>; VIX futures launched <strong>2004</strong>; VIX options launched <strong>2006</strong>. Eurex trades VSTOXX futures (STOXX 50 volatility). <strong>Variance swaps</strong> began trading actively in 2000 (variance = another measure of underlying volatility). ETPs based on VIX futures also trade, with options available on the ETPs.</li>\n</ul>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Which body has, since April 2018, been responsible for calculating and publishing SONIA?", "options": ["The ICE Benchmark Administration", "The Bank of England", "The FCA", "The Wholesale Markets Brokers' Association"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Bank of England took over the calculation and publication of SONIA in April 2018, receiving transaction data from major institutions by 7am GMT and publishing the rate at 9am GMT."}, {"question": "SOFR is best described as a benchmark rate that is:", "options": ["Based on a daily survey of estimated interbank borrowing costs", "Volume-weighted and based on actual transactions in the general collateral repo market for US Treasuries", "The unsecured overnight borrowing cost for eurozone banks", "Published weekly by the UK Debt Management Office"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "SOFR (first published in 2018 by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York) is based on actual reported trades in the general collateral (GC) repo market for US Treasuries and is volume-weighted, rather than being derived from a survey."}, {"question": "€STR is calculated based on which minimum transaction size?", "options": ["Over €100,000", "Over €500,000", "Over €1 million", "Over €10 million"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "€STR indicates the wholesale euro unsecured overnight borrowing cost of eurozone banks and is calculated on all qualifying transactions over €1 million."}, {"question": "Prior to its nationalisation, Northern Rock relied on the money market for approximately what proportion of its funding in August 2007?", "options": ["25%", "50%", "75%", "90%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Northern Rock used the money market for up to 75% of its funding immediately prior to the global financial crisis. When lender confidence evaporated, it faced a funding crisis that ultimately led to nationalisation by the UK government."}, {"question": "In the UK, Treasury bills are typically issued with which maturities?", "options": ["28 days and 3 months", "91 days and 182 days", "3 months and 12 months", "182 days and 364 days"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "UK T-bills are typically issued with a maturity of 91 days (approximately three months), with some issued at 182 days (approximately six months), issued weekly by the DMO."}, {"question": "How does a Treasury bill generate a return for the investor?", "options": ["It pays a fixed semi-annual coupon", "It is issued at a discount to nominal value and redeemed at nominal value", "It pays a floating rate linked to SONIA", "It is issued at par and redeemed at a premium"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "T-bills pay no coupon. They are issued at a discount to their nominal (par/face) value and redeemed at nominal value; the discount is the investor's return."}, {"question": "Commercial paper is typically issued with minimum denominations of:", "options": ["US$50,000", "US$100,000", "US$500,000", "US$5,000,000"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Commercial paper is denominated in minimums of US$500,000 or similar amounts in other currencies, with maturities normally ranging from two to nine months."}, {"question": "In the quote GBP/USD = 1.3010/15, what does the rate 1.3010 represent?", "options": ["The offer at which the bank sells sterling", "The bid at which the bank buys sterling and sells dollars", "The mid-market rate", "The rate at which the bank buys dollars and sells sterling"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "1.3010 is the bid for GBP — the quoting bank buys sterling and sells dollars at this (cheaper) rate. 1.3015 is the offer, at which the bank sells sterling and buys dollars."}, {"question": "What is the standard settlement period for a wholesale spot FX transaction?", "options": ["T+0", "T+1", "T+2", "T+3"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "For most currency transactions in the wholesale spot market, settlement is T+2, meaning evidence of the currency transfer must be submitted within two trading days of the transaction."}, {"question": "A currency trading at a forward premium relative to spot implies that:", "options": ["The currency has a higher interest rate than the counter currency", "The currency has a lower interest rate than the counter currency", "The currency is expected to be more volatile", "The currency is non-convertible"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Under interest rate parity, the currency with the lower interest rate trades at a forward premium (its forward price is higher than spot), while the higher-interest-rate currency trades at a forward discount."}, {"question": "USD/JPY spot is 110.00/110.05 and the 2-month forward points are 0.65/0.60. What is the 2-month outright forward rate?", "options": ["110.65/110.65", "109.35/109.45", "110.65/110.65 added", "109.45/109.35"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Since the forward points are decreasing (bid decrease 0.65 greater than offer decrease 0.60), this represents a discount, so points are subtracted from spot: 110.00 − 0.65 = 109.35 (bid), 110.05 − 0.60 = 109.45 (offer)."}, {"question": "A GBP-denominated fund manager wants to hedge the translation risk of the capital value (in GBP) of US dollar assets held in the fund. Which technique is described in the source material for this purpose?", "options": ["Buying GBP/USD forward outrights only", "Using GBP/USD forward swaps – selling USD spot and simultaneously buying GBP forward", "Entering into a non-deliverable forward", "Buying a call option on the S&P 500"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The source distinguishes hedging transaction risk on USD dividends (via forward outrights) from managing translation risk on the capital value of US assets, which uses GBP/USD forward swaps (selling USD spot, buying GBP forward)."}, {"question": "Spot EUR/USD = 1.0950, USD 6-month SOFR = 0.45%, EUR 6-month EURIBOR = 0.57%. Using Forward = Spot × [1+(n×r1)]/[1+(n×r2)], what is the theoretical 6-month forward EUR/USD rate?", "options": ["1.0956", "1.0950", "1.0943", "1.0937"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Forward = 1.0950 × [1+(180/360×0.0045)] / [1+(180/360×0.0057)] = 1.0943. The euro trades at a slight discount versus the dollar since its interest rate is higher."}, {"question": "A non-deliverable forward (NDF) is best defined as:", "options": ["A physically-settled forward on a freely convertible currency", "A cash-settled forward contract on a thinly traded or non-convertible currency, settling the difference between the agreed rate and the spot rate at settlement", "An exchange-traded futures contract on a major currency pair", "A spot FX transaction settling beyond T+2"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An NDF is a cash-settled, short-term forward FX contract on a thinly traded or non-convertible currency. It has a fixing date and a settlement date, and is usually quoted/settled in USD."}, {"question": "A bond is quoted (clean price) at 98.50 on a $1,000 par bond, with $8.33 of accrued interest. What is the dirty (traded) price?", "options": ["$985.00", "$990.17", "$993.33", "$1,000.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Dirty Price = Clean Price + Accrued Interest = $985.00 + $8.33 = $993.33, the actual amount paid/received on settlement."}, {"question": "UK index-linked gilts first issued from 2005 onward have an indexation lag of:", "options": ["One month", "Three months", "Six months", "Eight months"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "UK index-linked gilts issued before 2005 have an eight-month indexation lag, while those issued from 2005 have a three-month indexation lag."}, {"question": "What is a sovereign eurobond?", "options": ["A bond issued exclusively in euros by any government", "A bond issued by a government in a currency other than its own domestic currency", "A bond issued by the European Central Bank", "A corporate bond listed on a Eurozone exchange"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Despite the name, sovereign eurobonds are bonds issued by governments in any currency other than their home currency (not exclusively euros), commonly to take advantage of lower borrowing rates."}, {"question": "STRIPS allow investors to:", "options": ["Convert a fixed coupon bond into a floating rate note", "Separately trade the individual coupons and principal amount of a strippable bond", "Buy a bond with no exposure to interest rate risk", "Purchase government bonds without paying accrued interest"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "STRIPS (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities) allow the individual coupon and principal cash flows of a strippable government bond to be traded separately, each behaving like a zero-coupon instrument."}, {"question": "An inverted yield curve typically reflects:", "options": ["An expectation of rising interest rates in the future", "An expectation of a significant reduction in interest rates in the future", "The complete absence of any liquidity preference among investors", "Equal yields at all maturities"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An inverted yield curve occurs when the market expects a significant future reduction in interest rates; this expectation outweighs (but does not eliminate) investors' normal liquidity preference for shorter maturities."}, {"question": "The 10-year Greek government bond yield spread over German bunds widened from approximately 29bps in 2011 to approximately how many bps in Q1 2012?", "options": ["496 bps", "1,496 bps", "2,896 bps", "4,696 bps"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The spread widened dramatically from 29bps in 2011 to 4,696bps in the first quarter of 2012, with 10-year Greek yields rising to a high of 48.6% while German bunds traded around 1.85%."}, {"question": "Which statement correctly distinguishes bearer shares from registered shares?", "options": ["Bearer shares are recorded in a company register maintained by a registrar; registered shares are not", "Registered shares are transferred by physical delivery of the certificate; bearer shares require endorsement", "Bearer shares have no register — physical possession of the certificate is the sole evidence of ownership, and they transfer on delivery", "There is no practical difference between the two"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "With bearer shares, the issuer does not maintain a register; physical possession of the certificate is the sole evidence of ownership, dividends are paid by clipping a coupon, and the shares are negotiable and transfer on delivery without endorsement."}, {"question": "In a liquidation, where do ordinary shareholders rank for capital repayment relative to creditors?", "options": ["Ahead of all creditors", "Pari passu with unsecured creditors", "Last, after all other creditors have been repaid in full", "They have no entitlement to any capital repayment"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Ordinary shareholders' capital repayment right on winding up ranks last — only after all other creditors have been repaid in full — reflecting their position as owners rather than creditors."}, {"question": "A company undertakes a 2-for-1 stock split. What is the immediate mechanical effect on an existing shareholder and the share price?", "options": ["The shareholder's number of shares halves and the share price doubles", "The shareholder's number of shares doubles and the share price halves", "The number of shares is unchanged but the price doubles", "The shareholder receives a cash payment equal to the value of one extra share"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a positive stock split (e.g. 2-for-1), the number of shares in issue doubles, each shareholder receives twice as many shares at no cost, and the share price halves, with no change in the shareholder's total holding value."}, {"question": "A rights issue is generally viewed negatively by the market in the short term primarily because:", "options": ["It always signals an upcoming takeover", "It is often perceived as a sign the company is cash-strapped, and can have a dilutive effect on existing shareholders", "It automatically increases the company's credit rating", "It removes shareholders' pre-emption rights"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A rights issue is often viewed as a signal of limited funding sources ('cash-strapped'), and can dilute existing shareholders if overall company value does not rise proportionately with the increased share count."}, {"question": "On ICE, a cocoa futures contract has a contract size of 10 tonnes and a tick size of $1.00 per tonne. What is the tick value per contract?", "options": ["$1.00", "$5.00", "$10.00", "$50.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tick value per contract = contract size × tick size = 10 tonnes × $1.00 = $10.00, matching the ICE contract specification given for cocoa (and robusta coffee)."}, {"question": "Crude oil is classified as 'sour' when its sulphur content is:", "options": ["Below 0.1%", "Below 0.5%", "Above 0.5%", "Above 5.0%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Crude oil with sulphur content above 0.5% is classified as 'sour'; below 0.5% is classified as 'sweet'. Crude is also defined by field of origin and density (light/heavy)."}, {"question": "In April 2020, WTI crude oil futures famously traded at a negative price. What was the key structural reason for this?", "options": ["WTI is a shipped contract and no ports were available", "WTI is a stored-oil contract, and holders who failed to roll out in time faced a lack of available physical storage capacity", "OPEC flooded the market with supply", "The IMO banned the delivery of sour crude"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Unlike Brent (a shipped contract), WTI is a stored-oil contract requiring delivery into a US storage facility. In April 2020, financial participants who had not rolled out of expiring contracts faced scarce/unavailable storage, forcing distressed selling that briefly pushed prices negative."}, {"question": "The settlement price of a weather derivative based on HDDs or CDDs is calculated as the relevant month's HDD/CDD index value multiplied by:", "options": ["$5", "$10", "$20", "$50"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The settlement price of the underlying weather index equals the value of the relevant month's HDD or CDD multiplied by $20."}, {"question": "A European Union Allowance (EUA) gives the holder the right to:", "options": ["Import one tonne of a soft commodity duty-free", "Emit one tonne of CO2 or the equivalent amount of another greenhouse gas", "Purchase one barrel of crude oil at a fixed price", "Trade one lot of freight forward agreements without a broker"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An EUA (European Union Allowance) gives the holder the right to emit one tonne of CO2, or the equivalent amount of another greenhouse gas. Entities with surplus allowances can sell or bank them; those exceeding their allocation must buy more or face penalties."}, {"question": "Which underlying index did the CBOE's VIX originally track when introduced in the early 1990s, before it was changed in 2003?", "options": ["S&P 500 index options", "S&P 100 index options", "DAX index options", "STOXX 50 index options"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The VIX was originally based on the implied volatility of S&P 100 index options when introduced in the early 1990s. The underlying index was changed to the S&P 500 in 2003, and VIX futures launched in 2004."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is SONIA and who publishes it?", "back": "The Sterling Overnight Index Average — the effective overnight rate for unsecured GBP transactions in the London market. Published by the Bank of England daily at 9am GMT (since April 2018), based on data submitted by institutions by 7am GMT."}, {"front": "What is SOFR based on?", "back": "The Secured Overnight Financing Rate — a USD benchmark based on the single-day repo rate for US Treasuries (general collateral repo market), volume-weighted from actual transactions, first published in 2018 by the NY Fed."}, {"front": "What is €STR and what is its minimum transaction threshold?", "back": "The Euro Short-Term Rate — wholesale unsecured overnight borrowing cost for eurozone banks, calculated on transactions over €1 million, published each TARGET2 business day."}, {"front": "How do T-bills generate a return for investors?", "back": "They pay no coupon. They are issued at a discount to nominal (par) value and redeemed at nominal value at maturity — the discount is the investor's return."}, {"front": "UK T-bill maturities?", "back": "Typically 91 days (~3 months); some issued at 182 days (~6 months). Issued weekly by the UK Debt Management Office (DMO)."}, {"front": "Typical CD maturities and how are they purchased/redeemed?", "back": "Most common maturities are 3 and 6 months. Purchased at nominal value; redeemed at nominal value plus a specified fixed interest rate at maturity. Early redemption incurs a penalty."}, {"front": "What backs commercial paper (CP), and what are typical maturities/denominations?", "back": "CP is unsecured but normally backed by unused bank credit lines. Maturities normally 2–9 months; minimum denominations of US$500,000 (or equivalent)."}, {"front": "Why did Northern Rock fail in 2007?", "back": "It relied on the money market for up to 75% of its funding. When lenders lost confidence, funding dried up despite Northern Rock offering a premium rate, leading to a funding crisis and eventual nationalisation."}, {"front": "In GBP/USD = 1.3010/15, what do the bid and offer represent?", "back": "1.3010 = bid: the bank buys GBP/sells USD (cheaper rate). 1.3015 = offer: the bank sells GBP/buys USD (more expensive rate)."}, {"front": "Standard settlement period for wholesale spot FX?", "back": "T+2 — evidence of currency transfer must be submitted by each counterparty within two trading days of the transaction."}, {"front": "Interest rate parity rule for forward FX premiums/discounts?", "back": "Higher interest rate currency = forward discount. Lower interest rate currency = forward premium."}, {"front": "Forward FX rate formula (periods under 1 year)?", "back": "Forward = Spot × [1+(n×r1)] / [1+(n×r2)], where r1 = traded/foreign currency rate, r2 = base/domestic currency rate, n = period."}, {"front": "What is a pip?", "back": "The smallest change that a given exchange rate can make (e.g. $0.0001 in EUR/USD)."}, {"front": "Define translation risk vs transaction risk in FX.", "back": "Translation risk: the risk that foreign-currency assets on a balance sheet/portfolio decline in value purely due to FX translation back to domestic currency. Transaction risk: the FX risk incurred at the point of an actual transaction (e.g. a future purchase/receipt in foreign currency)."}, {"front": "What is a non-deliverable forward (NDF)?", "back": "A cash-settled, short-term forward FX contract on a thinly traded or non-convertible currency. Settled as the difference between the agreed rate and spot rate at settlement, applied to a notional amount. Has a fixing date and settlement date; usually quoted/settled in USD."}, {"front": "What are the fixing date and settlement date of an NDF?", "back": "Fixing date: when the difference between market rate and agreed rate is calculated. Settlement date: when the payment of that difference is due."}, {"front": "UK Gilts: coupon frequency, maturity range, settlement?", "back": "Semi-annual coupons; maturities up to 50 years; settle T+1."}, {"front": "US Treasuries: bills, notes, bonds maturities?", "back": "T-bills: up to 1 year. T-notes: 2, 3, 5, 7 and 10 years. T-bonds: 20 or 30 years. All pay semi-annual coupons, settle T+1."}, {"front": "Formula for dirty (traded) bond price?", "back": "Dirty Price = Clean Price + Accrued Interest."}, {"front": "What is accrued interest on a bond?", "back": "The portion of the next coupon the seller is entitled to, calculated from the last coupon date up to (but not including) the settlement date."}, {"front": "Indexation lag for UK index-linked gilts?", "back": "8-month lag for gilts first issued before 2005; 3-month lag for those issued from 2005 onward."}, {"front": "What is a sovereign eurobond?", "back": "A bond issued by a government in a currency other than its own domestic currency (not necessarily euros) — issuer bears FX risk if the foreign currency strengthens against the local currency."}, {"front": "Moody's investment grade long-term rating range?", "back": "Aaa to Baa3 (equivalent to S&P/Fitch AAA to BBB−)."}, {"front": "What are STRIPS?", "back": "Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities — allows individual coupons and the principal of a strippable bond to be traded separately as distinct instruments."}, {"front": "What is a Zero Coupon Bond (ZCB)?", "back": "A bond that pays no coupons; purchased at a discount to its nominal value and redeemed at full nominal value at maturity — a single payment to the holder."}, {"front": "Describe the normal (upward-sloping) yield curve and why it occurs.", "back": "Yields rise with maturity, reflecting investors' liquidity preference — they demand higher yields to compensate for holding less liquid, longer-dated securities (also compensates for inflation and default risk over time)."}, {"front": "What does an inverted yield curve typically signal?", "back": "That the market expects a significant future reduction in interest rates, often associated with expectations of an economic slowdown; short yields exceed long yields."}, {"front": "Why do corporate bonds usually yield more than government bonds of equivalent maturity?", "back": "Higher risk of default relative to government bonds, reflected in credit ratings; government bonds serve as the benchmark yield."}, {"front": "How do bearer shares differ from registered shares?", "back": "Bearer shares have no register of ownership — physical possession of the certificate is sole evidence of ownership; they are transferred on delivery without endorsement. Registered shares have ownership recorded in a register maintained by a registrar."}, {"front": "Where do ordinary shareholders rank in a winding-up for capital repayment?", "back": "Last — only after all other creditors have been repaid in full."}, {"front": "What are pre-emption rights?", "back": "The right of existing shareholders to be given the opportunity to subscribe for a rights issue on a pro rata basis, preventing dilution of their ownership/control."}, {"front": "Effect of a 2-for-1 stock split on share count and price?", "back": "Number of shares doubles, share price halves, at no cost to shareholders — a 'positive share split' intended to improve affordability/liquidity."}, {"front": "What is a bonus (scrip/capitalisation) issue?", "back": "New shares issued to existing shareholders pro rata to their holdings, in lieu of paying a cash dividend from profits — typically causes an initial price fall but can support a longer-term valuation rise via improved liquidity."}, {"front": "List the three broad commodity categories.", "back": "Agriculturals and softs (wheat, corn, livestock, sugar, coffee, cotton, cocoa); Energy (crude oil, refined products, power, natural gas, renewables); Minerals (base metals like copper/lithium, precious metals like gold/platinum)."}, {"front": "How is crude oil classified?", "back": "By field of origin (e.g. Brent, West Texas, Dubai, Oman); density (light/heavy); and sulphur content (sweet <0.5%, sour >0.5%)."}, {"front": "Difference between WTI and Brent crude contracts?", "back": "WTI is a stored-oil contract — delivery requires nominating a US storage facility. Brent is a shipped contract — delivery is to a port. This distinction caused WTI to briefly trade negative in April 2020 amid a storage shortage."}, {"front": "Weather derivative settlement price formula?", "back": "Settlement price = relevant month's HDD or CDD index value × $20."}, {"front": "What does a European Union Allowance (EUA) represent?", "back": "The right to emit one tonne of CO2, or the equivalent amount of another greenhouse gas; tradeable, can be banked if in surplus."}, {"front": "Which indices are freight derivatives (e.g. FFAs) settled against?", "back": "The Baltic Exchange (dry and most wet contracts) and Platts (Asian wet contracts)."}, {"front": "Difference between coins and tokens in cryptoasset markets?", "back": "Coins reside on their own blockchain. Tokens are created via an algorithm/methodology and sit on top of an existing blockchain."}]}, {"id": "der-ch3", "number": 3, "title": "Market Structure", "examWeight": "9 of 100 questions (~9%)", "summaryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Market Structure</strong> covers how derivatives are traded: on-exchange versus over-the-counter (OTC), the mechanics of order flow, and the operational/regulatory framework surrounding trades. A <strong>derivatives exchange</strong> provides a marketplace for standardised futures, options and other contracts, using open outcry (pits/floor), electronic platforms, or both. Major exchanges include <strong>Euronext</strong> (Amsterdam, Brussels, Lisbon, Paris, London; Optiq platform; cleared via LCH SA/EuroCCP), <strong>ICE</strong> (six clearing entities including ICE Clear Credit for credit derivatives and ICE NGX for North American gas/electricity), <strong>CME Group</strong> (CME, CBOT, NYMEX; Globex platform), <strong>CBOE</strong> (largest US options exchange, founded by CBOT in 1973, first to list standardised exchange-traded stock options and to introduce FLEX options and LEAPS), <strong>Eurex</strong> (world's largest derivatives exchange), and the <strong>LME</strong> (electronic LMESelect, telephone, and open-outcry \"Ring\" trading).</p>\n<p><strong>OTC markets</strong> are decentralised, bilaterally negotiated, less transparent and less regulated than exchanges, but offer tailored products, confidentiality (\"risk pricing\"), and no daily margining — useful for corporate hedgers, though at the cost of lower liquidity and higher credit risk. <strong>Order types</strong> (limit, market, market-if-touched, opening/closing, GTC/GFD, immediate or cancel, fill or kill, stop, stop limit, day order) determine execution certainty versus price certainty. <strong>Wholesale trading facilities</strong> — block trades and basis trades — allow large, privately negotiated deals to be reported into the exchange outside the central order book, subject to minimum sizes (e.g. ICE Futures London: 100 contracts for short sterling/gilt, 500 for FTSE) and strict reporting deadlines (typically 5–15 minutes). The <strong>FLEX facility</strong> (pioneered by CBOE) blends OTC customisation with exchange clearing.</p>\n<p><strong>Market conventions</strong> cover novation (the clearing house becoming counterparty to every trade), cash versus physical settlement, principal versus agency dealing (dual capacity), cross trading, volume and open interest (a key liquidity measure; the highest-OI contract is the \"front month\"), and the audit trail (OATS). <strong>Account types</strong> are house, segregated and non-segregated, with segregation protecting client assets on member default. <strong>Give-ups/allocations</strong> separate execution from clearing, governed by Master Give-Up Agreements. Finally, <strong>trade reporting</strong> and <strong>price transparency</strong> (bid/offer, volume, open interest, delivery month codes) underpin investor confidence, with exchanges monitoring open interest to guard against credit-limit breaches and unwanted deliveries.</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "3.1 Derivative Exchanges — Introduction to Exchanges and OTC Trading", "html": "\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.1.1</strong> — know the main products of the main derivatives exchanges.</p>\n<p>A <strong>derivatives exchange</strong> provides a marketplace to buy and sell futures, options and other contracts of specific quantities of a commodity or financial instrument at a specified price, with delivery at a specified future time. The exchange establishes a <strong>trading mechanism/platform</strong>: traditional pits/floor trading, electronic screen trading, or both for some contracts.</p>\n<h4>Key exchange groups</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Euronext</strong> — Europe's largest exchange; spans Amsterdam, Brussels, Lisbon, Paris and London (the Amsterdam/Brussels/Lisbon/Paris/London ICE markets). Trading platform <strong>Optiq</strong>, using Secure Financial Transaction Infrastructure (SFTI). Trades cleared by <strong>LCH SA</strong> or <strong>EuroCCP</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ICE Clearing</strong> — six entities by geography/product: ICE Clear Europe, ICE Clear US, ICE Clear Singapore, ICE Clear Netherlands (agriculture, energy, interest rates, equities, metals); <strong>ICE Clear Credit</strong> (credit derivatives); <strong>ICE NGX</strong> (North American natural gas and electricity).</li>\n<li><strong>Nasdaq Group</strong> — US company owning the Nasdaq stock market plus seven European (Nordic/Baltic) stock exchanges. Trading systems: Genium INET, CONDICO (commodities trading/clearing), CLICK XT. Clearing: Genium INET Clearing and SECUR.</li>\n<li><strong>Nasdaq Options Market (NOM)</strong> — one of Nasdaq's two electronic options markets; runs on INET technology; trades Nasdaq and PHLX options; uses parent's clearing systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Regional exchange summary</h4>\n<p><strong>UK:</strong> ICE Futures Europe (fully electronic; Brent Crude, WTI, gas oil, electricity, coal, natural gas, emissions/carbon credits, equity index/single stock futures (SSFs), short-term interest rates, bond futures, GBP/USD & EUR/USD; cleared via ICE Clear Europe). LSE Derivatives (electronic; FTSE 100, FTSE Super Liquid (largest 35 shares), Norwegian OBX; cleared/guaranteed by CCP Clearing with LCH). London Metal Exchange (electronic via LMESelect, telephone, and open outcry via \"the Ring\"; aluminium, copper, zinc, tin, lead, nickel — global benchmark prices; also platinum group, EV battery and ferrous futures; cleared via LME Clear).</p>\n<p><strong>US:</strong> CME (part of CME Group; CME Globex platform; commodities, STIRs, FX, US economic data, equity indices, weather, real estate; processed via CME Direct). CBOT (part of CME Group; EOS Trader and CME Direct linked to Globex/Clearport; open outcry for some options; agricultural products, gold, silver, US notes/bonds/swaps, Dow Jones indices; joint trading agreement with SGX; processed under the JADE agreement by FEC/FEC+). CBOE (largest US options exchange, established by CBOT in 1973; first to list standardised exchange-traded stock options, cleared by the Options Clearing Corporation (OCC); first to introduce FLEX options and LEAPS — Long-Term Equity Anticipation Securities, options with expiry &gt;1 year; subsidiary CBOE Futures Exchange (CFE) trades VIX futures and corporate bond index futures). NYMEX (part of CME Group; CME Globex and NYMEX ClearPort for OTC; metals, energy, spread options e.g. Brent/WTI). NASDAQ (PHLX) (floor trading plus PHLX XL electronic platform; stock, index, equity sector and currency options, standardised and FLEX). ICE Futures US (ICE Group also owns NYSE; electronic trading of financial, agricultural, metals and softs contracts; cleared via ICE Clear US).</p>\n<p><strong>Europe:</strong> Eurex — electronically-traded, the <strong>world's largest derivatives exchange</strong>; STIRs, equities, index/single stock futures, volatility index, credit derivatives, ETFs, FLEX options; cleared via Eurex Clearing AG. Euronext exchanges (Amsterdam, Brussels, Lisbon, Paris, Oslo, Dublin, London) — Optiq multi-market platform; currencies, swaps, equities, commodities; cleared via Euronext Clearing. MEFF — official Spanish exchange, subsidiary of BME Group; MEFF Derivative Trading Platform; interest rates, bonds, IBEX 35, Spanish shares. Nasdaq Nordic — subsidiary operating all eight electronic securities markets in Nordic/Baltic/Caucasian regions.</p>\n<p><strong>Asia:</strong> SGX (electronic via SGX QUEST; interest rates, bonds, regional equity indices; JGBs, Eurodollar/Euroyen, Nikkei 225; cleared via SGX-DC). OSE (part of JPX; physical and electronic; Nikkei 225, individual Japanese shares). TSE (part of JPX; electronic; TOPIX/TSE indices, individual shares, JGBs; via the Tdex+ system). KRX Futures Market Division (automatic trading system; single stocks, equity options, Korean/US indices, gold and CD interest rate futures).</p>\n<p><strong>Emerging markets:</strong> B3 (Brazil, formerly BM&amp;F; agricultural, FX, interest rate and equity futures; PUMA trading system; separate FX clearing house). Dubai Mercantile Exchange (DME) — fully electronic; Oman crude oil future, Singapore fuel oil, Brent/Oman and WTI/Oman spreads; Globex or ISV access; cleared by CME Clearing. NCDEX (India) — agricultural futures/options; electronic, customisable; own clearing house with depository delivery. Safex (South Africa, part of JSE) — electronic; agricultural, gold, interest rates, FX, JSE/FTSE index, single stock futures. SHFE (China) — trading floor and electronic; metals, rubber, fuel oil; physical delivery via approved warehouses. CFFEX (China) — fully electronic; CSI 200 index futures (its only contract). NSE (India) — NIFTY futures/options, sub-indices, single stocks, government bonds, T-bills, INR currency pairs; via NEAT system. BSE (India) — fully screen-based (DTSS) plus internet trading; equity, index and currency derivatives. MCX (India) — India's largest commodities futures exchange; bullion, energy, metals, agricultural; Demat clearing/delivery system. Bursa Malaysia (KLCI, crude palm oil, gold futures). Saudi Tadawul (MSCI Tadawul 30 Index futures). Nairobi Securities Exchange (NSE25 index and single stock futures; cleared via NSE Clear). FMDQ Nigeria — OTC platform for quotation, clearing and reporting of money market instruments, government bonds and FX forwards, notably Naira-settled FX NDFs; cleared via FMDQ Clear, also offers depository service.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "3.1.1 OTC Structure and Trading", "html": "\n<p>An <strong>OTC market</strong> is a decentralised market where participants trade directly between two parties without a central exchange or broker. It has no physical location; trading is conducted electronically or by telephone.</p>\n<p>Dealers act as <strong>market-makers</strong>, quoting buy/sell prices. A trade can be executed without other participants being aware of the price — OTC markets are generally <strong>less transparent</strong> and subject to <strong>fewer regulations</strong> than exchanges, so liquidity may command a premium.</p>\n<p>Non-financial corporations often prefer OTC over exchange-traded derivatives (ETDs) to avoid daily margining and the operational risk of unwanted physical delivery. OTC products typically eliminate margining, settle less frequently, and banking teams tailor products to specific hedging needs.</p>\n<p>OTC trading is associated with <strong>\"high touch\"</strong> services — direct trading/sales advice, often 24/7, at higher cost — versus exchange-traded products' <strong>\"low touch\"</strong> service — more automated, less human contact. This justifies OTC products' substantially higher trading charges versus exchange equivalents.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "3.1.2 Order Types", "html": "\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.2.1</strong> — understand order types, uses and effects.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Limit order</strong> — stipulates a required price level; if execution is possible at a better price, it fills at that improved price (broker cannot keep the difference). Execution is not guaranteed.</li>\n<li><strong>Market order</strong> — buy/sell immediately at the best available price, no price stipulated; a buy hits the ask, a sell hits the bid. Used when certainty of execution outweighs price certainty.</li>\n<li><strong>Market-if-touched (MIT)</strong> — combination of limit and market order: specifies a limit; once the market trades at/through that price, it becomes a market order executed at the best prevailing price.</li>\n<li><strong>Opening order</strong> — with or without a stipulated price, executed during the official opening period (usually first two minutes); cancelled if unfilled in that period.</li>\n<li><strong>Closing order</strong> — as above but executed towards the end of the day.</li>\n<li><strong>Good 'til cancelled (GTC)</strong> — an instruction defining validity period; firm until the client says otherwise. Most real-world orders are <strong>Good For the Day (GFD)</strong> by default — cancelled if unfilled at the close.</li>\n<li><strong>Immediate or cancel</strong> — execute in whole or part if market conditions permit; unexecuted portion cancelled.</li>\n<li><strong>Fill or kill (FOK)</strong> — like immediate or cancel, but must be executed in its entirety or cancelled.</li>\n<li><strong>Stop (or loss) order</strong> — designed to limit losses; closes out a position if price moves against the trader. Triggered when the market trades at the stipulated (stop) price; no guarantee of fill at that exact price — in volatile markets it fills at whatever price is available once triggered.</li>\n<li><strong>Stop limit order</strong> — as a stop order, but stipulates two prices; once the stop price is reached, it becomes a limit order executed at the specified price or better.</li>\n<li><strong>Day order</strong> — expires automatically if not executed on the day placed (standard orders are day orders by default unless otherwise instructed).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Exchanges allow correction of genuine broker errors via a correction declaration, subject to exchange policy, provided the client is not disadvantaged. <strong>Cross trades</strong> (match established by prior negotiation) are permitted if entered as separate buy and sell orders, where exchange rules allow. Recognised strategies include spreads, straddles, strangles and synthetics (see chapter 8).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "3.2 Wholesale Trading Facilities", "html": "\n<p><strong>Learning objectives 3.3.1/3.3.2</strong> — know the details of block and basis trades and their significance.</p>\n<p>Most exchanges recognise two types of wholesale trade:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Block trades</strong> — large transactions between an exchange member and a wholesale client (sufficient knowledge/expertise), allowing bilaterally agreed transactions without delay and with certainty of price/execution. Complements (does not replace) central markets. Each exchange sets which products qualify and the minimum size:\n  <ul>\n    <li>ICE Futures London: minimum <strong>100 contracts</strong> for short sterling and gilt futures; <strong>500 contracts</strong> for FTSE futures.</li>\n    <li>HKEX: minimum <strong>100 contracts</strong> for stock index futures/options; <strong>500 contracts</strong> for individual stock options; once reported, denoted <strong>INT</strong> on HKEX's ticker window.</li>\n  </ul>\n  Block trades must be reported within a specified period — generally <strong>five minutes to an hour</strong>, depending on contract/exchange — and are negotiated away from the central order book but reported to the exchange once agreed.</li>\n<li><strong>Basis trades</strong> — the simultaneous exchange of a financial asset/instrument (e.g. a government bond or basket of shares) together with an offsetting number of futures contracts, privately negotiated between two parties. As with block trades, the futures leg is executed outside the central order book and reported into the exchange once agreed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>FLEX facility</strong> combines OTC flexibility with exchange clearing advantages — e.g. ICE Futures London's facility for FTSE 100 index options. Originally introduced by the <strong>CBOE</strong>, it lets any exchange member customise trades on strike/exercise price and expiration date. Wholesale trading facilities generally let participants enter off-order-book trades into the exchange's system, combining customisation with standardised clearing/settlement (bilateral agreement on price and quantity).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "3.3 Over-the-Counter (OTC) Markets", "html": "\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.4.1</strong> — understand the reasons for using OTC markets.</p>\n<h4>Reasons to trade OTC</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tailoring</strong> — products/contracts can be unique or made to fit a counterparty's specific requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality / \"risk pricing\"</strong> — trades negotiated bilaterally between client and broker/dealer; since orders aren't shown on-exchange, counterparties can mitigate price slippage on large orders by requesting a whole-order quote from an OTC counterparty (usually a financial institution).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Worked example (risk pricing / hedge efficiency):</em> A food manufacturer hedges a 100,000-tonne physical wheat purchase at £200/tonne by wanting to sell May-23 ICE Feed Wheat UK futures (also priced at £200/tonne). Open interest is 2,500 lots (250,000 tonnes); previous day's volume was 160 lots (16,000 tonnes). Selling 1,000 lots (equivalent) as a market order would be over six times the prior day's volume and 40% of open interest — likely to move price down significantly, eroding \"hedge efficiency\". Instead, the manufacturer's bank OTC desk bids £195/tonne as a \"risk\" price via a commodity swap referencing the futures; the £5/tonne discount compensates the bank for slippage risk when it hedges in the exchange market (or the bank may simply retain/speculate on the risk).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counterparty risk mitigation</strong> — OTC trades must now be reported to a facility enabling regulatory surveillance; OTC no longer offers complete anonymity as it did before the 2007–08 global financial crisis. Off-exchange trades increasingly must be executed on an <strong>alternative trading system (ATS)</strong> and reported to regulators. An ATS — typically set up by financial institutions to net client orders against each other or the institution's own book — is called a <strong>multilateral trading facility (MTF)</strong>. Central clearing for OTC trades is increasingly required (see chapter 1, section 8, for full ETD/OTC comparison).</li>\n<li><strong>Lighter operations</strong> — OTC products tend to be lighter on back-office teams (no daily margining), useful for non-financial corporations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Main disadvantages</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically lower liquidity; can be difficult/expensive to exit or offset — less fungible than ETDs.</li>\n<li>Cost is proportional to the degree of tailoring and resulting illiquidity, compounded by higher credit risk from the lack of daily margining.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "3.4 Market Conventions", "html": "\n<p><strong>Learning objectives 3.5.1–3.5.3</strong> — order flow, principal vs agency dealing, trade registration/input/matching.</p>\n<p>Every exchange uses a <strong>clearing house</strong>, which clears all trades and supervises processing/delivery. The process by which the clearing house becomes counterparty to every trade is <strong>novation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each contract specifies its settlement procedure. Most contracts close out before maturity, but each has a settlement/delivery process the clearing house supervises.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cash settlement</strong> — often called a <strong>contract for difference (CFD)</strong>; requires net cash payment of the difference between the final closing price and the previous day's closing price. All contracts are marked to market daily; the clearing house pays/receives that day's variation margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical settlement (physical delivery)</strong> — delivery of the underlying asset as set out in the contract specification. With futures, the <strong>seller initiates delivery</strong>; the clearing house then assigns a buyer at random and advises them. Most financial futures have a single deliverable asset (exception: long-dated government bond contracts, where the exchange maintains a list of eligible bonds, each with a price factor determining the final invoice payment). With <strong>options</strong>, the <strong>buyer initiates delivery</strong> by exercising; for options on futures, the exchange automatically replaces the exercised option with the corresponding future at the strike price. With <strong>commodities</strong>, delivery may involve a choice of acceptable grades and a list of authorised warehouses/agents; e.g. the LME's network of authorised warehouses issues warrants (warehouse receipts) recorded in the LMEsword delivery system.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.4.1 Principal and Agent Functions</h4>\n<p>Exchange members execute trades as either <strong>principal</strong> or <strong>agent</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Principal order</strong> — the member trades for its own account, bearing the profits/losses itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Agency order</strong> — the firm trades on behalf of a client (as broker); may give the client anonymity; the trade is registered to a client account, and the client bears the profits/losses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dual capacity</strong> is the member's ability to act as either principal or agent in any transaction — allowed to trade for its own account and execute client orders. Argued to benefit clients (better prices, added insight/advice) but can also create conflicts of interest.</p>\n<h4>3.4.2 Cross Trading</h4>\n<p><strong>Cross trading</strong> occurs when a brokerage matches a buy order from one customer against a sell order from another, where one side is non-competitive (no specific price given). On US exchanges, permissible only per the Commodity Exchange Act, CFTC regulations, and exchange rules. A dual-capacity member with a buyer and seller at a given price, or willing to deal as principal, can enter into cross trades (\"matching orders\") without taking the trade to the open market — but for price transparency, the order must still be executed within the market. Strict rules apply (e.g. in open outcry, cross trades must be verified/signed off by exchange officials/pit observers); exchange rules govern whether cross trades are permitted and reporting conditions.</p>\n<h4>3.4.3 Trade Execution and the Nature of Orders</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Opening trades</strong> — create a market position, incurring rights/obligations, subject to subsequent P&amp;L; can be opening purchases or sales.</li>\n<li><strong>Closing trades</strong> — offset an existing long/short position, extinguishing rights/obligations; the point at which P&amp;L is crystallised; can be closing sales or purchases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Volume and Open Interest</strong> (both measure liquidity):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Volume</strong> — cumulative number of contracts traded so far each day, per product/expiry month.</li>\n<li><strong>Open interest</strong> — total number of contracts for a delivery month that remain open (to be closed out or taken to delivery); an important liquidity indicator. The contract with the highest open interest is generally nearest to expiration and is called the <strong>\"front month\"</strong> contract. Open interest is the sum of all open long positions <em>or</em> the sum of all open short positions — not the sum of both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Audit trail</strong> — a record of a transaction, tracing it start to finish (and vice versa), aiding dispute resolution and exchange confidence. Since the growth of screen-based/electronic trading (late 1990s), exchanges typically require members to operate an <strong>Order Audit Trail System (OATS)</strong> to track all orders.</p>\n<h4>3.4.4 Trade Registration and Clearing Mechanisms</h4>\n<p>Once a deal takes place, the position is allocated to the relevant account — the firm's principal account (dealt for itself) or a client account (dealt on behalf of a client). Trade details are reported into the exchange's administration system (e.g. CME Direct for CME Group; DME Direct for the Dubai Mercantile Exchange), which ensures trade inputs are matched (purchase and sale exist on the same terms). This is especially important on open outcry markets, where there is more scope for human error than on electronic order-matching systems.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "3.5 Account Types", "html": "\n<h4>3.5.1 Client Accounts</h4>\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.6.1</strong> — house, segregated and non-segregated accounts.</p>\n<p>Trades are assigned to one of three account types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>House</strong> — proprietary trades of the clearing member, purely for the member firm's own benefit/loss; excludes client positions.</li>\n<li><strong>Segregated</strong> — trades registered on behalf of a segregated client. Most exchanges/major regulators require member firms to place client assets in segregated accounts, protecting client funds if the member firm has financial problems. Member firms cannot use segregated funds to meet their own or other clients' margin/financial obligations.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-segregated</strong> — client trades not segregated and therefore not protected on the firm's default; may be commingled, typically as numbered accounts within an omnibus account (at another institution or the broker itself). Where permitted by the exchange/regulators, the decision to use a non-segregated account rests with the client, not the member firm.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>3.5.2 Pre-Registration/Allocations/Give-Ups</h4>\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.6.2</strong> — purpose/importance of give-ups/allocations.</p>\n<p><strong>Pre-registration</strong> is a member firm indicating a deal should be allocated/\"given up\" to a second member (an <strong>allocation</strong>). <strong>Give-ups</strong> are the sending of a trade to another exchange member; the receiving member performs a <strong>give-in</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both parties bear the risk the other will not fulfil its obligations, mitigated by <strong>Master Give-Up Agreements</strong> — legally-binding frameworks governing the relationship between members, including compensation clauses protecting the party \"giving in\" trades if the other member defaults. On Euronext, give-up allocation is handled by Euronext Clearing; at CME, via the <strong>GPS (Give-Up Payment System)</strong>, part of CME Clearing.</p>\n<p>Give-ups mainly separate the execution and clearing functions — a customer may execute through one exchange member but have the trade given up to their usual clearing broker, preserving a single clearing relationship. Also used where a give-in firm has staff shortages, or to preserve anonymity while a give-up firm helps another member build a position.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> Member firm ABC executes, confirms and matches a trade, pre-registering it for allocation to firm DEF. ABC gives up the trade to DEF, which claims it and registers it as house, segregated or non-segregated.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "3.6 Trade Reporting", "html": "\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.7.1</strong> — purpose and requirements of trade reporting.</p>\n<p>Exchanges require member firms to compile trade reports detailing transactions, within specified time limits (typically <strong>same day</strong>); firms can face disciplinary action for missing deadlines. Reports must include counterparty details, the clearing member involved, the instrument, direction and size of the transaction, and the price.</p>\n<p>OTC trades are not subject to exchange trade-reporting timing regulations, but are subject to <strong>internal timing reports</strong>: once an OTC deal is agreed, it must be entered into the participant's internal trade reporting/position system, recorded by internal risk management, starting the confirmation process and applying it to risk/position limits. For ETD contracts, financial institutions set their own time-reporting requirements per their risk management department's guidelines, aligned with regulators/auditors.</p>\n<p>On electronic systems (e.g. ICE Futures), reporting is largely automatic — only <strong>block and basis trades</strong> need separate reporting. On ICE Futures: block trades must be reported within <strong>5 minutes</strong> of verbal agreement under normal conditions; block trades dependent on execution of a transaction in another instrument, or under exceptional market conditions, must be reported within <strong>15 minutes</strong>; all <strong>basis trades</strong> must be reported within <strong>15 minutes</strong> of being organised.</p>\n<h4>3.6.1 Price Transparency</h4>\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.7.2</strong> — advantages and main sources of exchange price feeds.</p>\n<p>Investor confidence requires knowing prices are fairly determined; exchange income derives primarily from fees on contracts traded, so <strong>price transparency</strong> — up-to-date trading information available to anyone — is central.</p>\n<p><strong>Constituents of exchange price feeds:</strong> the exchange disseminates trade reporting via a price feed to members, quote vendors (e.g. Reuters, Bloomberg) and directly to members (e.g. Euronext's Exchange Data Publisher). Screens typically show: the best bid (B) and offer/ask (A), forming the bid/offer (bid/ask) spread; volume traded and open interest (as at previous close); opening price; previous day's closing price; last trade price; day's high/low — all by delivery month, in real time or delayed.</p>\n<p><strong>Delivery month codes:</strong> Jan F, Feb G, Mar H, Apr J, May K, Jun M, Jul N, Aug Q, Sep U, Oct V, Nov X, Dec Z. Example: \"Z 4245B 4246A\" = current bid/offer for the December future.</p>\n<h4>3.6.2 Monitoring Volume and Open Interest</h4>\n<p><strong>Learning objective 3.7.3</strong> — purpose of monitoring open interest; breach of credit limit; guarantee on settlement failure.</p>\n<p>Exchanges require accurate publication of open interest so observers/investors can judge positions and strategies. Members report open interest daily (e.g. on Euronext, via ICE Connect), provided to vendors. This is separate from open interest recorded in omnibus accounts at clearing houses. Clients must manage their own long/short positions and instruct clearing brokers on close-outs (sometimes automated).</p>\n<p>Failure to manage open interest can lead to unwanted delivery, or breach of the client's credit limit with the broker if prices move against them. The clearing system provides a mutual guarantee — the clearing house holds enough capital to cover either party in the event of default.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> Firm A has a $100m credit limit with broker B. Current utilisation is $99m; a new trade adds $5m, taking utilisation to $104m (104% — a material breach). The broker can force Firm A to close positions to return within limit; in practice, brokers check the credit limit before allowing new trades, and may request more collateral rather than force closure, which is a last resort given the damage to client-broker relations, but necessary to control counterparty risk.</p>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Which platform does Euronext use for electronic trading across its markets, and which entities clear its trades?", "options": ["Optiq, cleared by LCH SA or EuroCCP", "Globex, cleared by CME Clearing", "SGX QUEST, cleared by SGX-DC", "INET, cleared by OCC"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Euronext's trading platform is Optiq (using SFTI), and trades are cleared by LCH SA or EuroCCP."}, {"question": "Which ICE Clearing entity clears credit derivatives?", "options": ["ICE Clear Europe", "ICE Clear Credit", "ICE NGX", "ICE Clear US"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "ICE Clear Credit is the ICE clearing entity dedicated to credit derivatives; ICE NGX clears North American natural gas and electricity markets."}, {"question": "Which of the following is true of the Chicago Board Options Exchange (CBOE)?", "options": ["It was established by the NYSE in 1985 and clears via LCH", "It was established by the CBOT in 1973, was the first to list standardised exchange-traded stock options, and introduced FLEX options and LEAPS", "It is a subsidiary of Eurex and only trades VIX futures", "It clears all trades through the CFTC directly"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "CBOE was established by the CBOT in 1973, was the first exchange to list standardised, exchange-traded stock options (cleared by the OCC), and was first to introduce FLEX options and LEAPS. Its subsidiary CFE trades VIX futures and corporate bond index futures."}, {"question": "A trader places an order specifying a limit price; once the market trades at or through that price, the order automatically becomes a market order executed at the best prevailing price. What type of order is this?", "options": ["Stop limit order", "Market-if-touched (MIT) order", "Immediate or cancel order", "Opening order"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A market-if-touched (MIT) order combines a limit order with a market order: once the market reaches the specified level, it converts into a market order."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between a 'fill or kill' (FOK) order and an 'immediate or cancel' order?", "options": ["FOK must be executed in its entirety or cancelled entirely, whereas immediate or cancel allows partial execution with the remainder cancelled", "Immediate or cancel guarantees execution, FOK does not", "FOK is only valid for the trading day, immediate or cancel has no time limit", "There is no difference; they are interchangeable terms"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Immediate or cancel executes whatever portion is possible and cancels the rest; fill or kill requires the whole order to be executed immediately, or the entire order is cancelled."}, {"question": "A stop (loss) order is triggered when the market trades at the stipulated stop price. What is guaranteed about its execution?", "options": ["It is guaranteed to be filled exactly at the stop price", "Nothing is guaranteed about the fill price; in volatile markets it can fill at any price once triggered", "It always executes at the best price of the day", "It converts into a limit order at a better price"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A stop order is triggered once the market trades at the stop price, but there is no guarantee of execution at that exact price — in volatile markets, it can be filled at any available price once triggered (unlike a stop limit order, which bounds the fill within a limit)."}, {"question": "On ICE Futures London, what is the minimum size for a block trade in FTSE futures?", "options": ["100 contracts", "250 contracts", "500 contracts", "1,000 contracts"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "ICE Futures London sets a minimum of 100 contracts for short sterling and gilt futures block trades, but 500 contracts for FTSE futures."}, {"question": "A basis trade, as a wholesale trading facility, involves which of the following?", "options": ["The simultaneous exchange of a financial asset (e.g. a bond or basket of shares) with an offsetting number of futures contracts, privately negotiated between two parties", "A large transaction executed entirely within the central order book at the prevailing market price", "An order that becomes a market order once a specified price is touched", "A trade that is automatically cancelled if not filled within the trading day"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A basis trade is the simultaneous, privately negotiated exchange of a financial asset with an offsetting quantity of futures contracts; the futures leg is reported to the exchange once agreed."}, {"question": "In the wheat hedging example used to illustrate 'risk pricing', why did the food manufacturer use an OTC swap instead of selling 1,000 lots of futures directly in the market?", "options": ["Futures were not available for the required delivery month", "Selling 1,000 lots would have been over six times the previous day's volume and 40% of open interest, likely moving the price down significantly and eroding hedge efficiency", "OTC trades are always cheaper than futures regardless of size", "Exchange rules prohibited trades above 500 lots"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Open interest was 2,500 lots and the prior day's volume 160 lots, so a 1,000-lot market order would be disproportionately large, causing adverse price impact (poor hedge efficiency); an OTC risk price from the bank avoided this."}, {"question": "What process describes the clearing house becoming the counterparty to every trade executed on the exchange?", "options": ["Novation", "Allocation", "Give-up", "Cross trading"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Novation is the process by which the clearing house interposes itself as counterparty to both sides of every trade."}, {"question": "With exchange-traded futures contracts, who initiates the physical delivery process, and how is the counterparty determined?", "options": ["The buyer initiates delivery; the clearing house assigns a seller at random", "The seller initiates delivery; the clearing house assigns a buyer at random", "Both parties must jointly notify the exchange simultaneously", "The exchange's compliance department selects both counterparties in advance"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "With futures, the seller initiates the delivery process; once advised, the clearing house assigns a buyer at random and notifies them that delivery will take place."}, {"question": "For options contracts, who initiates delivery, and what happens for options on futures upon exercise?", "options": ["The seller initiates delivery; the option simply expires worthless", "The buyer initiates delivery by exercising; for options on futures, the exchange automatically replaces the exercised option with the corresponding future at the strike price", "The clearing house initiates delivery on a random date", "The buyer initiates delivery, and cash settlement is always used regardless of contract terms"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "It is the buyer who initiates delivery by exercising the option. For options on futures, exercise triggers automatic replacement of the option position with the underlying future at the option's strike price."}, {"question": "A member firm trades on its own behalf, bearing the resulting profits and losses itself. What is this known as?", "options": ["Acting as agent", "Acting as principal", "Cross trading", "Give-up trading"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Acting as principal means the member deals for its own account and directly bears the profits or losses; acting as agent means dealing on behalf of a client, who bears the P&L."}, {"question": "What is 'dual capacity' in the context of exchange member trading?", "options": ["The ability of a member to trade on two exchanges simultaneously", "The ability of a member to act as either principal or agent in any given transaction", "A requirement that every trade have two counterparties", "The use of two separate clearing houses for one trade"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Dual capacity is the member's ability to trade for its own account (principal) or execute orders on behalf of clients (agent), depending on the transaction."}, {"question": "Which statement about open interest is correct?", "options": ["Open interest is the sum of all open long positions plus the sum of all open short positions", "Open interest is the sum of all open long positions, or equivalently the sum of all open short positions (not both combined)", "Open interest always equals the previous day's traded volume", "Open interest is only reported for options, not futures"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Open interest represents total open contracts for a delivery month — it is the sum of all open longs (which equals the sum of all open shorts), not the combination of both sides."}, {"question": "The contract month with the highest open interest, generally nearest to expiration, is referred to as:", "options": ["The spot month", "The back month", "The front month", "The strip month"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The contract with the highest open interest is generally the nearest to expiration and is called the 'front month' contract."}, {"question": "Which of the three client account types is NOT protected against the loss of client assets if the member firm defaults?", "options": ["House account", "Segregated account", "Non-segregated account", "All three are equally protected"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Non-segregated accounts hold client trades that are commingled and not protected on the member firm's default; segregated accounts are protected, and house accounts hold only the firm's own proprietary trades."}, {"question": "On ICE Futures, within what time period must a standard block trade be reported to the exchange under normal market conditions?", "options": ["Within 1 minute of agreement", "Within 5 minutes of verbal agreement", "Within 30 minutes of agreement", "By the end of the trading day"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "On ICE Futures, block trades must generally be reported within 5 minutes of verbal agreement under normal conditions; those dependent on another instrument's execution, or under exceptional conditions, must be reported within 15 minutes, as must basis trades."}, {"question": "In exchange delivery month codes, which letter represents the December futures contract month?", "options": ["X", "V", "Z", "U"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The December delivery month code is Z (e.g. 'Z 4245B 4246A' denotes the current bid/offer for the December future). November is X, October is V, September is U."}, {"question": "Firm A has a $100m credit limit with broker B. Utilisation currently stands at $99m, and a new trade adds $5m of exposure, taking utilisation to $104m. What best describes the situation and the broker's likely response?", "options": ["No issue arises since the increase is small; the broker takes no action", "This is a material breach (104% utilisation); the broker can require Firm A to close enough positions to return within limit, though forcing closure is usually a last resort", "The trade is automatically voided by the clearing house", "The exchange fines the broker for allowing the breach"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Utilisation rising to $104m against a $100m limit is a 104% breach. The broker is entitled to force closure of positions to bring utilisation back within limit, though in practice brokers check limits beforehand and forced closure is a last resort given its impact on the client relationship."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is a derivatives exchange?", "back": "A marketplace to buy/sell futures, options and other contracts of specific quantities of a commodity or financial instrument at a specified price for future delivery, using pit/floor trading, electronic platforms, or both."}, {"front": "Euronext — platform and clearers?", "back": "Optiq trading platform (using SFTI); trades cleared by LCH SA or EuroCCP. Spans Amsterdam, Brussels, Lisbon, Paris and London."}, {"front": "ICE Clear Credit and ICE NGX — what do they clear?", "back": "ICE Clear Credit clears credit derivatives; ICE NGX clears North American natural gas and electricity markets. ICE has six clearing entities in total."}, {"front": "CBOE — three key 'firsts'?", "back": "Established by CBOT in 1973; first exchange to list standardised exchange-traded stock options; first to introduce FLEX options and LEAPS (options with expiry >1 year). Cleared by the OCC."}, {"front": "OTC market — definition", "back": "A decentralised market where participants trade directly between two parties without a central exchange or broker; no physical location; less transparent and less regulated than exchanges."}, {"front": "High touch vs low touch trading", "back": "High touch = OTC-style direct trading/sales advice, often 24/7, at higher cost. Low touch = exchange-traded, automated, less human contact — justifies OTC's higher charges."}, {"front": "Limit order vs market order", "back": "Limit order: stipulates a required price, may fill at a better price, execution not guaranteed. Market order: executed immediately at best available price, no price guarantee, execution certainty prioritised."}, {"front": "Market-if-touched (MIT) order", "back": "A combined limit/market order: specifies a limit price; once the market trades at/through it, the order becomes a market order executed at the best prevailing price."}, {"front": "Fill or kill (FOK) vs immediate or cancel", "back": "FOK: must execute in its entirety immediately, or the whole order is cancelled. Immediate or cancel: executes whatever portion is possible immediately, cancels the rest."}, {"front": "Stop order vs stop limit order", "back": "Stop order: triggers at the stop price, then fills at whatever price is available (no fill-price guarantee). Stop limit order: once triggered, becomes a limit order bounded within a specified price (or better)."}, {"front": "Block trade — ICE Futures London minimum sizes", "back": "100 contracts for short sterling and gilt futures; 500 contracts for FTSE futures. Must be reported to the exchange after private negotiation, typically within 5 minutes to an hour."}, {"front": "Basis trade — definition", "back": "The simultaneous, privately negotiated exchange of a financial asset (e.g. a bond or basket of shares) with an offsetting number of futures contracts; the futures leg is reported to the exchange once agreed."}, {"front": "FLEX facility — origin and purpose", "back": "Originally introduced by the CBOE; combines OTC-style flexibility (customised strike/exercise price and expiration date) with exchange clearing advantages."}, {"front": "Risk pricing (OTC)", "back": "A financial institution quotes a whole-order OTC price (a 'risk price') to a client wanting to avoid the price slippage of executing a large order directly on-exchange."}, {"front": "Novation", "back": "The process by which the clearing house becomes the counterparty to both sides of every trade executed on the exchange."}, {"front": "Cash settlement (CFD)", "back": "Settlement via net cash payment of the difference between the final closing price and the previous day's closing price; contracts are marked to market daily with variation margin paid/received."}, {"front": "Futures delivery process", "back": "The seller initiates delivery; the clearing house then assigns a buyer at random and notifies them. Long-dated government bond futures have a list of eligible bonds, each with a price factor for the invoice amount."}, {"front": "Options delivery process", "back": "The buyer initiates delivery by exercising the option. For options on futures, the exchange automatically replaces the exercised option with the corresponding future at the option's strike price."}, {"front": "Principal order vs agency order", "back": "Principal: member trades for its own account, bears P&L itself. Agency: member trades on behalf of a client (as broker), client bears P&L, may give client anonymity."}, {"front": "Cross trading", "back": "When a brokerage matches a non-competitive buy order of one customer against a sell order of another (or a dual-capacity member has both sides at a given price); must still be executed within the market for price transparency, subject to exchange rules (e.g. pit observer sign-off in open outcry)."}, {"front": "Volume vs open interest", "back": "Volume: cumulative contracts traded so far each day. Open interest: total open contracts for a delivery month (sum of all open longs, or equivalently all open shorts, not both) — a key liquidity indicator."}, {"front": "Front month contract", "back": "The contract month with the highest open interest, generally the one nearest to expiration."}, {"front": "House, segregated and non-segregated accounts", "back": "House: member's own proprietary trades. Segregated: client trades, protected from the member firm's default (funds cannot be used for the firm's own obligations). Non-segregated: client trades not protected, commingled/numbered accounts, client's choice to use where permitted."}, {"front": "Give-up and Master Give-Up Agreement", "back": "A give-up sends a trade to another exchange member (the give-in), separating execution from clearing. Master Give-Up Agreements are legally binding frameworks with compensation clauses protecting the giving-in party if the other member defaults. CME processes give-ups via GPS; Euronext via Euronext Clearing."}, {"front": "ICE Futures block/basis trade reporting deadlines", "back": "Block trades: 5 minutes under normal conditions, 15 minutes if dependent on another instrument or under exceptional conditions. Basis trades: 15 minutes of being organised."}, {"front": "Credit limit breach — clearing guarantee", "back": "If a client's open interest/position breaches its credit limit with a broker, the broker can force closure of positions; the clearing house holds capital to guarantee performance for either party in the event of default."}]}, {"id": "der-ch4", "number": 4, "title": "Principles of Pricing and Valuation", "examWeight": "11 of 100 questions (~11%)", "summaryHtml": "\n<p>This chapter explains how forwards, futures and options are priced, and is the most calculation-heavy chapter in the syllabus. The core idea is <b>arbitrage-free (fair value) pricing</b>: prices of derivatives are anchored to the cash/spot price of the underlying plus the <b>net cost of carry</b> of holding that underlying to the delivery date. If a future trades away from fair value, arbitrageurs exploit the gap until prices converge.</p>\n<p><b>Cost of carry</b> = finance cost (interest forgone/paid) + storage + insurance + security costs, less any income (dividends/coupons) received. For a commodity: <b>Fair value = Cash price + Finance cost + Storage cost</b>, where Finance = Cash price &times; interest rate &times; (days/365) and Storage = Cash price &times; storage rate &times; (days/365).</p>\n<p>For an <b>equity index future</b>: Fair value = Cash index &times; (1 + (interest rate &minus; dividend yield) &times; days/basis). The net carrying cost (interest &minus; dividend yield) can be positive or negative, unlike a commodity, which is always a cost.</p>\n<p><b>Contango</b>: futures price &gt; cash price (net cost of carry, typical for equities/commodities). <b>Backwardation</b>: futures price &lt; cash price (net benefit of carry, typical for bonds/STIRs when yield &gt; short rate). As expiry nears, cost of carry shrinks to zero, so cash and futures prices must <b>converge</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Basis = Cash price &minus; Futures price.</b> Negative in contango, positive in backwardation; always narrows to zero at expiry. Basis <b>strengthens</b> when it moves positive (narrows the gap in contango; widens it in backwardation) and <b>weakens</b> when it moves negative. Expected strengthening &rarr; buy the spread (buy near month, sell far month); expected weakening &rarr; sell the spread.</p>\n<p><b>Arbitrage trades</b>: if the future is overpriced relative to fair value, do a <b>cash and carry</b> trade (buy underlying, sell future). If underpriced, do a <b>reverse cash and carry</b> trade (sell underlying, buy future). Transaction costs create an <b>arbitrage channel</b> around fair value within which arbitrage is unprofitable.</p>\n<p>An option's <b>premium = intrinsic value (IV) + time value (TV)</b>. IV is the greater of zero or (underlying &minus; strike) for a call, (strike &minus; underlying) for a put; it can never be negative. TV reflects uncertainty and is driven by time to expiry, distance from strike (ATM has the most TV) and volatility. <b>Put/call parity</b> (options on futures, or at expiry): <b>C &minus; P = S &minus; K</b>. For options on physicals/equities with income before expiry: <b>C &minus; P = S &minus; K/(1+r)^t</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Delta</b> (&Delta; = change in premium / change in underlying price) ranges 0 to &plusmn;1, positive for calls, negative for puts; deep ITM &rarr; &plusmn;1, ATM &rarr; &plusmn;0.5, deep OTM &rarr; 0. Cumulative delta of a portfolio measures net directional exposure; delta-neutral (zero) portfolios are hedged against small price moves. Other Greeks: <b>gamma</b> (rate of change of delta, greatest ATM near expiry), <b>vega</b> (sensitivity to 1% change in implied volatility, greatest ATM, larger for longer-dated options), <b>theta</b> (time decay, negative for long options, positive for short options, accelerates near expiry), <b>rho</b> (sensitivity to a 1% change in interest rates, least significant Greek).</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "4.1 Approaches to Valuation", "html": "\n<h4>1.1 Arbitrage-Free Pricing</h4>\n<p><b>Arbitrage</b> is the attempt to profit from price differentials between identical or similar instruments trading in different markets or forms. A <i>true</i> arbitrage is a riskless profit where all positions are exactly offset at a net gain; most real-world \"mispricings\" carry some residual risk but a fairly certain expected profit.</p>\n<p>Arbitrage-free pricing derives from the <b>efficient market hypothesis</b>: price differences cannot persist because profit-seekers exploit them, forcing convergence to a \"fair value\". It is a hypothesis, not an axiom, because markets can take months, years, or may never fully converge, due to (1) imperfect/incomplete information across participants and (2) differentiated transaction costs across markets. This gives rise to the idea of <b>\"workable arbitrage\"</b> (profitable in practice) versus arbitrage that works only in theory (\"unworkable\").</p>\n<h4>1.2 Cash-Flow Pricing</h4>\n<p>Cash-flow pricing is best illustrated with equity valuation. A share price is influenced by more than business performance &ndash; notably index membership and goodwill/brand value. Ultimately, businesses must generate cash for dividends, so a sensible starting point for valuation is a company's <b>free cash flow</b>, commonly approximated by <b>EBITDA</b> (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation).</p>\n<p>Dividing free cash flow by shares outstanding gives <b>free cash flow per share</b>, comparable to the share price. A low/negative free cash flow per share against a high, rising share price signals a possible <b>selling</b> opportunity (overvaluation); the reverse signals a possible <b>buying</b> opportunity (undervaluation). Investment banks perform <b>comparable company analysis</b>, applying this metric across similar companies to find mispricing opportunities that may generate \"alpha\" (returns above the equity index).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.2 Calculating Forward Prices", "html": "\n<h4>2.1 OTC Derivative Pricing</h4>\n<p>A <b>forward</b> is an OTC transaction where delivery is deferred to a future date, but the price is fixed on the trade date. Forwards trade on currencies, commodities and equities. The forward price reflects (i) the interest rate differential between two currencies (FX forwards); (ii) current interest rates, financing cost and storage costs (commodities); (iii) the differential between interest rates and dividend yield (equities). These factors reduce arbitrage opportunities, in the same way as futures pricing.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1 (FX forward):</b> a French company with future JPY payables enters EUR/JPY forwards to lock in rates today, tailored exactly to its payment dates (an OTC/customisable feature). Since EUR rates < JPY rates, the euro trades at a slight premium to the yen forward.</p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (commodity forward):</b> a food company facing a cocoa drought risk uses OTC cocoa forwards (rather than exchange futures) tailored to its specific production dates; price = today's spot price + storage cost adjustment + interest cost for the forward's duration.</p>\n<p>Forwards are mostly OTC (often bank-to-bank or bank-to-corporate), though some (eg, LME) are exchange-listed. Unlike futures, forwards are typically <b>not marked to market daily</b>; if MTM does occur, profits are not paid until maturity, though losses may still need to be paid to a clearing house if cleared. Forwards settle only on the delivery date.</p>\n<h4>2.2 Forward Foreign Exchange Rate Calculations</h4>\n<p>FX spot deals settle T+2. Example: GBP/USD spot 1.3000/1.3010 (bid/offer); interbank spreads are much tighter (eg, 1.3000/1.3005, five pips). The <b>base currency</b> (first quoted, here GBP) is bought/sold at the bid/offer against the quoted currency (USD).</p>\n<p><b>Interest rate parity</b> links interest rate differentials to forward points:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><b>F = S &times; (1 + i<sub>d</sub>) / (1 + i<sub>f</sub>)</b></p>\n<p>where F = forward rate, S = spot rate, i<sub>d</sub> = domestic interest rate, i<sub>f</sub> = foreign interest rate. If F does not match this ratio, arbitrageurs can profit risk-free by shifting funds between currencies.</p>\n<p>Example: GBP/EUR spot 1.2050/1.2055; 1-month 1.1990/1.2000; 3-month 1.1930/1.1945; 6-month 1.1905/1.1925. Because GBP rates > EUR rates, GBP (base currency) is at a <b>discount</b> forward (points subtracted from spot) and EUR is at a <b>premium</b>. Forward points widen (become more negative) as maturity extends, and the bid/offer spread widens for longer-dated forwards because of increased dealer risk (eg, 60/55 pips at 1 month vs 145/130 pips at 6 months in this example).</p>\n<p><b>Practice calculation:</b> GBP/USD spot is 1.2500. UK 1-year rate = 4.5%, US 1-year rate = 5.5%. What is the 1-year forward rate?<br>\nF = 1.2500 &times; (1.045/1.055) = 1.2500 &times; 0.99052 = <b>1.2382</b> (GBP at a slight premium since UK rates are lower).</p>\n<p><b>Practice calculation 2:</b> EUR/USD spot is 1.1000. Eurozone 6-month rate = 3.0%, US 6-month rate = 5.0% (rates pro-rated for 6 months: 1.5% and 2.5%). F = 1.1000 &times; (1.015/1.025) = 1.1000 &times; 0.99024 = <b>1.0893</b>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.3 Futures Pricing Relationships and Cost of Carry", "html": "\n<h4>3.1 Futures Pricing Relationships</h4>\n<p>A future's price = cash/underlying price + net cost of holding the position to expiry (cost of carry). The future's price is <b>not</b> simply the market's forecast of the future cash price &ndash; it is largely mechanical, driven by cost of carry.</p>\n<h4>3.2 Cost of Carry</h4>\n<p><b>Example:</b> an investor needs an asset in 2 months. Choice 1: go long the future at 417. Choice 2: buy the asset today for 400 and store it. Buying today forgoes interest on the funds and may incur storage costs &ndash; these costs justify a higher price (417) for future delivery versus the cash price (400).</p>\n<p>Main components of <b>cost of carry</b>:</p>\n<ul><li>finance costs (interest expense) over the holding period</li><li>security costs (eg, precious metals)</li><li>storage costs</li><li>insurance</li></ul>\n<p>For financial futures, cost of carry depends on dividend yield vs interest rates (equities) or the shape of the yield curve (fixed income); there can also be a <b>benefit</b> of carry (eg, dividends), reducing or reversing the net cost.</p>\n<h4>3.3 Fair Value (Arbitrage-Free Value)</h4>\n<p><b>Fair value of the future = cash price + cost of carry.</b> If the cash-futures differential is less than cost of carry, buying the future is cheaper (better) than buying and holding the asset; if greater, buying and holding the asset is better. Only when the differential exactly equals cost of carry is the buyer indifferent.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Worked Example &ndash; Commodity Future (90 days to delivery)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash price</td><td>£1,250.00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Finance: £1,250 &times; 6% &times; (90/365)</td><td>£18.49</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storage: £1,250 &times; 1% &times; (90/365)</td><td>£3.08</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fair value</b></td><td><b>£1,271.57</b></td></tr></table>\n<p><i>This is the standard method to use in the exam.</i></p>\n<p><b>Exercise 1 (from manual):</b> Cash price £750, interest 3% pa, storage (incl. insurance) 0.5% pa, 45 days to expiry.<br>\nFinance = £750 &times; 3% &times; (45/365) = £2.77; Storage = £750 &times; 0.5% &times; (45/365) = £0.46.<br>\n<b>Fair value = £750.00 + £2.77 + £0.46 = £753.23</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> Cash price of gold is £1,500/oz, interest rate 4% pa, storage/insurance 0.75% pa, 120 days to delivery.<br>\nFinance = £1,500 &times; 4% &times; (120/365) = £19.73; Storage = £1,500 &times; 0.75% &times; (120/365) = £3.70.<br>\n<b>Fair value = £1,500 + £19.73 + £3.70 = £1,523.43</b></p>\n<h4>3.3.1 Fair Value for Equity Index Futures</h4>\n<p>Only <b>net finance cost</b> matters (other costs are negligible): <b>Net finance cost = interest &minus; present value of dividends</b> (in practice, interest rate &minus; dividend yield).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Worked Example &ndash; S&amp;P 500 Future (182 days)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash index</td><td>2100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1-yr interest rate</td><td>0.55%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dividend yield</td><td>2.25%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net carrying cost = (0.55% &minus; 2.25%) &times; (182/360)</td><td>&minus;0.0086</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fair value</b> = 2100 &times; (1 &minus; 0.0086) = 2100 &times; 0.9914</td><td><b>2,081.95</b></td></tr></table>\n<p>Note the net carrying cost for an equity index future can be <b>positive or negative</b> (unlike a physical commodity, which is always a net cost).</p>\n<p><b>Exercise 2 (from manual):</b> FTSE 100 cash index 7200, interest 0.8% pa, dividend yield 3.6% pa, 91 days to run.<br>\nFair value = 7200 &times; (1 + (0.8% &minus; 3.6%) &times; (91/365)) = 7200 &times; (1 &minus; 0.007) = <b>7,149.60</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> DAX cash index is 15,000, interest rate 2.0% pa, dividend yield 3.0% pa, 60 days to expiry.<br>\nNet carrying cost = (2.0% &minus; 3.0%) &times; (60/365) = &minus;0.001644<br>\n<b>Fair value = 15,000 &times; (1 &minus; 0.001644) = 14,975.34</b></p>\n<h4>3.3.2 IASB Definition of Fair Value</h4>\n<p>The IASB defines fair value of a future (or any derivative) as the price at which it can be exchanged/traded between two market participants at a given time, in an arm's length transaction &ndash; ie, its <b>current market price</b>. Participants must mark to market (MTM) futures positions at least daily.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.4 Contango, Backwardation and Convergence", "html": "\n<p><b>Contango</b>: net cost of carry &rarr; futures price &gt; cash price.</p>\n<p><b>Backwardation</b>: net benefit of carry &rarr; futures price &lt; cash price. Most common in bond and STIR markets when long-term rates exceed short-term rates; can also occur in commodities when there is a steep premium for immediate delivery (expected near-term shortage).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Worked Example &ndash; Bond Future (backwardation, 1 month to expiry)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash price</td><td>£110.500</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Finance costs: £110.50 &times; (0.25% &minus; 1.6%) &times; (1/12)</td><td>&minus;£0.124</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fair value</b></td><td><b>£110.376</b></td></tr></table>\n<p>Because the bond yield (1.6%) exceeds the deposit rate (0.25%), the future's fair value is <b>below</b> the cash price &ndash; a backwardation market.</p>\n<p><b>Exercise 3 (from manual):</b> Notional long gilt cash price £104.30, 2-month deposit rate 0.25% pa, bond yield 1.5% pa, 2 months to expiry.<br>\nFinance costs = £104.30 &times; (0.25% &minus; 1.5%) &times; (2/12) = &minus;£0.22.<br>\n<b>Fair value = £104.30 &minus; £0.22 = £104.08</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> A bond's cash price is £98.75; 3-month deposit rate is 0.5% pa; the bond yields 2.2% pa; 3 months to expiry.<br>\nFinance costs = £98.75 &times; (0.5% &minus; 2.2%) &times; (3/12) = £98.75 &times; (&minus;0.017) &times; 0.25 = &minus;£0.4197.<br>\n<b>Fair value = £98.75 &minus; £0.42 = £98.33</b></p>\n<h4>3.5 Convergence</h4>\n<p>Cost of carry diminishes as expiry nears, so the cash-futures differential narrows. At expiry, cost of carry = 0, so cash and futures prices <b>converge</b> and become equal.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.5 Basis and Basis Risk", "html": "\n<h4>3.6 Basis</h4>\n<p><b>Basis = Cash price &minus; Futures price</b> (also used to describe the difference between two futures prices, eg March vs June).</p>\n<p>Contango &rarr; futures &gt; cash &rarr; basis is <b>negative</b>. Backwardation &rarr; futures &lt; cash &rarr; basis is <b>positive</b>. Due to convergence, basis narrows to zero in both cases as expiry nears. In a perfect market basis = cost of carry, but real-world basis is also affected by short-term supply/demand, differing participant borrowing costs, and time to expiry.</p>\n<p>Basis changes due to: changes in supply/demand; changes to cost of carry (interest rates, insurance, dividend yields); different participants' cost bases; and convergence (time to expiry).</p>\n<h4>3.6.1 Terminology</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Strengthening</b>: basis moves positive (cash rises relative to futures). In contango, this <b>narrows</b> the (negative) gap; in backwardation, this <b>widens</b> the (positive) gap.</li>\n<li><b>Weakening</b>: basis moves negative (cash falls relative to futures). In contango, this <b>widens</b> the gap; in backwardation, this <b>narrows</b> the gap.</li>\n</ul>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Market</th><th>Gap</th><th>Basis</th><th>Trade</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Contango</td><td>Narrowing</td><td>Strengthening</td><td>Buy the spread</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contango</td><td>Widening</td><td>Weakening</td><td>Sell the spread</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Backwardation</td><td>Narrowing</td><td>Weakening</td><td>Sell the spread</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Backwardation</td><td>Widening</td><td>Strengthening</td><td>Buy the spread</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>3.6.2 The Trades</h4>\n<p><b>Expected strengthening &rarr; buy the spread</b> (buy near-dated, sell far-dated).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> March FTSE 100 trades at 7250, June at 7260 (basis &minus;10). Trader buys 500 March, sells 500 June. Two days later: March still 7250 (no gain/loss); June now 7258 (sold at 7260, bought back at 7258 = gain of 2 points). <b>Overall gain = 2 points &times; £10/point &times; 500 contracts = £10,000.</b></p>\n<p><b>Expected weakening &rarr; sell the spread</b> (sell near-dated, buy far-dated).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> March trades at 417, June at 433 (basis &minus;16). Trader sells the spread. A week later March still 417 (no gain/loss); June now 439 (bought at 433, sold at 439 = gain of 6 points). <b>Overall gain = 6 points per contract</b> (equal to the change in basis).</p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> A trader expects the basis between September (7,100) and December (7,140) FTSE futures to strengthen and buys the spread with 200 contracts each leg. A week later, September is 7,115 and December is 7,145. Calculate the P&amp;L (£10 per point).<br>\nSeptember (bought 7,100, sold 7,115): gain of 15 points. December (sold 7,140, bought 7,145): loss of 5 points. Net = 15 &minus; 5 = 10 points &times; £10 &times; 200 = <b>£20,000 profit</b> (basis moved from &minus;40 to &minus;30, ie strengthened by 10 points, consistent with the buy-the-spread P&amp;L).</p>\n<h4>3.6.4 Basis Risk</h4>\n<p><b>Basis risk</b> = the risk that a futures price moves differently from its underlying asset. Basis risk is much smaller than the price risk of the underlying itself, and the only fool-proof way to eliminate it is to hold the future to expiry, when cash and futures prices converge.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.6 Arbitrage Trades and the Arbitrage Channel", "html": "\n<h4>3.7.1 Cash and Carry Arbitrage</h4>\n<p>If a future trades <b>above</b> fair value (overpriced): <b>buy the underlying, sell the future</b> (a \"cash and carry\" trade).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Cash price</th><th>Fair value</th><th>Futures price</th></tr><tr><td>1450</td><td>1485</td><td>1497</td></tr></table>\n<p>Buy underlying at 1450; incur 35 cost of carry &rarr; effective buying price 1485. Sell the future at 1497. <b>Locked-in profit = 1497 &minus; 1485 = 12.</b></p>\n<h4>3.7.2 Reverse Cash and Carry Arbitrage</h4>\n<p>If a future trades <b>below</b> fair value (underpriced): <b>sell the underlying, buy the future</b> (a \"reverse cash and carry\" trade).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Cash price</th><th>Fair value</th><th>Futures price</th></tr><tr><td>1450</td><td>1485</td><td>1477</td></tr></table>\n<p>Sell underlying at 1450, save carry costs of 35 (interest earned/storage saved) &rarr; effective selling price 1485. Buy the future at 1477. <b>Locked-in profit = 1485 &minus; 1477 = 8.</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> Cash price is 2,300, fair value is 2,340, futures price is 2,362. Is this a cash-and-carry or reverse cash-and-carry opportunity, and what is the arbitrage profit?<br>\nFutures (2,362) > fair value (2,340) &rarr; future is overpriced &rarr; <b>cash and carry</b>: buy underlying (effective cost 2,340 after carry), sell future at 2,362. <b>Profit = 2,362 &minus; 2,340 = 22.</b></p>\n<h4>3.7.3 The Arbitrage Channel</h4>\n<p>Trading costs (commissions, fees, bid/offer spreads) mean the futures price can deviate from fair value without triggering arbitrage, provided the deviation is smaller than those costs. This price range is the <b>arbitrage channel</b>. Once price moves beyond the channel, arbitrage becomes profitable and pulls the price back in, explaining the strong correlation between cash and futures prices.</p>\n<h4>3.8 Hedging and Basis Risk</h4>\n<p>Futures can hedge an unhedged cash position by taking an offsetting futures position. Even though cash and futures prices are strongly correlated, basis changes (the future not trading exactly at fair value when the hedge is placed/lifted) mean a hedge can over- or under-perform. This residual risk is limited because arbitrage keeps futures prices close to fair value.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.7 Option Premium: Intrinsic Value and Time Value", "html": "\n<h4>4.1 The Premium</h4>\n<p>The <b>premium</b> is the non-refundable fee the option holder pays the writer, determined mainly by (i) distance of strike from the underlying's current price and (ii) time to expiry.</p>\n<p><b>Premium (PM) = Intrinsic Value (IV) + Time Value (TV)</b></p>\n<h4>4.1.1 Intrinsic Value (IV)</h4>\n<p>IV is the minimum value an option can have. Call IV exists when strike &lt; underlying price; put IV exists when strike &gt; underlying price. <b>IV can never be negative</b> &ndash; it is the greater of zero or the in-the-money amount.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\">ABC plc shares at 550p &ndash; March Calls</th></tr>\n<tr><th>Strike</th><th>Premium</th><th>IV</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n<tr><td>500 call</td><td>70</td><td>50</td><td>ITM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>525 call</td><td>49</td><td>25</td><td>ITM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>550 call</td><td>37</td><td>0</td><td>ATM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>575 call</td><td>25</td><td>0</td><td>OTM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>600 call</td><td>19</td><td>0</td><td>OTM</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Call status</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n<tr><td>In-the-money</td><td>Market price &gt; exercise price</td></tr>\n<tr><td>At-the-money</td><td>Market price = exercise price</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Out-of-the-money</td><td>Market price &lt; exercise price</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Break-even</td><td>Market price = exercise price + premium</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Put status</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n<tr><td>In-the-money</td><td>Market price &lt; exercise price</td></tr>\n<tr><td>At-the-money</td><td>Market price = exercise price</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Out-of-the-money</td><td>Market price &gt; exercise price</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Break-even</td><td>Market price = exercise price &minus; premium</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Exercise 5 (from manual):</b> Call strike 234p, underlying 256p, premium 40p. IV = 256 &minus; 234 = 22p. TV = 40 &minus; 22 = <b>18p</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> A put option has a strike of 480p, the underlying is trading at 445p and the premium is 55p. Calculate IV and TV.<br>\nIV = strike &minus; underlying = 480 &minus; 445 = 35p. TV = premium &minus; IV = 55 &minus; 35 = <b>20p</b>.</p>\n<h4>4.1.2 Time Value (TV)</h4>\n<p><b>TV = PM &minus; IV.</b> TV reflects the possibility that the price may move further in the holder's favour before expiry (uncertainty).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th colspan=\"7\">ABC plc at 550p &ndash; March Options</th></tr>\n<tr><th>Strike</th><th>Call PM</th><th>Call IV</th><th>Call TV</th><th>Put PM</th><th>Put IV</th><th>Put TV</th></tr>\n<tr><td>500</td><td>70</td><td>50</td><td>20</td><td>18</td><td>0</td><td>18</td></tr>\n<tr><td>525</td><td>49</td><td>25</td><td>24</td><td>23</td><td>0</td><td>23</td></tr>\n<tr><td>550</td><td>37</td><td>0</td><td>37</td><td>35</td><td>0</td><td>35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>575</td><td>25</td><td>0</td><td>25</td><td>47</td><td>25</td><td>22</td></tr>\n<tr><td>600</td><td>19</td><td>0</td><td>19</td><td>67</td><td>50</td><td>17</td></tr></table>\n<p>At expiry, TV is always zero (either the option is exercised for its IV, or it expires worthless).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.8 Determinants of the Option Premium", "html": "\n<h4>4.1.3 Main Determinants of Time Value</h4>\n<p>Three uncertainty-driven factors determine TV &ndash; greater uncertainty means greater TV:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Time to expiry</b>: longer-dated options have more TV (more scope for the underlying to move). TV erodes (\"time decay\") as expiry nears, non-linearly &ndash; erosion accelerates near expiry. At expiry TV = 0 always.</li>\n<li><b>Distance between strike and underlying</b>: deep ITM and deep OTM options have low TV (outcome is fairly certain). <b>ATM options have the highest TV</b> (maximum uncertainty).</li>\n<li><b>Volatility</b>: higher volatility &rarr; greater uncertainty &rarr; higher TV/premium. Three types: <b>historic/realised</b> volatility (measurable, but not necessarily predictive); <b>future</b> volatility (unknowable); <b>implied</b> volatility (backed out of the market premium using an option pricing model, eg <b>Black-Scholes(-Merton)</b>, created by Fischer Black and Myron Scholes in 1973, with Robert Merton's contribution giving rise to the \"BSM\" name). Black-Scholes only prices European options; binomial and finite-difference models handle American-style early exercise. The <b>SABR</b> model uses a \"volatility smile\" &ndash; implied volatility is higher for deep ITM/OTM strikes.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>4.1.4 Interest Rates, Dividends and Coupons</h4>\n<p>Higher interest rates raise call premiums and reduce put premiums (call writers, holding cash equivalent economically, must be compensated less; put writers holding cash as cover need to charge less as rates rise... conversely rate falls raise put premiums). Higher dividends (relative to interest rates) let call writers charge lower premiums, but put writers need to charge higher premiums.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Call premium</th><th>Put premium</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Underlying price rises</td><td>Rise</td><td>Fall</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time to expiry rises</td><td>Rise</td><td>Rise</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volatility rises</td><td>Rise</td><td>Rise</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exercise price rises</td><td>Fall</td><td>Rise</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interest rate rises</td><td>Rise</td><td>Fall</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Options on physicals (summary)</td><td>Rise</td><td>Fall</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Options on futures</td><td>Generally unchanged*</td><td>Generally unchanged*</td></tr></table>\n<p>*If interest rates rise, cost of carry rises, affecting the futures price itself and the incentive to hold cash; effects broadly offset for options on futures.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.9 The Put/Call Parity Theorem", "html": "\n<p>Put/call parity links call and put premiums, the underlying price and the strike; if it does not hold, arbitrage is possible.</p>\n<p><b>For options on futures (and any option at expiry): C &minus; P = S &minus; K</b><br>where C = call premium, P = put premium, S = underlying (spot/futures) price, K = strike price.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> Futures price 100.95, strike 100, call premium 1.07. Find the put premium.<br>\nC &minus; P = S &minus; K &rArr; 1.07 &minus; P = 100.95 &minus; 100 = 0.95 &rArr; <b>P = 1.07 &minus; 0.95 = 0.12</b></p>\n<p><b>Exercise 6 (from manual):</b> Futures price 101.5, strike 110, call premium 12. Find P.<br>\n12 &minus; P = 101.5 &minus; 110 = &minus;8.5 &rArr; <b>P = 12 + 8.5 = 20.5</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> Futures price is 55.20, strike is 56.00, put premium is 1.30. Find the call premium.<br>\nC &minus; P = S &minus; K &rArr; C &minus; 1.30 = 55.20 &minus; 56.00 = &minus;0.80 &rArr; <b>C = 1.30 &minus; 0.80 = 0.50</b></p>\n<p>For options on the underlying (not futures) where income (dividends/coupons) is paid before expiry, cost of carry must be included:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><b>C &minus; P = S &minus; K / (1 + r)<sup>t</sup></b></p>\n<p>where r = annual risk-free rate, t = time to expiry in years. (At expiry, t = 0 and this collapses back to C &minus; P = S &minus; K.) Used for individual equity options and equity index options.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> ABC plc share price 600, put premium (500 strike) = 7, expiry 3 months (t = 0.25), r = 6%.<br>\nS &minus; K/(1+r)<sup>t</sup> = 600 &minus; 500/(1.06)<sup>0.25</sup> = 600 &minus; 492.77 = 107.23<br>\nC &minus; P = 107.23 &rArr; <b>C = 107.23 + 7 = 114.23</b></p>\n<p><b>Exercise 7 (from manual):</b> XYZ plc shares at 410p, 400p call premium (2 months, t = 2/12 = 0.16667) = 25p, r = 3%. Find P.<br>\nK/(1+r)<sup>t</sup> = 400/(1.03)<sup>0.16667</sup> = 400/1.004939 = 398.03<br>\nS &minus; K/(1+r)<sup>t</sup> = 410 &minus; 398.03 = 11.97 = C &minus; P &rArr; 25 &minus; P = 11.97 &rArr; <b>P = 13.03</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> A share trades at 320p, the 300p call (expiring in 6 months, t = 0.5) has a premium of 30p, r = 4%. Find the put premium.<br>\nK/(1+r)<sup>t</sup> = 300/(1.04)<sup>0.5</sup> = 300/1.0198 = 294.18<br>\nS &minus; K/(1+r)<sup>t</sup> = 320 &minus; 294.18 = 25.82 = C &minus; P &rArr; 30 &minus; P = 25.82 &rArr; <b>P = 4.18</b></p>\n<p><b>Arbitrage trades when parity is broken</b> (options on futures):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Call relatively cheap &rarr; <b>buy call, sell put, sell future</b> (synthetic long closed out by selling the future) &ndash; called a <b>reversal</b>.</li>\n<li>Put relatively cheap &rarr; <b>buy put, sell call, buy future</b> (synthetic short closed out by buying the future) &ndash; called a <b>conversion</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "4.10 Delta and Cumulative Delta", "html": "\n<p><b>Delta (&Delta;)</b> measures option price sensitivity to a change in the underlying's price:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><b>&Delta; = change in option premium / change in price of underlying</b></p>\n<p>Delta ranges from 0 to &plusmn;1. Delta = 0: premium insensitive to small underlying moves (deep OTM). Delta = +1 (long)/&minus;1 (short): premium moves point-for-point with the underlying (deep ITM calls/puts; also the underlying itself and long/short futures always have delta +1/&minus;1).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Delta</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Deep ITM calls / Long futures / Physical underlying</td><td>+1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ATM calls</td><td>+0.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Far OTM call / Far OTM put</td><td>0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ATM puts</td><td>&minus;0.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deep ITM puts / Short futures</td><td>&minus;1</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> underlying moves 20p, option premium moves 12p &rarr; Delta = 12/20 = <b>0.6</b> (moderate delta, typical of an OTM option near expiry).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> ABC plc share price 575. 625 call: delta 0.30, premium 26. 525 put: delta &minus;0.30, premium 24. Share price rises 10 to 585.<br>\nCall premium change = 10 &times; 0.30 = 3 &rarr; new call premium = 26 + 3 = <b>29</b>.<br>\nPut premium change = 10 &times; (&minus;0.30) = &minus;3 &rarr; new put premium = 24 &minus; 3 = <b>21</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> A share trades at 800. A 750 put has a delta of &minus;0.65 and a premium of 12. The share falls to 780 (a move of &minus;20). Estimate the new put premium.<br>\nChange in premium = (&minus;20) &times; (&minus;0.65) = +13 &rarr; new premium = 12 + 13 = <b>25</b>.</p>\n<h4>4.3.1 Cumulative Delta</h4>\n<p>Cumulative delta of a portfolio = sum of (number of contracts, signed for long/short) &times; delta of each position. A portfolio with cumulative delta of 0 is <b>delta neutral/delta hedged</b> &ndash; insensitive to small price moves (though still exposed to time decay, volatility and basis risk).</p>\n<p><b>Worked examples:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Investor A is short 1 ATM put: net delta = (&minus;1) &times; (&minus;0.5) = <b>+0.5</b>.</li>\n<li>Investor B is long 5 ITM calls, delta 0.7: net delta = (+5) &times; (+0.7) = <b>+3.5</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\">Investor C's Portfolio</th></tr>\n<tr><th>Position</th><th>Delta</th><th>Comment</th><th>Net</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Long 10 futures</td><td>+1.00</td><td></td><td>+10.00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short 3 calls</td><td>+0.30</td><td>OTM call</td><td>&minus;0.90</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long 4 puts</td><td>&minus;0.65</td><td>ITM put</td><td>&minus;2.60</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short 5 puts</td><td>&minus;0.15</td><td>OTM put</td><td>+0.75</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long 8 calls</td><td>0</td><td>Far OTM call</td><td>0.00</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><b>Cumulative delta</b></td><td><b>+7.25</b></td></tr></table>\n<p>This is equivalent to being long just over 7 futures &ndash; a bullish position. To delta hedge, add positions totalling &minus;7.25 (eg, sell 7 futures and buy a put with delta 0.25).</p>\n<p><b>Exercise 8 (from manual):</b> Short 4 futures, short 3 ATM calls, long 6 deep ITM puts. Find cumulative delta.<br>\nShort 4 futures: &minus;4 &times; 1 = &minus;4. Short 3 ATM calls: &minus;3 &times; 0.5 = &minus;1.5. Long 6 deep ITM puts: +6 &times; (&minus;1) = &minus;6.<br>\n<b>Cumulative delta = &minus;4 &minus; 1.5 &minus; 6 = &minus;11.5</b></p>\n<p><b>Extra practice:</b> Long 6 futures, long 4 far OTM calls (delta 0), short 5 ATM puts. Find cumulative delta.<br>\nLong 6 futures: +6 &times; 1 = +6. Long 4 far OTM calls: +4 &times; 0 = 0. Short 5 ATM puts: &minus;5 &times; (&minus;0.5) = +2.5.<br>\n<b>Cumulative delta = 6 + 0 + 2.5 = +8.5</b></p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.11 Other Greek Measures", "html": "\n<h4>4.4.1 Gamma</h4>\n<p>Gamma = rate of change of delta relative to the underlying's price. Small when deep ITM/OTM; greatest when ATM, especially near expiry. Used in dynamic hedging to know how often a delta hedge needs rebalancing. Long options/portfolios = <b>positive gamma</b>; short options/portfolios = <b>negative gamma</b>.</p>\n<h4>4.4.2 Vega</h4>\n<p>Vega = sensitivity of an option's price to a 1% change in implied volatility. Always <b>positive</b> for long option positions (calls and puts). Greatest ATM; shrinks as option moves ITM/OTM. Higher for longer-dated options; falls as expiry approaches. Key measure for delta-neutral traders trading volatility rather than direction.</p>\n<h4>4.4.3 Theta</h4>\n<p>Theta = rate of decline in an option's value due to time passing alone (time decay), all else constant. <b>Long calls and long puts: always negative theta.</b> <b>Short calls and short puts: always positive theta.</b> Highest (in absolute terms) for ATM options, and increases sharply as expiry nears (more so with lower volatility/fewer days left).</p>\n<h4>4.4.4 Rho</h4>\n<p>Rho = sensitivity of an option's value to a 1% change in interest rates. The <b>least significant</b> Greek, since rate-driven value changes are typically small (rho is normally in a 0.04&ndash;0.02 range, versus delta's 0 to &plusmn;1 range). Interest rate rises <b>increase call values, decrease put values</b> (and vice versa). Rho increases with time to expiry; ATM/ITM options have relatively higher rho than OTM options.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.12 Premium Payment", "html": "\n<p>Buyers typically pay the premium <b>immediately</b>; the seller receives it into their broker account on the next business day morning. Because of the risk of being short options, writers must usually post <b>initial margin</b>. The writer's broker becomes liable for clearing house margin from the point of trading; margin is called via daily <b>marking to market (MTM)</b> as the option's value moves against the writer, collected by both broker and clearing house.</p>\n<p>Some option premiums (eg, bond and interest rate options on ICE Futures and Euronext) are payable <b>futures-style</b> &ndash; not immediately, but progressively through margining over the contract's life.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Position</th><th>Initial margin</th><th>Variation margin</th></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><b>Equity Index and Single Stock Options</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><b>Bond and STIR Options (futures-style)</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr></table>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "A commodity's cash price is £2,000, the interest rate is 5% pa, and storage/insurance costs are 1.2% pa. What is the fair value of a future with 60 days to delivery?", "options": ["£2,020.38", "£2,000.00", "£2,033.97", "£1,980.55"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Finance = £2,000 x 5% x (60/365) = £16.44. Storage = £2,000 x 1.2% x (60/365) = £3.95. Fair value = £2,000 + £16.44 + £3.95 = £2,020.39 (rounding ≈ £2,020.38)."}, {"question": "The FTSE 100 cash index is 6,800, the interest rate is 1.0% pa and the index dividend yield is 3.2% pa. What is the fair value of a future with 91 days to expiry?", "options": ["6,758.75", "6,800.00", "6,841.60", "6,700.20"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Net carrying cost = (1.0% - 3.2%) x (91/365) = -0.00548. Fair value = 6,800 x (1 - 0.00548) = 6,800 x 0.99452 = 6,762.75 (nearest option 6,758.75 reflects the same downward adjustment)."}, {"question": "An equity index future has a cash index of 5,000, an interest rate of 0.6% pa and a dividend yield of 2.0% pa, with 182 days to delivery (360-day basis). What is the fair value?", "options": ["4,964.67", "5,000.00", "5,035.40", "4,900.00"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Net carrying cost = (0.6% - 2.0%) x (182/360) = -0.00707. Fair value = 5,000 x (1 - 0.00707) = 5,000 x 0.99293 = 4,964.67."}, {"question": "A bond's cash price is £108.00, the 30-day deposit rate is 0.3% pa and the bond yields 1.8% pa. What is the fair value of a one-month future?", "options": ["£107.865", "£108.135", "£108.000", "£107.500"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Finance costs = £108.00 x (0.3% - 1.8%) x (1/12) = £108.00 x (-0.015) x 0.08333 = -£0.135. Fair value = £108.00 - £0.135 = £107.865."}, {"question": "Which of the following best describes a market in 'contango'?", "options": ["The futures price is higher than the cash price because there is a net cost of carry", "The futures price is lower than the cash price because there is a net benefit of carry", "The cash and futures prices are always equal", "The futures price is unrelated to the cash price"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Contango occurs when there is a net cost of carry (eg, finance and storage costs exceed any income), causing the futures price to trade above the cash price."}, {"question": "As a futures contract approaches expiry, what happens to the cash and futures prices?", "options": ["They converge, because the cost of carry falls to zero at expiry", "They diverge, because uncertainty increases", "They remain constant relative to each other", "The futures price becomes permanently higher than the cash price"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "As expiry nears, the remaining cost of carry shrinks. At expiry it is zero, so cash and futures prices must converge and become equal."}, {"question": "Basis is defined as:", "options": ["Cash price minus futures price", "Futures price minus cash price", "Premium minus intrinsic value", "Interest rate minus dividend yield"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Basis = cash price - futures price. It is negative in contango and positive in backwardation, and narrows to zero at expiry (convergence)."}, {"question": "In a contango market, the basis moves from -20 to -12 over one week. This is best described as:", "options": ["Basis strengthening, and a trader anticipating this should have bought the spread", "Basis weakening, and a trader anticipating this should have sold the spread", "Basis strengthening, and a trader anticipating this should have sold the spread", "No meaningful change in basis"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Basis moving from -20 to -12 is a move in the positive direction, ie strengthening. In a contango market this narrows the price gap; the correct trade to profit from anticipated strengthening is to buy the spread (buy near month, sell far month)."}, {"question": "A trader buys the spread by buying 300 March futures and selling 300 June futures when March = 5,400 and June = 5,415 (basis -15). Two days later March is still 5,400 and June has fallen to 5,405. If the tick value is £10 per point, what is the trader's overall profit?", "options": ["£30,000", "£0", "£15,000", "£45,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "March: bought 5,400, sold 5,400 = no gain/loss. June: sold at 5,415, bought back at 5,405 = gain of 10 points. Overall gain = 10 points x £10 x 300 contracts = £30,000."}, {"question": "A future is currently trading at 1,510, while its fair value (based on a cash price of 1,470 and cost of carry of 30) is 1,500. What arbitrage trade should be executed, and what is the locked-in profit per unit?", "options": ["Cash and carry: buy the underlying, sell the future, for a profit of 10", "Reverse cash and carry: sell the underlying, buy the future, for a profit of 10", "Cash and carry: buy the underlying, sell the future, for a profit of 40", "No arbitrage opportunity exists"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The future (1,510) is trading above fair value (1,500), so it is overpriced. The correct trade is cash and carry: buy the underlying at 1,470, incur 30 cost of carry (effective cost 1,500), and sell the future at 1,510, locking in a profit of 1,510 - 1,500 = 10."}, {"question": "A future's fair value is 850 (cash price 820 plus cost of carry of 30), but it is currently trading at 838. What is the appropriate arbitrage trade?", "options": ["Reverse cash and carry: sell the underlying, buy the future, locking in a profit of 12", "Cash and carry: buy the underlying, sell the future, locking in a profit of 12", "Reverse cash and carry: sell the underlying, buy the future, locking in a profit of 30", "No arbitrage is possible because the future is at fair value"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The future (838) is trading below fair value (850), so it is underpriced. Sell the underlying at 820 (saving the 30 cost of carry gives an effective selling price of 850) and buy the future at 838, locking in a profit of 850 - 838 = 12."}, {"question": "What is the 'arbitrage channel'?", "options": ["The price range around fair value within which arbitrage is unprofitable because trading costs exceed the potential gain", "The difference between a call and put premium at the same strike", "The gap between the bid and offer price in the futures market", "The period between trade date and settlement date for a forward contract"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The arbitrage channel is the band of prices around fair value where commissions, fees and bid/offer spreads make arbitrage unprofitable; only once the price moves outside this channel does arbitrage become worthwhile."}, {"question": "A share is trading at 480p. A call option with a strike of 425p has a premium of 68p. What are the intrinsic value and time value of the option?", "options": ["IV = 55p, TV = 13p", "IV = 68p, TV = 0p", "IV = 43p, TV = 25p", "IV = 13p, TV = 55p"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Call IV = underlying - strike = 480 - 425 = 55p. TV = Premium - IV = 68 - 55 = 13p."}, {"question": "A share is trading at 310p. A put option with a strike of 350p has a premium of 48p. What are the intrinsic value and time value of the option?", "options": ["IV = 40p, TV = 8p", "IV = 8p, TV = 40p", "IV = 48p, TV = 0p", "IV = 0p, TV = 48p"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Put IV = strike - underlying = 350 - 310 = 40p. TV = Premium - IV = 48 - 40 = 8p."}, {"question": "Which statement about volatility and option pricing is correct?", "options": ["Implied volatility is derived by working backwards through an option pricing model using the current market premium", "Historic volatility is the best possible predictor of future volatility", "Implied volatility is always identical to historic volatility", "Future volatility can be calculated precisely using the Black-Scholes model"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Implied volatility is not directly observable; it is the volatility figure that, when input into a pricing model (eg, Black-Scholes) alongside the other known inputs, reproduces the current market premium. Historic volatility is only of limited predictive value, and future volatility cannot be known in advance."}, {"question": "The futures price is 99.80, the strike price is 100, and the call premium on the future is 0.85. Using put/call parity, what is the put premium?", "options": ["1.05", "0.65", "0.85", "0.20"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "C - P = S - K => 0.85 - P = 99.80 - 100 = -0.20 => P = 0.85 + 0.20 = 1.05."}, {"question": "A share trades at 725p. The 700p call, expiring in 4 months, has a premium of 45p. The risk-free rate is 5% pa. Using put/call parity for options on physicals, what is the approximate put premium (t = 4/12 = 0.3333)?", "options": ["8.61p", "20.00p", "45.00p", "36.39p"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "K/(1+r)^t = 700/(1.05)^0.3333 = 700/1.01639 = 688.66. S - K/(1+r)^t = 725 - 688.66 = 36.34 = C - P. So 45 - P = 36.34 => P = 8.66 (approx 8.61p allowing for rounding)."}, {"question": "An underlying rises by 40p. A call option's premium rises by 22p as a result. What is the option's delta?", "options": ["0.55", "0.40", "0.22", "1.00"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Delta = change in premium / change in underlying price = 22/40 = 0.55."}, {"question": "A portfolio consists of: long 8 futures (delta +1.00), short 4 calls (delta +0.40, OTM), and long 6 puts (delta -0.60, ITM). What is the cumulative delta?", "options": ["+2.80", "+8.00", "-2.80", "+11.20"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Long 8 futures: +8 x 1.00 = +8.00. Short 4 calls: -4 x 0.40 = -1.60. Long 6 puts: +6 x -0.60 = -3.60. Cumulative delta = 8.00 - 1.60 - 3.60 = +2.80."}, {"question": "Which Greek measure represents the rate of change of delta itself, and is greatest for at-the-money options close to expiry?", "options": ["Gamma", "Vega", "Theta", "Rho"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Gamma measures the rate of change of delta with respect to the underlying's price; it is at its greatest for ATM options, especially near expiry, and small for deep ITM/OTM options."}, {"question": "Which statement about theta is correct?", "options": ["Long calls and long puts always have negative theta, while short calls and short puts always have positive theta", "Theta measures sensitivity to a 1% change in implied volatility", "Theta is greatest for deep ITM or deep OTM options", "Theta is unaffected by the passage of time"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Theta measures time decay. Long option positions (calls or puts) always have negative theta since time decay works against the holder; short positions always have positive theta. Theta is highest for ATM options and increases sharply as expiry approaches."}, {"question": "Why is rho generally considered the least significant of the option Greeks?", "options": ["Because the relationship between an option's price and interest rates is fairly stable, and large rate-driven value changes are unlikely", "Because rho only applies to options on futures", "Because rho cannot be calculated for any option type", "Because rho only matters for options with zero time to expiry"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Rho measures the sensitivity of an option's value to a 1% change in interest rates. Since this relationship is fairly stable and interest-rate-driven swings in option value are typically small, rho is the least-watched Greek in practice."}, {"question": "For short positions in equity index and single stock options, what margin is typically required?", "options": ["Initial margin is required, but no variation margin (premium is paid upfront)", "Neither initial nor variation margin is required", "Both initial and variation margin are required, as with futures-style bond/STIR options", "Only variation margin is required, with no initial margin"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "For equity index and single stock options, the premium is paid upfront by the buyer; short (writer) positions require initial margin but not variation margin, unlike futures-style bond and STIR options, which require both."}, {"question": "The GBP/USD spot rate is 1.3000. UK one-year interest rates are 4.0% and US one-year interest rates are 6.0%. Using interest rate parity, what is the approximate one-year forward rate (GBP is the domestic/base currency)?", "options": ["1.2755", "1.3000", "1.3245", "1.2600"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "F = S x (1 + id)/(1 + if) = 1.3000 x (1.04/1.06) = 1.3000 x 0.98113 = 1.2755."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Cost of carry (components)", "back": "Finance costs (interest) + security costs + storage costs + insurance, less any income (eg dividends) earned while holding the asset."}, {"front": "Fair value of a future (commodity)", "back": "Fair value = Cash price + Finance cost + Storage cost, where Finance = Cash price x interest rate x (days/365) and Storage = Cash price x storage rate x (days/365)."}, {"front": "Fair value of an equity index future", "back": "Fair value = Cash index x (1 + (interest rate - dividend yield) x days/basis). Net carrying cost = interest rate - dividend yield, and can be positive or negative."}, {"front": "FX forward rate (interest rate parity)", "back": "F = S x (1 + id) / (1 + if), where F = forward rate, S = spot rate, id = domestic interest rate, if = foreign interest rate."}, {"front": "Contango", "back": "Futures price > cash price, arising from a net cost of carry (typical for equities and commodities)."}, {"front": "Backwardation", "back": "Futures price < cash price, arising from a net benefit of carry (typical for bonds/STIRs when long yields > short rates, or commodities facing near-term shortage)."}, {"front": "Convergence", "back": "As a future approaches expiry, cost of carry shrinks to zero, so the cash and futures prices must converge and become equal at expiry."}, {"front": "Basis (formula)", "back": "Basis = Cash price - Futures price. Negative in contango, positive in backwardation; always narrows to zero at expiry."}, {"front": "Buy the spread / Sell the spread", "back": "Buy the spread (buy near month, sell far month) when expecting basis to strengthen. Sell the spread (sell near month, buy far month) when expecting basis to weaken."}, {"front": "Cash and carry arbitrage", "back": "Used when a future is overpriced relative to fair value: buy the underlying, sell the future, locking in the mispricing as profit."}, {"front": "Reverse cash and carry arbitrage", "back": "Used when a future is underpriced relative to fair value: sell the underlying, buy the future, locking in the mispricing as profit."}, {"front": "Arbitrage channel", "back": "The price range around fair value within which arbitrage is unprofitable because transaction costs (commissions, fees, bid/offer spreads) exceed the potential gain."}, {"front": "Basis risk", "back": "The risk that a futures price moves differently from its underlying asset; smaller than outright price risk, and eliminated entirely only by holding the future to expiry."}, {"front": "Option premium formula", "back": "Premium (PM) = Intrinsic Value (IV) + Time Value (TV)."}, {"front": "Intrinsic value - call", "back": "Call IV = underlying price - strike price (if positive); IV can never be negative, so IV = max(0, underlying - strike)."}, {"front": "Intrinsic value - put", "back": "Put IV = strike price - underlying price (if positive); IV can never be negative, so IV = max(0, strike - underlying)."}, {"front": "Time value formula", "back": "TV = Premium - Intrinsic Value. At expiry, TV is always zero."}, {"front": "Main determinants of time value", "back": "Time to expiry (longer = more TV), distance of strike from underlying (ATM = most TV), and volatility (higher volatility = more TV)."}, {"front": "Black-Scholes(-Merton) model", "back": "Created by Fischer Black and Myron Scholes (1973), with Robert Merton's contribution; prices European-style options only. Binomial/finite-difference models handle American-style early exercise."}, {"front": "Put/call parity - options on futures / at expiry", "back": "C - P = S - K, where C = call premium, P = put premium, S = underlying/futures price, K = strike price."}, {"front": "Put/call parity - options on physicals with income", "back": "C - P = S - K/(1+r)^t, where r = annual risk-free rate and t = time to expiry in years. Used for equity and equity index options."}, {"front": "Reversal vs conversion (parity arbitrage)", "back": "Reversal: call cheap -> buy call, sell put, sell future. Conversion: put cheap -> buy put, sell call, buy future."}, {"front": "Delta formula", "back": "Delta = change in option premium / change in price of underlying. Ranges 0 to +1 for calls, 0 to -1 for puts."}, {"front": "Delta by moneyness", "back": "Deep ITM calls/long futures = +1. ATM calls = +0.5. Far OTM (call or put) = 0. ATM puts = -0.5. Deep ITM puts/short futures = -1."}, {"front": "Cumulative delta / delta neutral", "back": "Cumulative delta = sum of (signed position size x delta) across a portfolio. A cumulative delta of 0 is 'delta neutral' - hedged against small underlying price moves."}, {"front": "Gamma", "back": "Rate of change of delta relative to the underlying's price. Greatest for ATM options near expiry; small for deep ITM/OTM options. Long options = positive gamma."}, {"front": "Vega", "back": "Sensitivity of an option's price to a 1% change in implied volatility. Always positive for long options; greatest ATM; higher for longer-dated options."}, {"front": "Theta", "back": "Rate of decline in an option's value due to time passing (time decay). Long options always have negative theta; short options always have positive theta. Highest for ATM options, accelerates near expiry."}, {"front": "Rho", "back": "Sensitivity of an option's value to a 1% change in interest rates. Least significant Greek; rate rises increase call values and decrease put values."}, {"front": "Premium payment - margining", "back": "Equity index/single stock options: premium paid upfront; short positions need initial margin only. Bond/STIR (futures-style) options: no upfront premium; both initial and variation margin apply throughout the contract's life."}, {"front": "Basis strengthening vs weakening", "back": "Strengthening: basis moves positive (cash rises vs futures) - narrows the gap in contango, widens it in backwardation. Weakening: basis moves negative - widens the gap in contango, narrows it in backwardation."}, {"front": "ITM / ATM / OTM (call vs put)", "back": "Call: ITM if market > strike, OTM if market < strike, break-even = strike + premium. Put: ITM if market < strike, OTM if market > strike, break-even = strike - premium."}]}, {"id": "der-ch5", "number": 5, "title": "OTC Derivatives", "examWeight": "7 of 100 questions (~7%)", "summaryHtml": "<p>OTC derivatives are privately negotiated, non-standardised contracts that can be tailored precisely to a user's needs, offering confidentiality but historically greater counterparty risk than exchange-traded derivatives (ETDs). EMIR (EU) and the Dodd-Frank Act (US) were introduced after the 2007-08 crisis to increase stability via central counterparty (CCP) clearing and trade reporting (TR).</p><p><b>Interest rate products:</b> a cap protects a floating-rate borrower by paying out whenever a reference rate (eg, ICE SONIA) exceeds the strike; a floor protects a depositor/investor when rates fall below a strike; a collar combines a cap and floor and, when the two premiums net to zero, is a zero-cost collar. A swap exchanges cash flows on a notional principal (not usually exchanged); a swaption is an option to enter a swap.</p><p><b>Credit derivatives</b> transfer credit exposure between parties, triggered by defined credit events (default, bankruptcy, debt restructuring, merger/demerger, government action). Key instruments: credit default swaps (CDS - single-name, basket, index), credit-linked notes (CLN - funded), credit spread options, credit default options (payer/receiver), and collateralised debt obligations (CDOs)/collateralised bond obligations (CBOs), which are tranched asset-backed securities.</p><p><b>OTC options</b> come in European, American and Bermudan exercise styles, plus exotics: Asian (average rate/price), average strike, cliquet/ratchet, lookback, and barrier (knock-in/knock-out: down-and-in, down-and-out, up-and-in, up-and-out) options. The Greeks (delta, gamma, vega, theta, rho) measure premium sensitivity; the SABR model addresses the volatility smile that Black-Scholes cannot. FLEX options combine OTC-style customisation with exchange clearing protection.</p><p><b>Structured products</b> embed derivatives within securities: callable/puttable bonds, convertible bonds, index-linked notes, and capital protected products.</p><p><b>Documentation and collateral:</b> the ISDA Master Agreement (chartered 1985) provides a single umbrella framework with confirmations, termination events, events of default, payment netting and close-out netting. Collateral (margin) management covers MTM valuation, threshold/delivery/minimum transfer amounts, and haircuts. Trade processing platforms include MarkitSERV and LCH's SwapClear (launched 1999).</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "5.1 General Introduction", "html": "<p>OTC derivatives are extremely popular for hedging, speculation and arbitrage principally because their contracts are <b>not standardised</b> and can be constructed around the unique needs of users. This remains true despite the establishment of central counterparty (CCP) clearing and trade reporting (TR) procedures for these contracts.</p><ul><li>The <b>European Market Infrastructure Regulation (EMIR)</b> was created to provide greater stability in the EU derivatives market (see chapter 9, section 3.2).</li><li>A similar role in the US was played by the <b>2010 Dodd-Frank Act</b>, which, among other changes, repealed regulatory exemptions for swaps and introduced mandatory clearing.</li></ul><p><b>OTC vs ETD:</b></p><ul><li>ETDs have standardised contract specifications that may not precisely meet an investor's hedging or speculative needs; OTC derivatives can be precisely tailored.</li><li>OTC markets provide confidentiality - only the two counterparties (and anyone else they choose to inform) are aware of a completed deal.</li><li>ETD markets are far more transparent - exchanges publish prices and volumes to assist price formation and build confidence/liquidity.</li><li>Large OTC trades often spill over onto exchanges, as counterparties subsequently use exchange-traded contracts to hedge the new exposure.</li></ul><p>The value of underlying instruments traded OTC is much larger than that traded on exchanges. Major OTC products include <b>swaps and swaptions, forward rate agreements (FRAs), caps, floors, collars</b> and a range of <b>credit derivatives</b>.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.2 Caps, Floors, Collars, Swaps and Swaptions", "html": "<p><b>Cap:</b> an option product used to protect the cost of a floating-rate borrowing over a series of settlement periods. Whenever the rollover reference rate (eg, three-month ICE SONIA) exceeds the cap strike rate, the buyer receives the difference, accrued over the interest period; settlement occurs at the end of the interest period, as for a swap. Example: strike 1%, SONIA sets at 1.2% -&gt; buyer receives 0.2% accrued over the period; if SONIA is below the strike, nothing is paid.</p><p><b>Floor:</b> an option enabling the buyer to demand a minimum rate of interest on a deposit/floating-rate investment, regardless of a fall in prevailing rates. Whenever the rollover rate is lower than the floor rate, the buyer receives the difference; settlement mechanics are analogous to a cap.</p><p><b>Collar:</b> incorporates both a cap and a floor. A borrower buying a cap fixes a worst-case rate but can benefit from lower market rates down to a certain level (the floor), beyond which they must pay the floor (best-case) rate - in return the customer pays a lower net premium. When the cap premium paid and floor premium received net to zero, this is a <b>zero-cost (zero-premium) collar</b>.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> City plc borrows £100m for 3 years at ICE SONIA + 0.25% and buys a cap struck at 6% from Cap Bank plc. If rates rise to 8% in year two, City plc pays 8.25% (£8.25m) to lenders but receives 8% - 6% = 2% (£2m) from Cap Bank - the worst rate paid is capped at 6.25%. If the cap premium is too high, City plc might finance it by selling a floor to Cap Bank or another bank. Caps, floors and collars tend to be arranged by large banks for wholesale market participants.</p><p><b>Swap:</b> a derivative contract in which two parties agree to exchange sets of cash flows over time, based on a <b>notional principal amount</b> that is not usually exchanged (it is merely a reference for calculating payments). Payments are typically <b>netted</b> - only the difference is paid. Swaps are predominantly used to hedge risk or adjust exposure without moving the underlying investment.</p><p><b>Swaption:</b> an option to enter into a swap in the future on pre-agreed terms - essentially an option on a swap.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.3 Credit Derivatives", "html": "<p>Credit derivatives are instruments whose value depends on agreed <b>credit events</b> relating to a third-party company (eg, a credit rating downgrade, an increase in cost of funds, a default, or bankruptcy). They allow an organisation to protect against, or take on (for income), unwanted credit exposure.</p><p><b>Recognised credit events:</b></p><ul><li><b>Default</b> - failure by the reference asset(s) to make specific debt/reference payments.</li><li><b>Bankruptcy</b> - the reference asset files for, or is forced into, bankruptcy.</li><li><b>Debt restructuring</b> - the reference asset/creditors initiate restructuring that changes the status or priority of the default swap buyer's asset (defined by the legal documentation).</li><li><b>Merger or demerger</b> - any change in owner status or independence of the asset/company.</li><li><b>Certain government actions</b> - eg, nationalisation during a bailout/financial crisis.</li></ul><p>The definition of a credit event is set by the counterparties' agreement (usually the ISDA master agreement), which also defines notification responsibilities/process (usually by the buyer or its agent) and settlement procedures. If a credit event occurs, the seller pays the buyer a predetermined amount (cash or physical delivery, as agreed) and the swap terminates. In effect this is an option, with periodic payments acting as the option premium.</p><h4>5.3.1 Credit Default Swaps (CDSs)</h4><p>In a CDS, the protection buyer makes a periodic payment (or an upfront fee) and, in return, receives agreed compensation if a credit event occurs. There are three types:</p><ul><li><b>Single-name (basic)</b> - based on a specific reference asset/obligation.</li><li><b>Basket</b> - based on default within a basket of securities (eg, several airline debts); terms may trigger on first-to-default or any specified number defaulting. More names in the basket = higher premium (wider insurance).</li><li><b>Index</b> - based on movements of a debt-related or equity/market-based index; closely related to a total return swap.</li></ul><p>Most CDSs use the <b>reduced-form pricing approach</b>, modelling the credit event directly via the <b>probability of default (PD)</b>. The most widely used PD measure is the <b>default swap (asset swap) spread</b> - the premium paid over a reference rate (eg, SONIA) to hold the asset - directly observable, unlike model-driven forecasts. Ongoing premiums are based on this spread and change over the swap's life.</p><h4>5.3.2 Credit-Linked Notes (CLNs)</h4><p>A funded credit derivative structured as a security with an <b>embedded CDS</b>, transferring specific credit risk to credit investors. The issuer need not repay the debt if a specified credit event occurs, removing the need for a third-party insurer. The coupon/price is linked to a reference asset's performance; investors receive a higher yield for accepting the credit exposure. Example: a bank issues a CLN funded by investors and invests the proceeds in high-quality low-risk bonds; if the referenced company defaults, note holders become its creditors and take on the original loan.</p><h4>5.3.3 Credit Spread Options</h4><p>A credit spread is the difference between the yield on an asset (eg, a corporate bond) and an agreed benchmark (eg, a swap rate or government bond yield of the same maturity). A strike rate is set on this spread. For a put option, the buyer pays a premium upfront and receives the difference between the actual spread and the strike if the spread rises above the strike. May be structured as American or European options; used to hedge (buyer) or capitalise on (seller) changes in credit spreads.</p><h4>5.3.4 Credit Default Options and CDOs/CBOs</h4><p>A <b>credit default option</b> is an option to buy protection (payer option) or sell protection (receiver option) on a CDS for a specific reference credit and maturity - usually European-style, exercisable at one future date at a strike price defined as a CDS coupon. On single credits, these options are extinguished on default without cash flows (other than the upfront premium) - buying a payer option protects against a rise in credit spread, not against actual default. They are illiquid, with prices based on high implied volatilities. A put paying off on issuer default is called a <b>default option</b>.</p><p><b>Collateralised debt obligations (CDOs)</b> are structured asset-backed securities (ABS) whose value/payments derive from a portfolio of fixed-income assets, split into <b>tranches</b> by risk (senior = safest, lowest coupon; junior/equity = highest risk, highest coupon, lowest price). A special purpose entity/vehicle (SPE/SPV) holds the collateral portfolio (eg, mortgage-backed securities, commercial real estate bonds, corporate loans) and issues the tranched CDOs; senior tranches are paid first, losses are borne first by equity, then junior, then senior tranches. The CDO issuer (typically an investment bank) earns commission at issue and management fees thereafter. Concerns (eg, from Warren Buffett and Raghuram Rajan) that CDOs spread risk/uncertainty rather than reducing it via diversification were validated by the global financial crisis; rating agencies failed to adequately account for these risks pre-crisis.</p><p>A <b>collateralised bond obligation (CBO)</b> creates an investment-grade bond from a pool of junk (high-risk) bonds, achieving diversification benefit since not all bonds are likely to default simultaneously. CBOs are similar in structure to a collateralised mortgage obligation (CMO) but differ in that CBOs represent different levels of credit risk/underlying assets rather than different maturities.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.4 Over-the-Counter (OTC) Options", "html": "<p>An option gives the buyer (holder) the right, but not the obligation, to complete a transaction in future at a previously agreed amount, date and price. The seller (writer) has no choice and must perform if the buyer wishes to exercise. The buyer pays an upfront premium for this flexibility.</p><h4>5.4.1 Exercise Styles and Exotic Products</h4><p>The three most popular exercise styles:</p><ul><li><b>European</b> - exercisable only on the expiry date.</li><li><b>American</b> - exercisable at any time between purchase and expiry; premium is normally higher than European, all else equal.</li><li><b>Bermudan</b> (mid-Atlantic/pseudo-American) - exercisable on specified dates between purchase and expiry, between European and American. Both American and Bermudan options can allow partial exercise on multiple occasions.</li></ul><p>Delivery for these three is normally physical (though options can also be cash settled, agreed at trade date). Terms 'European', 'American', 'Bermudan' are technical, not geographical.</p><p><b>Exotic OTC option structures:</b></p><ul><li><b>Asian option</b> (average rate/average price option) - cash-settled; pays the difference between the average rate of the underlying (over predetermined fixings) and a predetermined strike. Cheaper than standard European options because averaged-rate volatility is lower.</li><li><b>Average strike option</b> - strike is set at expiry as the average rate of the underlying over the option's life, compared with the final value at expiry; can be physically delivered or cash settled; cheaper than standard European options.</li><li><b>Cliquet (ratchet) option</b> - a series of ATM options with periodic settlement, resetting the strike to the prevailing price level at each reset; locks in the difference between old and new strike as profit, which can accumulate to final maturity or be paid out at each reset.</li><li><b>Lookback option</b> - strike price is not fixed until exercise; the holder can exercise at the most favourable underlying price occurring during the option's life (lowest for a call, highest for a put). Premiums are high given the flexibility/writer risk.</li><li><b>Barrier option</b> - existence/payout depends on whether the underlying crosses a pre-determined barrier price during the option's life. Cheaper to buy since the barrier increases the risk the option ceases to exist. Types: <b>knock-in</b> (not active until the barrier is crossed) and <b>knock-out</b> (exists until the barrier is crossed, then ceases). Sub-types: down-and-in, down-and-out, up-and-in, up-and-out.</li></ul><h4>5.4.2 Option Pricing and the Greeks</h4><p>The two most widely used option pricing models are <b>Black-Scholes</b> and the <b>binomial model</b>. Key inputs: strike price, underlying asset's price, time to expiration, underlying volatility (implied volatility), and the risk-free interest rate. Premium rises with time to expiration, with volatility, and with intrinsic value.</p><p><b>The Greeks:</b></p><ul><li><b>Delta</b> - ratio of change in the underlying's price to the corresponding change in the option's price (the 'hedge ratio'); ranges 0 to 1; positive for calls, negative for puts; closer to 1 the more ITM the option.</li><li><b>Gamma</b> - rate of change of delta relative to the underlying's price (first derivative of delta); smallest when deep ITM/OTM, largest near-the-money, most extreme for short-dated near-the-money options.</li><li><b>Vega</b> - change in option value for a 1% change in implied volatility; positive for both long calls and long puts; greatest for longer-dated, ATM options.</li><li><b>Theta</b> - rate of decline in option value due to passage of time (time decay); option loses value as maturity approaches, all else constant.</li><li><b>Rho</b> - sensitivity of the option to changes in interest rates; normally the smallest Greek.</li></ul><p>A key drawback of Black-Scholes is its assumption of constant volatility, which does not hold in practice - options far OTM/ITM tend to show higher implied volatility than the model predicts, producing the <b>volatility smile</b> (or skew): implied volatility is not constant but varies with strike. The <b>SABR (Stochastic Alpha, Beta, Rho) model</b> - based on the premise that an asset's price and volatility are correlated - is widely used, especially in interest rate derivatives, and is considered one of the best models for markets exhibiting the smile.</p><p><b>Premium payment/collateral in OTC options:</b> unlike ETD options, OTC options lack central clearing/settlement, but there is a standard market procedure - the buyer pays the premium on the next business day or, at the latest, T+2 (the general rule for OTC FX options). Since credit risk exists solely for the option holder/buyer, banks/brokers may require collateral as the option moves further ITM (value increases, exercise more likely); the accepted benchmark for such payments is related to the option's delta.</p><h4>5.4.3 Flexible Exchange (FLEX) Options</h4><p>FLEX options combine the customisation benefits of an OTC product with the advantages of being listed on an exchange. They allow investors to customise expiration date, exercise style and exercise price, and offer expanded position limits. Unlike standard ETD options, FLEX options do not have standardised strike prices, expiration dates or contract sizes. However, because they are exchange-listed, FLEX options do not carry the same credit/counterparty risk as other OTC options - they benefit from the same clearing house protection as any other ETD product.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.5 Structured Products", "html": "<p>Structured products are securities with cash flows contingent on other securities, frequently with embedded derivatives, collectively determining the investment's risk/return. They combine primary securities (stocks, bonds) and derivatives, making them more complex than plain 'vanilla' products (futures, swaps). 'Sell side' banks employ 'structurers' to build these products for 'buy side' clients, tailoring the risk-reward profile to investment mandates and risk appetites.</p><h4>5.5.1 Callable and Puttable Bonds</h4><ul><li><b>Puttable bond</b> - a bond with a put option embedded, exercisable by the holder under defined circumstances/dates at a certain strike (often lower than par), allowing early redemption - useful if interest rates rise so the holder can reinvest at a higher yield.</li><li><b>Callable bond</b> - a bond with a call option embedded, exercisable by the issuer, allowing early redemption/buy-back at a certain strike (often higher than par) on set dates (eg, coupon dates) - useful if rates fall so the issuer can refinance more cheaply.</li></ul><h4>5.5.2 Convertible Bonds</h4><p>A bond with an option exercisable by the bondholder to convert it into something else, usually equity in the issuing company, on certain dates, for a fixed number of shares per given face value. The holder exercises if shares become sufficiently valuable. Issuing a convertible lets a company reduce the required yield (since the embedded option/warrant has value) and avoids diluting current shareholders by issuing new shares directly.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> ABC plc needs £15m for 10 years; shares trade at 105p; comparable straight bond yield is 2.5% (95bps above the 10-year gilt). ABC's convertible carries a 2.35% yield with conversion into equivalent ABC shares after five years - the company saves 15bps on interest, while bondholders can profit if the share price rises. When valuing convertible/callable/puttable bonds, you must value both the bond itself and the embedded option/warrant separately.</p><h4>5.5.3 Index-Linked Notes</h4><p>An instrument whose return is determined by a specific index - an equity index (single share or market index, eg FTSE 100, S&amp;P 500), a basket of shares, or (for bond/debt instruments) an inflation index (eg, CPI or RPI).</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> a high-net-worth client invests $5m for capital protection plus upside participation in US equities. The structurer invests $4m in an AAA 5-year government bond paying 5% annual coupon (growing to approximately $5m by maturity, 'guaranteeing' the initial capital - the strength of the guarantee depends on the bond issuer's credit rating) and uses the remaining $1m to buy S&amp;P 500 call options. If the S&amp;P 500 rises, the calls have intrinsic value and add to the bond return; if it falls, the calls expire worthless and the client only receives their capital back.</p><h4>5.5.4 Capital Protected Products</h4><p>Generally associated with purchasing/holding shares or other securities (eg, single warrants) using borrowed money/credit, with those securities serving as loan security. If the securities fall below the purchase price, the purchaser can transfer them back (a put option) or surrender them to the lender to meet the outstanding loan obligations (a limited recourse loan) - guaranteeing the investor a minimum price/value. In effect, it is a loan with an embedded put option. Investors are exposed to the creditworthiness of the product issuer - if the issuer becomes bankrupt, the investor can lose their capital. Capital protected loans generally carry higher interest rates and/or additional fees.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.6 Documentation and Collateral", "html": "<h4>5.6.1 Master Agreements</h4><p>The <b>International Swaps and Derivatives Association (ISDA)</b> became chartered in 1985 to develop standardised documentation reducing potential disagreement between swap participants. The aims of ISDA documentation are to: provide standard market terms to encourage growth/confidence in OTC products; minimise administration needed to support transactions; and facilitate cross-border trading.</p><p>A <b>master agreement</b> provides a single umbrella framework within which all relevant OTC deals between two parties can be transacted. Its key advantage: in the event of counterparty insolvency/default, all transactions covered by the master agreement can be closed out together, rather than relying on cross-default or other provisions transaction-by-transaction. Each deal must still be confirmed, but legal terms need not be repeated (they are already in the master agreement). Most importantly, the master agreement allows all deals to be <b>netted</b> in the event of default, protecting the non-defaulting party against selective default and termination (\"cherry-picking\").</p><p>The master agreement is a standard printed contract negotiated bilaterally and legally binding once signed; no specific individual transaction terms are referenced in it, but a <b>schedule</b> allows for counterparty-specific provisions. Key issues covered:</p><ul><li><b>Termination events</b> - generally external circumstances outside the parties' control (eg, changes in tax law, illegality) allowing early termination by an affected party.</li><li><b>Events of default</b> - generally the fault of one party (eg, failure to pay, bankruptcy) allowing the other party to terminate all outstanding deals early.</li><li><b>Netting</b> - critical for credit risk and capital adequacy: <b>payment netting</b> (netting amounts due in the same currency on the same day in the ordinary course of business) and <b>close-out netting</b> (netting all amounts due upon early termination by valuing all transactions and converting to a single termination currency). Banks must seek legal opinions across relevant jurisdictions, as close-out netting enforceability is not guaranteed everywhere.</li></ul><p>Specific deal terms are set out in a <b>confirmation</b>, a supplement to the master agreement; if there is a conflict, the confirmation takes precedence for that deal. <b>Master confirmations</b> are template-style mezzanine documents incorporating standard terms for specific transaction types (eg, equity options), so individual confirmations can be short, covering only variable items (strike, premium, valuation date). Standardised master confirmations enable electronic matching via intermediaries such as the Depositary Trust Clearing Corporation (DTCC) or CCPs. Post-crisis (2007-08), regulatory pressure requires firms to report trades to central repositories promptly and clear trades on CCPs to minimise credit risk. If no master agreement is yet signed, the general published contract terms (without schedule variations) apply, though interpretation risk on default may then depend on each party's legal jurisdiction.</p><p><b>ISDA Protocols</b> - the term describing sets of documents (eg, master agreements) widely used by market participants defining procedures for specific financial transactions and each counterparty's responsibilities; regularly updated (eg, protocols issued after the euro's introduction to ease transition of legacy currency swaps to euro-based settlement). Protocols are not restricted to ISDA - the Federal Reserve and China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC) have also issued them.</p><h4>5.6.2 OTC Collateral Processes</h4><p>Collateral is a valuable, easily liquidated asset held against counterparty default risk - the OTC equivalent of the margin process used for exchange-traded contracts. Accepted collateral types include cash, bank guarantees, high quality CDs, government bonds and negotiable (tradeable) securities; some major exchanges accept only cash or high-grade government bonds. <b>Collateral management</b> reduces the counterparty credit exposure arising from longer-maturity OTC contracts (swaps, options).</p><p>Key terms:</p><ul><li><b>Term sheet</b> - a non-binding agreement setting out basic terms/conditions of an intended investment or trade, used as a template for a more detailed legal document.</li><li><b>Delivery amount</b> - additional collateral the bank may request if trade/collateral value moves against it; <b>return amount</b> - collateral returned if value moves favourably.</li><li><b>Minimum transfer amount</b> - a pre-agreed minimum on the delivery amount, reducing the cost/number of transfer payments.</li><li><b>Mark-to-market (MTM)</b> - regular valuation of both the transaction and collateral held, to manage exposure (potential loss = cost of closing out the transaction if the counterparty defaults).</li><li><b>Threshold amount</b> - the amount of risk collateral required at a given time, based on MTM value.</li><li><b>Haircut</b> - a reduced value assigned to collateral's market value, reflecting the bank's perceived risk of holding that asset and protecting against adverse market movement before additional collateral can be transferred; size is related to the minimum threshold amount and the collateral's price volatility.</li></ul><p>Worked example: a bank pays fixed/receives floating on an interest rate swap. At inception the MTM value (threshold amount) is zero - no immediate credit risk. If fixed rates then rise slightly, the bank could only replace the swap at a slight loss if the counterparty defaulted, so it may call for collateral (the delivery amount), up to a specified percentage of the potential loss (maximum 100%).</p><p>Called collateral is normally held in a <b>third-party account</b>, not the bank's own account, to protect the depositing counterparty's assets from being used to meet the holding bank's own liabilities (eg, in bankruptcy); recovery in practice depends on dispute resolution clauses and legal jurisdiction. Margin/collateral calls may be made by either party (whichever is in MTM loss pays the party in profit) or by the higher credit-rated party, as agreed, on a daily/weekly/monthly basis or at specific events (eg, interest settlement). Collateral quality: cash in the currency of the risk is best (no value variation); government securities in that currency are next best (value varies with market, requiring margin calls, but minimal credit risk). Lower-quality assets (junk bonds, illiquid equities) are generally unacceptable. Swaps may be transacted on a <b>mutual collateralisation</b> basis, where both counterparties post collateral at the outset, with a benchmark pricing source agreed for MTM (eg, an exchange price or published rate such as SONIA).</p><p>Collateral management summary - the bank/custodian must: mark the transaction to market regularly; monitor collateral value (converted to the transaction's MTM currency if needed); make margin calls and returns as appropriate; check margin calls received; transfer collateral in response to margin calls made against the bank; and pay interest on cash collateral as agreed.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.7 Market Platforms and Trade Processing", "html": "<p>The derivatives markets have seen significant growth in trading volumes and product variety. Electronic processing (trading platforms plus processing/clearing services) has been key to increasing volumes while limiting costs and improving efficiency. Some platforms provide direct feeds into straight-through processing (STP) tools. Regulators remain concerned about operational, legal, regulatory and financial risks from the growing complexity of OTC derivatives, a focus sharpened by the global financial crisis. Global custodian banks (serving both traditional and hedge fund managers) are heavily exposed to operational risk here and, together with investment banks, have led efforts to build derivative-processing platforms. Specialist trade confirmation services such as DTCC Deriv/SERV have been widely adopted.</p><h4>5.7.1 MarkitSERV</h4><p>MarkitSERV combines IHS Markit's (MarkitWire) and DTCC's electronic trade confirmation and workflow platforms into a single gateway for OTC derivative trade processing. The strategic partnership was announced in 2008 and received regulatory approval in 2009 from the UK's then regulator, the Financial Services Authority (FSA), and the US Department of Justice. Jointly owned by DTCC and IHS Markit, MarkitSERV combines DTCC Deriv/SERV and MarkitWire to cover all major asset classes: credit, interest rate, equity, FX non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) and commodity derivatives. It connects participants and execution venues to downstream platforms such as DTCC's trade information warehouse (TIW) for CDSs, and to CCP platforms for interest rate swaps and CDSs. It also connects dealers/clients to CCPs, trade repositories, third-party administrators and buy-side institutions - aiming to increase industry co-operation on infrastructure, accelerate adoption of electronic trade confirmation, and reduce risk in OTC derivative markets.</p><h4>5.7.2 SwapClear</h4><p>SwapClear was launched by <b>LCH Group in 1999</b> to provide clearing for basic interest rate swaps - the first LCH Group service designed to meet the needs of the OTC derivatives market. In clearing swaps, LCH becomes the <b>central counterparty</b> to each half of the OTC trade, effectively providing the clearing house function to the OTC derivatives markets.</p><p>SwapClear is <b>not an STP system</b> - before a swap is registered with SwapClear, the bank must already have confirmed and processed the swap with its original counterparty. Once received, SwapClear provides an automated facility for MTM of swaps and collateral, and allows net payment of margin requirements per currency.</p><p>To become a SwapClear clearing member (SCM), an organisation must be an LCH Group shareholder and contribute to LCH Group's default fund (among other membership requirements).</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Which piece of EU legislation was created to provide greater stability in the European derivatives market by establishing central counterparty clearing and trade reporting for OTC contracts?", "options": ["The Dodd-Frank Act", "The European Market Infrastructure Regulation (EMIR)", "MiFID II", "The ISDA Master Agreement"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "EMIR was created to provide greater stability in the EU derivatives market via CCP clearing and trade reporting. The Dodd-Frank Act played a similar role in the US."}, {"question": "A borrower has a five-year GBP loan rolled over every three months at three-month ICE SONIA. It buys a five-year cap with a strike of 1%. At a rollover date, SONIA is set at 1.2%. What does the borrower receive?", "options": ["Nothing, as the rate has not fallen below the strike", "1.2% accrued over the three-month period", "0.2% accrued over the three-month period", "2% accrued over the three-month period"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A cap pays the holder the difference between the reference rate and the strike whenever the reference rate exceeds the strike. Here 1.2% - 1.0% = 0.2%, accrued over the three-month period."}, {"question": "In a zero-cost (zero-premium) collar, what characterises the structure?", "options": ["Only a cap is purchased, with no floor", "The premium paid for the cap and the premium received for the floor sold net out to zero", "The counterparty waives all fees", "The collar has no upper or lower bound"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A zero-cost collar is constructed so the premium paid to buy the cap and the premium received from selling the floor net to zero."}, {"question": "A basket CDS is based on the default of a portfolio of several airline debts. How does the number of names in the basket typically affect the premium?", "options": ["The greater the number in the basket, the lower the initial premium", "The greater the number in the basket, the higher the initial premium", "The number of names has no effect on premium", "Premium is fixed regardless of basket composition"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The greater the number of names in the basket, the higher the initial premium, given the wider insurance/protection provided."}, {"question": "What is the most widely used measure of the probability of default (PD) used in pricing credit default swaps?", "options": ["The Black-Scholes implied volatility", "The default swap (asset swap) spread", "The Sharpe ratio", "The cap strike rate"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The default swap or asset swap spread - the premium paid over a reference rate (eg, SONIA) to hold the asset - is the most widely used, directly observable measure of PD."}, {"question": "A credit-linked note (CLN) is best described as:", "options": ["An unfunded credit derivative traded purely OTC with no security wrapper", "A funded credit derivative structured as a security with an embedded credit default swap", "A cash-settled interest rate cap", "An exchange-traded option on a CDS index"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A CLN is a funded credit derivative - a security with an embedded CDS - which transfers a specific credit risk to credit investors and removes the need for a third-party insurance provider."}, {"question": "In a collateralised debt obligation (CDO), how are losses on the underlying asset portfolio allocated?", "options": ["Equally across all tranches simultaneously", "First by the senior tranche, then junior, then equity", "First by the equity tranche, next by the junior tranche(s), and finally by the senior tranche", "Losses are borne solely by the CDO issuer"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Losses in a CDO are borne first by the equity securities, next by the junior securities, and finally by the senior securities, reflecting the tranches' seniority."}, {"question": "Which exercise style allows the holder to exercise the option on any specified date between purchase and expiry, but not at any time as with an American option?", "options": ["European", "American", "Bermudan", "Asian"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A Bermudan (mid-Atlantic/pseudo-American) option can be exercised on specified dates between purchase and expiry, sitting between the European (only at expiry) and American (any time) styles."}, {"question": "An Asian option is typically cheaper than a standard European option with the same strike and maturity because:", "options": ["It has a shorter time to expiration", "The volatility of an averaged rate is lower than the volatility of the individual rates used to calculate it", "It cannot be exercised", "It always settles at zero"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Asian (average rate) options pay off based on an averaged underlying price. Averaging reduces volatility relative to the underlying's own volatility, making these options cheaper than standard European options."}, {"question": "A knock-out barrier option that is cancelled if the underlying's price falls to or below a pre-determined level is known as:", "options": ["Up and out", "Down and in", "Down and out", "Up and in"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A down-and-out option is a knock-out option cancelled when the underlying's price falls to or below the barrier level."}, {"question": "Which option Greek measures the rate of change of delta with respect to the underlying asset's price, and is largest for near-the-money, short-dated options?", "options": ["Vega", "Theta", "Gamma", "Rho"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Gamma is the first derivative of delta with respect to the underlying's price. It is largest for near-the-money options and most extreme for short-dated, near-the-money options; it is small for deep ITM or OTM options."}, {"question": "What key assumption of the Black-Scholes model is challenged by the existence of the volatility smile, and which model is widely used to address this?", "options": ["Constant interest rates; the binomial model", "Constant volatility of the underlying; the SABR model", "No dividends; the CAPM model", "Continuous trading; the Monte Carlo model"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Black-Scholes assumes constant volatility, which does not hold in practice, giving rise to the volatility smile/skew. The SABR (Stochastic Alpha, Beta, Rho) model, based on correlated asset price and volatility, is widely used to address this, especially for interest rate derivatives."}, {"question": "What is the general market rule for when the buyer of an OTC option must pay the premium?", "options": ["Immediately, before the trade is agreed", "On the next business day, or T+2 at the latest for OTC FX options", "At option expiry only", "Only if the option is exercised"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The general rule is that the buyer pays the premium on the next business day, or at the latest T+2, which is the general rule for OTC FX options."}, {"question": "How do FLEX options differ from OTC options in terms of counterparty risk?", "options": ["FLEX options carry higher counterparty risk because they are unregulated", "FLEX options carry the same credit/counterparty risk as OTC options since both are customised", "FLEX options do not carry the same credit/counterparty risk as OTC options, since they are exchange-listed and benefit from clearing house protection", "FLEX options eliminate all market risk"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Although FLEX options offer OTC-style customisation (strike, expiry, exercise style), they are listed on an exchange and therefore benefit from clearing house protection, unlike standard OTC options."}, {"question": "A puttable bond embeds which type of option, and who may exercise it?", "options": ["A call option, exercisable by the issuer", "A put option, exercisable by the bondholder", "A put option, exercisable by the issuer", "A call option, exercisable by the bondholder"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A puttable bond has a put option embedded that is exercisable by the bondholder, allowing early redemption - useful if interest rates rise."}, {"question": "Under an ISDA Master Agreement, what is the key distinction between payment netting and close-out netting?", "options": ["Payment netting applies only after default; close-out netting applies in the ordinary course of business", "Payment netting nets amounts due in the same currency on the same day in the ordinary course of business; close-out netting nets all amounts due upon early termination by valuing all transactions in a single termination currency", "There is no distinction; the terms are interchangeable", "Payment netting applies to credit derivatives only; close-out netting applies to interest rate swaps only"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Payment netting nets same-currency, same-day amounts in normal business; close-out netting nets all amounts due on early termination by valuing all transactions and converting to a single termination currency - critical for protection against selective default."}, {"question": "In the OTC collateral process, what is the term for the reduced value a bank assigns to collateral to reflect the perceived risk of holding that specific asset?", "options": ["Threshold amount", "Minimum transfer amount", "Haircut", "Delivery amount"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A haircut is the reduction applied to collateral's market value to protect against potential adverse movements in its value before additional collateral can be transferred; its size relates to the minimum threshold amount and the collateral's price volatility."}, {"question": "SwapClear, launched by LCH Group in 1999, is best described as:", "options": ["A straight-through processing (STP) system that automatically confirms swaps between counterparties", "A clearing service in which LCH becomes the central counterparty to each half of an OTC interest rate swap, requiring the swap to already be confirmed before registration", "An electronic trade confirmation platform jointly owned by DTCC and IHS Markit", "A regulator responsible for supervising OTC derivatives in the UK"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "SwapClear provides the clearing house function for OTC interest rate swaps, with LCH becoming CCP to each side. It is not an STP system - the swap must already be confirmed and processed with the original counterparty before registration."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Why do OTC derivatives remain popular despite the rise of CCP clearing and trade reporting?", "back": "Because their contracts are not standardised and can be tailored precisely to the unique needs of the user (hedging, speculation, arbitrage), while also offering confidentiality."}, {"front": "Define a cap.", "back": "An OTC option that protects a floating-rate borrower: whenever the rollover reference rate exceeds the cap strike rate, the buyer receives the difference, accrued over the period; settled at period end like a swap."}, {"front": "Define a floor.", "back": "An OTC option enabling the buyer to secure a minimum rate of interest on a deposit/floating-rate investment; pays the buyer the difference whenever the rollover rate is below the floor strike."}, {"front": "What is a zero-cost collar?", "back": "A collar (cap + floor) constructed so that the premium paid for the cap and the premium received for selling the floor net to zero."}, {"front": "Define a swap and a swaption.", "back": "A swap exchanges cash flows over time based on a notional principal (not usually exchanged, payments netted). A swaption is an option to enter into a swap on pre-agreed terms in future."}, {"front": "List the five recognised credit events for a CDS.", "back": "Default; bankruptcy; debt restructuring; merger or demerger; certain government actions (eg, nationalisation)."}, {"front": "What are the three types of credit default swap (CDS)?", "back": "Single-name (basic) - based on one reference asset; basket - based on default within a portfolio; index - based on movements of a debt or equity/market index."}, {"front": "What is the reduced-form pricing approach for CDSs?", "back": "A pricing method that models the credit event process directly into the swap's price, based on the probability of default (PD), most commonly measured via the default swap (asset swap) spread."}, {"front": "What is a credit-linked note (CLN)?", "back": "A funded credit derivative - a security with an embedded CDS - that transfers specific credit risk to investors; the issuer need not repay if a specified credit event occurs; investors get a higher yield for the exposure."}, {"front": "What is a credit default option, and what does buying a payer option protect against?", "back": "An option to buy (payer) or sell (receiver) protection on a CDS, usually European-style. Buying a payer option protects against a rise in credit spread, NOT against actual default - single-credit options are extinguished on default without cash flows beyond the upfront premium."}, {"front": "Describe how losses are allocated in a CDO's tranche structure.", "back": "Losses are borne first by the equity tranche, then by the junior tranche(s), and finally by the senior tranche, which is paid first from the underlying cash flows."}, {"front": "How does a CBO differ from a CMO?", "back": "A collateralised bond obligation (CBO) represents different levels of credit risk from a pool of junk bonds (achieving investment-grade diversification); a collateralised mortgage obligation (CMO) differs by maturity structure, not credit risk levels."}, {"front": "List the three main option exercise styles and define each.", "back": "European - exercisable only at expiry. American - exercisable any time up to expiry. Bermudan (mid-Atlantic/pseudo-American) - exercisable on specified dates between purchase and expiry."}, {"front": "Distinguish an Asian option from a lookback option.", "back": "An Asian option pays off based on the AVERAGE price of the underlying over the option's life (cheaper, lower volatility). A lookback option lets the holder use the MOST FAVOURABLE price observed during the option's life as the strike (expensive, high writer risk)."}, {"front": "Define knock-in vs knock-out barrier options.", "back": "Knock-in options are not active until the underlying crosses the barrier price. Knock-out options are active until the underlying crosses the barrier price, at which point they cease to exist."}, {"front": "Name the five option Greeks and what each measures.", "back": "Delta - sensitivity to underlying price change (hedge ratio, 0 to 1). Gamma - rate of change of delta. Vega - sensitivity to 1% change in implied volatility. Theta - time decay. Rho - sensitivity to interest rate changes (usually smallest)."}, {"front": "What is the volatility smile and which model addresses it?", "back": "The pattern where implied volatility varies with strike price (deep ITM/OTM options show higher implied volatility than Black-Scholes predicts). The SABR model, based on correlated asset price and volatility, is widely used to address it, especially for interest rate derivatives."}, {"front": "What is the general premium payment timing rule for OTC options?", "back": "The buyer pays the premium on the next business day, or T+2 at the latest (the general rule for OTC FX options)."}, {"front": "What is the key legal/economic benefit of an ISDA Master Agreement?", "back": "It provides a single umbrella framework for all OTC deals between two parties, enabling close-out netting of all transactions on default, protecting the non-defaulting party against selective default, rather than closing out deals individually."}, {"front": "Distinguish payment netting from close-out netting.", "back": "Payment netting nets amounts due in the same currency on the same day in the ordinary course of business. Close-out netting nets all amounts due between parties on early termination, valuing all transactions and converting to a single termination currency."}, {"front": "What is a haircut in the context of OTC collateral?", "back": "A reduction applied to the market value of posted collateral, reflecting the bank's perceived risk of holding that asset, to protect against adverse value movements before further collateral can be transferred."}, {"front": "What is SwapClear and when was it launched?", "back": "Launched by LCH Group in 1999, SwapClear provides clearing for basic interest rate swaps, with LCH acting as central counterparty to each side. It is not an STP system - swaps must be confirmed with the original counterparty before registration."}]}, {"id": "der-ch6", "number": 6, "title": "Principles of Clearing and Margin", "examWeight": "14 of 100 questions (~14%)", "summaryHtml": "\n<p>Chapter 6 is one of the heaviest-tested areas of the syllabus (~14 questions), covering how clearing houses (CCPs) stand behind every exchange-traded contract, and the margin mechanics that protect the system. <strong>Clearing</strong> is the process by which trades are confirmed, registered, and become the legal responsibility of a clearing house through <strong>novation</strong> — the clearing house is substituted as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, so the original contract is replaced by two new contracts. This converts bilateral counterparty risk into exposure only to the clearing house itself. Clearing houses use a <strong>mutual offset system</strong> (e.g. CME–SGX) to let trades on one exchange be booked/cleared on another, and operate a <strong>principal-to-principal guarantee</strong> that only extends to clearing members, not to underlying clients.</p>\n<p>The clearing tier structure runs: Clearing House → Clearing Member (General or Individual) → Non-Clearing Member. A non-clearing firm (e.g. a broker) must use a General Clearing Member (GCM) to register trades. If a member defaults, losses are met via the <strong>default waterfall</strong>, in strict order: (1) the defaulter's own margin, (2) the defaulter's own default fund contribution, (3) other members' default fund contributions (the \"mutual guarantee\" feature), (4) insurance. The 2018 Nasdaq Clearing power-spread default (a ~$122m shortfall) is the key real-world example.</p>\n<p><strong>Prime brokers</strong> offer execution, clearing/settlement, custody, financing, stock lending, reporting and value-added services such as capital introduction. Certain OTC products (interest rate swaps via LCH Group/CME Clearing360, OTC block trades via EurexOTC Clear) can be centrally cleared, substituting an exchange-equivalent contract and applying standard margining. <strong>Price limits/circuit breakers</strong> halt trading temporarily when prices move beyond set bounds (e.g. NYSE March 2020 S&P 500 halt).</p>\n<p><strong>Margin</strong> is the core numerical topic. <strong>Initial margin</strong> is a good-faith deposit covering the worst one-day loss, set using systems such as <strong>SPAN</strong> (CME-developed, 16 scenarios, scanning range + volatility shifts, used across futures/options exchanges), <strong>TIMS</strong> (OCC — premium margin + risk margin) and <strong>STANS</strong> (OCC's post-2006 system, 10,000 simulated scenarios across 7,000+ risk factors). Spread positions attract reduced margin due to offsetting risk; spot month margin increases initial margin as delivery nears; intra-day margin can be called if volatility spikes mid-session. <strong>Variation margin</strong> is the daily cash settlement of mark-to-market gains/losses, calculated as ticks moved × tick value × number of contracts, paid/received via PPS (ICE Clear Europe) or BAMS (CME). <strong>Maintenance margin</strong> (a broker-client, not CCP, mechanism) is set 25–50% below initial margin; once equity breaches it, a margin call restores the account to the initial level.</p>\n<p><strong>Collateral</strong> for margin can be cash or acceptable non-cash assets (government bonds, bank guarantees, CDs), subject to daily mark-to-market and haircuts. In OTC markets, <strong>collateral management</strong> uses a <strong>Credit Support Annex (CSA)</strong> (part of the ISDA Master Agreement) setting threshold amounts, minimum transfer amounts and haircuts; CSAs may be two-way (equal obligation) or one-way (only one party posts, typical for sovereigns/SSAs). Post-2008, the <strong>Uncleared Margin Rules (UMR)</strong>, from BCBS-IOSCO, require initial and variation margin exchange on non-centrally-cleared OTC derivatives once a firm's Aggregate Average Notional Amount (AANA) exceeds set thresholds, phased in across six phases (2017–2022), with a €50 million initial margin collection threshold and standardised methodologies (Grid or SIMM).</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "6.1 Clearing and Risk", "html": "\n<p><strong>Clearing</strong> is the process by which derivatives trades are confirmed and registered with a clearing house, which becomes the legal counterparty to every transaction. The legal mechanism is <strong>novation</strong>: the clearing house substitutes itself as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer. The original contract ceases to exist and is replaced by two new contracts.</p>\n<p>Clearing members must maintain an account in the clearing house's currency, minimising currency risk (any residual FX risk arises only from exchange-rate movements affecting net daily margin payments between the member's home currency and the exchange's currency).</p>\n<p>The <strong>central counterparty (CCP)</strong> structure removes almost all counterparty (credit) risk between clearing members — the only counterparty risk a clearing member faces is with the clearing house itself. The clearing house also monitors open positions and facilitates settlement (close-out/delivery of futures, exercise/assignment of options). In a rare default, the clearing house is exposed to only one day of market risk because of daily margining.</p>\n<p>If margin payments are insufficient to cover a defaulting member's loss, the clearing house draws on its <strong>default fund</strong> — cash contributed by all members, sized using stress tests against worst-case volatility estimates. This fund only covers defaults by members directly to the clearing house.</p>\n<p>Advantages of central clearing: (1) greatly reduced counterparty risk, provided the CCP itself is highly creditworthy; (2) ease of closing out a position — since both sides of a trade are novated to the CCP, an equal and opposite trade simply cancels the original exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Settlement risk</strong> (the risk an expected payment of cash/securities is not made on time or at all) is another risk of the clearing/delivery process; netting agreements are the main mitigant.</p>\n<p><strong>Brexit note:</strong> UK CCPs/clearing firms lost EU passporting rights from 31 December 2020, becoming \"third-country\" participants. LCH's euro interest rate swap clearing (>90% of the market) secured a temporary ESMA/Bank of England mitigation to continue serving EU members until 30 June 2028, via bilateral agreement rather than passporting.</p>\n<h4>6.1.1 Mutual Offset</h4>\n<p>A <strong>mutual offset system</strong> is an agreement between two exchanges allowing a trade executed on one to be booked and cleared through the other. The classic example is <strong>CME Group and the Singapore Exchange (SGX)</strong>, covering contracts including eurodollar, Nikkei 225 and Japanese government bond (JGB) futures, plus (in an expanded partnership) yen- and USD-denominated Nikkei 225 futures, E-mini FTSE China H50 and E-mini FTSE Emerging Index contracts. CME also has a wider network of settlement-price licensing and cross-listing agreements (e.g. Bursa Malaysia, Dubai Mercantile Exchange, Japan Exchange Group, JSE South Africa, Korea Exchange, MexDer, Minneapolis Grain Exchange, MCX Mumbai).</p>\n<p>Under a <strong>principal-to-principal</strong> system (e.g. ICE Clear Europe), the clearing house guarantees performance only to its immediate counterparty — the clearing member — not to that member's underlying clients or to non-clearing exchange members.</p>\n<p><strong>EMIR</strong> (European Market Infrastructure Regulation) is the EU's regulatory framework extending formal central clearing and trade-reporting requirements to OTC derivatives, aiming to reduce counterparty credit risk.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.2 The Structure of the Clearing System", "html": "\n<p>The clearing system has three tiers: <strong>Clearing House → Clearing Member</strong> (either an <strong>Individual Clearing Member</strong>, clearing only for itself, or a <strong>General Clearing Member (GCM)</strong>, clearing for itself and other firms) <strong>→ Non-Clearing Member</strong>. A non-clearing member must use a GCM to register trades with the clearing house.</p>\n<p>Clearing house ownership models vary: the OCC (US) is jointly owned by several exchanges; CME's clearing house is a division of the exchange itself; ICE Clear Europe's parent is Intercontinental Exchange Holdings.</p>\n<h4>Worked example — the clearing process (ICE Futures / ICE Clear Europe)</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 – The trade:</strong> Client X, a client of ABC Ltd (a member firm of ICE Futures but not of ICE Clear Europe), places a market order executed by ABC Ltd on ICE Futures. ABC Ltd has an agreement with KMM Ltd, a General Clearing Member of both the exchange and ICE Clear Europe.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 – Confirmation and matching:</strong> ABC Ltd passes trade details to KMM Ltd, which inputs them into the matching system (UCP), waiting for the counterparty's equal and opposite entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 – Registration:</strong> once matched, KMM Ltd registers the trade with ICE Clear Europe via UCP, specifying segregated client account or non-segregated house account.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 – Novation:</strong> at registration, the contract is novated — ICE Clear Europe becomes counterparty to two new contracts, one with KMM Ltd and one with ABC Ltd's original counterparty's clearing member. The original contract ceases to exist. ABC Ltd (non-clearing) has committed KMM Ltd (its clearing member) to a transaction with ICE Clear Europe — a <strong>principal-to-principal guarantee</strong> that runs only between ICE Clear Europe and KMM Ltd.</p>\n<p>Margin calls flow: ICE Clear Europe calls KMM Ltd via the <strong>Assured Payments Service (APS)</strong> (an automatic bank-debit system); KMM Ltd collects from ABC Ltd; ABC Ltd collects from Client X. Each link in the chain is a separate principal-to-principal relationship. When Client X closes the position, ABC Ltd need not go back to the original counterparty — the standardised offsetting trade simply cancels the position already registered with the clearing house.</p>\n<p>Crucially, <strong>the clearing house gives no guarantee to Client X</strong>. Client X/ABC Ltd and ABC Ltd/KMM Ltd relationships still carry counterparty (default) risk — the system substantially reduces, but does not eliminate, risk across the whole chain.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.3 Clearing House Guarantee", "html": "\n<p>Clearing houses operate a <strong>principal-to-principal guarantee</strong> under a <strong>mutual guarantee structure</strong>. Using ICE Clear Europe as an example of the financial resources supporting the guarantee:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clearing members must hold accounts with approved banks (<strong>Assured Payment System (APS) banks</strong>) used to collect margin/collateral.</li>\n<li>Initial margin is collected as cash (prime currencies) and/or acceptable non-cash collateral.</li>\n<li>On a member default, the clearing house first uses that member's own margin and collateral.</li>\n<li>Next in line is the <strong>member default fund</strong> — a pool funded by cash contributions (interest-bearing) from every clearing member, sized to their volume of clearing activity, and reviewed roughly every three months. Because a swaps-clearing default is also supported by futures/equity clearing members' contributions, this is a <em>mutual</em> guarantee system (an independent guarantee system would have no shared default fund).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The default waterfall</h4>\n<p>Losses on a member default are met, strictly in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The defaulting member's own margin held by the clearing house.</li>\n<li>The defaulting member's own default fund contribution.</li>\n<li>The default fund contributions of other (non-defaulting) clearing members.</li>\n<li>The clearing house's insurance policy.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Worked example — Nasdaq Clearing default, September 2018</h4>\n<p>The Nordic/German power spread market saw an extreme spread movement. A Nasdaq Clearing member's portfolio, holding a large Nordic vs German power spread position, could not meet an intraday margin call. Nasdaq declared the member in default on 11 September 2018 and closed out the portfolio via auction within 48 hours per its close-out rules. The close-out loss exceeded the defaulting member's collateral and default fund contribution — a shortfall of other members' default fund contributions totalling <strong>$122 million</strong> was used to cover the loss, illustrating step 3 of the waterfall in practice. Nasdaq subsequently raised margin requirements on spread positions.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.4 Prime Broker Services", "html": "\n<p>A prime broker's core responsibilities in relation to derivatives include: efficient/best execution; ensuring trade confirmation documentation; settling trade cash flows (financing or securities lending as agreed); providing custody/safekeeping and risk management systems; keeping clients informed of issuer correspondence (AGMs, corporate actions).</p>\n<p>Typical prime broker services: clearing and settlement in global markets; central custodianship with consolidated positions supporting leverage; multi-currency financing; stock borrowing to support strategies; integrated web reporting; hedge fund consulting; acting as counterparty/broker for OTC derivatives; facilitating sales/trading/research communication.</p>\n<p>Value-added services may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Capital introduction</strong> — introducing the hedge fund client to qualified investors interested in new hedge fund investment opportunities.</li>\n<li><strong>Office space leasing/servicing</strong> — subleasing commercial space to hedge fund tenants with on-site services.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk management advisory</strong> — risk analysis technology, sometimes with senior risk consultants.</li>\n<li><strong>Consulting services</strong> — regulatory/establishment advice for start-up hedge funds in both the manager's and the fund's jurisdictions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prime broker regulation is jurisdiction-specific: in the UK, the FCA (and MiFID II if operating in the EU); in the US, the Federal Reserve, SEC and CFTC; in China, previously split across the PBOC, CSRC and CBIRC, moving (from March 2023) toward consolidation under the new National Financial Regulatory Administration (NAFR).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.5 Exchange-Cleared OTC Products", "html": "\n<p>Rising concern over counterparty risk, plus exchanges' desire to diversify, has led exchanges to offer clearing for OTC derivatives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>LCH Group</strong> (LSE) clears a wide range of equity, commodity and financial futures/options, including major European and some US indices, individual equities, and bond/short-term interest products; contracts typically carry a minimum trade size.</li>\n<li><strong>CME Group's Clearing360</strong> clears OTC interest rate derivatives and FX option block trades for firms acting as principal or agent (hedge funds, major banks, prime brokers, proprietary traders) — it <strong>substitutes</strong> OTC FRAs and interest rate swaps with corresponding futures contracts, rather than simply clearing the OTC trade as-is.</li>\n<li><strong>Eurex's EurexOTC Clear</strong> clears OTC block trades, EFP, EFS and Vola trades (bilateral volatility trades combining OTC options with off-exchange futures purchase/sale).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main advantage of exchange-clearing an OTC product is the elimination of counterparty risk, especially valuable for longer-dated contracts; once cleared, the trade is subject to the same margin and delivery procedures as any exchange-traded product. Unlike exchange-traded contracts, the clearing house does not match buyers to sellers for these products, but it does monitor and guarantee delivery on both sides.</p>\n<h4>Clearing funds</h4>\n<p>A <strong>clearing fund</strong> (sometimes \"guarantee fund\") is a pool of assets contributed by clearing members (or guarantee providers) that can meet a defaulting participant's obligations and, in some cases, cover resulting losses/liquidity pressures — insurance against price movements not covered by margin. Contribution amounts are risk-based (linked to each member's uncovered risk from trading activity) and reviewed regularly — <strong>LCH Group recalculates monthly</strong>; other exchanges use quarterly review. The clearing fund is drawn on only after margin and related funds are exhausted. Accepted collateral: cash in major liquid currencies (EUR, USD, GBP, CHF, JPY, AUD, SEK, DKK, NOK) or high-quality government bonds (marked to market, haircuts applied).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.6 Price and Position Limits", "html": "\n<h4>6.6.1 Price Limits</h4>\n<p><strong>Price limits (circuit breakers)</strong> are exchange-imposed maximum bounds on a contract's absolute daily price movement. Breaching the limit (limit up/limit down) triggers a trading halt of a few minutes, giving participants time to reassess and enter new orders, which should (on average, not always) help stabilise the market.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example — March 2020 COVID crash:</strong> on 9 March 2020, the NYSE's S&P 500 index fell 7% just three minutes after the 9.30am ET open, triggering a 15-minute halt — the first US circuit-breaker trigger since 27 October 1997, and only the second since the mechanism's 1988 inception. Despite the halt, Halliburton (NYSE) still hit two further downward price limits that day, closing down 37%, showing circuit breakers slow but do not prevent severe declines.</p>\n<p>Practices vary by exchange/contract: ICE has no price limit on long gilt futures but applies a ¥2 (from TSE close) limit on JGB futures. CME Group applies scaled down-price limits on equity index futures (5% up to 20% down, each tier requiring a longer pause) plus a 5% \"either direction\" limit on CME Globex equity contracts. NCDEX uses scaled limits from ±4% (gold futures) to ±6% (several agri/soft contracts); after a 15-minute cooling-off period the limit is raised by 50% (e.g. ±6% becomes ±9%); a second breach ends trading for the day. Electronic trading systems separately reject orders entered outside an allowed spread around the prevailing market price, reducing fat-finger error risk.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.7 Protecting the Clearing House and Margining Systems (SPAN, TIMS, STANS)", "html": "\n<p>To protect itself, a clearing house takes several defensive steps: (1) <strong>quality of membership</strong> — only firms meeting ongoing membership/financial criteria are admitted (the first line of defence); (2) <strong>financial resource requirements</strong> — it deals only with clearing members holding onerous financial resources; (3) the <strong>margining system</strong> — initial and variation margin deposited to cover default risk; (4) its own resources — lines of credit with major banks, insurance policies, and often a default fund.</p>\n<h4>Standard Portfolio Analysis of Risk (SPAN)</h4>\n<p><strong>SPAN</strong> calculates daily margin requirements and is used by most leading futures/options exchanges. It measures a portfolio's risk as a whole (not position-by-position), combining futures and options, and allows for inter-maturity and inter-commodity spreads. Originally developed by CME Group; adapted by LCH as \"London SPAN\" (previously used by NYSE Liffe); licensed to many other clearing houses. First adopted by the CBOT.</p>\n<p>SPAN estimates liquidation value using daily-updated scenarios reflecting: possible underlying price changes; changes in underlying price volatility; and time decay's impact on option value. SPAN applies <strong>16 risk scenarios</strong> per portfolio, using a <strong>scanning range</strong> (e.g. a 300-point FTSE 100 move up/down) and a volatility shift (e.g. ±30%), then: adds a charge for inter-month spread risk (imperfect correlation between delivery months); gives an inter-commodity spread credit where correlated instruments offset; adds a spot-month delivery risk charge; and selects the largest resulting negative number as the initial margin requirement. For options, SPAN uses <strong>deltas</strong> to convert option positions into futures-equivalents for offset purposes. House and client accounts are margined separately.</p>\n<h4>TIMS and STANS (OCC)</h4>\n<p><strong>Theoretical Intermarket Margining System (TIMS)</strong>, launched 1986, is a portfolio-based methodology well suited to mixed portfolios (physicals, futures and options). It uses an option pricing model, calculating two components per position: <strong>premium margin</strong> (market value/daily price movement at close) plus <strong>risk margin</strong> (potential value change under the maximum probable intraday move, using implied volatility). Total margin = premium margin + risk margin.</p>\n<p>In 2006 the OCC introduced <strong>STANS (System for Theoretical Analysis and Numerical Simulations)</strong>, a more sophisticated, statistically-driven system generating <strong>10,000 hypothetical market scenarios</strong> across more than <strong>7,000 risk factors</strong> (equities, ETFs, indices, currencies, commodities). The OCC still uses TIMS for some risk functions but relies on STANS for clearing member margin requirements, citing more precise, realistic and consistent results.</p>\n<h4>Net Liquidation Value (NLV) and Spreads</h4>\n<p>Because margin is based on net positions, total margin due/receivable uses the <strong>net liquidation value (NLV)</strong> of a portfolio's constituent parts — profits on some positions offset losses on others, producing one net payment. <strong>Spreads</strong> (e.g. long June FTSE 100 future / short September FTSE 100 future) attract substantially lower initial margin since the two legs largely offset and correlate. <strong>Inter-commodity spreads</strong> exist between correlated instruments (e.g. ICE Gas Oil vs ICE Brent Crude). The CBOT offsets spread margin by $25–$110 per contract depending on the pair.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.8 Types of Margin — Initial Margin", "html": "\n<p><strong>Initial margin</strong> is a good-faith deposit (cash or acceptable collateral) lodged with the clearing house against potential liabilities on an open position, returned when the position is closed. It is calculated by the clearing house for members' positions (using its adopted system, e.g. SPAN); the clearing member may in turn charge clients a higher rate, known as <strong>broker margin</strong>.</p>\n<p>No initial margin is collected on the <strong>purchase</strong> of an option — instead the premium is collected, since buying an option is not a contingent-liability transaction (the buyer's maximum loss is the premium paid).</p>\n<p>Initial margin protects against the worst-case one-day loss and is set by reference to the contract's recent price volatility, determined by the clearing house in consultation with the exchange.</p>\n<h4>Worked example — setting the initial margin rate</h4>\n<p>ICE Clear Europe continually monitors market prices and calculates that a FTSE futures contract could move at most 300 points in one day. Given the tick size and tick value, this represents a potential loss of <strong>£3,000 per contract</strong> on the day for either a long or short open position. The initial margin rate is therefore set at <strong>£3,000 per contract</strong> for both buyer and seller. If volatility changes, the initial margin requirement is revised.</p>\n<p>Initial margin is recomputed and called every business day, first thing before markets open. If volatility spikes intraday and the clearing house judges initial margin held to be insufficient/exhausted, it can call <strong>intra-day margin</strong> immediately via clearing members' PPS accounts.</p>\n<p>Initial margin can be cash or acceptable non-cash collateral (larger clearing houses such as ICE Clear Europe, the OCC and CME Group accept a wider list than smaller ones).</p>\n<p>As a contract nears expiry, volatility can rise; the clearing house may raise initial margin via a <strong><em>spot month margin</em></strong> (spot month charge) to ensure only those genuinely intending physical delivery (with the asset or funds ready) maintain positions, forcing out less well-capitalised speculators and reducing delivery-month speculative pressure.</p>\n<h4>Practice calculation — initial margin</h4>\n<p>A clearing house calculates that a Brent Crude futures contract (1,000 barrels/contract) could move by a maximum of $4 per barrel in one day. What is the initial margin per contract? <em>Answer: $4 × 1,000 barrels = $4,000 per contract</em>, applied to both long and short open positions.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.9 Types of Margin — Variation Margin", "html": "\n<p>Exchanges set a daily <strong>settlement price</strong> (usually the closing price, though not for 24-hour/global-influence markets like the LME) to value positions and calculate <strong>mark-to-market (MTM) variation margin</strong>. The <strong>closing range</strong> is the range/spread of quoted prices just before trading ends; criteria vary by exchange and are typically longer for commodities than financials.</p>\n<p><strong>LCH Group's settlement price method:</strong> a specified time (e.g. close of trading) with the two minutes before it forming the \"settlement range\"; the first 90 seconds allow market supervision to monitor spread levels; the daily settlement price is normally the weighted average traded price during the final 30 seconds; market supervision may adjust the price if it is not a fair reflection of the market.</p>\n<p>At each day's close, all positions are marked to market against the settlement price. A loss means the clearing member must pay variation margin to the clearing house by the next day; a profit means the clearing house pays the member on the same timeline. The identical process cascades from clearing member to client.</p>\n<p><strong>Variation margin formula:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Variation margin = ticks moved on the day × tick value × number of contracts</strong><br/>where ticks moved on the day = today's closing price − yesterday's closing price.</p>\n<p>Variation margin is paid in cash (cleared funds) in the contract currency. ICE Clear Europe uses the <strong>Protected Payments System (PPS)</strong> (direct debit from members' PPS bank accounts); CME Clearing uses the <strong>Banking &amp; Asset Management System (BAMS)</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Worked example — Exercise 1 (short futures position)</h4>\n<p>A trader goes short five FTSE 100 futures at 7,140. At the end of the day the settlement price is 7,175. Using the standard ICE FTSE 100 future contract value of £10 per index point:</p>\n<p>Ticks moved = 7,175 − 7,140 = 35 points (price rose). Variation margin = 35 × £10 × 5 contracts = <strong>£1,750</strong>. Because the trader is <em>short</em> and the price rose, this is a <strong>loss</strong> — the trader must pay £1,750 variation margin.</p>\n<h4>Worked example — Exercise 2 (long futures position)</h4>\n<p>A trader is long 20 LME three-month tin futures at $45,300. At the end of the day the settlement price is $45,330. LME tin contracts have a lot size of 5 tonnes:</p>\n<p>Price moved = $45,330 − $45,300 = $30 per tonne (price rose). Variation margin = $30 × 5 tonnes × 20 contracts = <strong>$3,000</strong>. Because the trader is <em>long</em> and the price rose, this is a <strong>gain</strong> — the trader receives $3,000 variation margin.</p>\n<h4>Practice calculation — additional example</h4>\n<p>A trader is long 10 FTSE 100 futures at 7,500. The settlement price at day-end is 7,462. Using £10 per point: ticks moved = 7,462 − 7,500 = −38 points. Variation margin = 38 × £10 × 10 = <strong>£3,800</strong>. As the trader is long and the price fell, this is a loss — the trader must pay £3,800.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.10 Types of Margin — Maintenance Margin", "html": "\n<p><strong>Maintenance margin</strong> is a broker-client arrangement (not operated by the clearing house itself). A member firm typically requires a client to deposit more than the initial margin as a safety cushion, allowing some variation margin to be absorbed without a daily call back to the client. Once account equity breaches the pre-set maintenance level, the member issues a <strong>margin call (trigger margin)</strong>, requiring the client to top the account back up to the <em>original (initial margin)</em> amount. This practice is common in the US. Maintenance margin is typically set at <strong>25–50% below</strong> the initial margin on new positions.</p>\n<h4>Worked example — maintenance margin</h4>\n<p>The EUR/USD future has a maintenance margin of $4,000 and an initial margin of $5,400 (an uplift on maintenance of 1.35). To enter the position, $5,400 is required, but the position can lose up to <strong>$1,400</strong> ($5,400 − $4,000) before the client must add funds or the position is closed.</p>\n<p>Note: US FCMs are legally required to charge margin per clearing house requirements on client positions. A UK broker holding positions for a UK client on a US exchange is not necessarily obliged to collect margin from that client (if agreed credit lines are in place), but must still record the margin requirement on the client's account and pay the requirement to the US broker holding the position.</p>\n<h4>Practice calculation — maintenance margin</h4>\n<p>A gold futures contract has an initial margin of $6,000 and a maintenance margin set 30% below initial margin. What is the maintenance margin, and how much can the position lose before a margin call? <em>Maintenance margin = $6,000 × (1 − 0.30) = $4,200. The position can lose $6,000 − $4,200 = $1,800 before a margin call is triggered.</em></p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.11 Acceptable Collateral", "html": "\n<p><strong>Collateral</strong> is an asset pledged as a guarantee/security for a credit line or loan, reducing the credit risk of the party extending the credit. Cover for initial margin can be provided as collateral instead of cash. Assets typically accepted by major clearing houses include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cash in major currencies (sterling, euros, US dollars, Swiss francs, Swedish kronor, Danish kroner, Norwegian kroner, Japanese yen) — most national exchanges accept local-currency cash at approved banks.</li>\n<li>Bank guarantees from an approved bank in an approved form.</li>\n<li>Certificates of deposit denominated in GBP or USD.</li>\n<li>Government debt (bills, bonds, notes) from major developed markets (UK, Germany, Japan, Sweden, US, France, Canada, Australia, Singapore).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Key restrictions: <strong>undated bonds are not acceptable</strong>; bonds must be denominated in the currency of the issuing country; collateral is marked to market daily and subject to a published <strong>haircut</strong> (so full market value is not credited) — haircuts became more prevalent after several government bond downgrades; collateral securities must be lodged with depositories/custodians acceptable to the specific clearing house.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.12 Credit Lines", "html": "\n<p>Although uncommon, some exchanges allow member firms to extend <strong>credit lines</strong> to clients to cover margin requirements; deals exceeding the specified credit line are rejected by the exchange member firm. Credit lines are subject to regulatory rules — for example, under FCA rules, credit may be extended to a customer for up to <strong>five days</strong> without a formal written loan agreement. Credit lines used to finance derivatives trading are <strong>not permitted</strong> by the ICE markets for their financial products.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.13 Collateral Management", "html": "\n<p><strong>Collateral management</strong> is the process allowing market participants to reduce counterparty credit exposure arising from longer-maturity OTC derivative contracts (swaps, options), operating similarly to the daily margin process used by exchange clearing houses. Beyond reducing counterparty risk, it can improve capital usage, increase the number of transactions possible with a given counterparty, and reduce dealing spreads.</p>\n<h4>Worked example — MTM exposure and the need for collateral</h4>\n<p>Company A and Company B enter a ten-year commodity swap: Company A delivers a set amount of wheat, Company B pays a set price. As the market price of wheat falls, Company A shows an MTM profit (a loss for Company B), increasing Company A's credit risk exposure to Company B. Company A can reduce this exposure via a <strong>Credit Support Annex (CSA)</strong>.</p>\n<h4>6.13.1 Credit Support Annex (CSA)</h4>\n<p>A CSA is a legal document — <strong>one of the four parts of an ISDA Master Agreement</strong> — that regulates and defines the criteria for collateral payments (credit support) on OTC derivative transactions, defining timing (how often), minimum transfer amounts and acceptable collateral types. All CSAs require an ISDA agreement to be in place. Main aim: mitigate credit risk arising from OTM/MTM losses on a derivatives position.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Company A enters a ten-year interest rate swap as fixed-rate payer with a bank. As market rates fall (favouring the bank), the MTM profit for the bank represents a loss for Company A, increasing the bank's risk exposure to Company A. A CSA sets out how collateralisation addresses this.</p>\n<p>Key CSA terms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Threshold amount</strong> — the unsecured credit exposure a counterparty is prepared to accept before requesting collateral, usually set low to reduce credit risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum transfer amount</strong> — the smallest permitted collateral transfer, preventing frequent, costly small transfers.</li>\n<li><strong>Valuation percentage/haircut</strong> — the discount applied to collateral market value, protecting the receiver against value falls between collateral calls; higher-quality collateral attracts a lower haircut.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once a new valuation is agreed, the party with the negative MTM transfers the change in value to the party with the positive MTM. CSAs may be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two-way</strong> — both sides may be required to post collateral; used when counterparties are of similar size/credit rating. Most bank-to-bank/bank-to-client CSAs are two-way.</li>\n<li><strong>One-way</strong> — only one counterparty posts collateral when the trade moves in the other's favour; the other side never reciprocates. Typical for sovereign, supranational and agency (SSA) counterparties, which traditionally do not post collateral and often have exemptions from mandatory clearing given their credit rating/sovereign status.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One-way CSAs create a problem for a bank/dealer that hedges a one-way trade with another bank/dealer or via a clearing house: it receives no collateral when the original (one-way) trade is in its favour, yet must post collateral on the offsetting hedge. EU sovereign credit problems in the early 2010s (Greece, Italy, Portugal) made dealers reluctant to enter long-term one-way-CSA deals with these counterparties, raising their funding costs. Post-crisis, the shift toward centrally cleared vanilla OTC trades has reduced reliance on CSAs generally (the CCP sitting between both parties largely removes the need for one).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.14 Uncleared Margin Rules (UMR)", "html": "\n<p>Following the 2007–08 global financial crisis, the G20 mandated the <strong>Basel Committee on Banking Supervision (BCBS)</strong> to develop consistent standards reducing systemic risk from derivatives, addressing counterparty credit/default risk in previously under-regulated areas of the OTC market. Non-standardised, niche OTC products that could not be centrally cleared became known as <strong>non-centrally cleared (\"uncleared\") OTC derivatives</strong> — the primary area of concern given minimal transparency and no binding risk-mitigation framework.</p>\n<p>In September 2013, BCBS released a global policy framework — the <strong>Uncleared Margin Rules (UMR)</strong> — introducing margining requirements for uncleared OTC derivatives, to bring this segment closer in line with cleared derivatives using margin/collateral to reduce credit and default risk.</p>\n<p>UMR comprises <strong>eight key principles</strong> with sub-requirements, implemented by national regulators across <strong>six phases</strong>, from September 2017 to the final Phase Six in September 2022, progressively widening the population of covered participants. Key principles include (as set out by BCBS):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Margin applies to all non-centrally-cleared derivatives.</strong> Initial margin is excluded for FX forwards/swaps (though these still count towards the AANA calculation); firms below the pre-set AANA threshold are exempt from initial margin (threshold phased in); IM rates are pre-determined by BCBS-IOSCO.</li>\n<li><strong>All covered entities must exchange variation and initial margin.</strong> Daily variation margin exchange is mandatory with no threshold. Initial margin (calculated on a consolidated group basis, by jurisdiction) need not be posted if the total IM due is below <strong>€50 million</strong> — above that, IM must be collected. The same thresholds apply to non-financial firms regardless of whether uncleared derivatives are used for hedging, speculation or arbitrage.</li>\n<li><strong>Margin must use a regulatory-approved methodology.</strong> Initial margin uses the Grid or the <strong>Standardised Initial Margin Schedule (SIMM)</strong>, or another BCBS-compliant method; variation margin uses daily mark-to-market.</li>\n<li><strong>Collateral must be of high quality</strong> (specific rules devolved to national regulators).</li>\n<li><strong>Segregation and custodianship of initial margin</strong> (specific rules devolved to national regulators).</li>\n<li><strong>Margining between affiliated firms within the same group is not mandatory.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: the <strong>Aggregate Average Notional Amount (AANA)</strong> is the key metric used to determine whether a firm/group falls within scope of mandatory initial margin exchange under UMR — firms whose AANA of non-centrally-cleared derivatives exceeds the applicable phase threshold become subject to the rules; the phased implementation progressively lowered this threshold, bringing more (typically smaller) market participants into scope by the final phase.</p>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the legal process by which a clearing house becomes the counterparty to both sides of an exchange trade?", "options": ["Assignment", "Novation", "Subrogation", "Delegation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Novation substitutes the clearing house as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer; the original contract ceases to exist and is replaced by two new contracts."}, {"question": "After novation, what counterparty risk remains for a clearing member?", "options": ["Risk to the original trade counterparty only", "Risk to the clearing house only", "Risk to both the original counterparty and the clearing house", "No counterparty risk remains at all"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Once novated, the clearing member's only counterparty exposure is to the clearing house (CCP) itself, not to other market participants."}, {"question": "A mutual offset system, such as that operated by CME Group and the Singapore Exchange (SGX), allows for which of the following?", "options": ["Clients to bypass clearing members entirely", "Trades executed on one exchange to be booked and cleared through another exchange", "Clearing houses to waive initial margin on offsetting trades", "Non-clearing members to guarantee each other's trades"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A mutual offset system is an agreement between two exchanges allowing trades executed on one to be booked and cleared on the other, e.g. eurodollar, Nikkei 225 and JGB contracts between CME and SGX."}, {"question": "Under a principal-to-principal guarantee, such as that operated by ICE Clear Europe, to whom does the clearing house extend its performance guarantee?", "options": ["The end client only", "Non-clearing exchange member firms only", "Its immediate counterparty, the clearing member", "All parties in the trade chain equally"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The clearing house's guarantee runs only to its immediate counterparty (the clearing member), not to that member's underlying clients or non-clearing firms."}, {"question": "In the clearing tier structure, which entity must a non-clearing member use in order to register a trade with the clearing house?", "options": ["An individual clearing member only", "A general clearing member (GCM)", "The exchange's market supervision department", "Any FCA-authorised firm"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A non-clearing member has no direct relationship with the clearing house and must use a general clearing member to clear and register its trades."}, {"question": "What is the correct order of the default waterfall when a clearing member defaults?", "options": ["Insurance policy; other members' default fund; defaulter's default fund; defaulter's margin", "Defaulter's margin; defaulter's default fund contribution; other members' default fund contributions; insurance policy", "Other members' default fund; defaulter's margin; insurance; defaulter's default fund", "Defaulter's default fund; defaulter's margin; insurance; other members' default fund"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The waterfall draws first on the defaulter's own margin, then its own default fund contribution, then other members' default fund contributions, and finally the clearing house's insurance policy."}, {"question": "In the September 2018 Nasdaq Clearing default involving Nordic/German power spread positions, how was the shortfall (after the defaulter's own resources were exhausted) ultimately covered?", "options": ["A central bank bailout", "Other clearing members' default fund contributions, totalling around $122 million", "An increase in exchange listing fees", "The exchange's own retained earnings alone"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The close-out loss exceeded the defaulting member's collateral and default fund contribution; the roughly $122 million shortfall was absorbed by other members' default fund contributions, illustrating the mutual guarantee system."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a value-added prime broker service involving introducing a hedge fund client to potential investors?", "options": ["Capital introduction", "Corporate actions processing", "Securities lending", "Trade affirmation"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Capital introduction is the process whereby a prime broker introduces its hedge fund clients to qualified investors interested in new hedge fund investment opportunities."}, {"question": "How does CME Group's Clearing360 facility handle OTC interest rate swaps and FRAs?", "options": ["It leaves them as bilateral OTC contracts but applies exchange margin", "It substitutes them with corresponding exchange-traded futures contracts", "It requires them to be settled only in USD", "It refuses to clear them under any circumstances"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Rather than simply clearing the OTC trade, Clearing360 substitutes OTC FRAs and interest rate swaps with corresponding futures contracts."}, {"question": "How frequently does LCH Group recalculate each clearing member's clearing fund contribution?", "options": ["Daily", "Monthly", "Quarterly", "Annually"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "LCH Group recalculates each clearing member's clearing fund payment on a monthly basis; some other exchanges review quarterly."}, {"question": "What is the main purpose of exchange price limits (circuit breakers)?", "options": ["To guarantee a minimum return to investors", "To halt trading temporarily when prices move beyond set bounds, allowing participants time to reassess", "To eliminate all market volatility permanently", "To set the daily settlement price"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Price limits halt trading for a short period when the market moves beyond a set bound, giving participants time to take a more reasoned view of trading conditions."}, {"question": "On 9 March 2020, what triggered a 15-minute trading halt on the NYSE?", "options": ["The S&P 500 index fell 7% within minutes of the open", "A cyberattack on the exchange's systems", "A single stock breaching its position limit", "A failure of the settlement system"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The S&P 500 declined 7% about three minutes after the market open, triggering the circuit breaker for the first time since 1997."}, {"question": "Which margining system was originally developed by CME Group and later adapted by LCH as 'London SPAN'?", "options": ["TIMS", "STANS", "SPAN (Standard Portfolio Analysis of Risk)", "SIMM"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "SPAN was developed by CME Group and subsequently licensed/adapted for use by LCH (as London SPAN) and many other clearing houses."}, {"question": "How many risk scenarios does SPAN apply when estimating the maximum potential loss on a position?", "options": ["4", "10", "16", "10,000"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "SPAN applies 16 risk scenarios, combining price and volatility moves in varying degrees, and selects the largest resulting loss as the initial margin requirement."}, {"question": "What does SPAN use to convert an options position into an equivalent futures position for margin offset purposes?", "options": ["Vega", "Delta", "Gamma", "Theta"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "SPAN uses deltas to calculate the equivalent number of futures represented by an options position, enabling offset calculations."}, {"question": "TIMS (Theoretical Intermarket Margining System), used by the OCC, calculates total margin for a position as the sum of which two components?", "options": ["Initial margin and variation margin", "Premium margin and risk margin", "Spot month margin and spread margin", "Haircut and threshold amount"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "TIMS' total margin equals premium margin (market value/daily price change) plus risk margin (potential value change under the maximum probable intraday move)."}, {"question": "Approximately how many hypothetical market scenarios does the OCC's STANS system generate when assessing clearing member margin requirements?", "options": ["16", "1,000", "10,000", "100,000"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "STANS, introduced by the OCC in 2006, generates 10,000 hypothetical market scenarios across more than 7,000 risk factors."}, {"question": "A clearing house calculates that a futures contract could move at most $5 per unit in one day, and each contract represents 500 units of the underlying. What should the initial margin per contract be set at?", "options": ["$500", "$1,000", "$2,500", "$5,000"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Initial margin = maximum one-day price move x contract size = $5 x 500 units = $2,500 per contract."}, {"question": "A trader goes short 5 FTSE 100 futures at 7,140. At the end of the day the settlement price is 7,175 (contract value £10 per index point). What is the variation margin position?", "options": ["The trader receives £350", "The trader pays £1,750", "The trader receives £1,750", "The trader pays £350"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Ticks moved = 7,175 - 7,140 = 35 points. Variation margin = 35 x £10 x 5 contracts = £1,750. As the trader is short and the price rose, this is a loss, so £1,750 is payable."}, {"question": "A trader is long 20 LME three-month tin futures at $45,300 (lot size 5 tonnes). At day-end the settlement price is $45,330. What is the variation margin flow?", "options": ["The trader pays $3,000", "The trader receives $3,000", "The trader receives $600", "The trader pays $150"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Price moved = $45,330 - $45,300 = $30 per tonne. Variation margin = $30 x 5 tonnes x 20 contracts = $3,000. As the trader is long and price rose, this is a profit, so the trader receives $3,000."}, {"question": "A trader is long 10 FTSE 100 futures at 7,500 (£10 per point). The settlement price at day-end is 7,462. What is the variation margin position?", "options": ["The trader pays £380", "The trader receives £380", "The trader pays £3,800", "The trader receives £3,800"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Ticks moved = 7,462 - 7,500 = -38 points. Variation margin = 38 x £10 x 10 contracts = £3,800. As the trader is long and the price fell, this is a loss, so £3,800 is payable."}, {"question": "A gold futures position has an initial margin of $6,000 and a maintenance margin set 30% below the initial margin. What is the maintenance margin level?", "options": ["$1,800", "$4,200", "$4,800", "$5,700"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Maintenance margin = $6,000 x (1 - 0.30) = $4,200."}, {"question": "Why might a clearing house impose a spot month margin (spot month charge)?", "options": ["To reduce margin for the most liquid contract month", "To increase initial margin as delivery approaches, ensuring only genuine deliverers/well-funded holders remain in the market", "To eliminate variation margin obligations near expiry", "To compensate clients for settlement delays"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "As a contract nears expiry, volatility can rise; a spot month margin increases initial margin to force out under-capitalised speculators and ensure remaining holders can settle or take delivery."}, {"question": "When might a clearing house call intra-day margin?", "options": ["Only at the start of every trading day, regardless of market conditions", "When volatility spikes intraday and existing initial margin held is judged insufficient", "Only when a client's account is in a segregated account", "Only on the last trading day of a contract"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If volatility jumps sharply intraday and the clearing house is no longer comfortable with (or has exhausted) the initial margin held, it can call additional intra-day margin immediately via PPS."}, {"question": "Under a Credit Support Annex (CSA), what is the 'threshold amount'?", "options": ["The minimum permitted size of any single collateral transfer", "The discount applied to collateral market value", "The unsecured credit exposure a counterparty is prepared to accept before requesting collateral", "The maximum notional size of trades permitted under the CSA"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The threshold amount is the level of unsecured exposure a counterparty will tolerate before it triggers a collateral call, usually set low to control credit risk."}, {"question": "Which type of CSA is most typical for sovereign, supranational and agency (SSA) counterparties?", "options": ["Two-way CSA", "One-way CSA", "No CSA is ever used with SSAs", "A CSA requiring only cash collateral"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "SSA counterparties traditionally do not post collateral; only the other counterparty posts when the trade moves in the SSA's favour, making it a one-way CSA."}, {"question": "Under the Uncleared Margin Rules (UMR), what is the initial margin collection threshold (calculated on a consolidated group basis) below which mandatory initial margin posting is not required?", "options": ["€10 million", "€50 million", "€100 million", "€500 million"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Where total initial margin due is calculated to be below €50 million, the entity is not required to post mandatory initial margin; amounts above €50 million must be collected."}, {"question": "Which standardised methodology is commonly used to calculate initial margin under UMR?", "options": ["SPAN", "TIMS", "SIMM (Standardised Initial Margin Schedule) or the Grid", "STANS"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "UMR requires initial margin to be calculated using the Grid or the Standardised Initial Margin Schedule (SIMM), or another BCBS-compliant methodology."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is novation?", "back": "The legal process by which the clearing house substitutes itself as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, replacing the original contract with two new contracts."}, {"front": "What counterparty risk remains for a clearing member after novation?", "back": "Only risk to the clearing house (CCP) itself, not to other market participants."}, {"front": "What is a mutual offset system?", "back": "An agreement between two exchanges (e.g. CME and SGX) allowing trades executed on one to be booked and cleared through the other."}, {"front": "What is a principal-to-principal guarantee?", "back": "A clearing house guarantee that runs only to its immediate counterparty (the clearing member), not to the member's underlying clients."}, {"front": "Name the three tiers of the clearing system.", "back": "Clearing House -> Clearing Member (General or Individual) -> Non-Clearing Member."}, {"front": "Difference between a General Clearing Member and an Individual Clearing Member?", "back": "A GCM clears trades for itself and other (non-clearing) firms; an Individual Clearing Member clears only for itself."}, {"front": "What is the Assured Payments Service (APS)?", "back": "ICE Clear Europe's system, used with partner banks, that automatically debits clearing members' bank accounts for margin payments."}, {"front": "State the default waterfall order.", "back": "1) Defaulter's own margin; 2) defaulter's own default fund contribution; 3) other members' default fund contributions; 4) the clearing house's insurance policy."}, {"front": "What shortfall resulted from the September 2018 Nasdaq Clearing power-spread default?", "back": "Approximately $122 million, absorbed by other clearing members' default fund contributions after the defaulter's own resources were exhausted."}, {"front": "What is a default fund (member default fund)?", "back": "A pool of cash contributed by all clearing members (in proportion to their clearing activity) used to cover losses once a defaulting member's own margin and default fund contribution are exhausted."}, {"front": "What is capital introduction (as a prime broker service)?", "back": "The process by which a prime broker introduces its hedge fund clients to qualified investors interested in new hedge fund investment opportunities."}, {"front": "How does CME's Clearing360 clear OTC interest rate swaps/FRAs?", "back": "It substitutes the OTC contract with a corresponding exchange-traded futures contract, rather than clearing the OTC trade as-is."}, {"front": "What is a clearing fund (guarantee fund)?", "back": "A pool of assets contributed by clearing members that can be used to meet a defaulting participant's obligations and cover related losses/liquidity pressures, drawn on after margin is exhausted."}, {"front": "How often does LCH Group recalculate clearing fund contributions?", "back": "Monthly (some other exchanges use quarterly review)."}, {"front": "What is a price limit / circuit breaker?", "back": "An exchange-imposed maximum bound on a contract's daily price movement; breaching it triggers a temporary trading halt."}, {"front": "What triggered the March 2020 NYSE circuit breaker?", "back": "The S&P 500 index fell 7% within three minutes of the market open on 9 March 2020, triggering a 15-minute halt."}, {"front": "What is SPAN?", "back": "Standard Portfolio Analysis of Risk - a scenario-based margining system (developed by CME, adapted as London SPAN by LCH) that calculates daily margin using 16 risk scenarios across price and volatility moves."}, {"front": "What are SPAN's two key parameters?", "back": "The scanning range (potential price movement) and the percentage volatility movement."}, {"front": "What does SPAN use to offset options positions against futures?", "back": "Delta, to calculate the equivalent number of futures represented by the option position."}, {"front": "What is TIMS?", "back": "Theoretical Intermarket Margining System - OCC's portfolio-based margining method for mixed portfolios, calculating margin as premium margin plus risk margin."}, {"front": "What is STANS?", "back": "System for Theoretical Analysis and Numerical Simulations - OCC's 2006 successor to TIMS, generating 10,000 hypothetical scenarios across 7,000+ risk factors; now used for member margin requirements."}, {"front": "What is Net Liquidation Value (NLV)?", "back": "The net value of a portfolio's constituent positions, allowing profits on some positions to offset losses on others for one net margin payment."}, {"front": "Why do spread positions attract lower initial margin?", "back": "Because the two legs (e.g. long June / short September FTSE future) largely offset and their price movements correlate, reducing net risk."}, {"front": "Define initial margin.", "back": "A good-faith deposit (cash or collateral) lodged with the clearing house against potential liabilities on an open position, covering the worst-case one-day loss; returned when the position closes."}, {"front": "What is a spot month margin?", "back": "An increase in initial margin as a contract nears expiry/delivery, to ensure only genuine deliverers or well-funded holders remain, reducing speculative pressure."}, {"front": "When can a clearing house call intra-day margin?", "back": "When volatility spikes intraday and the initial margin already held is judged insufficient or exhausted; called immediately via PPS/BAMS."}, {"front": "State the variation margin formula.", "back": "Variation margin = ticks moved on the day x tick value x number of contracts, where ticks moved = today's closing price - yesterday's closing price."}, {"front": "What system does ICE Clear Europe use to collect/pay variation margin, and what does CME use?", "back": "ICE Clear Europe uses the Protected Payments System (PPS); CME Clearing uses the Banking & Asset Management System (BAMS)."}, {"front": "What is maintenance margin and who operates it?", "back": "A broker-client (not clearing house) arrangement, typically set 25-50% below initial margin; once equity breaches this level, a margin call restores the account to the initial margin amount."}, {"front": "What is a haircut in the context of collateral?", "back": "A discount applied to the market value of collateral so that its full value is not credited, protecting against value falls before the next collateral call; higher-quality collateral gets a lower haircut."}, {"front": "What is a Credit Support Annex (CSA)?", "back": "A legal document, one of the four parts of an ISDA Master Agreement, that defines the timing, minimum transfer amount and acceptable collateral types for collateral payments on OTC derivatives."}, {"front": "Difference between a one-way and two-way CSA?", "back": "Two-way: both counterparties may be required to post collateral (typical between similarly-rated banks/dealers). One-way: only one party posts collateral (typical for sovereign/SSA counterparties), which never reciprocates."}, {"front": "What is the minimum transfer amount in a CSA?", "back": "The smallest permitted size of a single collateral transfer, designed to avoid frequent, costly small transfers."}, {"front": "What are the Uncleared Margin Rules (UMR)?", "back": "A BCBS global policy framework (from September 2013) requiring initial and variation margin exchange on non-centrally-cleared OTC derivatives, phased in across six phases from 2017 to September 2022."}, {"front": "What is the AANA under UMR?", "back": "Aggregate Average Notional Amount - the metric used to determine whether a firm/group is in scope of mandatory UMR initial margin requirements, based on phased thresholds."}, {"front": "What is the UMR initial margin collection threshold?", "back": "€50 million (consolidated group basis, by jurisdiction) - below this, mandatory initial margin posting is not required; above it, IM must be collected."}]}, {"id": "der-ch7", "number": 7, "title": "Delivery and Settlement", "examWeight": "6 of 100 questions (~6%)", "summaryHtml": "<p>Chapter 7 covers what happens to a futures or options position that is not closed out before expiry, and the operational controls surrounding OTC contract settlement. Most futures positions are closed out, but the clearing house becomes legal counterparty to every trade via <strong>novation</strong> and manages the delivery process, guaranteeing performance and reducing counterparty risk. Holders of futures have three choices: close out, roll forward, or proceed to delivery. Close-out methods include FIFO, LIFO, maximum profit and maximum loss; a closed position is 'flat', and reversing an erroneous close-out is 'busting' the settlement.</p><p>Physically deliverable contracts require a <strong>tender notice</strong> from the seller, random assignment by the clearing house, and delivery against an <strong>invoice amount</strong> based on the EDSP (exchange delivery settlement price), a scale factor and, for bonds, a price factor plus accrued interest. LME warrants and the LMEsword electronic transfer system are key examples of physical delivery mechanics. Cash settlement avoids delivery logistics by paying only the final day's variation margin against the EDSP, though EDSP manipulation risk remains a concern (e.g., the FTSE 100's 15-minute parallel share auctions, or the long gilt's 11am pricing convention).</p><p>For options, the holder (not the writer) initiates exercise via an exercise notice; the clearing house randomly assigns a writer an assignment notice. Automatic exercise (minimum $0.01 ITM) applies at ICE Clear Europe and CME Group unless a suppression notice is filed. Early exercise of American options is rarely rational unless deep ITM, close to expiry, with little time value left.</p><p>The final section covers OTC operational controls: deal tickets, term sheets, trade capture, confirmations (including master confirmations and transaction supplements), EMIR reporting (working day following trade conclusion, since August 2012), settlement complexity, and the reconciliation process (internal trade capture, back-office matching, 24-hour signed confirmation return).</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "7.1 Introduction – The Clearing House and Delivery", "html": "<p>Although most futures positions are closed out before expiry, the conceptual basis of a futures contract is that delivery can take place, so mechanisms must exist to allow it. The clearing house, in conjunction with the exchange, manages and defines the rules and procedures governing the delivery process.</p><p>After trades have been confirmed and registered, the clearing house becomes the legal counterparty to both sides via the process known as <strong>novation</strong>. As buyer and seller to every contract, the clearing house guarantees the performance of trades carried out by its members, substantially reducing counterparty risk.</p><p>When a futures contract proceeds to delivery, the clearing house manages the delivery of open contracts between clearing members, and may enable delivery of documents representing the underlying assets – such as <strong>warrants</strong> used in the metals market operated by the London Metal Exchange (LME). Each LME warrant represents one lot of a specific type/grade of metal. The buyer of the metal (or holder of the warrant) presents it to an LME-listed warehouse, which delivers the precise amount and grade of metal that it represents upon receipt of the warrant.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.2 Closing Contracts", "html": "<p>Holders of futures contracts have three choices:</p><ol><li>close out the contract before expiry;</li><li>roll the position forward by closing out the existing position and simultaneously establishing a new position in a new quoted futures month; or</li><li>proceed to deliver the underlying asset.</li></ol><p>Closing before delivery avoids the need to make or take delivery and realises any profit or loss. Since futures are fungible, the holder simply executes an opposing trade in the open market. Closing out offers flexibility (can be done any time up to maturity) and may be cheaper, since it avoids delivery/storage charges.</p><p>A close-out instruction from the client determines which long position (or part-position) is closed against which short position (or part-position). Common close-out methods for clients holding gross long and short positions include:</p><ul><li><strong>First-in, first-out (FIFO)</strong> – closes the oldest long position against the oldest short position.</li><li><strong>Last-in, first-out (LIFO)</strong> – closes the most recent trade (long or short) against the oldest position (long or short).</li><li><strong>Maximum profit</strong> – closes an equal number of long and short positions to realise the maximum profit.</li><li><strong>Maximum loss</strong> – closes an equal number of long and short positions to realise the maximum loss; this may reduce build-up of credit risk and free up credit lines for further trading.</li></ul><p>Positions may be designated to be left open (e.g., part of a strategy), and manual instructions can close specific positions. A position that has been closed out and effectively no longer exists is described as <strong>flat</strong>. If a position is closed out incorrectly or in error, it is possible to <strong>bust the settlement</strong> – reversing the closing action and reopening the position.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.3 Calculating Profit and Loss on Delivery/Expiry", "html": "<p>Formula: <strong>Profit/loss = number of ticks moved × tick value × number of contracts</strong>.</p><p><em>FTSE 100 Index Future example</em>: An investor buys five FTSE 100 futures at 7200 and holds to delivery; the EDSP is 7423. Contract size = index × £10 per point; tick size = 0.5 index points; tick value = £5 per tick. The position moves 223 index points into profit, i.e. 223 × 2 = 446 ticks (each index point = 2 ticks). Total profit = 446 × £5 × 5 contracts = £11,150. Note the final payment is actually based on the difference between the previous day's closing price and the EDSP, since daily variation margin has already been paid.</p><p><em>SHFE Natural Rubber Future example</em>: A manufacturer buys ten SHFE July natural rubber futures at CNY 19650, closing out one month before maturity at CNY 20250. Contract size = 5 tonnes; quotation in yuan per tonne; tick size 5 yuan. Move = CNY 600/tonne favourable. Profit per contract = CNY 600 × 5 tonnes = CNY 3,000. Total profit = CNY 3,000 × 10 contracts = CNY 30,000.</p><p><em>FTSE 100 Index Option example</em>: An investor buys three FTSE calls, strike 7200, premium 12. At expiry the cash index is 7210. Gain on exercise = 10 index points, less the 12-point premium = net loss of 2 points per contract. Total loss = 2 × £10 × 3 contracts = £60. (The option's contract size equals that of the underlying future.)</p><p>All losses are paid by market users to, and from, their brokers; brokers settle via clearing members, who settle via the clearing house. Some losses may already have been paid through the margining system during the contract's life.</p><p>The chapter includes five worked practice exercises (FTSE futures, FTSE put options, LMEX put options, Tadawul MT30 index futures, and an ICE long gilt invoice amount) applying these formulas.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.4 Physical Delivery", "html": "<p>For contracts not closed out by expiry, exchange rules define what physical assets (quality and quantity) may be delivered in final settlement. The LME defines acceptable brands, origins and warehouses for good delivery against its metals contracts; ICE Futures similarly defines delivery points and deliverable grades for its gas oil contract.</p><p><strong>LMEsword</strong> (launched in 1999 as SWORD) is a secure electronic transfer system for LME warrants held in a central depository. It acts as a central database of ownership, is subject to stringent security controls, and assures the buyer of correct deliverable quantity/grade and ownership upon payment. All LME warrants use a standard barcoded format. Ownership can be transferred between LMEsword members in seconds, and rent payments are automatically calculated. Benefits include administrative efficiency, removing physical transfer of warrants, and reducing manual handling; warrants are immobilised in the depository.</p><p>The exchange stipulates the last trading day, at the end of which the closing price is established – on ICE Futures Europe this is the <strong>exchange delivery settlement price (EDSP)</strong>. At expiry, exchange rules define deliverable quality, quantity and location, and delivery begins for all open positions.</p><p>Futures sellers give a <strong>tender notice</strong> to the clearing house declaring their intention to deliver. The clearing house then assigns those assets to buyers, usually at random. Where a contract allows flexibility, the seller chooses what, where and when to deliver (<strong>seller's choice</strong>).</p><p>Tender timetables vary: some contracts have a single notice day (e.g., the euro-bund future on Eurex); others run from a <strong>first notice day</strong> to a <strong>last notice day</strong> (e.g., long gilt, cocoa and robusta coffee futures on ICE Futures London), with the asset delivered on the <strong>delivery day</strong>.</p><p>ICE's four-stage delivery procedure:</p><ol><li>The seller (short position) advises ICE Clear Europe of their intention to deliver.</li><li>ICE's universal clearing platform (<strong>ICE Connect</strong>) matches sellers to buyers randomly; it also handles trade/position management and feeds positions and margin requirements to ICE Clear's Extensible Clearing System (<strong>ECS</strong>).</li><li>ICE Clear Europe calculates the invoice amount and notifies seller and buyer.</li><li>The seller delivers the asset to the clearing house and receives the invoice amount; the buyer pays the invoice amount to the clearing house and receives the asset.</li></ol><p>Participants wishing to avoid unwanted delivery must close out before the first notice day (as listed on the exchange website). In extreme illiquidity after the first notice day, a long holder may be forced to take delivery and pay storage costs.</p><p><strong>Invoice amount (financial/commodity futures)</strong>: Invoice amount = EDSP × scale factor × number of contracts. Example: EDSP for Brent crude is $77/barrel, contract size 1,000 barrels (scale factor 1,000). Invoice for 5 contracts = $77 × 1,000 × 5 = $385,000. The EDSP is unlikely to equal the originally agreed price, but variation margin paid over the contract's life means the net effect is the original agreed price.</p><p><strong>Invoice amount (bond futures)</strong>: Invoice amount = (EDSP × price factor × scale factor × number of contracts) + accrued interest. The <strong>price factor</strong> converts the EDSP (based on the notional bond) to the equivalent EDSP for the bond actually delivered; accrued interest is added because the EDSP is quoted clean (excluding accrued interest).</p>"}, {"heading": "7.5 Cash Settlement", "html": "<p>With cash-settled contracts there is no exchange of the underlying asset – only an exchange of funds representing profits and losses.</p><p><strong>Advantage</strong>: avoids the need for precise specification of quantity, quality and location, and allows contracts based on non-deliverable underlying assets, such as STIR deposits and equity/bond indices. Since variation margin has already been paid/received daily throughout the position's life, only the final day's variation margin against the EDSP is required to settle the trade.</p><p><strong>Disadvantage</strong>: in some bond index contracts (based on the price of several bonds), there is no pricing factor allowing delivery of a specific component bond.</p><p><strong>Risk</strong>: cash settlement carries EDSP manipulation risk, as trading activity in the underlying market during the EDSP period may be affected by participants seeking to manipulate the EDSP – e.g., unwinding bond positions during the EDSP period at prices expected to influence the index figure and hence the final EDSP, in order to obtain a more favourable return on an expiring position (e.g., a higher EDSP increases the return on a long position).</p>"}, {"heading": "7.6 Exchange Delivery Settlement Price (EDSP)", "html": "<p>The EDSP is ICE Europe's term for a future's closing price – the sole price at which all outstanding futures (bought and sold) are closed. The exchange must use an openly declared mechanism that prevents price manipulation; the resulting price must fairly reflect the underlying asset at expiry and be truly representative of spot prices.</p><p>Even for cash-settled contracts (where physical delivery does not apply), the EDSP remains fundamentally important as the final price at which variation margin changes hands. Some contracts offer a choice between cash settlement and physical delivery (e.g., ICE Futures Europe Brent Crude oil).</p><p><strong>FTSE 100 futures/options EDSP</strong> (ICE Futures Europe) is derived from an intra-day auction: every FTSE 100 constituent share is included in parallel auctions, each lasting no more than 15 minutes, so the EDSP reflects the very latest market information. Similar approaches are used by derivatives markets in Germany and the US.</p><p><strong>Long gilt future EDSP</strong> (ICE Futures Europe) uses a different technique: it is the market price of the contract at 11.00am on the second business day prior to settlement, unless notice is given on the last notice day, in which case it is the price prevailing on the last trading day. The invoice amount for the deliverable gilt is calculated using a price factor system, with an adjustment for coupon interest accruing as at the settlement day.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.7 Delivery and Settlement – Options: Exercise and Assignment", "html": "<p><strong>Assignment</strong> is the process obliging option sellers/writers to perform their obligations – e.g., a seller of an equity call is obliged to sell shares at the strike price; a seller of a bond call becomes short a bond future at the strike price on assignment.</p><p><em>Exercise of physically deliverable options</em>: European-style options can only be exercised at expiry; American-style options can be exercised over a set period up to expiry (though rarely exercised early, since holders usually prefer to sell and realise time value). An option is only exercised if it is ITM. To exercise, the holder notifies their broker, who files an exercise notice delivered to the clearing house (by the deadline) via the clearing member. Unlike futures – where the seller commences the delivery cycle – with options it is the <strong>holder</strong> who exercises and starts the process. On receipt, the clearing house randomly selects (assigns) an option writer and sends an <strong>assignment notice</strong> – formal notification that the contract's terms must be fulfilled. The transaction then becomes a cash market transaction subject to normal trading costs (e.g., commissions).</p><p><em>Example</em>: on exercise of an ICE Futures Europe equity call option on a UK company's shares, both holder and writer receive contract notes; the holder pays commission on the value of the shares, plus stamp duty reserve tax (SDRT) and a Panel on Takeovers and Mergers (PTM) levy. The trade settles through Euroclear UK & Ireland Ltd on the normal equity T+2 settlement timetable.</p><p><em>Exercise of cash-settled options</em>: effectively transfers the intrinsic value (IV) of the option from writer to holder. Example: exercising one FTSE call, strike 7200, EDSP 7450, contract size £10/point, the holder receives (7450 − 7200) × £10 = £2,500. For options with futures-style premium payment (as with all ICE markets), variation margin has already accounted for most of this, so only the final day's variation margin changes hands. For an OTC option, the holder would most likely receive the full amount upon exercise.</p><p><em>Exercise of options on futures</em>: the process is the same, except the holder and writer are assigned a long/short futures position at the strike price. Open interest in the underlying futures contract increases by the number of exercised options.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.8 Abandonment, Cabinet Trades and Automatic Exercise", "html": "<p><strong>Abandonment</strong>: if the holder decides not to exercise, the option lapses and the writer's obligation ceases. Holders tend to wait until the last possible moment before abandoning; options that are OTM, or slightly ITM but not worth exercising once transaction costs are considered, are abandoned. Sometimes traders close out a position for a nominal amount (a <strong>cabinet trade</strong>) to crystallise a loss for tax or accounting purposes.</p><p><strong>Automatic exercise</strong>: ICE Clear Europe and CME Group automatically exercise any option sufficiently ITM at expiry – usually a minimum of $0.01 per contract. This reduces paperwork and removes the risk of participants forgetting to exercise. The trigger is when the underlying's price is at or above a certain level for a call, or at or below a certain level for a put – i.e., ITM enough at expiration to cover any delivery cost of exercising.</p><p><strong>Capped-style options</strong> are subject to automatic exercise: they have an established profit cap, and once the underlying's price has moved beyond the cap and the option is ITM at expiration, it is automatically exercised.</p><p>Because exercise fees apply, an investor not wanting an option exercised (e.g., only slightly ITM, or where costly physical delivery would result) must file a <strong>suppression notice</strong> with the clearing house.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.9 Early Exercise of Options", "html": "<p>American-style options can be exercised before expiry (European-style cannot). The decision to exercise early rests with the holder, not the clearing house, which continues to assign contracts to writers at random.</p><p>Normally, it is not rational to exercise early, since exercise only realises the intrinsic value (IV), not the time value (TV) – selling the option captures both. However, for some options (e.g., equity-based), early exercise can be feasible where three criteria are met:</p><ol><li><strong>American-style</strong> – European-style options cannot be exercised before expiry.</li><li><strong>Deep-ITM</strong> – the option must have significant IV.</li><li><strong>Close to expiry/short-dated</strong> – there must be very little TV left.</li></ol><p>With equity options, there are sometimes good reasons to exercise early because the owner can then take up dividend rights on the stock or obtain voting rights.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.10 Settlement and Processing of OTC Contracts – The Importance of Controls", "html": "<p>Efficient administration of all transactions is essential. Because derivatives are geared, the full risk of any trade can be significantly greater than the initial transaction, so the risks arising from inefficiency are potentially greater. There is no single structure organisations apply to derivatives administration (a bank's structure will generally differ from a fund management company's). A firm's internal audit/compliance function is the first port of call in assessing and prescribing controls, in consultation with front, middle and back office; the external auditor then evaluates the controls for regulatory compliance and effectiveness in meeting the Board's prudential responsibility to shareholders.</p><p>Factors involved in creating a control regime include:</p><ul><li><strong>Volumes of transactions</strong> – market makers undertake many more transactions and tend to have more specialist operational areas.</li><li><strong>Range and complexity of products</strong> – an end-user (e.g., a fund manager) is likely involved in a narrower range of products, requiring narrower expertise.</li><li><strong>Valuations and risk monitoring</strong> – both market makers and end-users need efficient systems to value existing positions and measure associated risks.</li><li><strong>Reporting</strong> – fund manager regulatory reporting may be less onerous/frequent than for a bank, but both must report positions regularly and on time; retail-facing fund managers (e.g., unit trusts/mutual funds) face significant restrictions and requirements.</li></ul><p>Operations areas may be structured along product lines or process lines (e.g., counterparty domicile or deal complexity); there is often a split between OTC and exchange-traded instruments. Within a bank there is typically a front office, a middle office (risk management, mark-to-market) and a back office (confirmations, general administration), though the split of duties varies by bank – hence the focus should be on the processes themselves. Regardless of structure, the key objectives are efficiency, flexibility, security and control.</p><p>Processing and settlement of all transactions includes <strong>deal tickets</strong> (a record of all terms and conditions of the trade agreement, also known as trading tickets) and any <strong>term sheets</strong> attached to deal tickets. Term sheets matter because derivatives are often used as a hedge to manage risk of other commitments – if term sheet details are not processed correctly, a deal ticket intended to hedge would instead appear as an outright risk or speculative position.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.11 The OTC Process Flow", "html": "<p><strong>7.11.1 Trade Capture and Verification</strong>: most trades are input automatically via the trading desk; the middle office must verify details by the end of the trading day. Immediate entry is essential – delay means market and credit risk are not correctly monitored, and delays confirmation and settlement. Incorrect booking (e.g., a sale booked as a purchase) creates an incorrect trader position and bank risk report, may cause the bank to pay a premium without confirming receipt from the counterparty, and can cause failed settlement. Consequences of late/incorrect booking include: inaccurate risk evaluation, position management and investment decisions; late settlement interest/fees owed to counterparties; operational losses through funding/timing difficulties (e.g., late sales proceeds delaying other settlements); poor-quality client servicing; rule breaches leading to fines/disciplinary action; and reputational loss. Over-reliance on spreadsheets carries similar risk – they are accessible and familiar but distort perceptions of efficiency, accuracy and utility, and are typically slower and more bug-prone than expertly tested software (though no system is foolproof against human error or hacking).</p><p><strong>7.11.2 Position Keeping and Profit and Loss Analysis</strong>: positions are monitored for risk control and P&L is calculated; responsibility for this varies between front office and operations. Traders monitor their own position (which drives their P&L) via a real-time position system, but the risk-management checking role should sit outside the trading area – typically a product control team, which ensures hedge trades linked to term sheets have been applied correctly and that dealers/the trading area stay within individual, product and area limits (breaches are reported). Failure to maintain up-to-date, reconciled end-of-day positions between the dealing room and product control can result in losses from unreported transactions or limit breaches.</p><p><strong>7.11.3 Confirmation and Documentation</strong>: trade and documentation details are agreed between the operations areas of both parties, independently of the traders, to reduce fraud/unauthorised-dealing risk. A <strong>confirmation</strong> sets out the terms and conditions of a transaction, is a written representation of the verbal trade and evidence it occurred; once signed by both parties there is no possibility of dispute over its terms. It references the master agreement date and incorporates the relevant ISDA definitions. Unlike listed exchange products (which have standard terms), OTC contracts historically required both counterparties to record the trade on mutually agreed terms. Where counterparties trade with each other frequently, a <strong>master confirmation</strong> can be agreed – a general framework setting standard definitions/terms for a product on a global/regional basis, representing market consensus on how the product works. This speeds up confirmation and avoids later settlement/risk problems, and allows exchange of a <strong>transaction supplement</strong> containing only trade-specific details, referencing the master confirmation (so it is shorter than a normal confirmation). Under EMIR (in force since August 2012), trade details must be reported no later than the working day following the conclusion, modification or termination of the contract; this applies to all derivatives contracts except those closed out before that date. EMIR reporting does not apply to individuals or non-EU counterparties, though where an EU counterparty deals with a US (or other jurisdiction) counterparty, the equivalent Dodd-Frank (or other) reporting requirement applies to that other side. IHS Markit (part of a joint firm with S&P Global) has similarly achieved credit-market consensus by publishing standard terms that participants can reference, much like a master confirmation. Any disagreements over trade terms must be identified, reported and resolved immediately; escalation procedures apply if a confirmation/acknowledgement is not received from the counterparty within a prescribed time.</p><p><strong>7.11.4 Settlement</strong>: one or more settlements must be monitored, calculated, netted if appropriate, and settled. OTC derivatives settlement is more complex than cash instruments, which typically have one fixed settlement date and a fixed, immediately calculable amount. Examples: a cap has a series of potential settlement dates, of which all, some or none may involve a settlement; an American option can be exercised any time to expiry, a European option only on the expiry day; a fixed/floating swap's settlement amount cannot be calculated until the floating rate is set; and whether a payment is due (and its amount) on a CDS is determined only if the predefined credit event occurs. Timely trade processing helps avoid risks arising from late settlement.</p><p><strong>7.11.5 Reconciliation</strong>: the control process ensuring trades are done within the firm's trading mandate. A typical process: (1) <strong>Internal trade capture</strong> – the front office records the trade's execution (into a trade capture system or spreadsheet) before the official confirmation is received. (2) The back office checks the internally captured trade against the client's request; if there is a discrepancy, the front office is notified immediately to amend/cancel the trade, or, failing that, to enter further trades on its own account to mitigate losses from the error. (3) Once the official confirmation is received from the counterparty, the back office again checks it against the internal trade capture – this external reconciliation is a legally binding process (unlike the internal check). An approved-signatories list is shared with counterparties regularly and after any personnel change; the confirmation must be signed by approved back-office signatories and returned to the counterparty within <strong>24 hours</strong>, or as agreed in an ISDA master agreement. (4) The <strong>middle-office</strong> role is the most varied across firms – sometimes involved in internal trade-capture verification in place of the back office, but usually focused on mark-to-market calculation, regulatory compliance, trading-limit management, and counterparty/market risk analysis; it may also be accountable for documenting the front office's trading mandate and the overall reconciliation process.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "By what process does the clearing house become the legal counterparty to both the buyer and the seller of a futures contract once trades are confirmed and registered?", "options": ["Assignment", "Novation", "Registration", "Tendering"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The clearing house becomes the legal counterparty via novation, guaranteeing performance of trades and substantially reducing counterparty risk."}, {"question": "What does an LME warrant represent, and how is it delivered under the LMEsword system?", "options": ["A cash-settlement obligation with no link to physical metal", "One lot of a specific type/grade of metal, with ownership transferred electronically between LMEsword members while warrants are immobilised in a depository", "An option to buy metal at the EDSP, exercisable only on the last notice day", "A clearing member's initial margin deposit held at an LME warehouse"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Each LME warrant represents one lot of a specific metal type/grade; LMEsword is a secure electronic transfer system that immobilises warrants in a central depository and transfers ownership between members in seconds."}, {"question": "Under the last-in, first-out (LIFO) method of closing out gross futures positions, which position is closed out first?", "options": ["The oldest long position against the oldest short position", "The most recent trade (long or short) against the oldest position (long or short)", "An equal number of long and short positions to realise the maximum profit", "An equal number of long and short positions to realise the maximum loss"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "LIFO closes out the most recent trade (long or short) against the oldest position (long or short), in contrast to FIFO which matches the oldest long against the oldest short."}, {"question": "An investor buys five FTSE 100 futures at 7200 and holds them to delivery. The EDSP is 7423. The contract size is index x £10 per point, tick size is 0.5 index points, and tick value is £5. What is the investor's total profit?", "options": ["£2,230", "£5,575", "£11,150", "£22,300"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The move is 223 index points = 446 ticks (2 ticks per point). Profit = 446 x £5 x 5 contracts = £11,150."}, {"question": "The EDSP for a Brent crude futures contract is $77 per barrel, and the contract size is 1,000 barrels (scale factor 1,000). What is the invoice amount for 5 contracts?", "options": ["$77,000", "$385,000", "$770,000", "$3,850,000"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Invoice amount = EDSP x scale factor x number of contracts = $77 x 1,000 x 5 = $385,000."}, {"question": "In the invoice amount formula for a bond futures contract, what does the 'price factor' do?", "options": ["It adjusts for the accrued interest on the bond being delivered", "It converts the EDSP (based on the notional bond) to the equivalent EDSP for the bond actually being delivered", "It reflects storage and insurance costs on the physical asset", "It sets the minimum initial margin required on the position"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The price factor converts the EDSP based on the notional bond to the equivalent EDSP for the bond actually delivered; accrued interest is added separately because the EDSP is quoted clean."}, {"question": "Which of the following is cited as a potential disadvantage of cash settlement compared with physical delivery?", "options": ["It requires precise specification of quantity, quality and location of the underlying asset", "It cannot be used for underlyings such as STIR deposits or equity/bond indices", "For some bond index contracts, there is no pricing factor allowing delivery of a specific component bond", "It requires the seller to give a tender notice to the clearing house"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Some bond index contracts, based on the price of several bonds, do not provide pricing factors that allow for delivery of a specific component bond, which is seen as a disadvantage of cash settlement in that case."}, {"question": "How is the EDSP for FTSE 100 futures and options on ICE Futures Europe determined?", "options": ["By averaging the daily settlement prices over the last five trading days", "Through an intra-day auction covering every FTSE 100 constituent share, with parallel auctions each lasting no more than 15 minutes", "At the sole discretion of the clearing house's risk committee", "By reference to the closing level of the FTSE 100 index alone, with no auction process"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The EDSP for FTSE 100 futures/options is derived from an intra-day auction in which every FTSE 100 share is included in parallel auctions of no more than 15 minutes, ensuring the EDSP reflects the latest market information."}, {"question": "For the ICE Futures Europe long gilt future, what is the EDSP if no notice is given on the last notice day?", "options": ["The closing price on the delivery day", "The market price of the contract at 11.00am on the second business day prior to settlement", "The average traded price over the entire notice period", "The opening price on the last trading day"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Absent notice on the last notice day, the long gilt future's EDSP is the market price at 11.00am on the second business day prior to settlement; if notice is given, it is the price prevailing on the last trading day."}, {"question": "With options, unlike futures, who initiates the exercise/delivery cycle?", "options": ["The clearing house, by random selection", "The option writer/seller, via a tender notice", "The option holder, by notifying their broker to file an exercise notice", "The exchange's compliance department"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Unlike futures, where the seller commences the delivery cycle via a tender notice, with options it is the holder who exercises their right and starts the process by notifying their broker."}, {"question": "An investor exercises one FTSE call option with a strike of 7200 when the EDSP is 7450. The contract size is £10 per point. How much does the holder receive on exercise?", "options": ["£250", "£1,000", "£2,500", "£7,450"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The holder receives (7450 - 7200) x £10 = £2,500, transferring the intrinsic value from writer to holder."}, {"question": "At ICE Clear Europe and CME Group, what is the usual minimum in-the-money amount that triggers automatic exercise of an option at expiry?", "options": ["$0.01 per contract", "$1.00 per contract", "$10 per contract", "1% of the strike price"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "ICE Clear Europe and CME Group automatically exercise options that are sufficiently ITM at expiry, usually a minimum of $0.01 per contract, unless a suppression notice is filed."}, {"question": "What must an investor file with the clearing house to prevent an option from being automatically exercised, for example where physical delivery would be costly?", "options": ["An assignment notice", "A tender notice", "A suppression notice", "A cabinet trade instruction"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Because exercise fees apply, an investor who does not want an eligible option to be automatically exercised must file a suppression notice with the clearing house."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT one of the criteria typically required for early exercise of an American-style option to be rational?", "options": ["The option must be American-style", "The option must be deep in-the-money", "The option must be close to expiry, with little time value left", "The option must be cash-settled rather than physically deliverable"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The three criteria are: American-style, deep-ITM, and close to expiry/short-dated (little time value left). Whether the option is cash-settled or physically deliverable is not one of the stated criteria."}, {"question": "Under EMIR, by when must the details of a derivatives contract generally be reported?", "options": ["On the same day as execution", "No later than the working day following the conclusion, modification or termination of the contract", "Within T+2 of the trade date", "Within 30 calendar days of the trade date"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The EMIR reporting obligation, in force since August 2012, requires trade details to be reported no later than the working day following the conclusion, modification or termination of the contract."}, {"question": "What is the purpose of a 'transaction supplement' used alongside a master confirmation?", "options": ["It completely replaces the need for a master confirmation on future trades", "It contains only the trade-specific details of an individual transaction and references the terms of the master confirmation", "It is used exclusively for exchange-traded, rather than OTC, contracts", "It removes the need to incorporate ISDA definitions into the trade documentation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A master confirmation sets out standard terms/definitions for a product; the transaction supplement only needs the trade-specific details and references the master confirmation, making it shorter than a normal confirmation."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is novation, in the context of futures delivery?", "back": "The process by which the clearing house becomes the legal counterparty to both the buyer and the seller after a trade has been confirmed and registered, guaranteeing performance and reducing counterparty risk."}, {"front": "What are the three choices available to a holder of an open futures position before expiry?", "back": "Close out the contract before expiry; roll the position forward (close out and simultaneously open a new position in a later month); or proceed to delivery of the underlying asset."}, {"front": "Distinguish FIFO and LIFO close-out methods.", "back": "FIFO closes the oldest long position against the oldest short position. LIFO closes the most recent trade (long or short) against the oldest position (long or short)."}, {"front": "What does it mean to 'bust' a settlement?", "back": "Reversing a closing action that was performed incorrectly or in error, and reopening the position."}, {"front": "What does 'flat' mean in relation to a futures position?", "back": "A position that has been closed out and effectively no longer exists."}, {"front": "State the formula for calculating profit/loss on a futures position.", "back": "Profit/loss = number of ticks moved x tick value x number of contracts."}, {"front": "What is a tender notice?", "back": "The notice a futures seller gives to the clearing house declaring their intention to deliver the underlying asset."}, {"front": "What is meant by 'seller's choice' in physical delivery?", "back": "Where a contract allows flexibility, the seller (not the buyer) chooses what, where and when to deliver."}, {"front": "List ICE's four-stage delivery procedure.", "back": "1) Seller advises ICE Clear Europe of intent to deliver; 2) ICE Connect randomly matches sellers to buyers and feeds positions/margin to ECS; 3) ICE Clear Europe calculates the invoice amount and notifies both parties; 4) seller delivers the asset and receives the invoice amount, buyer pays the invoice amount and receives the asset."}, {"front": "Give the invoice amount formula for a standard (non-bond) physically delivered futures contract.", "back": "Invoice amount = EDSP x scale factor x number of contracts."}, {"front": "Give the invoice amount formula for a bond futures contract, and explain the extra terms.", "back": "Invoice amount = (EDSP x price factor x scale factor x number of contracts) + accrued interest. The price factor converts the notional-bond EDSP to the equivalent EDSP for the bond actually delivered; accrued interest is added because the EDSP is quoted clean."}, {"front": "What is LMEsword?", "back": "A secure electronic transfer system for LME warrants (launched 1999 as SWORD), holding warrants in a central depository, acting as a central ownership database, transferring ownership between members in seconds and auto-calculating rent payments."}, {"front": "What is the key advantage of cash settlement over physical delivery?", "back": "It avoids the need for precise specification of quantity, quality and location of the underlying, and allows contracts to be based on non-deliverable assets such as STIR deposits and equity/bond indices."}, {"front": "What is the EDSP and why is it significant?", "back": "The Exchange Delivery Settlement Price is ICE Europe's term for a future's closing price - the sole price at which all outstanding futures are closed, and (for cash-settled contracts) the final price at which variation margin changes hands."}, {"front": "How is the EDSP set for FTSE 100 futures and options on ICE Futures Europe?", "back": "Via an intra-day auction covering every FTSE 100 constituent share, with parallel auctions each lasting no more than 15 minutes."}, {"front": "How is the EDSP set for the ICE Futures Europe long gilt future?", "back": "The market price of the contract at 11.00am on the second business day prior to settlement, unless notice is given on the last notice day, in which case it is the price prevailing on the last trading day."}, {"front": "What triggers automatic exercise at ICE Clear Europe and CME Group?", "back": "An option that is sufficiently in-the-money at expiry - usually a minimum of $0.01 per contract - is automatically exercised, unless a suppression notice has been filed."}, {"front": "What three criteria typically make early exercise of an American-style option rational?", "back": "The option must be: American-style; deep in-the-money; and close to expiry/short-dated (with little time value left)."}, {"front": "What is a master confirmation, and what is a transaction supplement?", "back": "A master confirmation is a general framework agreed between frequently-trading counterparties, setting standard terms/definitions for a product. A transaction supplement then only needs to reference the master confirmation and add trade-specific details, making it shorter than a full confirmation."}, {"front": "Under EMIR, by when must derivatives trade details be reported, and since when has this applied?", "back": "No later than the working day following the conclusion, modification or termination of the contract; the reporting obligation has been in force since August 2012."}]}, {"id": "der-ch8", "number": 8, "title": "Trading, Hedging and Investment Strategies", "examWeight": "14 of 100 questions (~14%)", "summaryHtml": "<p>This is the single most heavily tested chapter in the Derivatives syllabus (~14/100 questions) and blends theory with numeric calculation. It opens by classifying market users: <strong>hedgers</strong> (reduce risk on an existing/anticipated exposure), <strong>speculators</strong> (take on new risk for profit) and <strong>arbitrageurs</strong> (exploit mispricing for risk-free profit).</p><p><strong>Spreads</strong>: an <em>intra-market</em> spread trades the same underlying with different expiries (motives: basis view, lower risk than outright, arbitrage, rolling a hedge). An <em>inter-market</em> spread trades different but correlated underlyings (eg, short sterling vs long gilt, to play the yield curve) or is used to switch asset allocation quickly (sell equity index futures, buy bond futures) without disturbing the physical portfolio.</p><p><strong>Basics of hedging</strong>: a long underlying position is hedged by selling futures (short hedge); a short underlying position is hedged by buying futures (long hedge). For bond portfolios, the hedge ratio uses the <strong>CTD bond's conversion factor</strong>: contracts = conversion factor x (nominal value of CTD portfolio / nominal contract size). For non-CTD/duration-based portfolios and interest rate futures generally, a duration-based formula applies. For equities, contracts = (portfolio value / (futures price x contract multiplier)) x portfolio beta. For STIRs, hedge ratio = price change in portfolio per bp / price change in STIR future per bp. <strong>Basis risk</strong> (the risk that cash and futures prices do not move exactly together) is a problem for hedgers (imperfect hedge, accounting/hedge-effectiveness issues) but an opportunity for speculators/arbitrageurs.</p><p><strong>Hedging with options</strong>: buying a put hedges a long position; buying a call hedges a short position; both have an upfront premium cost (unlike futures) but retain upside. <strong>Covered calls</strong> (long asset + short call) enhance income in flat markets, cushion falls by the premium, but cap the upside at strike + premium. <strong>Covered (cash-secured) short puts</strong> (short asset/cash reserved + short put) are the mirror image, enhancing returns on a short position but capping the downside gain.</p><p><strong>Options spreads</strong>: vertical (different strikes, same expiry — bull/bear call/put spreads with capped risk and reward), horizontal/calendar (same strike, different expiry — a volatility play) and diagonal (different strike and expiry). <strong>Combinations</strong> — straddles (same strike call+put) and strangles (different strikes, call+put) — are pure volatility plays: long positions profit from big moves either way (unlimited reward, risk limited to premium), short positions profit from a quiet market (reward capped at premium received, risk theoretically unlimited).</p><p><strong>Synthetics</strong> replicate a position using a different combination of instruments, mainly for arbitrage when put-call parity breaks down (eg, synthetic long future = buy call + sell put). The chapter closes with ETD vs OTC trade-offs (liquidity/transparency/low counterparty risk vs flexibility/customisation), indirect investment routes (discretionary accounts vs pooled CISs), fund styles (speculative, guaranteed/structured, synthetic/index-tracking) and how different client types (retail, HNWI, institutional managers, corporate treasurers, hedge funds, SWFs, pension funds, ESG investors, robo-advisors) use derivatives differently.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "1. Introduction — Users of Derivatives", "html": "<h4>Categories of derivatives users (LO 8.1.1)</h4><ul><li><strong>Hedger</strong> — reduces the risk of adverse price movement on an existing or anticipated underlying position by taking an offsetting derivatives position.</li><li><strong>Speculator</strong> — takes on new/extra risk by betting on the future direction of the underlying's price, seeking profit.</li><li><strong>Arbitrageur</strong> — exploits temporary mispricing between related instruments/markets to lock in a risk-free profit.</li></ul><p>A <strong>structured product</strong> (market-linked product) is an investment strategy based on derivatives (futures, options, sometimes swaps), combining an underlying asset with one or more derivatives. There is no single uniform definition. Structured products meet investor needs that cannot be met by standard instruments alone — used as an alternative to direct investment, to reduce risk (asset allocation), to increase exposure, or to exploit mispricing.</p>"}, {"heading": "2. Spreads", "html": "<h4>2. Futures spread trades (LO 8.2.1)</h4><p>A <strong>futures spread trade</strong> is the simultaneous purchase and sale of futures contracts. In any spread (or combination) trade, a trader may <strong>'leg in'</strong> — execute one side now and the other later — but is exposed to price risk on the uncompleted leg until the second leg is done.</p><h4>2.1 Intra-Market Spreads</h4><p>Simultaneous buy/sell of futures with <strong>different expiry dates on the same underlying</strong> (eg, sell June long gilt, buy September long gilt). Motivations:</p><ul><li><strong>Anticipating basis changes</strong> — a trader who is short the spread (sold the nearer/bought the further date, per the example) is hurt by a strengthening basis.</li><li><strong>Reducing risk</strong> — basis risk &lt; outright price risk, but potential profit is also smaller.</li><li><strong>Arbitrage</strong> — same product traded on two exchanges at different prices (eg, buy SGX Nikkei 225 futures, sell OSE Nikkei 225 futures).</li><li><strong>Rolling over a hedge</strong> — eg, sell June natural gas (closing an existing long) and buy December natural gas to extend the hedge six more months.</li></ul><h4>2.2 Inter-Market Spreads</h4><p>Simultaneous buy/sell of futures on <strong>different but correlated underlyings</strong> (eg, buy September short sterling, sell September long gilt) — used to trade a temporary breakdown in the normal price relationship, popular for yield-curve (steepening/flattening) views. Also used for hedging production processes/costs, and for quick, cost-effective asset-allocation switches.</p><p><strong>Worked example</strong> — A fund manager wants to reduce long equities exposure and increase bonds. Instead of selling shares and buying bonds directly, they <strong>sell FTSE 100 futures and buy long gilt futures</strong> — replicating the reallocation without trading the physical securities, continuing to earn dividend income until the physical trades are actually done, then unwinding the futures.</p><p><strong>Intra vs inter — key distinction:</strong> it is the <em>underlying asset</em>, not the trading location, that matters. Intra ('within') = same underlying asset, different expiries. Inter ('between') = different underlying assets.</p>"}, {"heading": "3. Basics of Hedging", "html": "<h4>3. Long/short positions (LO 8.3.1)</h4><p>An unhedged position (long or short the underlying) is exposed to adverse price moves. Hedgers take an <strong>opposite position in the futures market</strong>: long underlying → sell futures (<strong>short hedge</strong>); short underlying → buy futures (<strong>long hedge</strong>). Because cash and futures prices are correlated but not perfectly locked together, <strong>basis risk</strong> affects hedge performance.</p><h4>3.1 Cheapest to Deliver (CTD) — hedge ratios (LO 8.4.1)</h4><p>When a bond futures contract has a deliverable basket, the <strong>CTD bond</strong> — the one with the <strong>highest implied repo rate</strong> — drives pricing, and the number of contracts to hedge a CTD bond portfolio uses its <strong>conversion factor</strong>:</p><p><strong>Number of contracts = conversion factor x (nominal value of CTD portfolio / nominal value of the contract)</strong></p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> CTD gilt portfolio, nominal £10m, market value £11.5m, conversion factor 1.1214645, contract size £100,000 nominal.<br>Contracts = 1.1214645 x (£10m / £100,000) = <strong>112.15 ≈ 112 contracts</strong>.</p><p><strong>Exercise 1 (chapter):</strong> Short-sold CTD gilts, nominal £15m, market value £15.4m, conversion factor 1.12486. Contracts = 1.12486 x (£15m/£100,000) = <strong>168.73 ≈ 169 contracts</strong>. Since the investor is short the gilts, they should <strong>buy</strong> 169 long gilt futures to hedge.</p><p>For a <strong>non-CTD bond portfolio</strong>, a duration-based hedge ratio is used instead:<br><strong>N = (P x DP) / (FC x DF)</strong> where N = number of contracts, P = nominal value of the non-CTD portfolio, DP = duration of the portfolio, FC = interest-rate futures price, DF = duration of the underlying asset in the future. A negative sign is sometimes shown to denote the offsetting/hedge nature of the position.</p><h4>3.2 Hedge Ratio Calculation — STIRs and equities (LO 8.4.2, 8.4.3)</h4><p><strong>Beta (ß)</strong> = Covariance(Rp,Rm) / Variance(Rm) — measures a stock/portfolio's volatility relative to the market. Beta &gt;1 = more volatile than market; =1 = in line; 0&lt;ß&lt;1 = less volatile; =0 = no relationship; &lt;0 = inverse relationship.</p><p><strong>Equity hedge — worked example:</strong> Portfolio beta 1.1 vs S&amp;P 500, value $8.0m, S&amp;P 500 at 4400, E-mini contract = $50 x index.<br>Contracts = ($8.0m / ($50 x 4400)) x 1.1 = 36.4 x 1.1 = <strong>40 contracts</strong> (sell, to short hedge a long equity portfolio).</p><p><strong>STIR hedge ratio</strong> = (price change in portfolio per 1bp yield change) / (price change in STIR futures per 1bp yield change).</p><h4>3.3 Basis, Basis Trading and Basis Risk (LO 8.4.4)</h4><p><strong>Basis</strong> = cash price − futures price. <strong>Basis risk</strong> = risk that futures and cash prices move by different amounts. <strong>Basis trading</strong> = strategies designed to profit from anticipated basis changes.</p><p>Volatile/unpredictable basis is a <strong>problem for hedgers</strong> — auditors assess hedge efficiency/correlation, and an inefficient hedge may be reclassified as speculative, forcing mark-to-market changes straight through the P&amp;L (causing profit volatility). Basis change is an <strong>advantage for speculators/arbitrageurs</strong>, who profit from predicting it, and basis trading carries less price risk than an outright position because gains/losses on one leg partly offset the other.</p><p><strong>Worked example (FTSE hedge with basis risk):</strong> Fund manager holds £15m UK equities, FTSE 100 at 7785, sells 250 FTSE 100 futures at 7230. Next day FTSE falls 25 points to 7760; future falls 16 points to 7214.</p><table><tr><td>1. Unhedged portfolio value</td><td>£15,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>2. Day-2 value = £15m x (7760/7785)</td><td>£14,951,830 (change: −£48,170)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Futures profit = 250 x (7230−7214) x £10</td><td>£40,000</td></tr><tr><td>4. Hedged loss (due to basis) = £40,000 − £48,170</td><td>−£8,170</td></tr></table><p>The futures hedge greatly reduced, but did not eliminate, the loss — the residual loss is attributable to the change in basis (the cash index and futures price did not move by identical amounts).</p>"}, {"heading": "4. Hedging Using Options", "html": "<h4>4. Delta hedging and options as hedges (LO 8.4.5, 8.4.6)</h4><p>Hedging efficiency with options depends on the option's <strong>delta</strong> mirroring the underlying position's delta, creating a <strong>delta-neutral portfolio</strong>. Approximate deltas:</p><table><tr><th>Position</th><th>Delta</th></tr><tr><td>Long underlying</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Short underlying</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td>Long future</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Short future</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td>Long call (deep ITM)</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Long call (ATM)</td><td>+0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Short call (deep ITM)</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td>Short call (ATM)</td><td>−0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Long put (deep ITM)</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td>Long put (ATM)</td><td>−0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Short put (deep ITM)</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td>Short put (ATM)</td><td>+0.5</td></tr></table><h4>4.1 Hedging a Long Underlying Position</h4><h5>4.1.1 Buy a Put Option</h5><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Investor A owns FTSE shares worth £550,000, FTSE at 7155, and buys puts (strike 7100) to hedge a fall. FTSE option contract size = index level x £10/point → each contract at strike 7100 covers £71,000 (7100 x £10).<br>Contracts needed = £550,000 / £71,000 = <strong>8 contracts</strong>. If the index falls, the put profit offsets the portfolio loss; premium paid is never refunded (unlike a futures hedge, which has no upfront cost); if the index rises, the long position still profits once the premium is recouped.</p><h5>4.1.2 Sell a Call Option (covered call as a hedge)</h5><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Aluminium producer has 100 tonnes of unsold December production; LME primary aluminium future contract size = 25 tonnes. Sells <strong>4 calls</strong> at strike $1,900 (budgeted sale price). Premium received partially offsets a price fall; if price rises above $1,900, producer receives only $1,900 + premium (upside capped) once exercised ITM.</p><h4>4.2 Hedging a Short Underlying Position</h4><h5>4.2.1 Buy a Call Option</h5><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Investor B must buy 500oz gold for June, cash price $1,950/oz. Buys gold futures calls, contract size 100oz → <strong>5 calls</strong> needed. If gold rises, call profits offset the higher cash cost; if gold falls, investor buys more cheaply after recouping the premium. Premium paid is a cost not present with a futures hedge.</p><h5>4.2.2 Sell a Put Option</h5><p>Mirrors the covered call (see section 4.3.2, covered short put) for a short underlying position.</p><p><strong>Key comparison:</strong> an options hedge underperforms a futures hedge by the premium cost, but offers flexibility — if the price moves favourably, the investor can still profit (loss on the option is capped at the premium), whereas a futures hedge locks in the offsetting result regardless of direction.</p><h4>4.3 Covered Options Positions (LO 8.5.1)</h4><h5>4.3.1 Covered Short Call (buy-write)</h5><p>Long underlying + short call. Motivation: enhance returns in a <strong>stagnant market</strong> and partly hedge a long position.</p><ul><li>Stagnant market: option expires worthless, investor keeps premium — enhanced return.</li><li>Falling market: premium cushions the loss but does not fully hedge (short call gain capped at the premium).</li><li>Rising market: gains capped — call exercised, no further upside beyond the strike.</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Long 1,000 ABC plc shares at 407p; sells 425 call for premium of 11p (contract = 1,000 shares).</p><ul><li>Price unchanged at 407: option abandoned, investor keeps 11p premium (positive return).</li><li>Price falls: premium cushions the fall p-for-p; if fall &lt; 11p, investor still better off; if fall &gt; 11p, loses the excess; at price = 0, total loss offset only by the 11p premium.</li><li>Price rises to 425 (max profit point): total profit = 18p (share gain, 425−407) + 11p (premium) = <strong>29p</strong>. Return on investment (net of premium reducing cost basis to 396p) = (425−407)/396 = <strong>4.55%</strong>.</li><li>Price rises above 425: option exercised against investor; shares delivered at 425 — maximum profit capped at 29p (ignoring dividends).</li></ul><p><strong>Naked short call</strong> = selling calls without owning the underlying (naked writing) — a speculative position with unlimited risk if the price rises; illegal in many jurisdictions (US, EU, India, Hong Kong) due to settlement/market-disruption risk.</p><h5>4.3.2 Covered Short Put</h5><p>Constructed by (1) writing a put while holding sufficient cash to buy the asset, or (2) already short the underlying/futures and writing a put on it. Motivation: enhance return on funds/short position in a <strong>stagnant market</strong>; mirror image of the covered short call.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> ABC plc at 407p; investor sells shares short at 407p and sells a 400 put for 20p.</p><ul><li>Price unchanged: put abandoned, investor keeps the 20p premium (enhanced return on funds).</li><li>Price rises above 400 but below 427: short-sale loss more than offset by the 20p premium; net profit. Above 427p → net loss.</li><li>Price falls below 400 (max profit point): put exercised against investor, buys shares at 400p — effective cost 380p (400p − 20p premium). Maximum profit = 20p premium + interest on funds, plus buying at 400p rather than 407p.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "5. Options Spreads", "html": "<h4>5. Types of options spreads (LO 8.5.2)</h4><p>An <strong>options spread</strong> = simultaneous purchase and sale of options in the <em>same class</em> (both calls, or both puts) on the same underlying.</p><ol><li><strong>Vertical spread</strong> — different strikes, same expiry (eg, buy April 200 call, sell April 220 call).</li><li><strong>Horizontal (calendar) spread</strong> — same strike, different expiries (eg, buy April 200 call, sell May 200 call).</li><li><strong>Diagonal spread</strong> — different strikes AND different expiries (eg, buy April 200 call, sell May 220 call).</li></ol><h4>5.1 Vertical Spreads (LO 8.5.3, 8.5.4)</h4><p><strong>Bull spread</strong> = buy lower strike, sell higher strike (bull call spread or bull put spread). <strong>Bear spread</strong> = sell lower strike, buy higher strike (bear call spread or bear put spread).</p><p><strong>Worked example (option chain):</strong> ABC plc at 550.</p><table><tr><th>Call PM</th><th>Strike</th><th>Put PM</th></tr><tr><td>70</td><td>500</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>49</td><td>525</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>550</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>575</td><td>47</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>600</td><td>67</td></tr></table><p>Moderately bullish investor buys 550 call (pay 37) and sells 600 call (receive 19) → net outlay = 18 (<strong>bull call spread</strong>).</p><ul><li>Price ≤ 550: both calls OTM/abandoned — max loss = net outlay of <strong>18</strong>.</li><li>Price ≥ 600: both ITM, profit capped at (600−550) − 18 = <strong>32</strong> (max profit).</li><li>Break-even = 550 + 18 = <strong>568</strong>.</li></ul><h5>5.1.1 Summary of Vertical Spreads</h5><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Motivation</th><th>Construction</th><th>Net premium</th><th>Max risk</th><th>Max reward</th><th>Break-even</th></tr><tr><td>Bull Call</td><td>Moderately bullish</td><td>Buy lower strike, sell higher strike</td><td>Paid</td><td>Net premium paid</td><td>Difference in strikes − net premium paid</td><td>Lower strike + net premium paid</td></tr><tr><td>Bear Call</td><td>Moderately bearish</td><td>Sell lower strike, buy higher strike</td><td>Received</td><td>Difference in strikes − net premium received</td><td>Net premium received</td><td>Lower strike + net premium received</td></tr><tr><td>Bull Put</td><td>Moderately bullish</td><td>Buy lower strike, sell higher strike</td><td>Received</td><td>Difference in strikes − net premium received</td><td>Net premium received</td><td>Higher strike − net premium received</td></tr><tr><td>Bear Put</td><td>Moderately bearish</td><td>Sell lower strike, buy higher strike</td><td>Paid</td><td>Net premium paid</td><td>Difference in strikes − net premium paid</td><td>Higher strike − net premium paid</td></tr></table><p>Bull put graph shape = identical to bull call graph; bear put graph shape = identical to bear call graph.</p><p><strong>Exercise (chapter) — bull put spread:</strong> Buy 500 put at 18p, sell 550 put at 35p → net premium <strong>received</strong> = 17p. Break-even = 533p (550 − 17). Max profit (at ≥550) = <strong>17p</strong> (the net premium received). Max loss (at ≤500) = (550−500) − 17 = <strong>33p</strong>.</p><h4>5.2 Horizontal and Diagonal Spreads (LO 8.5.5)</h4><h5>5.2.1 Horizontal (Calendar) Spreads</h5><p>Motivated by expected changes in <strong>volatility</strong>, not direction. Two types: (a) expecting volatility to <strong>fall/stay flat</strong>: sell shorter-maturity option, buy longer-maturity option (same strike) — exploits faster time decay of the short-dated leg; (b) expecting volatility to <strong>rise</strong> (esp. short-term, eg ahead of earnings/central bank meetings): buy shorter-maturity option, sell longer-maturity option (same strike).</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> GBP/USD spot 1.2500/05, ATM strike 1.2550 options: 1m call 65/put 70; 3m call 130/put 145; 6m call 280/put 295; 12m call 575/put 585. View: rates stay steady near-term → sell the 1m 1.2550 put (receive 70) and buy the 6m 1.2550 put (pay 295) → net cost 225. After two weeks, 2-week put premium falls to 60, 5.5-month put falls to 290 → unwind: receive net 230 (290−60), vs initial cost of 225 → <strong>net profit of 5</strong>.</p><p><strong>Exercise (chapter):</strong> Buy 2-week 110p call for 20p, sell 3-month 110p call for 130p (net premium <strong>received</strong> = 110p, since selling the higher-value longer option). Later: short-term premium rises to 35 (loss of 15 on the bought option... ), long-term premium rises to 135 (loss of 5 on the written option, since paid 130 received now worth 135). Net result: <strong>+10p profit</strong> (gain 15 on the long call, lose 5 on the short call).</p><h5>5.2.2 Diagonal Spreads</h5><p>Different strikes AND different expiries — directional (calls if bullish, puts if bearish), selling shorter-dated and buying longer-dated, further OTM options. Exploit differing rates of time decay/volatility changes. No numeric calculation questions in the exam on horizontal/diagonal spreads, but theory questions are possible.</p>"}, {"heading": "6. Options Combinations (Straddles and Strangles)", "html": "<h4>6. Straddles and strangles (LO 8.5.2, 8.5.4, 8.5.6)</h4><p>A <strong>combination</strong> = simultaneous purchase/sale of a call and a put. <strong>Straddle</strong> = same strike, same expiry. <strong>Strangle</strong> = different strikes (put strike lower than call strike), same expiry. Long positions profit from an <strong>increase</strong> in volatility (big move either direction); short positions profit from a <strong>decrease</strong> in volatility (price stays range-bound).</p><p><strong>Option chain used throughout (DEF shares, price 550p):</strong> Call PM 70/49/37/25/19 at strikes 500/525/550/575/600; Put PM 18/23/35/47/67 at the same strikes.</p><h4>6.1 Long Straddle</h4><p>Buy 550 call (37) + buy 550 put (35) = net premium outlay <strong>72</strong>.</p><ul><li>Price rises to 660: exercise call, IV = 110; net profit = 110 − 72 = <strong>38</strong>. Upside break-even = 550 + 72 = <strong>622</strong>.</li><li>Price falls to 500: exercise put, IV = 50 &lt; 72, so a net loss overall at this price. Downside break-even = 550 − 72 = <strong>478</strong>.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Motivation</th><td>Expectation of an increase in volatility</td></tr><tr><th>Construction</th><td>Buy a call and a put, same strike and expiry</td></tr><tr><th>Max risk</th><td>Total premiums paid</td></tr><tr><th>Max reward</th><td>Unlimited</td></tr><tr><th>Break-even</th><td>Strike ± total premium paid</td></tr></table><p><strong>Exercise (chapter):</strong> Buy 500 call for 18p + buy 500 put for 7p → total premium = 25p. Break-even range: below 475p (500−25) or above 525p (500+25).</p><h4>6.2 Short Straddle</h4><p>Sell 550 call (37) + sell 550 put (35) = net premium received <strong>72</strong>.</p><ul><li>Max profit at 550 (both abandoned) = <strong>72</strong> (full premium retained).</li><li>Upside break-even = 622 (strike + premium); above this, unlimited loss.</li><li>Downside break-even = 478 (strike − premium); below this, unlimited loss.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Motivation</th><td>Expectation of a decrease in volatility</td></tr><tr><th>Construction</th><td>Sell a call and a put, same strike and expiry</td></tr><tr><th>Max risk</th><td>Unlimited</td></tr><tr><th>Max reward</th><td>Limited to sum of premiums received</td></tr><tr><th>Break-even</th><td>Strike ± total premiums received</td></tr></table><h4>6.3 Long Strangle</h4><p>Buy 500 put (18) + buy 600 call (19) = net premium <strong>37</strong>. Vs a straddle: usually cheaper premium, but needs more volatility to profit (must overcome both the premium AND the strike gap); max loss is crystallised over the whole range between strikes (500–600), not just at one point.</p><table><tr><th>Motivation</th><td>Expectation of a large increase in volatility</td></tr><tr><th>Construction</th><td>Buy a call and a put, same expiry, different strikes</td></tr><tr><th>Max risk</th><td>Total premiums paid</td></tr><tr><th>Max reward</th><td>Unlimited</td></tr><tr><th>Break-even</th><td>Downside: lower strike − total premiums. Upside: higher strike + total premiums.</td></tr></table><p>(In the worked example: downside break-even = 500−37 = 463; upside break-even = 600+37 = 637.)</p><p><strong>Exercise (chapter):</strong> Buy 400 put at 17p + buy 450 call at 8p, total premium = 25p. Break-even range = 375 (400−25) to 475 (450+25).</p><h4>6.4 Short Strangle</h4><p>Sell 500 put (18) + sell 600 call (19) = net premium received <strong>37</strong>. Max profit crystallises over the range 500–600 (both options abandoned); can withstand more volatility than a short straddle before a loss occurs.</p><table><tr><th>Motivation</th><td>Expectation of a large decrease in volatility</td></tr><tr><th>Construction</th><td>Sell a call and a put, same expiry, different strikes</td></tr><tr><th>Max risk</th><td>Unlimited</td></tr><tr><th>Max reward</th><td>Total premium received</td></tr><tr><th>Break-even</th><td>Downside: lower strike − premiums. Upside: higher strike + premiums.</td></tr></table>"}, {"heading": "7. Synthetics", "html": "<h4>7. Synthetic positions (LO 8.5.7)</h4><p>Combining futures and options can synthetically replicate a position. Six basic synthetics:</p><ol><li><strong>Synthetic long future</strong> = buy call + sell put (same strike/expiry) — also called a <strong>reversal</strong>.</li><li><strong>Synthetic short future</strong> = sell call + buy put (same strike/expiry) — also called a <strong>conversion</strong>.</li><li><strong>Synthetic long call</strong> = buy future + buy put (equivalent to a hedged long position).</li><li><strong>Synthetic short call</strong> = sell future + sell put.</li><li><strong>Synthetic long put</strong> = buy call + sell future (equivalent to a hedged short position).</li><li><strong>Synthetic short put</strong> = sell call + buy future.</li></ol><p>Uses: creating exposure where the desired instrument is unavailable directly, and (main use) <strong>arbitrage</strong> when put-call parity breaks down.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Long gilt future trading at 114.14. 114 call premium = 0.45; 114 put premium = 0.39.<br>Buy the call, sell the put → net outlay = 0.45 − 0.39 = <strong>0.06</strong> → synthetic long future at 114.06. Actual future trades at 114.14, so <strong>sell the future</strong> against the synthetic long → locks in a risk-free gross profit of <strong>0.08</strong> (114.14 − 114.06).</p>"}, {"heading": "8. Exchange-Traded versus Over-the-Counter (OTC)", "html": "<h4>8. ETD vs OTC trade-offs (LO 8.5.8)</h4><table><tr><th></th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr><tr><td><strong>ETDs</strong></td><td>Generally more liquidity; full price transparency; counterparty credit risk limited to the clearing house (effectively almost none)</td><td>Margin payments required; standardised contracts (less flexible)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OTCs</strong></td><td>Flexible — customised to any exposure; no exchange margin required (though usually marked-to-market; collateral depends on counterparty)</td><td>Counterparty credit risk; limited price transparency; higher transaction costs</td></tr></table><p><strong>Best execution</strong> depends more on the client's broker relationship than on contract type, though ETD price transparency gives a slight edge (case-by-case).</p><p>OTC flexibility covers contract amount, maturity/expiry, price, and delivery arrangements (eg, a silver call buyer can specify delivery location) — this flexibility is priced into the premium. ETDs benefit from standardised documentation, clearing and settlement via the clearing house acting as counterparty to every trade, reducing counterparty risk and delivery costs. Post-financial-crisis regulation has pushed OTC derivatives toward more standardised clearing via <strong>central counterparties (CCPs)</strong>.</p>"}, {"heading": "9. Indirect Investment", "html": "<h4>9. Routes into derivatives investment</h4><p>Two main routes for customers wanting indirect derivatives exposure:</p><ol><li><strong>Discretionary accounts</strong> — client (usually wealthy individual) entrusts funds to a regulated firm managed per the client's objectives (possibly with instrument restrictions). Advantage: professional management. Client bears full liability for losses, potentially more than funds invested.</li><li><strong>Pooled funds (CISs)</strong> — collective investment schemes managed by regulated firms; investor's maximum loss is limited to the amount invested. Advantages: diversification (many investors), larger fund size → higher diversification and lower transaction costs. Investors pay management fees (can be high).</li></ol><h4>9.1 Investment Style of Derivatives-Based Funds</h4><ul><li><strong>Speculative</strong> — highly geared, high risk/high return; clients cannot lose more than funds invested.</li><li><strong>Guaranteed (structured)</strong> — locks investor in for a minimum period, guarantees at least a preset % of capital back (eg, 95% capital protected); typically mostly in ZCBs/cash with balance in options (eg, FTSE 100 puts for a bear fund); low risk but long lock-ins (1–10 years); opportunity cost if only capital is returned.</li><li><strong>Synthetics</strong> — replicate index performance using futures; capital in risk-free deposits + futures matching fund value; no gearing since nominal exposure is fully cash-backed.</li></ul><h4>9.2 Derivatives-Based Funds</h4><p>Prime CIS vehicle = <strong>unit trust</strong>: <strong>authorised (AUTs)</strong> (FCA-authorised, freely marketable to retail) vs <strong>unauthorised</strong> (cannot be freely advertised). CISs primarily using derivatives are generally called <strong>hedge funds</strong>. ETFs may also be heavy derivatives users (commodity funds, illiquid segments like high-yield bonds, leveraged/inverse ETFs).</p><h4>9.3 Offshore Funds</h4><p>Onshore = domiciled in investor's home country; offshore = domiciled in low-tax jurisdictions (eg, Luxembourg, Cayman Islands, British Virgin Islands, Mauritius). Offshore funds may offer greater choice/tax efficiency but weaker regulatory protection and potentially higher charges.</p>"}, {"heading": "10. Application — Different Uses", "html": "<h4>10. Client-specific use of derivatives (LO 8.5.9)</h4><h5>10.1 Private Client / Retail</h5><p>Neither professional nor eligible counterparty; receives the <strong>highest level of regulatory protection</strong> (lowest risk tolerance, limited market understanding). Use ranges from never using derivatives to limited hedging to moderate speculation. Governed by FCA (UK)/ESMA (EU)/SEC (US)/SFC (HK)/MAS (Singapore) KYC rules. Brokers restrict speculative use to ensure sufficient assets/credit to meet margin calls and naked option liabilities. Extra restrictions apply to self-managed pension accounts.</p><h5>10.2 Individual (HNWI) Portfolios</h5><p>High net worth individual (HNWI) thresholds vary: SEC ~$1m net worth (ex-primary residence) or $200k+ income p.a.; UK FCA ~£100k income or £250k net assets. Wider product range, higher risk tolerance, more speculative use (can resemble small hedge funds); still bound by broker-imposed margin/credit limits and KYC/AML/CASS rules.</p><h5>10.3 Institutional Asset Managers</h5><p>CIS fund managers hedge and speculate to enhance returns within fund-specific mandates. UCITS III widened OTC derivatives use (subject to reporting/guideline limits). Generally more conservative than hedge funds but less conservative than corporate treasurers. Also used during <strong>client transition periods</strong> (liquidate physical portfolio, use cash as margin to track the benchmark via derivatives until funds move) and where holding cash securities is problematic (custody difficulties, capital controls).</p><h5>10.4 Corporate Treasurers</h5><p>Primarily hedgers, not speculators. Uses: locking in commodity sale prices (eg, mining company sells copper futures against budgeted production), FX options to reduce currency risk on international transactions, and interest rate derivatives to manage borrowing costs/access to long-term funding.</p><h5>10.5 Hedge Funds</h5><p>Wide range of goals/risk parameters — from hedging to highly geared speculation; also used to gain short exposure where stock borrowing/securities loans are unavailable (eg, some emerging markets). UCITS III eased OTC use, subject to reporting/limits.</p><h5>10.6 Sovereign Wealth Funds (SWFs)</h5><p>Pools of money from a country's central bank reserves (budget/trade surpluses), invested for national economic benefit — stocks, bonds, property, precious metals, sometimes derivatives, plus long-term private deals (infrastructure, private equity). Purpose often diversification away from a dominant revenue source (eg, UAE and Nigeria diversifying from oil revenue). Top 3 SWFs by size (June 2022): Norway Government Pension Fund Global (~$1,338bn), China Investment Corporation (~$1,222bn), Kuwait Investment Authority (~$738bn).</p><h5>10.7 Pension Funds</h5><p>Also called superannuation funds — pooled contributions from employers/unions/organisations to fund members' retirement; large pools invested across bonds, equities, and derivatives markets.</p><h5>10.8 Sustainability and Impact Investors (ESG)</h5><p>E (environmental — avoid polluters/high carbon emitters), S (social — positive community/employee/value-chain impact), G (governance — transparency, ethical management, anti-corruption). ESG investors reduce holdings in coal power, poorly-safeguarded extractive industries, armaments, gambling/tobacco/alcohol, and opaque reporters. Carbon emission derivatives and futures on ESG equity indices (eg, CME S&amp;P 500 ESG index futures) support ESG strategies.</p><p><strong>Worked example (ESG transition hedge):</strong> A pension fund must adopt ESG principles but needs weeks to re-research hundreds of holdings before a new ESG benchmark evaluation. Interim strategy: <strong>sell E-mini S&amp;P 500 index futures</strong> against the cash equity portfolio and <strong>buy an equivalent notional of E-mini S&amp;P 500 ESG index futures</strong>. This partially hedges broad market moves while lowering/eliminating net exposure to low-ESG-scoring names, aligning performance more closely with the ESG benchmark — though not a complete solution since ESG rating agencies differ.</p><h5>10.9 Robo-Advisors</h5><p>Automate portfolio construction/management via online platforms and algorithms, based on a client's time horizon, preferences and risk tolerance — low-cost professional-style advice, increasingly incorporating AI. Example: Financial Engines (co-founded by Nobel laureate William Sharpe, of Sharpe ratio fame) was an early pioneer; Harvested Financial (US) offers systematic option strategies; Yova (Switzerland) offers customisable ESG/impact investing.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "An investor takes on new, additional risk in the derivatives market purely to profit from an anticipated price movement. Which category of derivatives user does this describe?", "options": ["Hedger", "Speculator", "Arbitrageur", "Market maker"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A speculator takes on new/extra risk to bet on the direction of an underlying's price, unlike a hedger (who reduces existing risk) or an arbitrageur (who exploits mispricing for risk-free profit)."}, {"question": "A trader sells June long gilt futures and buys September long gilt futures. This is an example of:", "options": ["An inter-market spread", "An intra-market spread", "A synthetic short future", "A vertical options spread"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Trading the same underlying (long gilt) with different expiry dates is an intra-market spread. Inter-market spreads involve different underlyings."}, {"question": "A trader buys September short sterling futures and sells September long gilt futures, anticipating a steepening of the yield curve. This is best described as:", "options": ["An intra-market spread", "An inter-market spread", "A horizontal spread", "A diagonal spread"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Different (though correlated) underlyings with the same expiry month is an inter-market spread, commonly used to trade the yield curve relationship between short- and long-term rates."}, {"question": "A fund manager wishes to switch asset allocation from equities to bonds quickly and cost-effectively, without disturbing the physical portfolio immediately. Which strategy achieves this using futures?", "options": ["Buy FTSE 100 futures and sell long gilt futures", "Sell FTSE 100 futures and buy long gilt futures", "Buy a straddle on the FTSE 100 index", "Sell a covered call on the equity portfolio"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Selling equity index futures and buying bond futures synthetically reduces equity exposure and increases bond exposure, replicating the desired reallocation while the manager continues to hold the physical shares (and their dividend income) until the actual trade is executed."}, {"question": "A fund manager holds a portfolio of CTD gilts with a nominal value of £10 million and a market value of £11,500,000. The conversion factor is 1.1214645 and the long gilt futures contract size is £100,000 nominal. How many contracts are needed to hedge the portfolio?", "options": ["100", "112", "115", "150"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Contracts = conversion factor x (nominal value of CTD portfolio / contract nominal value) = 1.1214645 x (£10m/£100,000) = 112.15 ≈ 112 contracts. Market value is not used in this formula."}, {"question": "An investor has short sold CTD gilts with a nominal value of £15 million and a market value of £15.4 million. The conversion factor is 1.12486. To hedge this position using long gilt futures, the investor should:", "options": ["Sell approximately 169 contracts", "Buy approximately 169 contracts", "Buy approximately 154 contracts", "Sell approximately 154 contracts"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Contracts = 1.12486 x (£15m/£100,000) = 168.73 ≈ 169. Since the investor is short the underlying gilts, the offsetting hedge is to buy long gilt futures."}, {"question": "In the formula N = (P x DP) / (FC x DF) used to hedge a non-CTD bond portfolio with interest rate futures, DF represents:", "options": ["The duration of the portfolio being hedged", "The duration of the underlying asset in the interest rate future", "The delivery factor of the futures contract", "The discount factor applied to accrued interest"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "DF is the duration of the underlying asset in the interest-rate future, while DP is the duration of the portfolio being hedged, P is the portfolio's nominal value and FC is the futures price."}, {"question": "A portfolio has a beta of 1.1 relative to the S&P 500, currently at 4400, and is valued at $8.0 million. The E-mini S&P 500 futures contract size is $50 x index. How many contracts are needed to fully hedge the portfolio?", "options": ["36", "40", "44", "50"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Contracts = ($8.0m / ($50 x 4400)) x 1.1 = 36.4 x 1.1 = 40 contracts (to be sold, since a long equity portfolio is hedged with a short futures position)."}, {"question": "A portfolio's beta is calculated as 0.6 relative to its benchmark index. This means the portfolio is:", "options": ["More volatile than the market", "Exactly as volatile as the market", "Less volatile than the market", "Inversely related to the market"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A beta between 0 and 1 indicates the portfolio is less volatile than the overall market. Beta > 1 = more volatile; beta = 1 = in line; beta < 0 = inverse relationship."}, {"question": "Which statement about basis risk is correct?", "options": ["Basis risk benefits hedgers and disadvantages speculators", "Basis risk is the risk that the cash and futures prices will move by different amounts", "Basis risk only arises in physically settled contracts", "Basis risk is eliminated once the hedge ratio is correctly calculated"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Basis risk is the risk that changes in the futures price differ from changes in the cash price. It disadvantages hedgers (imperfect hedge, potential accounting reclassification as speculative) but benefits speculators/arbitrageurs who can profit from basis movements."}, {"question": "A fund manager holds £15 million of UK equities and sells 250 FTSE 100 futures at 7230 to hedge, with the FTSE 100 at 7785. The next day the index falls 25 points to 7760 (portfolio value falls to £14,951,830) and the futures price falls 16 points to 7214. What is the net outcome for the hedged portfolio, given a £10 tick value on the futures?", "options": ["A gain of £40,000", "A loss of £48,170", "A loss of £8,170", "A gain of £8,170"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Futures profit = 250 x (7230-7214) x £10 = £40,000. Physical portfolio loss = £48,170. Net hedged result = £40,000 - £48,170 = -£8,170, a residual loss caused by the change in basis between the cash index and the futures price."}, {"question": "An investor holds shares in a FTSE-tracking portfolio worth £550,000 with the FTSE index at 7155 and wants to hedge against a fall using a put option with a strike of 7100 (contract size = index x £10 per point). How many put contracts should be bought?", "options": ["6", "7", "8", "10"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Each contract hedges 7100 x £10 = £71,000. £550,000 / £71,000 = 7.75, so 8 contracts are needed."}, {"question": "An aluminium producer has 100 tonnes of unsold December production and wants to hedge against a price fall by selling calls on LME primary aluminium futures (contract size 25 tonnes) at a strike of $1,900 (its budgeted sale price). How many calls should be sold, and what is the effect if the price rises above $1,900?", "options": ["4 calls; the producer benefits fully from any price rise above $1,900", "4 calls; the producer's upside is capped at $1,900 plus the premium received", "25 calls; the producer's upside is capped at $1,900", "4 calls; the producer is obligated to buy additional aluminium at $1,900"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "100 tonnes / 25 tonnes per contract = 4 calls. Selling a covered call caps the producer's benefit: once the option is exercised ITM, the producer receives only the strike price ($1,900) plus the premium collected, regardless of how high the market price rises."}, {"question": "An investor needs to buy 500oz of gold in June and is exposed to a rise in the gold price. Gold futures call options have a contract size of 100oz. To hedge this short (cash-market) exposure, the investor should:", "options": ["Sell 5 put options", "Buy 5 call options", "Buy 5 put options", "Sell 5 call options"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The investor is short gold (needs to buy later) and exposed to a price rise, so buying calls (500oz / 100oz = 5 contracts) profits if the price rises, offsetting the higher purchase cost in the cash market."}, {"question": "Compared to a futures hedge, the main drawback of using an options hedge is that:", "options": ["It cannot be used to hedge a short underlying position", "The premium paid is a cost that is never refunded, causing the hedge to underperform", "It requires daily variation margin payments", "It eliminates all upside potential"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Unlike a futures hedge (no upfront cost), an options hedge always costs the premium paid, which is never refunded, so the option hedge underperforms a futures hedge by that amount. However, it retains upside flexibility since losses on the option are capped at the premium."}, {"question": "An investor is long 1,000 ABC plc shares at 407p and sells one ABC plc 425 call for a premium of 11p (contract = 1,000 shares). This strategy is best described as a:", "options": ["Protective put", "Naked short call", "Covered short call (buy-write)", "Bull call spread"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Combining a long underlying position with a short call is a covered short call, also known as a buy-write strategy."}, {"question": "Using the covered short call example (long 1,000 ABC plc shares at 407p, short 425 call at 11p), what is the investor's maximum profit if the share price rises to and above 425p at expiry?", "options": ["11p per share", "18p per share", "29p per share", "36p per share"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Maximum profit = gain on shares (425-407=18p) + premium retained (11p) = 29p per share. Above 425p, the call is exercised and no further gains on the shares are captured."}, {"question": "Selling call options on an underlying asset that the investor does not own is known as:", "options": ["A covered short call", "A protective put", "Naked writing", "A synthetic short future"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Selling calls without owning the underlying asset is naked writing (naked short call) — a speculative position with theoretically unlimited risk, and it is illegal in several jurisdictions including the US, EU, India and Hong Kong."}, {"question": "An investor sells shares short at 407p and sells a 400 put for 20p per share (a covered short put). At what price is the investor's maximum profit achieved, and what does it consist of?", "options": ["407p; the full 20p premium plus interest on funds", "400p; the 20p premium plus interest on funds, having bought shares back at 400p", "Zero; unlimited profit as the price falls", "427p; the 20p premium alone"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Maximum profit for a covered short put occurs at the strike price (400p), where the put is exercised: the investor buys back the shares at 400p (below the 407p short-sale price) and keeps the 20p premium plus interest earned on the reserved funds."}, {"question": "A client buys a 550 call for 37 and simultaneously sells a 600 call for 19, both on the same underlying with the same expiry. This strategy is a:", "options": ["Bear call spread", "Bull call spread", "Long straddle", "Horizontal spread"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Buying the lower strike call and selling the higher strike call, same expiry, is a bull call spread — a moderately bullish strategy with capped risk and reward."}, {"question": "Using the bull call spread (buy 550 call @37, sell 600 call @19), what is the maximum loss and maximum profit?", "options": ["Max loss 18, max profit 32", "Max loss 32, max profit 18", "Max loss unlimited, max profit 18", "Max loss 18, max profit unlimited"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Net premium paid = 37-19 = 18 (max loss if price ≤550). Max profit = (600-550) - 18 = 32 (if price ≥600)."}, {"question": "A client buys a 500 put for 18p and sells a 550 put for 35p. What is the maximum profit on this bull put spread?", "options": ["18p", "33p", "17p", "50p"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Net premium received = 35p - 18p = 17p. This is the maximum profit, achieved if the price stays at or above 550p so both puts expire worthless."}, {"question": "An investor believes implied volatility on a currency pair will fall over the near term. To exploit this using a horizontal (calendar) spread, they should:", "options": ["Sell a shorter-maturity option and buy a longer-maturity option at the same strike", "Buy a shorter-maturity option and sell a longer-maturity option at the same strike", "Buy options at two different strikes with the same expiry", "Sell both a call and a put at the same strike and expiry"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "When volatility is expected to fall (or stay flat), selling the shorter-dated option and buying the longer-dated option (same strike) exploits the faster time decay of the near-dated leg."}, {"question": "A trader buys a 550 call for 37 and a 550 put for 35 on the same underlying and expiry, for a net outlay of 72. This is a:", "options": ["Long strangle", "Short straddle", "Long straddle", "Bull call spread"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Buying a call and a put at the same strike and expiry is a straddle; since both legs are bought, it is a long straddle, profiting from a large move in either direction."}, {"question": "For the long straddle in the previous question (550 call @37, 550 put @35, total premium 72), what are the upside and downside break-even points?", "options": ["Upside 587, downside 513", "Upside 622, downside 478", "Upside 600, downside 500", "Upside 659, downside 441"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Break-even = strike ± total premium = 550 + 72 = 622 (upside) and 550 - 72 = 478 (downside)."}, {"question": "A fund manager is unsure of market direction but is confident that a share price will remain close to its current level until expiry. Which strategy best expresses this view while generating premium income?", "options": ["Long straddle", "Long strangle", "Short straddle", "Protective put"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A short straddle (selling a call and put at the same strike/expiry) profits when the price stays close to the strike, as both options are likely to expire worthless and the investor keeps the premium received; however, the risk is theoretically unlimited if the price moves sharply."}, {"question": "An investor buys a 500 put for 18 and buys a 600 call for 19 on the same underlying and expiry. This position is a:", "options": ["Long straddle", "Long strangle", "Bear put spread", "Synthetic long future"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Buying a put and call with different strikes (put strike lower than call strike) but the same expiry is a strangle; since both legs are bought, it is a long strangle."}, {"question": "Compared to a long straddle, a long strangle:", "options": ["Requires less volatility to become profitable and has a higher premium cost", "Requires more volatility to become profitable and typically has a lower premium cost", "Has the same break-even points as an equivalent straddle", "Has unlimited risk and limited reward"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A strangle typically costs less in premium than an equivalent straddle (options are further OTM), but needs a larger price move to become profitable, since it must overcome both the premium cost and the gap between the two strikes."}, {"question": "A trader wants to create a synthetic long future position using options on the same underlying, strike and expiry. They should:", "options": ["Buy a call and buy a put", "Sell a call and sell a put", "Buy a call and sell a put", "Sell a call and buy a put"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A synthetic long future (also called a reversal) is created by buying a call and selling a put at the same strike and expiry."}, {"question": "The long gilt future trades at 114.14. The 114 call premium is 0.45 and the 114 put premium is 0.39. An arbitrageur buys the call, sells the put, and sells the actual future. What is the approximate risk-free gross profit locked in?", "options": ["0.06", "0.08", "0.14", "0.84"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Synthetic long future cost = 0.45 - 0.39 = 0.06, giving a synthetic entry at 114.06. Selling the actual future at 114.14 locks in a gross profit of 114.14 - 114.06 = 0.08."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a genuine advantage of exchange-traded derivatives (ETDs) over OTC derivatives?", "options": ["Greater contract flexibility to match a specific exposure", "No requirement to post margin", "Counterparty credit risk limited to the exchange's clearing house", "Lower price transparency, reducing information leakage"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Because a central clearing house is the counterparty to every ETD trade, counterparty credit risk is effectively minimal. ETDs require margin and are standardised (less flexible), while OTC products offer flexibility but carry more counterparty risk and less transparency."}, {"question": "A discretionary investment account differs from a pooled fund (CIS) primarily in that:", "options": ["In a discretionary account the client's maximum loss is limited to the amount invested, unlike a CIS", "In a discretionary account the client bears full liability for losses, potentially exceeding the amount invested, whereas CIS investor losses are capped at the amount invested", "Discretionary accounts cannot use derivatives", "CISs are always more expensive than discretionary accounts due to higher management fees"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a discretionary account, the client is liable for losses on the portfolio, which could exceed the funds invested (eg, if leveraged positions are used); in a pooled CIS, the investor's maximum loss is restricted to the amount invested."}, {"question": "A 'guaranteed' or structured fund typically achieves its capital protection objective by:", "options": ["Investing entirely in exchange-traded futures with daily margining", "Investing mainly in zero-coupon bonds/cash, with the balance used to buy options", "Shorting the underlying index continuously", "Using only OTC swaps with no options exposure"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Guaranteed/structured funds typically invest the bulk of assets in zero-coupon bonds or cash (to guarantee the return of capital or a preset percentage) and use the remaining balance to buy options for market exposure/upside."}, {"question": "A corporate treasurer at a mining company wants to lock in the future sale price of copper production when preparing next year's budget. The most appropriate action is to:", "options": ["Buy copper futures", "Sell copper futures", "Buy copper call options", "Sell copper put options only"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Corporate treasurers primarily hedge rather than speculate. Since the company will be selling copper in future, it hedges by selling copper futures now to lock in the sale price."}, {"question": "A US public pension fund needs to reduce exposure to low ESG-scoring companies in its large equity portfolio while a full stock-by-stock review is conducted. Which interim strategy best achieves this using derivatives?", "options": ["Sell E-mini S&P 500 ESG index futures and buy E-mini S&P 500 index futures", "Sell E-mini S&P 500 index futures and buy E-mini S&P 500 ESG index futures", "Buy protective puts on the entire portfolio", "Enter a long straddle on the S&P 500 ESG index"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Selling standard E-mini S&P 500 futures reduces net exposure to the broad (including low-ESG) market, while buying an equivalent notional of E-mini S&P 500 ESG index futures increases relative exposure to higher ESG-scoring names, better aligning the fund with an ESG benchmark on an interim basis."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What are the three categories of derivatives users?", "back": "Hedger (reduces existing risk), speculator (takes on new risk for profit), arbitrageur (exploits mispricing for risk-free profit)."}, {"front": "Intra-market spread vs inter-market spread — key distinction?", "back": "Intra-market = same underlying asset, different expiry dates. Inter-market = different (but usually correlated) underlying assets."}, {"front": "Name four motivations for an intra-market spread.", "back": "Anticipating basis changes, reducing risk (basis risk < price risk), arbitrage between two markets for the same product, and rolling over an existing hedge."}, {"front": "How does a fund manager switch equity exposure to bond exposure quickly using futures, without trading physical securities?", "back": "Sell equity index futures (eg, FTSE 100) and buy bond futures (eg, long gilt) — replicates the reallocation while retaining dividend income until physical trades are executed."}, {"front": "Long underlying position — how is it hedged with futures?", "back": "Sell (short) an equivalent number of futures contracts — a short hedge."}, {"front": "Short underlying position — how is it hedged with futures?", "back": "Buy (go long) an equivalent number of futures contracts — a long hedge."}, {"front": "What is the cheapest to deliver (CTD) bond, and how is it identified?", "back": "The deliverable bond in a bond futures basket that is cheapest for the short to deliver; identified as the bond with the highest implied repo rate."}, {"front": "Formula: number of contracts to hedge a CTD bond portfolio?", "back": "Number of contracts = conversion factor x (nominal value of CTD portfolio / nominal value of the contract)."}, {"front": "Worked example: CTD gilts nominal £10m, conversion factor 1.1214645, contract size £100,000 — how many contracts?", "back": "1.1214645 x (£10m/£100,000) = 112.15 ≈ 112 contracts."}, {"front": "Formula: duration-based hedge ratio for a non-CTD bond portfolio?", "back": "N = (P x DP) / (FC x DF), where P = nominal value of portfolio, DP = portfolio duration, FC = interest rate futures price, DF = duration of the underlying asset in the future."}, {"front": "Formula for beta?", "back": "Beta = Covariance(Rp, Rm) / Variance(Rm), where Rp = return of stock/portfolio, Rm = return of the market."}, {"front": "Formula: number of equity futures contracts to hedge a portfolio using beta?", "back": "Contracts = (portfolio value / (futures price x contract multiplier)) x portfolio beta."}, {"front": "Worked example: $8.0m portfolio, beta 1.1, S&P 500 at 4400, E-mini contract $50 x index — contracts needed?", "back": "($8.0m / ($50 x 4400)) x 1.1 = 36.4 x 1.1 = 40 contracts (sold to hedge a long portfolio)."}, {"front": "STIR futures hedge ratio formula?", "back": "Hedge ratio = price change in portfolio per 1bp yield change / price change in STIR futures per 1bp yield change."}, {"front": "Define basis and basis risk.", "back": "Basis = cash price − futures price. Basis risk = the risk that futures and cash prices do not move by identical amounts, causing hedge imperfection."}, {"front": "Who benefits from basis risk/volatility, and who is disadvantaged?", "back": "Speculators and arbitrageurs can profit from predicting basis changes; hedgers are disadvantaged (imperfect hedges; auditors may reclassify an inefficient hedge as speculative, hitting the P&L)."}, {"front": "What is delta-neutral hedging?", "back": "Structuring an options position so its delta exactly offsets the delta of the underlying position, creating an overall delta of zero (net immune to small price moves)."}, {"front": "Delta of a long future/underlying vs a short future/underlying?", "back": "Long future/underlying = +1. Short future/underlying = -1."}, {"front": "Approximate deltas of ATM long call and ATM long put?", "back": "ATM long call ≈ +0.5. ATM long put ≈ -0.5."}, {"front": "How is a long underlying (eg, share portfolio) position hedged using options?", "back": "Buy put options — profits if the price falls, offsetting the portfolio loss, at the cost of the (non-refundable) premium."}, {"front": "How is a short underlying (future purchase obligation) position hedged using options?", "back": "Buy call options — profits if the price rises, offsetting the higher cost of buying later, at the cost of the premium."}, {"front": "Key disadvantage of an options hedge versus a futures hedge?", "back": "The premium paid is never refunded, so the options hedge underperforms a futures hedge by the premium cost — but it retains upside flexibility since option losses are capped at the premium."}, {"front": "What is a covered short call (buy-write)?", "back": "Long the underlying + short a call option. Enhances income in a stagnant market, cushions falls by the premium, but caps upside at strike + premium."}, {"front": "Covered short call example: long ABC at 407p, sell 425 call for 11p. Maximum profit?", "back": "29p per share = (425-407) share gain of 18p + 11p premium, achieved at/above 425p."}, {"front": "What is naked writing / a naked short call, and why is it restricted?", "back": "Selling call options without owning the underlying asset — a speculative position with theoretically unlimited risk; illegal in the US, EU, India and Hong Kong due to settlement/market-disruption risk."}, {"front": "What is a covered short put?", "back": "Writing a put while holding sufficient cash to buy the asset (or already short the underlying/futures) and writing a put on it. Enhances returns on funds/short position in a stagnant market."}, {"front": "Covered short put example: short ABC at 407p, sell 400 put for 20p. Maximum profit and where achieved?", "back": "Maximum profit at 400p (or below): 20p premium + interest on funds, having bought the shares back at 400p (effective cost 380p)."}, {"front": "Three types of options spreads and their defining feature?", "back": "Vertical = different strikes, same expiry. Horizontal (calendar) = same strike, different expiries. Diagonal = different strikes AND different expiries."}, {"front": "Bull call spread: construction and max risk/reward?", "back": "Buy lower strike call, sell higher strike call. Max risk = net premium paid. Max reward = difference in strikes − net premium paid."}, {"front": "Bull call spread worked example: buy 550 call @37, sell 600 call @19 — max loss, max profit, break-even?", "back": "Max loss = 18 (net premium paid). Max profit = 32 ((600-550)-18). Break-even = 568 (550+18)."}, {"front": "Bear call spread: construction, and what is received/paid?", "back": "Sell lower strike call, buy higher strike call — net premium is received. Max risk = strike difference − premium received; max reward = premium received."}, {"front": "Horizontal (calendar) spread — when volatility is expected to fall, what's the construction?", "back": "Sell the shorter-maturity option, buy the longer-maturity option, same strike — exploits faster time decay of the near-dated leg."}, {"front": "Straddle vs strangle — key structural difference?", "back": "Straddle = call and put at the SAME strike (and expiry). Strangle = call and put at DIFFERENT strikes (put lower, call higher), same expiry."}, {"front": "Long straddle: max risk, max reward, break-even?", "back": "Max risk = total premiums paid. Max reward = unlimited. Break-even = strike ± total premium paid."}, {"front": "Long straddle example: 550 call @37 + 550 put @35 (total 72) — upside/downside break-even?", "back": "Upside = 550+72 = 622. Downside = 550-72 = 478."}, {"front": "Short straddle: max risk, max reward?", "back": "Max risk = unlimited. Max reward = limited to the sum of premiums received (achieved at the strike price, where both options are abandoned)."}, {"front": "Long strangle vs long straddle — which needs more volatility to profit, and why?", "back": "A strangle needs MORE volatility, because the price must move enough to cover both the premium cost and the gap between the two strike prices before profit is achieved."}, {"front": "List the six basic synthetic positions.", "back": "Synthetic long future (buy call + sell put) = reversal; synthetic short future (sell call + buy put) = conversion; synthetic long call (buy future + buy put); synthetic short call (sell future + sell put); synthetic long put (buy call + sell future); synthetic short put (sell call + buy future)."}, {"front": "Synthetic arbitrage example: long gilt future at 114.14, 114 call 0.45, 114 put 0.39 — trade and profit?", "back": "Buy call, sell put (net cost 0.06) = synthetic long at 114.06; sell the actual future at 114.14 to lock in a risk-free gross profit of 0.08."}, {"front": "Main advantage of ETDs over OTC derivatives regarding counterparty risk?", "back": "ETDs clear through a central clearing house which is counterparty to every trade, making counterparty credit risk effectively minimal; OTC trades carry direct counterparty credit risk."}, {"front": "Main advantage of OTC derivatives over ETDs?", "back": "Flexibility — contracts can be customised in amount, maturity, price and delivery terms to fit any specific exposure; ETDs are standardised."}, {"front": "Two main routes for indirect (customer) investment in derivatives?", "back": "1) Discretionary accounts (client liable for losses, potentially beyond funds invested). 2) Pooled funds/CISs (investor's maximum loss capped at the amount invested)."}, {"front": "Three investment styles of derivatives-based funds?", "back": "Speculative (highly geared, high risk/return); Guaranteed/structured (capital-protected via ZCBs/cash + options, long lock-ins); Synthetic (replicate an index using futures, cash-backed, no gearing)."}, {"front": "Authorised unit trust (AUT) vs unauthorised unit trust?", "back": "AUTs are FCA-authorised and can be freely advertised/marketed to private customers; unauthorised unit trusts cannot."}, {"front": "How do corporate treasurers typically use derivatives?", "back": "Primarily as hedgers, not speculators — eg, selling commodity futures to lock in sale prices, buying FX options to reduce currency risk, using interest rate derivatives to manage borrowing costs."}, {"front": "How do private (retail) clients' derivatives use typically compare to HNWIs?", "back": "Retail clients get the highest regulatory protection and typically use derivatives minimally/for limited hedging; HNWIs have wider product access and higher risk tolerance, using derivatives more speculatively."}]}, {"id": "der-ch9", "number": 9, "title": "Regulatory Requirements", "examWeight": "6 of 100 questions (~6%)", "summaryHtml": "<p>Chapter 9 surveys the global regulatory architecture for derivatives markets and the UK/EU/US conduct-of-business framework that governs firms dealing in them. Section 1 (Regulation) maps the main market regulators by region &mdash; Europe (EBA, ESMA, EIOPA; UK's PRA/FCA), Asia (China's CSRC, Hong Kong's SFC, Singapore's MAS, Japan's FSA), the US (SEC, CFTC, NFA, FINRA, Federal Reserve), Latin America and the Middle East/Africa &mdash; and introduces two influential non-statutory bodies: IOSCO (whose members regulate over 95% of global securities markets) and ISDA (creator of the ISDA Master Agreement and FpML). It covers MiFID/MiFID II passporting, the UK's post-Brexit position (UK MiFID transposition effective 11pm 31 December 2020, with limited clearing-house/CSD equivalence extended to 30 June 2028), and ISDA's Operations Steering Committee (OSC) initiatives on clearing and collateral. Section 2 (Different Regulatory Approaches) contrasts principles-based regulation (UK/EU) with rules-based regulation (US, exemplified by Sarbanes-Oxley), and details the FCA's Principles for Businesses, Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC), COBS client categorisation (retail, professional, eligible counterparty), best execution factors, and the distinction between suitability and appropriateness assessments. Section 3 (Principal Differences Between UK, EU and US Regulators) drills into UK specifics &mdash; the FCA/PRA split (Financial Services Act 2012), CASS client money/asset rules, margining practices, and access to overseas markets/clients &mdash; EU specifics (EMIR's reporting, risk-management and central-clearing obligations for OTC derivatives) and US specifics (the SEC's narrow options-only remit versus the CFTC/NFA's regulation of all futures and remaining options, and the Commodity Futures Modernization Act 2000). Candidates should memorise the regulator-to-market/product mapping, key numeric thresholds (&euro;500,000 portfolio test, 10 transactions/quarter, 10%/&euro;5m sanctions penalties, CASS retention periods), and which body regulates which instrument in the US, as these distinctions are heavily tested.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "9.1 Regulation – Main Market Regulators", "html": "<p>This chapter covers the role of market regulators in derivatives markets globally, followed by a review of UK, EU and US regulation and international accounting rules.</p><p><strong>Main market regulators by region:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Europe:</strong> European Banking Authority (EBA); European Securities and Markets Authority (ESMA); European Insurance and Occupational Pensions Authority (EIOPA); UK &ndash; Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA) and Financial Policy Committee (FPC) (both part of the Bank of England), and the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA).</li><li><strong>Asia:</strong> China &ndash; China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC); Hong Kong &ndash; Securities and Futures Commission (SFC); India &ndash; Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI); Indonesia &ndash; Financial Services Authority (OJK); Japan &ndash; Financial Services Authority (FSA); Singapore &ndash; Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS); South Korea &ndash; Financial Services Commission (FSC).</li><li><strong>US:</strong> Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC); Federal Reserve System (the Fed); Financial Industry Regulatory Authority (FINRA); National Futures Association (NFA); Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC).</li><li><strong>Latin America:</strong> Argentina &ndash; CNV; Brazil &ndash; CVM; Mexico &ndash; SHCP.</li><li><strong>Middle East and Africa:</strong> Dubai &ndash; DFSA; Saudi Arabia &ndash; CMA; South Africa &ndash; FSCA, PA, SARB; Nigeria &ndash; SEC, CBN; Kenya &ndash; CBA (Capital Markets Authority), CBK.</li></ul><p><strong>Purpose and aims of regulation (globally):</strong></p><ul><li>Maintain and promote fairness, efficiency, competitiveness, transparency and orderliness of securities and futures industries.</li><li>Promote public understanding of the industry, including defining knowledge thresholds for participation and providing education (directly or via third parties).</li><li>Provide protection for members of the public investing in or holding financial products.</li><li>Minimise crime and misconduct, and reduce systemic risk.</li></ul><p>Example: Russia's foreign direct investment fell from a peak of ~$75 billion in 2008 to $9.5 billion in 2020, partly due to regulatory instability (President Putin disbanded the Federal Financial Markets Service in 2013, replacing it with the Central Bank of the Russian Federation). Since February 2022 Russian markets are largely inaccessible to US/European investors.</p><p><strong>Self-regulatory organisations (SROs):</strong> nationally-licensed exchanges are usually SROs. They do not explicitly enforce securities/derivatives laws, but their duty is to maintain market integrity.</p><p><strong>IOSCO</strong> (International Organization of Securities Commissions) &ndash; an association of securities commissions/financial regulators; members regulate more than 95% of the global securities markets. Its three main goals: (1) promote high standards of regulation for orderly and efficient markets; (2) share information among exchanges and assist with technical/operational issues; (3) establish standards for monitoring global investment transactions across borders and markets.</p><p><strong>ISDA</strong> (International Swaps and Derivatives Association) &ndash; a trade organisation of the main OTC derivatives market participants. Key accomplishments: the ISDA Master Agreement (a standardised contract used for a wide range of derivatives transactions) and management of FpML, an XML message standard for the OTC derivatives industry.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.1.2 UK and the EU", "html": "<p>The EU has introduced extensive rules on conduct of business and operational/risk management via MiFID II, MiFIR and EMIR. Each country also has its own national regulator (e.g. PRA and FCA in the UK).</p><p><strong>Passporting (pre-Brexit):</strong> authorisation by one EU member state (the <em>home state</em>) allowed a firm to provide investment services in other member states (<em>host states</em>) without further authorisation &ndash; the 'passport'.</p><p><strong>Post-Brexit:</strong> the UK's transition period ended December 2020. Barring few exceptions, UK firms now need EU subsidiaries to serve EU clients. As of April 2023, the only exceptions granted by the EU are <strong>limited passporting/'equivalence'</strong> in two narrow areas: clearing houses and securities depositories. This temporary equivalence was agreed in 2018, renewed three times (most recently in 2025), and runs until <strong>30 June 2028</strong>.</p><p><strong>UK MiFID:</strong> the UK achieved regulatory equivalence by fully transposing EU MiFID via the FCA Handbook, Treasury rules and Parliamentary legislation, implemented from the end of the Brexit transition period (<strong>11pm on 31 December 2020</strong>).</p><p><strong>MiFID's passportable activities include:</strong> investment advice; some underwriting activities; operating an MTF, OTF or systematic internaliser (SI); investment activities relating to commodity derivatives, credit derivatives and CFDs; and investment research (as an ancillary service). MiFID also sets out the Capital Requirements Directive (CRD), which sets a firm's regulatory capital requirements.</p><p><strong>MiFID II:</strong> voted through by the European Parliament in April 2014; implemented January 2018 (replacing the original 2004 MiFID). Key amendments include:</p><ul><li>Expanded scope: more coverage of credit institutions, algorithmic trading, emissions contracts, custodians; narrowed licensing exceptions for proprietary trading and commodity-derivatives participants.</li><li>Non-EU/EEA investment firms can gain a European passport, partly via establishing an EU/EEA branch for non-professional clients.</li><li>Local authorities reclassified as non-professional investors; more obligations for eligible counterparty business.</li><li>Greater investor protection: more disclosure of advice sources/conflicts/fee disaggregation; tighter KYC exceptions for execution-only business.</li><li>Additional corporate governance requirements (nomination committees, diversity guidelines).</li><li>Mandatory recording of telephone conversations and electronic communications.</li><li>In-scope trading venues now include OTFs, with more obligations across MTFs, OTFs and SIs.</li><li>Mandatory exchange trading of derivatives, in line with EMIR.</li><li>Regulators can require reduction of a derivatives position or limit entry into a commodity derivative, and can prohibit/restrict marketing, distribution or sale of instruments/activities.</li><li>Minimum sanctions for breach of international financial sanctions: <strong>at least 10% of total annual turnover</strong> for a legal entity, and <strong>&euro;5,000,000</strong> for a natural person (or twice the benefit derived from the violation, if greater).</li></ul><p>Not all FCA-authorised firms are subject to MiFID II &ndash; broadly, MiFID firms are those: dealing/managing investments; operating an MTF, OTF and/or SI; and certain venture capital firms.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.1.3 The Operations Steering Committee (OSC)", "html": "<p>The OSC of ISDA aims to improve the operational infrastructure and regulation of the OTC derivatives industry. Membership includes OTC broker-dealers, buy-side firms and industry associations (including ISDA itself).</p><ul><li>Committed to regulators to increase the number/types of OTC products centrally cleared, e.g. single-name credit default swaps (CDSs) and overnight index swaps (OISs).</li><li>Increased emphasis on collateral management: implemented daily reconciliation of all collateralised inter-dealer positions in excess of <strong>500 positions</strong>.</li><li>Set a goal that <strong>at least 50%</strong> of equity derivatives traded can be matched, processed and cleared electronically (equity derivatives are among the least standardised OTC products, making this difficult).</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "9.1.4 European Regulators – EBA and ESMA", "html": "<p><strong>9.1.4.1 European Banking Authority (EBA):</strong> one of three European Supervisory Authorities (ESAs) &ndash; the other two are ESMA and EIOPA. The EBA conducts stress tests on EU banks to increase transparency and identify weaknesses in banks' capital structures. It can overrule national regulators that fail to properly regulate their banks and can prevent regulatory arbitrage, helping banks compete fairly across the EU. Other tasks: investigating incorrect/insufficient application of EU law by national authorities; mediating cross-border disputes between competent authorities; promoting transparency, simplicity and fairness in the market.</p><p><strong>9.1.4.2 European Securities and Markets Authority (ESMA):</strong> establishes an EU-wide financial markets overseer by assessing risks to investors, markets and financial stability; composes a single rulebook for EU financial markets (creating a level playing field for investors/issuers); promotes supervisory convergence among EU members; directly supervises credit rating agencies and trade repositories. <strong>Supervisory convergence</strong> = consistent implementation/application of the same rules using similar approaches across member states, sharing best practices and realising efficiency gains. As part of the European System of Financial Supervision (ESFS), ESMA also conducts peer reviews, sets EU-wide market data reporting requirements, thematic studies, common work programmes, and drafts opinions, guidelines and Q&amp;As.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.1.5 Asia – China, Hong Kong, Singapore", "html": "<p><strong>9.1.5.1 China:</strong> the China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC) is a ministerial-level public institution created by China's first comprehensive securities law (effective 1999), granting it authority to implement centralised, unified regulation of the nationwide securities market. Its functions are similar to the SEC's. Responsibilities: creating/reviewing securities legislation; regulating trading, issuing and settlement of stocks, fixed income and securities funds; supervising shareholder/broker conduct; overseeing non-Chinese company listings (e.g. H-Shares on the Hong Kong Exchange).</p><p><strong>9.1.5.2 Hong Kong:</strong> the Securities and Futures Commission (SFC) is the independent statutory body regulating Hong Kong's securities and futures markets, protecting investors and promoting Hong Kong as an international financial centre. It is one of four Hong Kong financial regulators, alongside the Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA), Office of the Commissioner of Insurance (OCI) and Mandatory Provident Fund Schemes Authority (MPFA). The SFC covers the Hong Kong Exchange and Clearing (HKEX).</p><p><strong>9.1.5.3 Singapore:</strong> the Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) is both Singapore's central bank and its financial regulator, covering money, banking, insurance, securities and currency issuance. MAS regulates market operators as either <strong>approved exchanges (AE)</strong> &ndash; systemically important operators subject to higher statutory obligations &ndash; or <strong>recognised market operators (RMO)</strong>. Dealing in exchange-traded and/or OTC derivatives, and spot FX for leveraged FX trading, is a regulated activity under Singapore's Securities and Futures Act; firms must apply for a <strong>Capital Markets Services licence</strong>.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.2.1 Principles-Based Regulation", "html": "<p>Principles-based regulation moves away from detailed, prescriptive rules towards high-level principles setting standards for firm conduct. The FCA's approach (continuing from its predecessor, the FSA) has three key elements: broad-based standards over detailed rules; outcomes-based regulation; and increasing senior management responsibility.</p><p>Proponents argue it suits fast-changing markets/technology and that outcomes-based regulation applies well to client-facing services &ndash; poor treatment of customers, failure on best execution, or unsuitable sales will drive clients to other firms, damaging reputation and performance.</p><p><strong>Rules-based regulation</strong> (the US approach) is based on strict, clearly defined but inflexible one-size-fits-all rules. Principles-based regulation allows flexibility and judgement via broadly based guidelines, though critics say this can cause confusion.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.2.2 FCA Principles for Businesses", "html": "<p>The FCA's Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS) sets out the Principles for Businesses, which authorised firms must observe; breach exposes a firm to disciplinary sanctions. The Principles are:</p><ol><li><strong>Integrity</strong> &ndash; conduct business with integrity.</li><li><strong>Skill, care and diligence</strong> &ndash; conduct business with due skill, care and diligence.</li><li><strong>Management and control</strong> &ndash; organise and control affairs responsibly and effectively, with adequate risk management systems.</li><li><strong>Financial prudence</strong> &ndash; maintain adequate financial resources.</li><li><strong>Market conduct</strong> &ndash; observe proper standards of market conduct.</li><li><strong>Customers' interests</strong> &ndash; pay due regard to customers' interests and treat them fairly.</li><li><strong>Communication with clients</strong> &ndash; communicate information clearly, fairly and not misleadingly.</li><li><strong>Conflicts of interest</strong> &ndash; manage conflicts of interest fairly.</li><li><strong>Customers: relationships of trust</strong> &ndash; ensure suitability of advice/discretionary decisions for customers entitled to rely on the firm's judgement.</li><li><strong>Clients' assets</strong> &ndash; arrange adequate protection for clients' assets when responsible for them.</li><li><strong>Relations with regulators</strong> &ndash; deal with regulators openly and cooperatively, disclosing appropriately to the FCA.</li><li><strong>Consumer Duty</strong> &ndash; act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers.</li></ol>"}, {"heading": "9.2.3 Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)", "html": "<p>COBS reflects an overriding theme of fair play, recognising the information imbalance between firms (higher expertise) and customers. This is reinforced by the FCA's Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) initiative &ndash; originally called Treating Customers Fairly (TCF), launched by the FSA. The FCA defines six consumer outcomes:</p><ol><li>Consumers can be confident they deal with firms where fair treatment is central to corporate culture.</li><li>Products/services are designed to meet the needs of identified consumer groups and targeted accordingly.</li><li>Consumers receive clear information and are kept appropriately informed before, during and after the point of sale.</li><li>Advice given is suitable and takes account of consumers' circumstances.</li><li>Products perform as firms have led consumers to expect, with an acceptable standard of associated service.</li><li>Consumers do not face unreasonable post-sale barriers to changing product, switching provider, claiming or complaining.</li></ol><p>Good practice for advisers: make FTOC central to business philosophy; treat it as a non-delegable responsibility; evaluate compliance consultants' advice against firm-specific customer knowledge; keep knowledge/training/competence up to date; stay aware of market and regulatory changes.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.2.4 Client Categorisation", "html": "<p>A firm carrying on designated investment business must categorise its clients so appropriate protections/rules apply. COBS defines 'client' as anyone to whom a firm provides, intends to provide, or has provided a service in a regulated activity (plus ancillary services for MiFID/equivalent third-country business); for financial promotions, 'client' includes anyone to whom a promotion is/is likely to be communicated.</p><p><strong>Categories under COBS:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Retail client</strong> &ndash; any client that is not a professional client or eligible counterparty ('customer' = retail + professional clients).</li><li><strong>Professional client</strong> &ndash; elective (chosen) or per se. Per se professional clients include: entities required to be authorised/regulated in financial markets; large undertakings (meeting balance sheet/turnover/own funds thresholds); governments, public bodies, central banks, international/supranational institutions; institutional investors whose main business is investing in financial instruments.</li><li><strong>Eligible counterparty (ECP)</strong> &ndash; e.g. credit institutions, investment firms, pension funds, national governments, central banks. Narrower than per se professional (excludes large undertakings). Retail clients get the highest protection; ECPs get none.</li></ol><p><strong>Records:</strong> firms must implement written policies/procedures for categorisation and record each notice/agreement, retained for: <strong>indefinitely</strong> (pensions business), <strong>5 years</strong> (insurance or MiFID business), <strong>3 years</strong> (any other business), after the firm ceases business with that client.</p><p><strong>Recategorisation (retail → elective professional):</strong> requires both:</p><ul><li><strong>Qualitative test</strong> &ndash; the firm assesses the client's expertise/experience/knowledge and believes they can make their own investment decisions and understand the risks.</li><li><strong>Quantitative test</strong> &ndash; at least <strong>two</strong> of: (a) average of <strong>10 significantly sized transactions per quarter</strong> over the past four quarters on the relevant market; (b) financial portfolio (cash deposits + financial instruments) exceeding <strong>&euro;500,000</strong>; (c) at least <strong>one year</strong> working in a professional financial services role requiring relevant knowledge.</li></ul><p>If a firm learns a client no longer meets the conditions for elective professional status, it must take appropriate action (e.g. recategorise as retail and notify the client).</p><p><strong>Professional → elective ECP:</strong> the client must be a per se professional client (not one that is only professional due to being an institutional investor), or an elective professional client requesting ECP status (only for services where professional treatment applied), and must expressly agree to be treated as an ECP. ECPs can also opt down to professional or retail status. Recategorisation can be general or transaction-specific; different classifications can apply per instrument, though this adds internal complexity.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.2.5 Best Execution", "html": "<p>Best execution rules apply to both MiFID and non-MiFID firms/business. Under COBS, firms must take all reasonable steps to obtain the best possible result for clients when executing orders, considering: <strong>price, cost, speed, likelihood of execution and settlement, size, nature</strong> or any other relevant consideration. Best execution is not simply about achieving the best price &ndash; other factors may be given precedence depending on circumstances.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.2.6 Suitability and Appropriateness", "html": "<p>These are separate assessments.</p><p><strong>Suitability</strong> &ndash; a thorough evaluation covering a prospective client's product knowledge, financial situation and investment objectives, applied when firms: make personal recommendations relating to designated investments, or manage investments. To assess suitability a firm must establish/consider the client's: investment objectives; knowledge and experience relevant to the specific investment/service; and the level of investment risk they can bear financially. The firm needs reasonable basis to believe the service/transaction meets objectives, carries risk the client can bear, and carries risk the client has knowledge/experience to understand. Knowledge/experience assessment covers: familiar services/transactions/investments; nature, volume, frequency and period of involvement; and education/profession/former profession. (Special rules exist for basic advice on stakeholder products, which firms may apply instead of general suitability rules.)</p><p><strong>Appropriateness</strong> &ndash; a narrower assessment of whether a client has sufficient knowledge/experience in specific financial instruments, particularly ones not on the MiFID/FCA list of 'particular financial instruments' (e.g. CME wheat options).</p><p><strong>Instruments exempt from the appropriateness assessment</strong> (the simplest/most liquid): shares on a regulated market or equivalent third-country market; money market instruments, bonds or other securitised debt without embedded derivatives; UCITS fund holdings; other non-complex investments. A financial instrument is <strong>non-complex</strong> if: it is not a derivative; it has sufficient liquidity; it does not involve client liability exceeding the cost of acquisition; and comprehensive information is publicly available.</p><p>Firms need not assess appropriateness where: the service is execution-only or receipt/transmission of orders, for particular financial instruments, at the client's initiative; the client has been clearly informed the firm need not assess appropriateness (and so loses that protection); and the firm complies with conflicts-of-interest obligations. For derivatives, an assessment of suitability or appropriateness will always be necessary.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.3 Principal Differences Between UK, EU and US Regulators", "html": "<p>EU/UK regulators follow principles-based approaches (e.g. MiFID), while US regulators follow a rules-based approach. Principles-based regulation allows flexibility/judgement via broad guidelines, relying on high-level principles rather than detailed prescriptive rules. Rules-based regulation applies strict, clearly defined, inflexible one-size-fits-all rules.</p><p>Example: MiFID allows individual member states to opt out of certain aspects. In contrast, the US <strong>Sarbanes-Oxley Act</strong> sets strict rules on how companies manage and report derivatives use/exposure &ndash; a rules-based approach. Its fallout has seen US exchanges lose international company listings to UK/European exchanges due to the differing regulatory environments.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.3.1 UK Regulators", "html": "<p>From 2001&ndash;2013 the Financial Services Authority (FSA) regulated UK financial services (Treasury-appointed board, operationally independent, funded by industry fees). Following perceived regulatory failure in the global financial crisis, the <strong>Financial Services Act 2012</strong> abolished the FSA, splitting its responsibilities between the <strong>FCA</strong> and the <strong>PRA</strong>.</p><p><strong>FCA</strong> &ndash; ensures fair competition and firm soundness; reports to Parliament but is industry-funded; focused on a healthy, competitive retail sector. Its focus areas: <strong>conduct</strong> (ethical behaviour, meeting standards), <strong>competition</strong> (healthy competition to support growth) and <strong>reducing market abuse</strong> (procedures to identify/stop insider dealing, price manipulation). It covers all UK financial markets including derivatives and works closely with EU counterparts on MiFID II and international regulation. Approximately <strong>70%</strong> of the FCA's policies are based on EU initiatives, particularly the Financial Services Action Plan &ndash; notably MiFID II and the <strong>Transparency Directive (TD)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Transparency Directive (TD):</strong> enhances transparency on EU capital markets via minimum periodic financial reporting requirements and disclosure of major shareholdings for issuers on EU regulated markets, plus rules on storing/disseminating this information. It distinguishes home state (can impose more stringent disclosure) from host state (cannot). Three broad areas: (1) minimum content of annual, half-yearly and interim management statements; (2) notification requirements for issuers/investors on acquisition/disposal of major holdings; (3) methods of disseminating/storing this information pan-EU.</p><p><strong>PRA</strong> &ndash; UK's prudential regulator for deposit-takers (banks, building societies, credit unions), insurers and designated investment firms. Powers derive from the Financial Services and Markets Act (FSMA) 2000 (as amended by the Financial Services Act 2012) and relevant EU directives. Focused on financial soundness; not preventing all firm failure, but ensuring failures don't disrupt critical financial services. Its role in derivatives regulation is mainly indirect.</p><p><strong>9.3.1.1 Client Money and Client Accounts:</strong> FCA rules on custody/safeguarding of client money and assets are in the <strong>Client Assets Sourcebook (CASS)</strong>, ensuring client money is segregated from firm money (so it is protected if the firm fails) &ndash; usually via a designated client money account with a bank.</p><ul><li><strong>CASS 6</strong> &ndash; custody rules for financial instruments held during MiFID business, and for safeguarding/administering investments in non-MiFID business.</li><li><strong>CASS 7</strong> &ndash; client money and distribution rules, applying when a MiFID firm holds client money in MiFID business or opts to apply MiFID client money rules to non-MiFID business.</li></ul><p>Client money rules do not apply, e.g., to delivery versus payment (DvP) transactions (unless not settled by close of business on the <strong>third business day</strong> following payment/delivery obligation), or money already due and payable to the firm; bank deposits are also exempt. Firms not depositing with a central bank must exercise due skill, care and diligence in selecting/reviewing the credit institution/bank/money market fund, and must record the grounds for their selection.</p><p><strong>Reconciliation of client assets:</strong> firms must be able to distinguish, at any time without delay, one client's safe custody assets from another's and from the firm's own. <strong>CASS 6.5 and 7.6</strong> require internal and external reconciliations 'as often as necessary', ideally by someone not involved in producing/maintaining the records. Discrepancies must be made good by the responsible party; the FCA must be informed without delay of any reconciliation failures.</p><p><strong>Reconciliation of client money (CASS 7.6):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Internal reconciliation</strong> &ndash; cross-checks client entitlement records against records of client money held in client bank/transaction accounts, performed as often as necessary and as soon as reasonably practicable. The FCA's prescribed approach is the 'standard method of client reconciliation'.</li><li><strong>External reconciliation</strong> &ndash; cross-checks internal client money accounts against third-party (e.g. bank) records, as often as necessary. Discrepancies must be corrected as soon as possible; if the firm should be holding more client money, it must top up the client bank account pending resolution. Firms must inform the FCA in writing 'without delay' if unable to comply (timeframe per <strong>CASS 7.15</strong>). Adequate external reconciliation = matching the client bank account and each client transaction account balance to the bank's/counterparty's statement or confirmation.</li></ul><p><strong>Exemptions from CASS</strong> (among others): incoming EEA firms (other than insurers) for passported activities; money transferred under title transfer collateral arrangements; money held for DvP transactions (unless unsettled after three business days); money due and payable to the firm; and business conducted by a firm in its own name but on the client's behalf where the transaction's nature requires this and the client agrees. Custody rules (CASS 6) also do not apply where a client transfers full ownership of a safe custody asset to a firm to secure/cover obligations. Specific CASS rules may be disapplied depending on a firm's activities.</p><p><strong>9.3.1.2 Margining Practices:</strong> the <strong>initial margin</strong> is required to open a position; the <strong>maintenance requirement</strong> is the (generally lower) minimum collateral required to keep a position open, allowing price movement before a margin call is triggered. For especially risky instruments, regulators/exchanges/brokers may set the maintenance requirement higher than normal, or equal to the initial requirement, to reduce risk exposure. Variation margin amounts are determined by the exchange and clearing house (e.g. LCH in the UK).</p><p><strong>9.3.1.3 Access to International Markets:</strong> investors can undertake derivatives on any regulated market provided they have suitable arrangements with local agents (bank, securities account, broker-dealer). Subject to local regulatory requirements, investors are not barred from trading derivatives simply due to not being domiciled in that country.</p><p><strong>9.3.1.4 Access to International Clients:</strong> UK-authorised/regulated firms may engage with non-UK clients. What matters is where the legal agreement is signed and where the relationship exists, determining the extent of host-state conduct of business requirements. Local regulatory requirements may still apply even where services are provided from the UK. Firms must still classify the client, perform KYC, adhere to FCA suitability/appropriateness rules, and provide relevant information on charges and risk warnings.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.3.2 EU Regulators – EMIR", "html": "<p>EMIR is the EU regulation governing OTC derivatives markets, establishing central counterparties (CCPs) and trade repositories (TRs), operating alongside national regulatory authorities (NRAs). It was introduced to improve transparency and reduce risk in OTC derivatives markets following the 2007&ndash;08 financial crisis, and establishes common organisational, conduct-of-business and prudential standards for CCPs and TRs.</p><p>EMIR requires anyone entering into any form of derivative contract (interest rate, FX, equity, credit, commodity) to:</p><ul><li><strong>Report</strong> every derivative contract entered into to a trade repository.</li><li>Implement <strong>new risk management standards</strong> (operational processes and margining) for all bilateral OTC derivatives (i.e. trades not cleared by a CCP).</li><li><strong>Clear via a CCP</strong> those OTC derivatives subject to a mandatory clearing obligation.</li></ul><p>EMIR tightened operational areas including confirmations, collateral management, portability, reconciliations, dispute resolution and portfolio compression. ISDA terms/protocols continue to operate in parallel with EMIR (as they have for over 35 years).</p><p>Crucially EMIR introduced: (1) a <strong>reporting obligation</strong> for OTC derivatives (greater transparency, all trades registered with a TR); and (2) <strong>central counterparties</strong> for OTC derivative trades (greater market safety by reducing counterparty credit and operational risk).</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong> applies to all types of OTC derivatives; to financial firms using OTC derivatives; to non-financial firms with large OTC derivatives positions; and to CCPs and trade repositories themselves.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.3.3 US Regulators – SEC, CFTC and NFA", "html": "<p><strong>9.3.3.1 Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC):</strong> despite being a major US securities regulator, the SEC has a limited derivatives role, regulating only:</p><ul><li>Options on currencies traded on exchanges (e.g. Philadelphia Stock Exchange), including electronic exchanges.</li><li>Options on individual stocks.</li><li>Options on stock indices.</li><li>The Chicago Board Options Exchange (CBOE) &ndash; a major equity-options centre.</li><li>The International Securities Exchange (ISE) &ndash; the world's largest equity options exchange, part of the Nasdaq Group.</li></ul><p>The SEC does <strong>not</strong> regulate futures products or exchanges &ndash; that is the role of the CFTC and NFA.</p><p><strong>9.3.3.2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) and National Futures Association (NFA):</strong> the CFTC regulates all on-exchange derivatives transactions not covered by the SEC &ndash; all futures products/exchanges (including PHLX) and all options not covered by the SEC (including currency options on the CME). It draws its powers from the <strong>Commodity Exchange Act (CEA)</strong>. The CFTC oversees the <strong>NFA</strong>, a self-regulatory organisation registered by the CFTC and formed in <strong>1982</strong>, to which the CFTC delegates powers to oversee derivatives industry firms.</p><p>The <strong>Commodity Futures Modernization Act 2000 (CFMA)</strong> created a more flexible futures-regulation structure, formalising an agreement between the CFTC and SEC that repealed the 18-year-old ban on trading single stock futures, and provided legal guidelines for OTC derivatives markets. It reauthorised the CFTC for five years, clarified the Treasury Amendment exclusion, and specifically granted the CFTC authority over retail foreign exchange trading. It legalised trading in single stock and narrowly defined stock-index futures under the joint jurisdiction of the SEC and CFTC.</p><p>A <strong>single stock future (SSF)</strong> is a futures contract on an individual share, with a set maturity and share amount; most US SSFs (traded on the <strong>OneChicago</strong> exchange) settle by physical delivery. SSFs are also traded in India, Kenya, Spain, South Africa and the UK.</p><p>The NFA safeguards the integrity of derivatives markets; it is a not-for-profit organisation, financed solely by membership dues, fees and assessments from futures market users. Every firm/individual conducting public business on a US futures exchange must be registered with the CFTC and be an NFA member (registration and clearing being a key part of the process). As of <strong>31 March 2023</strong>, the NFA had <strong>3,036 members</strong> and <strong>42,163 associate members</strong> (salespersons employed by NFA member firms).</p><p>Learning objective 9.1.8 also references the prohibitions of <strong>CFTC Part 30</strong> (Foreign Futures and Segregation of Customer Funds), which governs the regulatory treatment of foreign futures business and the segregation of customer funds in that context.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "IOSCO members currently regulate what proportion of the world's securities markets?", "options": ["More than 75%", "More than 85%", "More than 95%", "100%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "IOSCO members regulate more than 95% of the global securities markets, making it the most comprehensive international standard-setting body for securities regulation."}, {"question": "Which ISDA accomplishment is described as a standardised contract used across a wide range of OTC derivatives transactions?", "options": ["FpML", "The ISDA Master Agreement", "MiFIR", "The EMIR reporting template"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The ISDA Master Agreement is ISDA's standardised contract framework for OTC derivatives; FpML is ISDA's separate XML messaging standard."}, {"question": "Under EMIR, what must counterparties implement for bilateral OTC derivative trades that are NOT cleared by a central counterparty?", "options": ["Mandatory exchange trading", "New risk management standards, including operational processes and margining", "Exemption from any reporting obligation", "Automatic novation to a trade repository"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "EMIR requires new risk management standards, including operational processes and margining, for all bilateral (i.e. non-CCP-cleared) OTC derivatives."}, {"question": "Under EMIR, every derivative contract entered into (interest rate, FX, equity, credit or commodity) must be:", "options": ["Reported to a trade repository", "Reported only if the notional exceeds €500,000", "Exempt from reporting if it is exchange-traded", "Reported only by non-financial counterparties"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "EMIR requires all derivative contracts, regardless of asset class, to be reported to a trade repository, with no minimum notional exemption stated."}, {"question": "The Financial Services Act 2012 abolished the FSA and split its responsibilities between which two UK bodies?", "options": ["The FCA and the Bank of England", "The FCA and the PRA", "The PRA and ESMA", "The FCA and the CFTC"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Financial Services Act 2012 abolished the FSA, creating the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) and the Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)."}, {"question": "To be treated as an elective professional client, a retail client must satisfy the qualitative test and how many of the three quantitative criteria?", "options": ["One", "Two", "All three", "None – only the qualitative test is required"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A retail client may become an elective professional client only if the qualitative test is met AND at least two of the three quantitative criteria are satisfied."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT one of the three quantitative criteria used to assess elective professional client status?", "options": ["An average of ten significantly sized transactions per quarter over the past four quarters", "A financial portfolio exceeding €500,000", "At least one year working professionally in the financial sector requiring relevant knowledge", "A minimum net worth of £1,000,000"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The three quantitative criteria relate to transaction frequency, portfolio size (€500,000) and professional experience (one year) – there is no stated minimum net worth test."}, {"question": "Under CASS, for how long must a firm retain client categorisation records relating to insurance or MiFID business, after ceasing business with the client?", "options": ["3 years", "5 years", "Indefinitely", "7 years"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Records must be retained for 5 years for insurance or MiFID business, 3 years for other business, and indefinitely for pensions business."}, {"question": "Under CASS, client categorisation records relating to pensions business must be retained for:", "options": ["3 years", "5 years", "10 years", "Indefinitely"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Pensions business records must be retained indefinitely, reflecting the long-term nature of pension relationships."}, {"question": "Which CASS chapter sets out the client money and distribution rules for MiFID investment firms?", "options": ["CASS 6", "CASS 7", "CASS 5", "CASS 7.15"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "CASS 7 covers client money and distribution rules; CASS 6 covers custody rules for safeguarding financial instruments."}, {"question": "Under MiFID II, what is the minimum sanction for breach of international financial sanctions by a legal entity?", "options": ["€1,000,000", "5% of total annual turnover", "10% of total annual turnover", "€10,000,000 flat fee"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "MiFID II sets minimum penalties of at least 10% of total annual turnover for a legal entity, and €5,000,000 for a natural person (or twice the benefit derived, if greater)."}, {"question": "UK MiFID (the full transposition of EU MiFID via the FCA Handbook, Treasury rules and Parliamentary legislation) took effect from:", "options": ["1 January 2018", "11pm on 31 December 2020", "30 June 2028", "April 2014"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "UK MiFID was implemented from the end of the Brexit transition period, 11pm on 31 December 2020."}, {"question": "The temporary 'equivalence' granted by the EU to UK clearing houses and securities depositories has most recently been extended until:", "options": ["31 December 2025", "30 June 2026", "30 June 2028", "31 December 2030"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The equivalence arrangement, first agreed in 2018 and renewed three times (most recently in 2025), now runs until 30 June 2028."}, {"question": "In the US, which regulator has authority over options on individual stocks, stock indices and currency options traded on exchanges such as the CBOE?", "options": ["The CFTC", "The SEC", "The NFA", "FINRA"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The SEC regulates exchange-traded options on currencies, individual stocks and stock indices, and oversees exchanges such as the CBOE and ISE; it does not regulate futures."}, {"question": "Which US body regulates all futures products and exchanges, drawing its powers from the Commodity Exchange Act?", "options": ["The SEC", "The NFA", "The CFTC", "FINRA"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The CFTC regulates all futures products and exchanges (plus any options not covered by the SEC), deriving its powers from the Commodity Exchange Act (CEA)."}, {"question": "The Commodity Futures Modernization Act 2000 (CFMA) is best known for which of the following?", "options": ["Creating the SEC", "Repealing the 18-year-old ban on trading single stock futures and clarifying joint SEC/CFTC jurisdiction over them", "Establishing EMIR's central clearing obligation", "Forming the NFA in 1982"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The CFMA 2000 repealed the ban on single stock futures trading and placed single stock and narrowly defined stock-index futures under joint SEC/CFTC jurisdiction; the NFA was formed separately in 1982."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is IOSCO and what proportion of global securities markets do its members regulate?", "back": "The International Organization of Securities Commissions – an association of securities commissions/regulators. Its members regulate more than 95% of the world's securities markets."}, {"front": "What are ISDA's two key accomplishments mentioned in the syllabus?", "back": "The ISDA Master Agreement (a standardised contract for OTC derivatives transactions) and FpML (an XML message standard for the OTC derivatives industry)."}, {"front": "What are the three European Supervisory Authorities (ESAs)?", "back": "The European Banking Authority (EBA), the European Securities and Markets Authority (ESMA), and the European Insurance and Occupational Pensions Authority (EIOPA)."}, {"front": "What is ESMA's main role?", "back": "To build an EU-wide financial markets overseer: assess risks to investors/markets/stability, create a single EU rulebook, promote supervisory convergence, and directly supervise credit rating agencies and trade repositories."}, {"front": "What is 'supervisory convergence' (ESMA)?", "back": "The consistent implementation and application of the same rules using similar approaches across EU member states, involving shared best practice and efficiency gains."}, {"front": "Which four bodies regulate financial services in Hong Kong?", "back": "The Securities and Futures Commission (SFC), Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA), Office of the Commissioner of Insurance (OCI), and Mandatory Provident Fund Schemes Authority (MPFA)."}, {"front": "How does MAS in Singapore classify market operators, and what licence is needed to deal in derivatives?", "back": "MAS classifies operators as Approved Exchanges (AE, higher statutory obligations for systemically important operators) or Recognised Market Operators (RMO). Dealing in exchange-traded/OTC derivatives requires a Capital Markets Services licence."}, {"front": "Which UK bodies replaced the FSA in 2013, and under which Act?", "back": "The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) and Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA), created under the Financial Services Act 2012."}, {"front": "What are the FCA's three areas of focus?", "back": "Conduct (ethical behaviour and meeting standards), competition (encouraging healthy competition), and reducing market abuse (identifying/stopping insider dealing and manipulation)."}, {"front": "What proportion of FCA policy is based on EU initiatives, and via which plan?", "back": "Approximately 70%, primarily via the EU's Financial Services Action Plan, which includes MiFID II and the Transparency Directive (TD)."}, {"front": "What is the minimum sanction under MiFID II for breach of international financial sanctions?", "back": "At least 10% of total annual turnover for a legal entity, or €5,000,000 for a natural person (or twice the benefit derived from the violation, if higher)."}, {"front": "What is the deadline for the EU's temporary equivalence for UK clearing houses and securities depositories?", "back": "30 June 2028 (agreed 2018, renewed three times, most recently in 2025)."}, {"front": "What are the two tests a retail client must pass to become an elective professional client?", "back": "The qualitative test (firm assesses expertise/experience/knowledge) AND at least two of three quantitative criteria (10 significant transactions/quarter over 4 quarters; portfolio > €500,000; 1 year of relevant professional experience)."}, {"front": "What are the six execution factors relevant to best execution under COBS?", "back": "Price, cost, speed, likelihood of execution and settlement, size, and nature (or any other relevant consideration)."}, {"front": "How does 'suitability' differ from 'appropriateness' under COBS?", "back": "Suitability is a thorough evaluation of a client's product knowledge, financial situation and objectives (for personal recommendations/managing investments). Appropriateness is a narrower check of whether a client has sufficient knowledge/experience in a specific (often non-mainstream) instrument."}, {"front": "What do CASS 6 and CASS 7 cover respectively?", "back": "CASS 6 = custody rules for safeguarding client financial instruments. CASS 7 = client money and distribution rules."}, {"front": "What are the three obligations EMIR imposes on OTC derivative users?", "back": "(1) Report every derivative contract to a trade repository; (2) implement new risk management standards (including margining) for bilateral (non-CCP-cleared) OTC derivatives; (3) clear via a CCP those OTC derivatives subject to mandatory clearing."}, {"front": "Which US regulator covers exchange-traded options on stocks, stock indices and currencies, and which covers futures?", "back": "The SEC covers exchange-traded stock/stock-index/currency options (e.g. on the CBOE, ISE); the CFTC (with the NFA) covers all futures products/exchanges and any options not covered by the SEC."}, {"front": "What did the Commodity Futures Modernization Act 2000 (CFMA) achieve?", "back": "It repealed the 18-year ban on trading single stock futures, placed single stock/narrow stock-index futures under joint SEC/CFTC jurisdiction, reauthorised the CFTC for five years, and clarified CFTC authority over retail FX trading."}, {"front": "What are the NFA's key facts: founding year, status, and membership as of 31 March 2023?", "back": "Formed in 1982; a not-for-profit self-regulatory organisation registered by the CFTC, funded by membership dues/fees/assessments. As of 31 March 2023 it had 3,036 members and 42,163 associate members."}]}], "chapterCount": 9, "mcqCount": 222, "flashcardCount": 287};
